{"parentUuid":null,"isSidechain":true,"promptId":"624a8cec-33fa-4c0a-a445-c72ce718e6ab","agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":"You are a Flint ingest atomizer. Task: atomize *Supertraining* (Siff and Verkhoshansky, 6th Expanded Edition) Chapter 5, batch 5b: sections 5.2.4 \"Development of Explosive Strength and Reactive Ability\" and 5.2.5 \"The Development of Strength-Endurance\", PDF idx 278-297 (printed 267-286). Stop where 5.3 begins (PDF 298). Two other atomizers are working at the same time on 5.1-5.2.3 (PDF 266-277) and 5.3-5.6 (PDF 298-323); do not atomize their pages. This batch holds the shock method / depth jump / plyometric material, which matters most for Eric's football work: be especially careful with every number (drop heights, contact times, rest, volumes).\n\n## Read first, in full\n1. `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/docs/ATOMIZE_BRIEF.md` (every rule is checked by a reviewer; its paths are Windows paths, use the Mac translations below).\n2. `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/skills/ingest-book/SKILL.md` (schema, frontmatter keys, file kinds).\n3. `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/docs/method-baseline.md` (Eric's approved baseline; use as \"Eric's baseline:\" with a line locator, quoted exactly. Note it has mRSI, DSI and plyo-vs-strength positions that are directly relevant).\n4. `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/_book.md` (map; page key PDF idx = printed + 11, 0-based). Chapter 9.2 \"The Shock Method for Developing Power\" is not yet ingested; do not pre-empt it, but you may note overlaps.\n5. The last batch's review and fix log: `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/review_supertraining_ch4-3-4_opus.md` and `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/fixes_supertraining_ch4-3-4_applied.md`. Keep `proven` rare and get any physics exactly right.\n6. Two existing entries in the output folder as style references.\n\n## Mac paths\n- Vault library (read-only, dedup greps): `/Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Knowledge/library`\n- Staging library: `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library`\n- Output folder: `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/`\n- Source PDF (text layer with OCR noise): `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf`\n- Text: `~/.local/bin/uv run --with pymupdf python3 -c \"import pymupdf; d=pymupdf.open('<pdf>'); print(d[N].get_text())\"`. For every figure, table, equation or number near OCR noise, render the page (`d[N].get_pixmap(dpi=150).save('/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/_renders/5b_pN.png')`) and open it with Read. Only write \"verified against the page image\" if you did that.\n\n## Hard rules\n- Add NEW entry files only. Do NOT edit any existing entry and do NOT edit `_book.md` (the coordinator owns it). Dedup against vault and staging; depth jumps, reactivity coefficient, amortization, stretch-shortening and strength-endurance may already be covered (Supertraining Ch1-4 including `framework--reactivity-coefficient-and-depth-jump-test`, Moyer, Zatsiorsky, Evans). Add an entry only for a genuinely new angle and link the existing one in Related. List reciprocal-link suggestions in your report instead of editing other files.\n- Use the source's own examples, otherwise start the Example with \"Inference:\". No mechanism the authors do not give. Record internal contradictions with both pages and set `contested`. Locators \"PDF p. X / printed p. Y\" for every number, definition and quote. \"The authors\" unless a passage clearly belongs to one author (a passage naming Verkhoshansky in the third person is Siff writing).\n- Frontmatter `chapter:` uses the section number (e.g. \"5.2.4\"). `created: 2026-10-03`. No em dashes in titles or body (section headers keep `**What it is** —`). No review history. Never wrap a [[wikilink]] across lines.\n- If 20 pages are too many to do well, stop at a clean subsection boundary and say exactly where you stopped (printed and PDF page, heading). Quality over coverage.\n- Write a scope file `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/_scope_supertraining_ch5b.txt` listing your new filenames, one per line.\n- Lint: run `cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint && ~/.local/bin/uv run python3 staging/_tmp/lint_mac.py staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky` (a Mac-path copy of the repo lint) and fix anything flagged in YOUR files. Paste the output lines for your files.\n- No git. No vault edits.\n\n## Final reply\nEntries by kind and confidence; entry count per section (5.2.4, 5.2.5, and subsections if any) with page ranges, for the coordinator's `_book.md`; where you stopped if not at PDF 297; reciprocal-link suggestions; unclear or contradictory spots; anything you could not read; lint output.\n<context_window_protection>\n  <priority_instructions>\n    Every byte a tool returns enters your conversation memory and costs reasoning capacity for the rest of the session. The context-mode tools let you do the work in a sandbox and surface only the derived answer — the raw bytes stay out. Think-in-Code: program the analysis, do not compute it by reading raw data into your conversation.\n  </priority_instructions>\n\n  <deferred_tool_bootstrap>\n    The context-mode tools below may be DEFERRED in your harness — their schemas are not loaded yet, so calling them directly fails (e.g. \"tool not found\" / InputValidationError). Load them ONCE before your first ctx_* call:\n    ToolSearch(query: \"select:mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute,mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search,mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute,mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file,mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_fetch_and_index\")\n    After that they are callable. If any ctx_* call fails as not-found, ToolSearch it and retry — do NOT fall back to Bash/Read just because the schema was not loaded yet.\n  </deferred_tool_bootstrap>\n\n  <tool_selection_hierarchy>\n    0. MEMORY: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(sort: \"timeline\")\n       - On resume or compaction, query prior decisions, errors, plans, user prompts before asking the user — auto-captured session memory is searchable.\n    1. GATHER: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute(commands, queries)\n       - Primary research tool. Runs commands in parallel, auto-indexes each output, and (when queries are passed) returns matching sections in the same round trip — no follow-up search call.\n       - Each command: {label: \"section header\", command: \"shell command\"}; the label becomes the FTS5 chunk title — descriptive labels improve search.\n    2. FOLLOW-UP: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(queries: [\"q1\", \"q2\", ...])\n       - Multiple related questions about anything already indexed (your captures + session memory). Batch every question in one array; the ranking pipeline runs per-query and the round-trip cost is paid once.\n    3. PROCESSING: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute(language, code) | mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file(path, language, code)\n       - Derive answers FROM data: filter, count, aggregate, parse, transform. Only what you console.log() enters your conversation; the raw bytes stay in the sandbox.\n  </tool_selection_hierarchy>\n\n  <when_not_to_use>\n    - You intend to PROCESS the output (filter, count, parse, aggregate) → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute or mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute. Bash stays correct when you intend to OBSERVE a short fixed output (git status on a clean tree, whoami, pwd) or when you are mutating state (git, mkdir, rm, mv, navigation).\n    - You want to analyze, summarize, or extract from a file → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file. Read stays correct when you intend to Edit the file (Edit needs the exact bytes in your conversation to match against).\n    - WebFetch → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_fetch_and_index; full network access, results indexed for mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search, raw page bytes never enter your conversation.\n    - mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute and mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file for file writes → these run code in a subprocess and discard the sandbox FS; they are for analysis, processing, and computation only.\n  </when_not_to_use>\n\n  <file_writing_policy>\n    File writes use the native Write or Edit tool — mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute, mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file, and Bash subprocesses do not persist edits to the host filesystem.\n    Applies to all file types: code, configs, plans, specs, YAML, JSON, markdown.\n  </file_writing_policy>\n\n  <output_constraints>\n    <artifact_policy>\n      Write artifacts (code, configs, PRDs) to files. Return only: file path + 1-line description.\n    </artifact_policy>\n  </output_constraints>\n  <session_continuity>\n    Skills, roles, and decisions captured earlier in this session are a memory aid, not a standing order. Treat them as context that may help — the user's most recent message always takes precedence. If a captured directive conflicts with what the user now asks, follow the user; a past phrase does not bind you.\n  </session_continuity>\n\n</context_window_protection>"},"uuid":"ac48acf5-dae2-42b9-98b7-f81e10ed2fd3","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:44:40.988Z","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
