"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"831de3b5-6ad4-4dfc-812d-83c183d34f14","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"624a8cec-33fa-4c0a-a445-c72ce718e6ab","agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01G1B7ras1vGw1QybkKoSi6r","type":"tool_result","content":"---\ntype: book-index\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\ntitle: \"Supertraining (6th Edition, Expanded)\"\nauthor: Yuri Verkhoshansky and Mel C. Siff\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\ningest-tier: full-text\nsource: \"TB's Training Info/Verkhoshansky/Supertraining (6th Edition - Expanded Version) - Yuri Verkhoshansky & Mel Siff.pdf (Drive); staging copy md5 d3453c5e459eb08ec0a89a4300a66e54\"\ncreated: 2026-09-26\n---\n\n# Supertraining (6th Edition, Expanded) — ingest map\n\nPDF is real text: 591 pages, 576 with extractable text (`extract_pdf.py --probe`:\n`{\"pages\": 591, \"text_pages\": 576, \"scanned\": false}`), not scanned.\n\n**Print-to-PDF page offset = +11** (PDF page = printed page + 11). Verified at six\nindependent chapter/section starts, not just the offset's origin point: Ch1 (printed 1 =\nPDF 12, \"STRENGTH AND THE MUSCULAR SYSTEM\"), Ch2 (printed 95 = PDF 106, \"SPORT SPECIFIC\nSTRENGTH TRAINING\"), Ch3 (printed 125 = PDF 136, \"FACTORS INFLUENCING STRENGTH\nPRODUCTION\"), Ch4 (printed 201 = PDF 212, \"THE MEANS OF SPECIAL STRENGTH TRAINING\"), Ch7\n(printed 393 = PDF 404, \"STRENGTH TRAINING METHODS\"), and the Addendum's second section\n(printed 563 = PDF 574, \"9.2 The Shock Method for Developing Power\"). The offset holds\nunchanged from the front matter through page 574 of 591; no evidence of a mid-book\nrepagination (no unnumbered plate inserts were found at the checked points).\n\n**PDF numbers are 0-based:** the \"PDF p. N\" locators in this book's entries are the\nextractor's 0-based page index (printed + 11), matching the offset above. In a normal\n1-based PDF viewer (and the Read tool's `pages` parameter), the same page shows as\nprinted + 12, i.e. one more than the entries' \"PDF p.\" number.\n\n**Confidence rule for this book:** `proven` only for findings that are established,\nwidely-replicated physiology or mechanics (e.g. force-velocity relationship, motor unit\nrecruitment order, connective tissue loading response) independent of this text.\nSoviet-era empirical findings and training prescriptions original to Verkhoshansky, Siff,\nor the Soviet sport-science tradition they cite are capped at `supported`, even when\nstated with confidence in the text, because they are largely single-lab or\npractice-derived rather than independently replicated in the modern literature available\nto this ingest. `contested` is set where modern evidence, or another library book, takes\na different position (e.g. the two-factor fitness-fatigue model vs. classical\nsupercompensation, already flagged as a live tension in `periodization--bompa`).\n\n| # | Chapter | Printed pp | PDF idx | Status | Entries |\n|---|---------|-----------|---------|--------|---------|\n| 1 | Strength and the Muscular System | 1-94 | 12-105 | done | 41 (see section rows below) |\n| 1.intro | Objectives … Strength Deficit (unnumbered lead-in) | 1-9 | 12-20 | done | 6 |\n| 1.1 | Preliminary Issues | 10-16 | 21-27 | done | 6 |\n| 1.2 | Fundamental Biomechanics of Strength | 17-23 | 28-34 | done | 5 |\n| 1.3 | A Philosophy of Physical Training | 24-26 | 35-37 | done | 1 |\n| 1.4 | Specificity in Training | 27-31 | 38-42 | done | 3 |\n| 1.5 | Strength and Fitness | 32-32 | 43-43 | done | 1 |\n| 1.6 | The Nature of Strength | 33-35 | 44-46 | done | 3 |\n| 1.7 | The Muscle Complex | 35-48 | 46-59 | done | 4 |\n| 1.8-1.11 | Classification, Cocontraction, Types, Triphasic Nature of Muscle Action | 49-54 | 60-65 | done | 2 |\n| 1.12 | Types of Muscle Fibre | 55-64 | 66-75 | done | 2 |\n| 1.13 | The Mechanism of Muscle Growth | 65-68 | 76-79 | done | 1 |\n| 1.14 | Neurophysiological Aspects of Exercise | 69-72 | 80-83 | done | 1 |\n| 1.15 | Bioenergetics and the Energy Systems | 73-81 | 84-92 | done | 2 |\n| 1.16 | Adaptation and the Training Effect | 82-94 | 93-105 | done | 4 |\n| 2 | Sport Specific Strength Training | 95-124 | 106-135 | done | 12 (see section rows below) |\n| 2.1.1 | Increasing the Working Effect of Movements | 97-99 | 108-110 | done | 1 |\n| 2.1.2 | Perfecting the Motor Pattern of Sports Movements | 100-104 | 111-115 | done | 3 (one entry also spans 2.2.2) |\n| 2.1.3 | Perfecting the Kinesiological Pattern of Movements | 105-106 | 116-117 | done | 1 |\n| 2.2.2 | Sports Implications of Strength Indices | 109 | 120 | done | 2 (includes the entry that also spans 2.1.2) |\n| 2.2.3 | The Functional Topography of the Muscular System | 110-111 | 121-122 | done | 1 |\n| 2.2.4 | Motor Specialisation in Developing Sports Mastery | 112-115 | 123-126 | done | 1 |\n| 2.3.1 | The Structure of Physical Fitness | 116 | 127 | done | 1 |\n| 2.3.2 | The Interrelation between Motor Abilities | 117-119 | 128-130 | done | 2 |\n| 2.3.4 | General Concepts of the Structure of Physical Fitness | 120-124 | 131-135 | done | 1 |\n| 3 | Factors Influencing Strength Production | 125-200 | 136-211 | done | 60 (see section rows below) |\n| 3.1 | Regimes of Muscular Work | 125-128 | 136-139 | done | 2 |\n| 3.2.1 | Explosive Strength | 129-141 | 140-152 | done | 5 |\n| 3.2.2 | Strength-Endurance | 142-143 | 153-154 | done | 1 |\n| 3.3.1-3.3.9 | Influence of External Conditions on Strength (pre-working state through the secular increase in strength) | 144-161 | 155-172 | done | 13 |\n| 3.4.1 | The Warm-up and Pre-Activity Preparation | 162-163 | 173-174 | done | 1 |\n| 3.4.2 | The After-Effect of Muscle Activity | 164-166 | 175-177 | done | 2 |\n| 3.4.3 | Additional Movement | 166-167 | 177-178 | done | 1 |\n| 3.4.4 | Preparatory Movement | 167 | 178 | done | 0 (no new angle beyond existing entries; deferred to Ch3a [[claim--pre-working-muscle-state-ranks-performance]] and Ch1 [[principle--short-amortization-preserves-elastic-energy]]; see dedup note) |\n| 3.4.5 | Coordination in Muscular Work | 167-168 | 178-179 | done | 1 |\n| 3.4.6 | Efficiency of Energy Expenditure | 168-170 | 179-181 | done | 1 |\n| 3.4.7 | Emotion and Other Psychological Factors | 170-171 | 181-182 | done | 2 |\n| 3.4.8 | The Effect of Cold Application | 171-172 | 182-183 | done | 1 |\n| 3.4.9 | Breathing and Strength Production | 172-173 | 183-184 | done | 1 |\n| 3.4.10 | Strength Development and Proprioception | 173-174 | 184-185 | done | 1 |\n| 3.5.1 | A Definition of Flexibility | 174-176 | 185-187 | done | 3 |\n| 3.5.2 | The Effects of Stretching | 176 | 187 | done | 1 |\n| 3.5.3 | The Neuromuscular Component of Flexibility | 176-179 | 187-190 | done | 2 |\n| 3.5.4 | Components of Joint Flexibility | 179 | 190 | done | 1 |\n| 3.5.5 | Parameters of Flexibility | 179-180 | 190-191 | done | 1 |\n| 3.5.6 | Soft Tissue Biomechanics and Flexibility | 180-182 | 191-193 | done | 5 |\n| 3.5.7 | The Influence of Exercise on Connective Tissue | 182 | 193 | done | 1 |\n| 3.5.8 | Stretching Techniques | 182-187 | 193-198 | done | 6 (1 more entry for the \"Is Stretching Always Necessary?\" coda is filed under chapter 3.6, printed 192-193, since it falls in that section's page range; see 3.6 row) |\n| 3.5.9 | Low Flexibility versus Non-functional Muscle Tension | 187-188 | 198-199 | done | 2 |\n| 3.6 | The Stretching Matrix System (incl. \"Is Stretching Always Necessary?\") | 188-193 | 199-204 | done | 2 |\n| 3.7 | The Movement Matrix System | 193-200 | 204-211 | done | 4 |\n| 4 | The Means of Special Strength Training | 201-254 | 212-265 | done | 64 (see section rows below) |\n| 4.1 | The Problem of Training Means (4.1.1 Characteristics of Strength Increase, 4.1.2 The Effect of the Strength Training Means) | 201-206 | 212-217 | done | 6 |\n| 4.2 | Different Means of Strength Development (4.2.1 Electrostimulation through 4.2.9 Muscle Isolation) | 207-241 | 218-252 | done | 35 (see section rows below) |\n| 4.2.1 | Electrostimulation (incl. the unnumbered 4.2 lead-in classifying the four stimulation mechanisms, printed 207) | 207-216 | 218-227 | done | 8 |\n| 4.2.2 | Resistance and Strength Training | 216-220 | 227-231 | done | 4 |\n| 4.2.3 | Kinetic Energy and Strength Processes | 220-223 | 231-234 | done | 3 |\n| 4.2.4 | Isometric Training | 223-230 | 234-241 | done | 7 |\n| 4.2.5 | Eccentric Training | 230-231 | 241-242 | done | 2 |\n| 4.2.6 | Isokinetic and Other Training Means (4.2.6.1-4.2.6.4) | 231-237 | 242-248 | done | 6 |\n| 4.2.7 | The Use of Training Machines (4.2.7.1-4.2.7.5) | 237-240 | 248-251 | done | 3 |\n| 4.2.8 | The Concept of Symmetric Training | 240-241 | 251-252 | done | 1 |\n| 4.2.9 | The Concept of Muscle Isolation | 241 | 252 | done | 1 |\n| 4.3 | Dynamic Correspondence as a Means of Strength | 241-247 | 252-258 | done | 13 |\n| 4.4 | Strength Training and General Endurance | 248-254 | 259-265 | done | 10 |\n| 5 | The Methods of Special Strength Training | 255-312 | 266-323 | pending | 0 |\n| 6 | Organisation of Training | 313-392 | 324-403 | pending | 0 |\n| 7 | Strength Training Methods | 393-420 | 404-431 | pending | 0 |\n| 8 | Designing Sport Specific Strength Programmes | 421-480 | 432-491 | pending | 0 |\n| — | References and Bibliography | 481-494 | 492-505 | skip | n/a (reference list, not atomized) |\n| — | Index | 495-502 | 506-513 | skip | n/a |\n| 9 | Addendum (9.1 Laws of Sports Mastery, 9.2 The Shock Method for Developing Power) | 503-579 (verify) | 514-590 | pending | 0 |\n\nThis row-level table is the resume checkpoint: each 1.x row is set to `done` with its\nreal entry count immediately after that section is atomized, never batched.\n\n_Batch boundary: Chapter 2 is done (12 entries). Chapter 3 is now fully done: sections 3.1\nthrough 3.4.10 (32 entries, prior session) plus 3.5 Flexibility and Sporting Performance\nthrough 3.5.9, 3.6 The Stretching Matrix System, and 3.7 The Movement Matrix System (28\nentries, this session covering printed 174-200, PDF idx 185-211) for 60 entries total in\nChapter 3. One dedup note from this session: the 3.5.6 restatement (printed 181) of \"bony\ninsertion weakest at slow loading rate, soft tissue weakest at fast loading rate\" was NOT\nre-atomized, since it duplicates [[claim--collagen-loading-curve-and-injury-risk]] (Ch1,\nKennedy 1976/Frankel & Nordin 1980); only the genuinely new adjacent detail (the graded\nbone-tendon transitional-stiffness system and its disruption by anabolic steroids) got its\nown entry, [[claim--tendon-insertion-transitional-stiffness-gradient-disrupted-by-steroids]].\nChapters 4-9 remain pending for later sessions; resume at Chapter 4, The Means of Special\nStrength Training (printed 201, PDF idx 212)._\n\n_Batch boundary, this session: Chapter 4 section 4.1 \"The Problem of Training Means\" (4.1.1\nCharacteristics of Strength Increase, 4.1.2 The Effect of the Strength Training Means;\nprinted 201-206, PDF idx 212-217) is done, 6 entries. The short unnumbered lead-in before\n4.1 (page 201/PDF 212, history-of-the-field narrative) produced no entry of its own; it is\nscene-setting with no discrete claim. One dedup note: the 4.1.1 passage at printed 202\n(PDF 213) repeats, via the same Verkhoshansky 1977 citation chain, the\nKorobkov/Gerasimov/Vasiliev \"strength gains are roughly uniform at 20/40/60/80% of 1RM in\nearly training\" finding and the Hettinger & Muller (1953) one-third-max threshold finding\nalready captured in [[claim--strength-training-threshold-stimulus]] (Ch1, 1.2); that\nidentical material was NOT re-atomized. The genuinely new adjacent material in 4.1.1\n(Zimkin 1961 and Muller & Hettinger 1957 training-age-dependent load percentages;\nChernyavsky 1966 light/heavy load-proportion result) became\n[[claim--strength-and-speed-training-response-shifts-with-training-age]]. Two further new\nclaim entries cover delayed onset of strength gains\n([[claim--delayed-strength-training-effect-lag-before-gains-appear]]) and detraining/\nretention timelines, including an unreconciled internal contradiction between the\nChernyavsky 20-session decay curve and the Vasiliev/McMorris & Eklins 40-session retention\ndata ([[claim--strength-detraining-and-retention-timelines]]). Section 4.1.2's \"Algebraic\nRelations\" passage (Commutative/Associative Laws applied to training order) and its\nAcute/Delayed Training Effect and Particular/Cumulative Effect taxonomy became two framework\nentries ([[framework--non-commutative-non-associative-training-order]],\n[[framework--acute-delayed-particular-cumulative-training-effect-taxonomy]]); the remaining\n\"Steps Towards Structuring the Training Process\" material (training load's five\ncharacteristics; volume's two functions; structured system beats random combination even at\nlower volume, citing Verkhoshansky 1966/1970, Tatyan 1974, Khodykin 1975; Figure 4.1,\nverified against the page image) became\n[[heuristic--structured-load-progression-beats-volume-alone-or-random-combination]]. Chapter\n4 sections 4.2 (Different Means of Strength Development: electrostimulation, resistance and\nkinetic-energy training, isometric, eccentric, isokinetic, machines, symmetric training,\nmuscle isolation), 4.3 (Dynamic Correspondence) and 4.4 (Strength Training and General\nEndurance) remain pending; resume at 4.2, printed 207 / PDF idx 218, \"4.2 Different Means of\nStrength Development\" / \"4.2.1 Electrostimulation.\"_\n\n_Batch boundary, this session: Chapter 4 section 4.2 \"Different Means of Strength\nDevelopment\" is now fully done, all nine subsections (4.2.1 Electrostimulation through 4.2.9\nMuscle Isolation; printed 207-241, PDF idx 218-252), 35 entries total (8+4+3+7+2+6+3+1+1 per\nthe section rows above). Every table and figure encountered was opened as a page image\n(Figs 4.2, 4.3, 4.6, 4.7, 4.8, 4.11, 4.12, 4.13, 4.14, 4.16) before being cited; Figs 4.4,\n4.5, 4.9 and 4.10 are also cited and were checked against the page images (PDF idx 230 and\n234). Fig 4.15 (PDF idx 239, isometric endurance against forearm-immersion temperature,\nClarke et al 1958; its caption is image-only) is cited in\n[[claim--isometric-endurance-limits-and-recovery-phases]] and was checked against the page\nimage. Voice: 4.2.1\nElectrostimulation has first-person Siff material only on printed p.215: \"the ES training\napplied to the author by Serge Reding\" (Siff as the subject) and \"Work that I witnessed\" in\nRatov's Moscow laboratory. The printed p.209 Reding passage names Siff in the third person\n(\"one of the authors (Siff)\"), and the printed p.208 Ratov passage is the authors'.\n[[case-study--reding-electrostimulation-protocol-siff-account]] and\n[[claim--ratov-functional-electrostimulation-emg-triggered-sport-specific]] attribute each\npassage accordingly. The MACS/MICS \"integrated theory\" of\nelectrostimulation (4.2.1.4-4.2.1.5, citing Becker, Nordenstrom and Pilla on bioelectric\ncell-signalling) is rated `contested`, with its mainstream-status judgment labelled\nInference, rather than presented as settled physiology;\nsee [[claim--macs-mics-integrated-theory-of-electrostimulation]]. Two forward/backward\ncross-references the book itself makes were completed as reciprocal links: the 4.2.2 passage\non load-dependent proximal/distal muscle recruitment explicitly forward-references 4.2.9\n(now [[claim--muscle-recruitment-consistency-and-proximal-distal-shift-with-load]] linked to\n[[claim--muscle-isolation-is-load-dependent-mostly-illusory-under-heavy-load]]), and the\nCh3.3.4.4 entry [[claim--natural-asymmetry-caution-against-isokinetic-symmetry-correction]]\nexplicitly deferred \"symmetric training\" to 4.2.8, now fulfilled by\n[[claim--pursuing-perfect-bilateral-symmetry-can-be-counterproductive]] (reciprocal link\nrecommended on the Ch3 entry, not made since it is outside this session's assigned\nwrite-scope). [[heuristic--isokinetic-testing-limitations-for-functional-sport-specificity]]\n(4.2.6.2) substantially extends the existing Ch2 entry\n[[heuristic--isometric-dynamometry-misrepresents-dynamic-functional-strength]] with a\ndetailed, itemised mechanism list; a reciprocal link on that Ch2 entry is recommended but not\nmade (outside this session's write-scope). No entries were needed for chapter 4's own\nelectrostimulation/isometric/eccentric/isokinetic/symmetric-training/muscle-isolation\nconcepts already covered elsewhere in this folder or in the vault; dedup was checked against\nMoyer's `framework--isometric-training-variables-and-adaptations` (vault, a distinct\ndose-variable framework, not duplicated), Handford 2024's eccentric-training-methods paper\n(vault, not duplicated, no direct overlap found), and Bishop 2016's asymmetry paper (vault,\nalready linked from the Ch3 entry this session's 4.2.8 entry completes). Chapter 4 sections\n4.3 (Dynamic Correspondence as a Means of Strength) and 4.4 (Strength Training and General\nEndurance) remain pending; resume at 4.3, printed 241 / PDF idx 252, \"4.3 Dynamic\nCorrespondence as a Means of Strength\" / \"4.3.1 The Amplitude and Direction of Movement.\"_\n\n_Batch boundary, this session: Chapter 4 sections 4.3 \"Dynamic Correspondence as a Means of\nStrength Training\" (4.3.1-4.3.6, including the \"Parachute Resistance Training\" subsection;\nprinted 241-248, PDF idx 252-259) and 4.4 \"Strength Training and General Endurance\" (with its\nunnumbered subsections Oxidative Capacity and Muscular Endurance, Strength Training and General\nEndurance, The Process of Functional Specialisation, Factor Analysis; printed 248-253, PDF idx\n259-264) are done: 13 and 10 entries. Two page notes for the rows above: 4.3.6 runs onto\nprinted 248 (PDF 259), where 4.4 starts mid-page; and printed 254 (PDF 265) is blank. Figs\n4.17-4.24 and Tables 4.1-4.2 were checked against page images (PDF 253-257, 260-264).\nSupertraining had no entry for the five dynamic-correspondence criteria themselves (only the\nCh1.4 ten-dimension specificity entry and Evans's applied case study);\n[[framework--five-criteria-of-dynamic-correspondence]] is now the anchor. Contested in this\nbatch: [[claim--endurance-rests-on-muscle-oxidative-and-strength-qualities-not-vo2max]]\n(raise VO2 max p.249 vs not p.250; less glycolysis p.249 vs high glycolytic productivity\np.253) and [[claim--strength-training-raises-haemoglobin-myoglobin-and-aids-distance-sport]]\n(Pattengale & Holloszy 1967 is a treadmill-running study by its title). Chapter 4 is now\ncomplete; resume at Chapter 5, The Methods of Special Strength Training, printed 255 / PDF idx\n266._\n_book.md\ncase-study--high-jumper-factor-structure-by-qualification.md\ncase-study--reding-electrostimulation-protocol-siff-account.md\ncase-study--skater-factor-structure-by-qualification.md\nclaim--400m-stride-length-held-by-local-strength-endurance.md\nclaim--absolute-leg-strength-connects-to-sprint-speed-only-indirectly-via-jump.md\nclaim--active-flexibility-correlates-more-with-sporting-proficiency-than-passive.md\nclaim--adaptive-reconstruction-vs-supercompensation.md\nclaim--all-round-athletes-main-regime-cyclic-and-phasic-tonic.md\nclaim--average-or-peak-force-times-velocity-is-not-power.md\nclaim--bilateral-deficit-and-limb-training-specificity.md\nclaim--collagen-loading-curve-and-injury-risk.md\nclaim--continuous-vs-interval-circuit-training-for-concurrent-goals.md\nclaim--delayed-strength-training-effect-lag-before-gains-appear.md\nclaim--differential-tissue-adaptation-rates.md\nclaim--eccentric-strength-and-energetics-vs-concentric.md\nclaim--eccentric-training-damage-adaptation-and-injury-risk-tradeoff.md\nclaim--eccentric-vs-concentric-training-adaptation-contradictory-findings.md\nclaim--endurance-rests-on-muscle-oxidative-and-strength-qualities-not-vo2max.md\nclaim--energy-system-contribution-by-sport-table.md\nclaim--exercise-economy-improves-with-skill-and-interval-style-costs-more.md\nclaim--female-male-strength-ratio-varies-by-muscle-group-and-bodymass.md\nclaim--fiber-recruitment-not-simply-graded-by-force.md\nclai