,"attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"11af9c66-5359-4dc9-a6e5-f18e6e809dad","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:44:56.004Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"11af9c66-5359-4dc9-a6e5-f18e6e809dad","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"624a8cec-33fa-4c0a-a445-c72ce718e6ab","agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014FLoKoJfA8TyMGUWuiAXRE","type":"tool_result","content":"# Fixes applied: Supertraining 4.3-4.4 (Ch4-3-4), 23 entries\n\nFixer: Claude Opus. Date: 2026-10-03. Review: `staging/review_supertraining_ch4-3-4_opus.md` (round 1, GO AFTER FIXES: 8 entry FIX rows plus 1 `_book.md` FIX).\nSource checked: `staging/books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf`. I extracted the text of PDF idx 254, 255, 256, 257, 258, 260, 263 and 264 with PyMuPDF. I rendered page images for PDF 255, 256, 257, 258, 260, 263 and 264 to `staging/_renders/fix_p<N>.png` and opened PDF 256 (Fig 4.22 and its passage), PDF 257 (power equations, Eq 4.1) and PDF 264 (Table 4.2). Page key: PDF idx = printed + 11.\nScope: the 23 files in `staging/_scope_supertraining_ch4-3-4.txt`; only the 8 flagged entries and `_book.md` were edited. No new entries were written, so the scope file is unchanged.\n\n| file | what changed |\n|---|---|\n| claim--power-equals-force-times-velocity-only-when-force-is-constant.md | **Title** is now \"The Authors Warn That P = F x V Is Misapplied: Average or Peak Force Times Velocity Is Not Power When Force Varies; Use the Force-Time Curve, a Force Plate or High-Speed Video\". The slug is unchanged, so the one inbound link (RFD entry) still resolves.<br>**What it is:** the scope line now reads \"Scope of `proven`: only the identity P = dW/dt = F·v, which holds instant by instant for any force (Inference, see Why it works)\". It says the authors' constant-force statement is imprecise and is not part of that scope. Power is now quoted as \"The rate of doing work at any instant\" (PDF 257 image).<br>**Why it works:** rewritten in full. The old first sentence (\"This is calculus, and the authors state it ... wrong instantaneous picture\") endorsed the constant-force claim in the writer's voice. Now:<br>(a) The authors' two sentences are quoted with the locator (\"if F is constant over the interval concerned\"; \"If the force is not constant ... equation (4.1)\").<br>(b) The scope sentence follows.<br>(c) An Inference gives the physics: dW/dt = F·ds/dt = F·v at every instant. Constant force is needed only when one force value stands for a whole interval. What fails is multiplying an average or peak force by an average or peak velocity (the average of a product is not the product of averages, and the peaks need not coincide). The authors' \"It is inadequate to refer to average or peak force alone\" is quoted as the point the warning targets.<br>**When NOT:** \"The physics is sound; the wording around it is loose.\" is replaced by \"The authors' statement that P = F.V needs constant force is imprecise (see What it is); their practical warning against single average or peak values stands.\"<br>**Confidence:** `confidence: proven` kept, scoped to the instantaneous identity only (textbook mechanics, which the brief allows as `proven`). |\n| framework--five-criteria-of-dynamic-correspondence.md | **When NOT:** the Inference \"when the criteria conflict (for example ... breaks the regime of work ...)\" is replaced. The new text says the book gives no ranking among the criteria, then quotes three sentences from PDF 256 / p.245: the \"incongruous and artificial (Fig 4.22)\" sentence, \"this occurs only when an exercise is attempted which fails to take into consideration the appropriate criteria\", and that the Fig 4.22 exercise \"may be appropriate if applied carefully\". Then: \"So they do not treat the criteria as conflicting. Inference: in practice, meeting all five at once may still force a compromise; the book does not address that case.\" |\n| heuristic--train-force-at-the-accentuated-joint-angle.md | **What it is:** the Fig 4.22 paragraph now quotes the full \"can sometimes lead to such initial positions using weights or pulley machines which are incongruous and artificial (Fig 4.22)\" sentence. It then adds the dropped \"However, this occurs only when an exercise is attempted which fails to take into consideration the appropriate criteria\" (PDF 256 / p.245). It also quotes the text's own account of the two partners (\"simulates the vertical loading which sprinting normally imposes\"; \"intended to strengthen the power of the hip flexors\"). The caption and \"applied carefully\" sentences are kept. |\n| heuristic--parachute-resistance-sizing-progression-and-contrast-release.md | **Why it works:** first sentence replaced. It now quotes the authors' \"increases rapidly with velocity (actually the square of the velocity)\" and \"allows us to use these devices more knowledgeably\", plus the next sentence on the page, \"Naturally, running speed determines the degree of drag resistance\" (PDF 255 / p.244), so the size-and-speed point is in their words. \"standard fluid mechanics\" is removed. The new Inference reads: \"velocity-squared drag is the textbook drag equation, and this relation is the only part of the entry that could rate `proven`\". It is merged with the existing Inference, so there is one, not two. |\n| claim--parachute-resistance-tradeoffs-for-dynamic-correspondence.md | **When to apply:** the populations are now labelled: \"(Inference: for example, heavy athletes or athletes returning from lower-limb injury; the book names no population)\". \"(hurdlers, jumpers)\" is changed to \"(the book names hurdlers and jumpers)\". |\n| heuristic--same-exercise-different-regime-boxer-vs-shot-putter.md | **When to apply:** now reads \"single fast actions against a set resistance call for heavier loads and few repetitions (Inference: with full recovery between efforts; the book does not state rest).\" This matches the source's \"rapid single movements against a specific resistance\" (PDF 258 / p.247). |\n| case-study--high-jumper-factor-structure-by-qualification.md | **When to apply:** \"and less to plantarflexor power\" is removed. Added: \"Inference: the falling plantarflexor share may justify a smaller plantarflexor emphasis, but the book reports a factor share, not a training reduction.\" |\n| case-study--skater-factor-structure-by-qualification.md | **When NOT:** added \"Class 1 factor IV (2.7) is smaller than factor V (8.9), against the order of factors by share (verified against the page image, PDF p. 264 / printed p. 253)\", followed by a labelled Inference: the value may be a misprint, with the arithmetic (35.9 + 23.3 + 16.8 + 12.7 + 8.9 = 97.6), so treat 2.7 as unclear.<br>**When to apply:** the Inference no longer quotes \"2.7% to 36.5%\". It now says the rise to first factor at Master of Sport (36.5%) is consistent with the teaching point, and that the Class 1 value is unclear, \"so the size of the change should not be quoted.\" The table keeps the printed 2.7. |\n| _book.md | **Line 96:** now reads `| 4 | The Means of Special Strength Training | 201-254 | 212-265 | done | 64 (see section rows below) |`. 64 = 6 (4.1) + 35 (4.2) + 13 (4.3) + 10 (4.4). The 4.2.x sub-rows sum to 35. A count of `chapter: \"4` frontmatter on disk also gives 64 (6/35/13/10).<br>**Batch note:** deleted the sentence \"The Chapter 4 parent row still reads \"in progress \\| 6\" and was not changed in this session.\" No other line touched. |\n\n## Disagreements with the review\n\n1. **Power entry: the review's scope sentence is not used word for word.** The review says the constant-force form \"is true only when one force value stands for the whole interval\". That \"only\" is too strong: average power also equals average force times velocity when velocity is constant. I wrote \"Constant force is needed only when one force value stands for a whole interval: then power over the interval equals that F times the average velocity\", and put it inside the Inference because it is my physics, not the authors'. I also rewrote the first sentence of Why it works, which the review's fix (3) left in place. That sentence (\"This is calculus, and the authors state it: P = F.V is the special case ... with constant force\") still endorsed the imprecise claim in the writer's voice. The substance agrees with the review: the authors' constant-force condition is imprecise, and their warning about average or peak values stands.\n2. **Five-criteria entry: I did not adopt the review's paraphrase that \"awkward positions such as Fig 4.22 arise only when ...\".** The source reads two ways. It calls the Fig 4.22 positions \"incongruous and artificial\". It says that happens \"only when\" the criteria are ignored. It then says the Fig 4.22 exercise itself \"may be appropriate if applied carefully\" (PDF 256 / p.245, text and image). So the figure is both an example of awkward loading and a usable exercise. I quoted all three sentences rather than having the entry say Fig 4.22 is the ignored-criteria case. I agree with the review's core point: the authors deny a conflict among the criteria, and the old \"breaks the regime of work\" reading is not in the source.\n3. **Skater entry: misprint speculation labelled.** The review's text gives the \"2.7 may be a misprint (12.7 would restore the order ...)\" reasoning unlabelled. That is reviewer or fixer reasoning, so it is now an \"Inference:\" and carries the column sum.\n4. **Parachute sizing: one extra quote.** I added the \"Naturally, running speed determines the degree of drag resistance\" sentence (PDF 255 / p.244). Without it, \"so parachute size and running speed together set the load\" would sit as unlabelled writer voice.\n5. **OBLA (optional missing idea): not added as an entry.** Existing entries already cover it.\n   - `claim--endurance-rests-on-muscle-oxidative-and-strength-qualities-not-vo2max` quotes \"Since OBLA depends on VO2 max, it is considered useful to strive to raise VO2 max ...\" (contested list) and the Christensen & Hogberg 1950 \"approximately 50% more effective\" sentence (When NOT).\n   - `claim--local-blood-flow-not-vo2max-tracks-competition-period-endurance-gains` quotes \"OBLA (lactate threshold) and VO2 max levels can change independently\" and \"OBLA can rise\" while VO2 max falls.\n   - The only uncaptured sentence is the definitional opener (PDF 260 / printed p. 249): \"Practical improvement of the metabolic processes in endurance sports is associated with increasing the potential to utilise aerobic sources of energy by raising the Onset of Blood Lactate Accumulation (OBLA), i.e. that level of oxygen consumption at which the anaerobic processes begin to be prominently activated.\"\n   - A stand-alone entry would mostly repeat those quotes. The sentence belongs in the What it is of the endurance entry at a later pass. That entry is GO, so it is outside this fix scope.\n\nNo review claim was found false on the page. Every FIX row's problem quote and source quote matched the extracted text, and the Table 4.2 and Eq 4.1 readings matched the rendered images.\n\n## Checks\n\nGrep proof, run from the book folder (one line per fixed file, plus counts of deleted phrases):\n\n```\nclaim--power-equals-force-times-velocity-only-when-force-is-constant.md:14:# The Authors Warn That P = F x V Is Misapplied\nclaim--power-equals-force-times-velocity-only-when-force-is-constant.md:16:Scope of `proven`: only the identity P = dW/dt = F·v, which holds instant by instant for any force (Inference, see Why it works)\nclaim--power-equals-force-times-velocity-only-when-force-is-constant.md:18:Scope of `proven`: only the identity P = dW/dt = F·v, which holds instant by instant for any force\nclaim--power-equals-force-times-velocity-only-when-force-is-constant.md:18:Inference: because dW = F·ds, the rate of work is dW/dt = F·ds/dt = F·v at every instant, whatever the force history, so the instantaneous product F(t)·v(t) is exact\nclaim--power-equals-force-times-velocity-only-when-force-is-constant.md:22:The authors' statement that P = F.V needs constant force is imprecise (see What it is)\ndeleted phrase count: 0 / 0      (\"gives a wrong instantaneous picture\" / \"The physics is sound\")\nframework--five-criteria-of-dynamic-correspondence.md:29:So they do not treat the criteria as conflicting. Inference: in practice, meeting all five at once may still force a compromise; the book does not address that case\nold phrase count: 0              (\"when the criteria conflict\")\nheuristic--train-force-at-the-accentuated-joint-angle.md:18:but add: \"However, this occurs only when an exercise is attempted which fails to take into consideration the appropriate criteria\nheuristic--parachute-resistance-sizing-progression-and-contrast-release.md:28:Inference: velocity-squared drag is the textbook drag equation, and this relation is the only part of the entry that could rate `proven`\nold phrase count: 0              (\"standard fluid mechanics\")\nclaim--parachute-resistance-tradeoffs-for-dynamic-correspondence.md:25:(Inference: for example, heavy athletes or athletes returning from lower-limb injury; the book names no population)\nheuristic--same-exercise-different-regime-boxer-vs-shot-putter.md:20:(Inference: with full recovery between efforts; the book does not state rest)\ncase-study--high-jumper-factor-structure-by-qualification.md:30:Inference: the falling plantarflexor share may justify a smaller plantarflexor emphasis, but the book reports a factor share, not a training reduction\ncase-study--skater-factor-structure-by-qualification.md:32:Class 1 factor IV (2.7) is smaller than factor V (8.9), against the order of factors by share (verified against the page image, PDF p\ncase-study--skater-factor-structure-by-qualification.md:30:so the size of the change should not be quoted\nold phrase count: 0              (\"2.7% to 36.5%\")\n_book.md:96:| 4 | The Means of Special Strength Training | 201-254 | 212-265 | done | 64 (see section rows below) |\nremoved sentence count: 0        (\"was not changed in this session\")\n```\n\nMechanical greps over all 23 scoped files (run under bash with `F=$(cat ../../../_scope_supertraining_ch4-3-4.txt | tr -d \"\\r\")`; the body em-dash check strips the `**Header** —` prefix with sed and then greps for the dash, because macOS grep has no `-P`):\n\n##### framework--reactivity-coefficient-and-depth-jump-test.md\n---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"3.2.1\"\ntopic: reactive ability testing\ncategory: [testing, plyometrics, reactive-strength]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-09-26\n---\n\n# The Reactivity Coefficient (RC) and Verkhoshansky's Depth-Jump Reactivity Test\n\n**What it is** — Reactive ability (the neuromuscular capacity to generate explosive force\nvia a preliminary stretch immediately before concentric work) is quantified with a\n**Reactivity Coefficient**, which the text defines three ways without fully reconciling\nthem: earlier (2.2.1, printed 108) as \"the explosive strength index relative to body\nweight,\" RC = Fmax/(tmax.W) = RFDmax/W; here, the lab-only form RC = K2/K1 (a ratio of two\ncharacteristics of muscular work, e.g. power, before and after the stretch), and the\nfield-practical form **RC = Fmax / (tmax x m x g)**, where Fmax is peak force, tmax is time\nto reach it, m is the moving mass (or bodymass), and g is gravitational acceleration. Despite\nthat formula, the text separately states that in the reactivity test itself, \"the\ncoefficient of reactivity was determined from the length of the support period and analysis\nof the mean take-off force\" (printed 137), not a literal Fmax/tmax calculation; the text\ndoes not explain how the two are reconciled. Verkhoshansky's own \"reactivity test\" protocol:\na series of vertical jumps without arm-swing, performed after a depth-jump from 0.4 m,\nunloaded and then with 10, 20, 30, and 40 kg added (fixed absolute loads, not expressed as a\npercentage of the athlete's So); jump height and support-period duration are recorded and\nplotted against added mass. Findings from this\nprotocol: reactive ability correlates with strength most strongly at large loads (R = 0.95\nat 30-40 kg loads, weak at lighter loads); RC correlates more strongly with support-period\ntime than with force in every load condition; absolute strength has a *negative* effect on\ncontraction speed with small/no added load but a *positive* effect with large added load\n(40 kg), while high *relative* strength has the opposite pattern (positive with light\nloads, negative with heavy loads); and cross-sport testing (Fig 3.9-3.10) found jumpers\nhad the highest RC and middle-distance runners the lowest, with RC showing a strong linear\nrelationship (R ≈ 0.95) to triple-jump performance specifically. (PDF pp. 146-149, printed\npp. 135-138, \"Quickness and Reactive Ability,\" Figs 3.7-3.10.)\n\n**Why it works** — The stated mechanism: a preliminary stretch elastically deforms the\nmuscle-tendon complex, storing potential energy that converts to kinetic energy on the\nsubsequent contraction, adding to active muscular force. **Inference:** RC's specific\nformula (force over time-to-peak times system mass) is not explained sentence-by-sentence\nin the text beyond this general stretch-energy mechanism; the fact that it tracks support-period\ntime more tightly than raw force output is reported as an empirical correlation finding,\nnot derived from a stated causal mechanism.\n\n**When to apply** — Use the RC formula's logic (peak force relative to time-to-peak and\nsystem mass) as the conceptual ancestor of modern reactive strength index (RSI) metrics.\n**Eric's baseline:** modified RSI (mRSI) from daily CMJ testing is already the department's\nchosen daily-readiness marker on ForceDecks (`method-baseline.md`, \"Force plate and\nneuromuscular monitoring,\" citing `2026-02-19-sportsmith-6otb-er.md`). **Inference:** this\nframework is a plausible historical/conceptual ancestor of that metric (RC = F/(t x m x g)\nversus mRSI = jump height / time to take-off, not contact time); this lineage claim is not\nstated in either source. The load-dependent strength-speed finding here\n(absolute strength helps at heavy loads, hurts at light loads; relative strength does the\nreverse) is a useful interpretive lens for reading an individual athlete's mRSI trend\nalongside their absolute- and relative-strength testing numbers.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — This is a single-lab (Verkhoshansky) protocol using a specific\n0.4 m depth-jump height and 10-40 kg external loading increments on a now-dated athlete\npopulation; the precise correlation values (R=0.95, etc.) should not be assumed to hold for\na different depth, load range, or sport population without direct testing. It is also not a\nplyometric-prescription framework by itself: it measures reactive ability, it does not\nprescribe how to train it (see [[framework--shock-method-and-plyometric-terminology]] for\nthat).\n\n**Example** — **Inference:** two athletes with identical CMJ height can have very different\nRC/RSI: Verkhoshansky's data implies the athlete whose reactive ability holds up (or\nimproves) as external load increases from 0 to 40 kg is a different training case than one\nwhose reactive ability only shows up unloaded. The former likely has more absolute strength\nto draw on, the latter is leaning more on relative strength/elasticity. This example is not\nin the source.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--shock-method-and-plyometric-terminology]] (Ch1, the plyometric\ntraining method this test is designed to evaluate); [[framework--strength-deficit-jump-comparison-estimate]]\n(Ch1, a related but distinct static-vs-countermovement jump comparison for a different\npurpose, estimating strength deficit rather than reactive ability); [[supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--optimal-depth-jump-drop-height-for-power-vs-force]]: Ch4.2.3, variable drop heights and loaded depth jumps, Figs 4.9-4.10; [[heuristic--rfd-as-correspondence-criterion-and-impulse-cannot-compare-movements]] (4.3.4, which lists RC in its RFDmax/W form beside power and impulse as measures of explosive strength, and rejects impulse for comparing different movements)\n##### framework--shock-method-and-plyometric-terminology.md\n---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"1.6\"\ntopic: shock method and plyometrics\ncategory: [plyometrics, terminology, stretch-shortening-cycle]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-09-26\n---\n\n# The Shock Method (Original Russian \"Udarnyi Metod\"), and Why \"Plyometrics\" Is a Loose Translation\n\n**What it is** — Verkhoshansky developed the \"shock method\" (udarnyi metod) as a special\nspeed-strength training method exploiting connective and elastic tissue's ability to\nstore and release elastic energy during stretch-shortening of the muscle complex (running,\njumping, throwing, rapid lifting). The rise in concentric strength after rapid \"shock\"\nloading comes from two sources: increased muscle tension from the myotatic (stretch)\nreflex, and explosive release of elastic energy stored in connective/elastic tissue during\nthe eccentric phase. Historically, \"pliometric\" \"was apparently coined to mean eccentric\ncontraction\"; in this scheme of nomenclature (the text does not state it to be\nspecifically Russian), isometric retained its meaning of static contraction, miometric\nmeant concentric contraction, and pliometric meant eccentric contraction (printed 34); the\nterm \"plyometrics,\" as adopted in the West, has drifted to describe a broader category of\njumping drills, many of which do not reproduce true shock-method characteristics. The\ntext's precise redefinition: the term plyometrics should refer to \"an eccentric muscle\ncontraction ... suddenly terminated in an explosive isometric contraction, thereby\nproducing a powerful myotatic reflex, a sharp extension of the passive components of the\nmuscle complex and a subsequent explosive concentric contraction,\" and \"should not refer\nto ordinary jumping\" (printed 34-35). (PDF pp. 45-46, printed pp. 34-35.)\n\n**Why it works** — Inference: the stretch-shortening cycle mechanism (reflex potentiation\nplus elastic energy return) is documented in modern SSC/plyometric research independent of\nthis text (e.g. Komi 2000, \"Stretch-shortening cycle: a powerful model to study normal and\nfatigued muscle,\" J Biomech 33:1197-1206); this entry's contribution is the terminology\nhistory and the precise mechanical definition, which distinguishes genuine shock-method\nwork from ordinary jumping drills marketed under the same name.\n\n**When to apply** — **Inference:** useful when auditing whether a program's \"plyometric\"\nexercises actually meet the text's precise definition (a sudden eccentric-to-isometric-\nto-concentric sequence) versus being ordinary jump training relabeled; this matters for\npredicting whether the intended reactive-strength adaptation is actually being trained.\nThis library already traces Verkhoshansky's shock-method lineage at a higher level in\n[[fundamentals-of-sprinting--moyer/framework--soviet-lineage-of-sprint-training-ideas|The\nSoviet Lineage Behind Modern Sprint-Training Ideas]]; that entry credits him with \"shock\nmethod (plyometrics)\" but does not cover the terminology history or mechanism given here,\nso this entry adds a genuinely new angle rather than duplicating it.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — **Inference:** do not assume every drill called \"plyometric\" in\ncurrent commercial or popular usage (e.g. step-aerobics-style jumping) reproduces the\nshock-method mechanism. \"Russian scientists prefer to retain the term 'shock method' ...\nand 'depth jumps'\" (printed 34), while Siff separately proposes \"powermetrics\" for the\nwhole collection of jumping and rebound methods (Siff, 1998, printed 35). This terminology\ndistinction does not itself tell a coach which drills are safe or appropriate for a given\nathlete; that is a separate training-age and readiness question.\n\n**Example** — **Inference:** a coach programming \"plyometrics\" for a novice athlete should\ncheck whether the intended exercise involves a true rapid eccentric-to-concentric shock\ntransition (a depth jump) or is really just a submaximal hop/jump drill; the two carry very\ndifferent neuromuscular demands despite sharing the popular label.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--determinants-of-strength-structural-functional]],\n[[fundamentals-of-sprinting--moyer/framework--soviet-lineage-of-sprint-training-ideas]]\n(reciprocal link recommended there: note that Supertraining Ch1.6 gives the\nisometric/miometric/pliometric terminology scheme and the precise mechanical definition of\nthe shock method that this lineage table only names in passing); [[supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--optimal-depth-jump-drop-height-for-power-vs-force]]: Ch4.2.3, the 0.75-1.15 m drop-height range and the \"shock method\" origin, p.222-223","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"6a79d5b0-4865-4bf0-b3f0-b21d669f1f07","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:44:56.223Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"# Fixes applied: Supertraining 4.3-4.4 (Ch4-3-4), 23 entries\n\nFixer: Claude Opus. Date: 2026-10-03. Review: `staging/review_supertraining_ch4-3-4_opus.md` (round 1, GO AFTER FIXES: 8 entry FIX rows plus 1 `_book.md` FIX).\nSource checked: `staging/books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf`. I extracted the text of PDF idx 254, 255, 256, 257, 258, 260, 263 and 264 with PyMuPDF. I rendered page images for PDF 255, 256, 257, 258, 260, 263 and 264 to `staging/_renders/fix_p<N>.png` and opened PDF 256 (Fig 4.22 and its passage), PDF 257 (power equations, Eq 4.1) and PDF 264 (Table 4.2). Page key: PDF idx = printed + 11.\nScope: the 23 files in `staging/_scope_supertraining_ch4-3-4.txt`; only the 8 flagged entries and `_book.md` were edited. No new entries were written, so the scope file is unchanged.\n\n| file | what changed |\n|---|---|\n| claim--power-equals-force-times-velocity-only-when-force-is-constant.md | **Title** is now \"The Authors Warn That P = F x V Is Misapplied: Average or Peak Force Times Velocity Is Not Power When Force Varies; Use the Force-Time Curve, a Force Plate or High-Speed Video\". The slug is unchanged, so the one inbound link (RFD entry) still resolves.<br>**What it is:** the scope line now reads \"Scope of `proven`: only the identity P = dW/dt = F·v, which holds instant by instant for any force (Inference, see Why it works)\". It says the authors' constant-force statement is imprecise and is not part of that scope. Power is now quoted as \"The rate of doing work at any instant\" (PDF 257 image).<br>**Why it works:** rewritten in full. The old first sentence (\"This is calculus, and the authors state it ... wrong instantaneous picture\") endorsed the constant-force claim in the writer's voice. Now:<br>(a) The authors' two sentences are quoted with the locator (\"if F is constant over the interval concerned\"; \"If the force is not constant ... equation (4.1)\").<br>(b) The scope sentence follows.<br>(c) An Inference gives the physics: dW/dt = F·ds/dt = F·v at every instant. Constant force is needed only when one force value stands for a whole interval. What fails is multiplying an average or peak force by an average or peak velocity (the average of a product is not the product of averages, and the peaks need not coincide). The authors' \"It is inadequate to refer to average or peak force alone\" is quoted as the point the warning targets.<br>**When NOT:** \"The physics is sound; the wording around it is loose.\" is replaced by \"The authors' statement that P = F.V needs constant force is imprecise (see What it is); their practical warning against single average or peak values stands.\"<br>**Confidence:** `confidence: proven` kept, scoped to the instantaneous identity only (textbook mechanics, which the brief allows as `proven`). |\n| framework--five-criteria-of-dynamic-correspondence.md | **When NOT:** the Inference \"when the criteria conflict (for example ... breaks the regime of work ...)\" is replaced. The new text says the book gives no ranking among the criteria, then quotes three sentences from PDF 256 / p.245: the \"incongruous and artificial (Fig 4.22)\" sentence, \"this occurs only when an exercise is attempted which fails to take into consideration the appropriate criteria\", and that the Fig 4.22 exercise \"may be appropriate if applied carefully\". Then: \"So they do not treat the criteria as conflicting. Inference: in practice, meeting all five at once may still force a compromise; the book does not address that case.\" |\n| heuristic--train-force-at-the-accentuated-joint-angle.md | **What it is:** the Fig 4.22 paragraph now quotes the full \"can sometimes lead to such initial positions using weights or pulley machines which are incongruous and artificial (Fig 4.22)\" sentence. It then adds the dropped \"However, this occurs only when an exercise is attempted which fails to take into consideration the appropriate criteria\" (PDF 256 / p.245). It also quotes the text's own account of the two partners (\"simulates the vertical loading which sprinting normally imposes\"; \"intended to strengthen the power of the hip flexors\"). The caption and \"applied carefully\" sentences are kept. |\n| heuristic--parachute-resistance-sizing-progression-and-contrast-release.md | **Why it works:** first sentence replaced. It now quotes the authors' \"increases rapidly with velocity (actually the square of the velocity)\" and \"allows us to use these devices more knowledgeably\", plus the next sentence on the page, \"Naturally, running speed determines the degree of drag resistance\" (PDF 255 / p.244), so the size-and-speed point is in their words. \"standard fluid mechanics\" is removed. The new Inference reads: \"velocity-squared drag is the textbook drag equation, and this relation is the only part of the entry that could rate `proven`\". It is merged with the existing Inference, so there is one, not two. |\n| claim--parachute-resistance-tradeoffs-for-dynamic-correspondence.md | **When to apply:** the populations are now labelled: \"(Inference: for example, heavy athletes or athletes returning from lower-limb injury; the book names no population)\". \"(hurdlers, jumpers)\" is changed to \"(the book names hurdlers and jumpers)\". |\n| heuristic--same-exercise-different-regime-boxer-vs-shot-putter.md | **When to apply:** now reads \"single fast actions against a set resistance call for heavier loads and few repetitions (Inference: with full recovery between efforts; the book does not state rest).\" This matches the source's \"rapid single movements against a specific resistance\" (PDF 258 / p.247). |\n| case-study--high-jumper-factor-structure-by-qualification.md | **When to apply:** \"and less to plantarflexor power\" is removed. Added: \"Inference: the falling plantarflexor share may justify a smaller plantarflexor emphasis, but the book reports a factor share, not a training reduction.\" |\n| case-study--skater-factor-structure-by-qualification.md | **When NOT:** added \"Class 1 factor IV (2.7) is smaller than factor V (8.9), against the order of factors by share (verified against the page image, PDF p. 264 / printed p. 253)\", followed by a labelled Inference: the value may be a misprint, with the arithmetic (35.9 + 23.3 + 16.8 + 12.7 + 8.9 = 97.6), so treat 2.7 as unclear.<br>**When to apply:** the Inference no longer quotes \"2.7% to 36.5%\". It now says the rise to first factor at Master of Sport (36.5%) is consistent with the teaching point, and that the Class 1 value is unclear, \"so the size of the change should not be quoted.\" The table keeps the printed 2.7. |\n| _book.md | **Line 96:** now reads `| 4 | The Means of Special Strength Training | 201-254 | 212-265 | done | 64 (see section rows below) |`. 64 = 6 (4.1) + 35 (4.2) + 13 (4.3) + 10 (4.4). The 4.2.x sub-rows sum to 35. A count of `chapter: \"4` frontmatter on disk also gives 64 (6/35/13/10).<br>**Batch note:** deleted the sentence \"The Chapter 4 parent row still reads \"in progress \\| 6\" and was not changed in this session.\" No other line touched. |\n\n## Disagreements with the review\n\n1. **Power entry: the review's scope sentence is not used word for word.** The review says the constant-force form \"is true only when one force value stands for the whole interval\". That \"only\" is too strong: average power also equals average force times velocity when velocity is constant. I wrote \"Constant force is needed only when one force value stands for a whole interval: then power over the interval equals that F times the average velocity\", and put it inside the Inference because it is my physics, not the authors'. I also rewrote the first sentence of Why it works, which the review's fix (3) left in place. That sentence (\"This is calculus, and the authors state it: P = F.V is the special case ... with constant force\") still endorsed the imprecise claim in the writer's voice. The substance agrees with the review: the authors' constant-force condition is imprecise, and their warning about average or peak values stands.\n2. **Five-criteria entry: I did not adopt the review's paraphrase that \"awkward positions such as Fig 4.22 arise only when ...\".** The source reads two ways. It calls the Fig 4.22 positions \"incongruous and artificial\". It says that happens \"only when\" the criteria are ignored. It then says the Fig 4.22 exercise itself \"may be appropriate if applied carefully\" (PDF 256 / p.245, text and image). So the figure is both an example of awkward loading and a usable exercise. I quoted all three sentences rather than having the entry say Fig 4.22 is the ignored-criteria case. I agree with the review's core point: the authors deny a conflict among the criteria, and the old \"breaks the regime of work\" reading is not in the source.\n3. **Skater entry: misprint speculation labelled.** The review's text gives the \"2.7 may be a misprint (12.7 would restore the order ...)\" reasoning unlabelled. That is reviewer or fixer reasoning, so it is now an \"Inference:\" and carries the column sum.\n4. **Parachute sizing: one extra quote.** I added the \"Naturally, running speed determines the degree of drag resistance\" sentence (PDF 255 / p.244). Without it, \"so parachute size and running speed together set the load\" would sit as unlabelled writer voice.\n5. **OBLA (optional missing idea): not added as an entry.** Existing entries already cover it.\n   - `claim--endurance-rests-on-muscle-oxidative-and-strength-qualities-not-vo2max` quotes \"Since OBLA depends on VO2 max, it is considered useful to strive to raise VO2 max ...\" (contested list) and the Christensen & Hogberg 1950 \"approximately 50% more effective\" sentence (When NOT).\n   - `claim--local-blood-flow-not-vo2max-tracks-competition-period-endurance-gains` quotes \"OBLA (lactate threshold) and VO2 max levels can change independently\" and \"OBLA can rise\" while VO2 max falls.\n   - The only uncaptured sentence is the definitional opener (PDF 260 / printed p. 249): \"Practical improvement of the metabolic processes in endurance sports is associated with increasing the potential to utilise aerobic sources of energy by raising the Onset of Blood Lactate Accumulation (OBLA), i.e. that level of oxygen consumption at which the anaerobic processes begin to be prominently activated.\"\n   - A stand-alone entry would mostly repeat those quotes. The sentence belongs in the What it is of the endurance entry at a later pass. That entry is GO, so it is outside this fix scope.\n\nNo review claim was found false on the page. Every FIX row's problem quote and source quote matched the extracted text, and the Table 4.2 and Eq 4.1 readings matched the rendered images.\n\n## Checks\n\nGrep proof, run from the book folder (one line per fixed file, plus counts of deleted phrases):\n\n```\nclaim--power-equals-force-times-velocity-only-when-force-is-constant.md:14:# The Authors Warn That P = F x V Is Misapplied\nclaim--power-equals-force-times-velocity-only-when-force-is-constant.md:16:Scope of `proven`: only the identity P = dW/dt = F·v, which holds instant by instant for any force (Inference, see Why it works)\nclaim--power-equals-force-times-velocity-only-when-force-is-constant.md:18:Scope of `proven`: only the identity P = dW/dt = F·v, which holds instant by instant for any force\nclaim--power-equals-force-times-velocity-only-when-force-is-constant.md:18:Inference: because dW = F·ds, the rate of work is dW/dt = F·ds/dt = F·v at every instant, whatever the force history, so the instantaneous product F(t)·v(t) is exact\nclaim--power-equals-force-times-velocity-only-when-force-is-constant.md:22:The authors' statement that P = F.V needs constant force is imprecise (see What it is)\ndeleted phrase count: 0 / 0      (\"gives a wrong instantaneous picture\" / \"The physics is sound\")\nframework--five-criteria-of-dynamic-correspondence.md:29:So they do not treat the criteria as conflicting. Inference: in practice, meeting all five at once may still force a compromise; the book does not address that case\nold phrase count: 0              (\"when the criteria conflict\")\nheuristic--train-force-at-the-accentuated-joint-angle.md:18:but add: \"However, this occurs only when an exercise is attempted which fails to take into consideration the appropriate criteria\nheuristic--parachute-resistance-sizing-progression-and-contrast-release.md:28:Inference: velocity-squared drag is the textbook drag equation, and this relation is the only part of the entry that could rate `proven`\nold phrase count: 0              (\"standard fluid mechanics\")\nclaim--parachute-resistance-tradeoffs-for-dynamic-correspondence.md:25:(Inference: for example, heavy athletes or athletes returning from lower-limb injury; the book names no population)\nheuristic--same-exercise-different-regime-boxer-vs-shot-putter.md:20:(Inference: with full recovery between efforts; the book does not state rest)\ncase-study--high-jumper-factor-structure-by-qualification.md:30:Inference: the falling plantarflexor share may justify a smaller plantarflexor emphasis, but the book reports a factor share, not a training reduction\ncase-study--skater-factor-structure-by-qualification.md:32:Class 1 factor IV (2.7) is smaller than factor V (8.9), against the order of factors by share (verified against the page image, PDF p\ncase-study--skater-factor-structure-by-qualification.md:30:so the size of the change should not be quoted\nold phrase count: 0              (\"2.7% to 36.5%\")\n_book.md:96:| 4 | The Means of Special Strength Training | 201-254 | 212-265 | done | 64 (see section rows below) |\nremoved sentence count: 0        (\"was not changed in this session\")\n```\n\nMechanical greps over all 23 scoped files (run under bash with `F=$(cat ../../../_scope_supertraining_ch4-3-4.txt | tr -d \"\\r\")`; the body em-dash check strips the `**Header** —` prefix with sed and then greps for the dash, because macOS grep has no `-P`):\n\n##### framework--reactivity-coefficient-and-depth-jump-test.md\n---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"3.2.1\"\ntopic: reactive ability testing\ncategory: [testing, plyometrics, reactive-strength]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-09-26\n---\n\n# The Reactivity Coefficient (RC) and Verkhoshansky's Depth-Jump Reactivity Test\n\n**What it is** — Reactive ability (the neuromuscular capacity to generate explosive force\nvia a preliminary stretch immediately before concentric work) is quantified with a\n**Reactivity Coefficient**, which the text defines three ways without fully reconciling\nthem: earlier (2.2.1, printed 108) as \"the explosive strength index relative to body\nweight,\" RC = Fmax/(tmax.W) = RFDmax/W; here, the lab-only form RC = K2/K1 (a ratio of two\ncharacteristics of muscular work, e.g. power, before and after the stretch), and the\nfield-practical form **RC = Fmax / (tmax x m x g)**, where Fmax is peak force, tmax is time\nto reach it, m is the moving mass (or bodymass), and g is gravitational acceleration. Despite\nthat formula, the text separately states that in the reactivity test itself, \"the\ncoefficient of reactivity was determined from the length of the support period and analysis\nof the mean take-off force\" (printed 137), not a literal Fmax/tmax calculation; the text\ndoes not explain how the two are reconciled. Verkhoshansky's own \"reactivity test\" protocol:\na series of vertical jumps without arm-swing, performed after a depth-jump from 0.4 m,\nunloaded and then with 10, 20, 30, and 40 kg added (fixed absolute loads, not expressed as a\npercentage of the athlete's So); jump height and support-period duration are recorded and\nplotted against added mass. Findings from this\nprotocol: reactive ability correlates with strength most strongly at large loads (R = 0.95\nat 30-40 kg loads, weak at lighter loads); RC correlates more strongly with support-period\ntime than with force in every load condition; absolute strength has a *negative* effect on\ncontraction speed with small/no added load but a *positive* effect with large added load\n(40 kg), while high *relative* strength has the opposite pattern (positive with light\nloads, negative with heavy loads); and cross-sport testing (Fig 3.9-3.10) found jumpers\nhad the highest RC and middle-distance runners the lowest, with RC showing a strong linear\nrelationship (R ≈ 0.95) to triple-jump performance specifically. (PDF pp. 146-149, printed\npp. 135-138, \"Quickness and Reactive Ability,\" Figs 3.7-3.10.)\n\n**Why it works** — The stated mechanism: a preliminary stretch elastically deforms the\nmuscle-tendon complex, storing potential energy that converts to kinetic energy on the\nsubsequent contraction, adding to active muscular force. **Inference:** RC's specific\nformula (force over time-to-peak times system mass) is not explained sentence-by-sentence\nin the text beyond this general stretch-energy mechanism; the fact that it tracks support-period\ntime more tightly than raw force output is reported as an empirical correlation finding,\nnot derived from a stated causal mechanism.\n\n**When to apply** — Use the RC formula's logic (peak force relative to time-to-peak and\nsystem mass) as the conceptual ancestor of modern reactive strength index (RSI) metrics.\n**Eric's baseline:** modified RSI (mRSI) from daily CMJ testing is already the department's\nchosen daily-readiness marker on ForceDecks (`method-baseline.md`, \"Force plate and\nneuromuscular monitoring,\" citing `2026-02-19-sportsmith-6otb-er.md`). **Inference:** this\nframework is a plausible historical/conceptual ancestor of that metric (RC = F/(t x m x g)\nversus mRSI = jump height / time to take-off, not contact time); this lineage claim is not\nstated in either source. The load-dependent strength-speed finding here\n(absolute strength helps at heavy loads, hurts at light loads; relative strength does the\nreverse) is a useful interpretive lens for reading an individual athlete's mRSI trend\nalongside their absolute- and relative-strength testing numbers.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — This is a single-lab (Verkhoshansky) protocol using a specific\n0.4 m depth-jump height and 10-40 kg external loading increments on a now-dated athlete\npopulation; the precise correlation values (R=0.95, etc.) should not be assumed to hold for\na different depth, load range, or sport population without direct testing. It is also not a\nplyometric-prescription framework by itself: it measures reactive ability, it does not\nprescribe how to train it (see [[framework--shock-method-and-plyometric-terminology]] for\nthat).\n\n**Example** — **Inference:** two athletes with identical CMJ height can have very different\nRC/RSI: Verkhoshansky's data implies the athlete whose reactive ability holds up (or\nimproves) as external load increases from 0 to 40 kg is a different training case than one\nwhose reactive ability only shows up unloaded. The former likely has more absolute strength\nto draw on, the latter is leaning more on relative strength/elasticity. This example is not\nin the source.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--shock-method-and-plyometric-terminology]] (Ch1, the plyometric\ntraining method this test is designed to evaluate); [[framework--strength-deficit-jump-comparison-estimate]]\n(Ch1, a related but distinct static-vs-countermovement jump comparison for a different\npurpose, estimating strength deficit rather than reactive ability); [[supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--optimal-depth-jump-drop-height-for-power-vs-force]]: Ch4.2.3, variable drop heights and loaded depth jumps, Figs 4.9-4.10; [[heuristic--rfd-as-correspondence-criterion-and-impulse-cannot-compare-movements]] (4.3.4, which lists RC in its RFDmax/W form beside power and impulse as measures of explosive strength, and rejects impulse for comparing different movements)\n##### framework--shock-method-and-plyometric-terminology.md\n---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"1.6\"\ntopic: shock method and plyometrics\ncategory: [plyometrics, terminology, stretch-shortening-cycle]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-09-26\n---\n\n# The Shock Method (Original Russian \"Udarnyi Metod\"), and Why \"Plyometrics\" Is a Loose Translation\n\n**What it is** — Verkhoshansky developed the \"shock method\" (udarnyi metod) as a special\nspeed-strength training method exploiting connective and elastic tissue's ability to\nstore and release elastic energy during stretch-shortening of the muscle complex (running,\njumping, throwing, rapid lifting). The rise in concentric strength after rapid \"shock\"\nloading comes from two sources: increased muscle tension from the myotatic (stretch)\nreflex, and explosive release of elastic energy stored in connective/elastic tissue during\nthe eccentric phase. Historically, \"pliometric\" \"was apparently coined to mean eccentric\ncontraction\"; in this scheme of nomenclature (the text does not state it to be\nspecifically Russian), isometric retained its meaning of static contraction, miometric\nmeant concentric contraction, and pliometric meant eccentric contraction (printed 34); the\nterm \"plyometrics,\" as adopted in the West, has drifted to describe a broader category of\njumping drills, many of which do not reproduce true shock-method characteristics. The\ntext's precise redefinition: the term plyometrics should refer to \"an eccentric muscle\ncontraction ... suddenly terminated in an explosive isometric contraction, thereby\nproducing a powerful myotatic reflex, a sharp extension of the passive components of the\nmuscle complex and a subsequent explosive concentric contraction,\" and \"should not refer\nto ordinary jumping\" (printed 34-35). (PDF pp. 45-46, printed pp. 34-35.)\n\n**Why it works** — Inference: the stretch-shortening cycle mechanism (reflex potentiation\nplus elastic energy return) is documented in modern SSC/plyometric research independent of\nthis text (e.g. Komi 2000, \"Stretch-shortening cycle: a powerful model to study normal and\nfatigued muscle,\" J Biomech 33:1197-1206); this entry's contribution is the terminology\nhistory and the precise mechanical definition, which distinguishes genuine shock-method\nwork from ordinary jumping drills marketed under the same name.\n\n**When to apply** — **Inference:** useful when auditing whether a program's \"plyometric\"\nexercises actually meet the text's precise definition (a sudden eccentric-to-isometric-\nto-concentric sequence) versus being ordinary jump training relabeled; this matters for\npredicting whether the intended reactive-strength adaptation is actually being trained.\nThis library already traces Verkhoshansky's shock-method lineage at a higher level in\n[[fundamentals-of-sprinting--moyer/framework--soviet-lineage-of-sprint-training-ideas|The\nSoviet Lineage Behind Modern Sprint-Training Ideas]]; that entry credits him with \"shock\nmethod (plyometrics)\" but does not cover the terminology history or mechanism given here,\nso this entry adds a genuinely new angle rather than duplicating it.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — **Inference:** do not assume every drill called \"plyometric\" in\ncurrent commercial or popular usage (e.g. step-aerobics-style jumping) reproduces the\nshock-method mechanism. \"Russian scientists prefer to retain the term 'shock method' ...\nand 'depth jumps'\" (printed 34), while Siff separately proposes \"powermetrics\" for the\nwhole collection of jumping and rebound methods (Siff, 1998, printed 35). This terminology\ndistinction does not itself tell a coach which drills are safe or appropriate for a given\nathlete; that is a separate training-age and readiness question.\n\n**Example** — **Inference:** a coach programming \"plyometrics\" for a novice athlete should\ncheck whether the intended exercise involves a true rapid eccentric-to-concentric shock\ntransition (a depth jump) or is really just a submaximal hop/jump drill; the two carry very\ndifferent neuromuscular demands despite sharing the popular label.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--determinants-of-strength-structural-functional]],\n[[fundamentals-of-sprinting--moyer/framework--soviet-lineage-of-sprint-training-ideas]]\n(reciprocal link recommended there: note that Supertraining Ch1.6 gives the\nisometric/miometric/pliometric terminology scheme and the precise mechanical definition of\nthe shock method that this lineage table only names in passing); [[supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--optimal-depth-jump-drop-height-for-power-vs-force]]: Ch4.2.3, the 0.75-1.15 m drop-height range and the \"shock method\" origin, p.222-223","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"11af9c66-5359-4dc9-a6e5-f18e6e809dad","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
