{"parentUuid":"7c81588b-8cd7-4bb7-841c-0e91761b5447","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffZZJJCHRexfUXMA6z6E","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Taz3FtdXtZ1QFQ9XgCwphm","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky; for f in claim--optimal-depth-jump-drop-height-for-power-vs-force.md heuristic--match-plyometric-prescription-to-joint-angle-and-fiber-type.md framework--strength-endurance-taxonomy-and-absolute-vs-partial.md; do echo \"##### $f\"; cat $f; done","description":"Read related depth jump and strength-endurance entries"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":9292,"cache_read_input_tokens":88726,"output_tokens":253,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":9292},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":253,"cache_read_input_tokens":88726,"cache_creation_input_tokens":9292,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":9292,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01Taz3FtdXtZ1QFQ9XgCwphm":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky; for f in claim--optimal-depth-jump-drop-height-for-power-vs-force.md heuristic--match-plyometric-prescription-to-joint-angle-and-fiber-type.md framework--strength-endurance-taxonomy-and-absolute-vs-partial.md; do echo \"##### $f\"; cat $f; done","description":"Read related depth jump and strength-endurance entries"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffZZJ25DRvnFweoat2cD","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"da819662-a296-43c4-a6d4-e564c328d10c","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:44:58.717Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"da819662-a296-43c4-a6d4-e564c328d10c","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"624a8cec-33fa-4c0a-a445-c72ce718e6ab","agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Taz3FtdXtZ1QFQ9XgCwphm","type":"tool_result","content":"##### claim--optimal-depth-jump-drop-height-for-power-vs-force.md\n---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: claim\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"4.2.3\"\ntopic: optimal depth-jump drop height\ncategory: [plyometrics, testing, programming]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-09-27\n---\n\n# The Optimal Depth-Jump Drop Height Is 0.75-1.15 Metres: Maximal Power at the Low End, Maximal Force at the High End\n\n**What it is** — Fig 4.7 (PDF p. 232, printed p. 221; Verkhoshansky, 1977) plots force, in\nbodyweights, against time for three vertical jumps: (1) jumping from rest from a low squat,\n(2) the usual jump with a dip or amortisation phase, (3) a jump after a depth jump from\n0.4 m. The caption gives jump heights of 0.67 m, 0.74 m and 0.80 m respectively. Verified\nagainst the page image (readings from the figure, approximate): curve 1 peaks early, about\n1.4-1.5 BW at about 0.08-0.1 s, and ends about 0.3 s; curve 2 first drops below bodyweight\n(about 0.6 BW at about 0.15 s), then peaks about 1.4 BW at about 0.5 s and ends about 0.8 s;\ncurve 3 peaks about 3 BW at about 0.1 s and ends about 0.25 s. Curves 1 and 2 have similar\npeaks, and curve 2's is the later; only curve 3 is clearly higher and earlier.\n\nDepth jumps from a range of drop heights (Fig 4.10, PDF p. 234, printed p. 223): the text\nstates that \"maximal force (Fmax) increases up to a height of 1.15 metres, then diminishes\nsharply, while movement time begins by changing insignificantly, then increasing sharply.\nMaximum power and reactivity are achieved using a drop height of approximately 0.75 metres\"\n(PDF p. 233, printed p. 222). Verified against the page image (approximate): Fmax peaks near\n1.1-1.15 m and falls sharply to 1.55 m; the figure also carries a Power curve, not named in\nthe caption, peaking about 0.75 m; RC peaks about 0.8 m; ground contact time t dips slightly\nto a minimum about 0.75 m, then rises sharply. The authors conclude that the optimal range of\ndepth jumping for stimulating muscular activity is 0.75-1.15 metres, \"because at the lower\nend of this range the muscles are working at maximal power and at the upper end they are\nworking at maximal dynamic force\". This finding \"forms the basis for the recommendations for\nusing depth jumps in the training of advanced jumpers (Verkhoshansky 1963, 1964, 1966)\",\nthough it is \"preferable to err on the conservative side of using considerably lower jump\nheights with anyone who is unused to this type of training and to strictly limit the volume\nof this type of 'shock' training\" (p.222).\n\nThe book states the result past 1.15 m two ways. The Fig 4.10 text says Fmax \"diminishes\nsharply\" past 1.15 m; the next paragraph says further increases in height \"significantly\ndecrease the magnitude of the dynamic parameters of the take-off\", support time increases\nquickly because the eccentric-concentric switch is lengthened, and \"the maximum dynamic force\nof take-off and the speed of muscular contraction stabilise and do not increase any further\"\n(p.222). The figure shows a sharp fall. Both are recorded. The contradiction concerns only what\nhappens past the optimum; both readings agree force does not rise past 1.15 m, so it does\nnot dispute the entry's claim (optimal range 0.75-1.15 m), and confidence is `supported`. The take-off comprises amortisation (kinetic energy of the fall\nabsorbed, with increasing depth of knee flexion) and the thrust proper (fairly constant\nspeed of muscle contraction) (p.222).\n\nLoaded depth jumps (Fig 4.9, PDF p. 234, printed p. 223; the caption is on the page image, not\nin the text layer): after an unloaded depth jump (M = mass of athlete) and with an extra 10,\n20, 30 and 40 kg. Verified against the page image: rebound height h2, force F and RC fall and\ncontact time t rises as load increases from M to M+40 kg. The text groups Figs 4.9 and 4.10\nas showing the same tendency as the thrown-load experiment \"under other conditions, although\nthese have their own peculiarities\", and concludes \"it is not beneficial to increase kinetic\nenergy by use of heavier loads\" (p.222; see\n[[claim--thrown-load-height-decreases-with-load-increases-with-drop-height]]). (PDF pp.\n232-234, printed pp. 221-223, \"4.2.3 Kinetic Energy and Strength Processes,\" Figs 4.7, 4.9,\n4.10.)\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' stated mechanism is elastic-energy storage during\namortisation and its release in the thrust (see\n[[framework--kinetic-energy-stimulation-mechanical-basis-and-advantages]]), and for heights\npast the optimum the lengthened switch from eccentric to concentric work (p.222). Inference:\nat greater drop heights more of the force capacity may go to braking, which would fit force\nrising to 1.15 m while power and reactivity peak earlier; the book does not state this.\n\n**When to apply** — For advanced jumpers (Verkhoshansky 1963, 1964, 1966; p.222), use about\n0.75 m when the training goal is maximal power and reactivity, and up to about 1.15 m when\nthe goal is maximal dynamic force, per this protocol. For athletes unused to depth-jump\ntraining, start considerably lower and strictly limit volume, per the authors' caution.\nInference: this gives a citable historical anchor for programming depth-jump height, distinct\nfrom the fixed 0.4 m depth-jump reactivity test already in this library (see\n[[framework--reactivity-coefficient-and-depth-jump-test]]).\n\n**When NOT to apply** — This is single-lab, era-specific data (Verkhoshansky, 1963-1977) on\nan unspecified population (the authors do not state training age, sport or sample size for\nthe drop-height experiment); do not apply 0.75 m and 1.15 m to every athlete without\naccounting for the factors the authors name: whether the athlete is used to this training,\nbodymass, additional load and fall height (p.222). Do\nnot cite the book as saying force merely plateaus past 1.15 m, or merely falls: it says both\n(p.222). Do not conflate this variable drop-height range with the fixed 0.4 m reactivity test\n(see Related).\n\n**Example** — The book's own example is the Fig 4.7, 4.9 and 4.10 data described above; no\nfurther example is added here.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--kinetic-energy-stimulation-mechanical-basis-and-advantages]],\n[[claim--thrown-load-height-decreases-with-load-increases-with-drop-height]],\n[[framework--reactivity-coefficient-and-depth-jump-test]] (Ch3.2.1, a related but distinct\nfixed 0.4 m depth-jump protocol measuring reactive ability rather than optimal training drop\nheight),\n[[framework--shock-method-and-plyometric-terminology]].\n##### heuristic--match-plyometric-prescription-to-joint-angle-and-fiber-type.md\n---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"1.7\"\ntopic: plyometric prescription by joint angle and fibre type\ncategory: [plyometrics, individualization, fiber-type]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-09-26\n---\n\n# Deeper Knee Flexion Reduces \"Shock\" Loading; Fibre-Type Mix Should Guide Jump Tempo\n\n**What it is** — Two related findings on individualizing plyometric prescription.\n(1) Soft-tissue spring stiffness around the knee drops sharply with flexion angle\n(Greene & McMahon, cited PDF p. 58, printed p. 47: roughly 120 kN/m at 0 degrees down to\n30 kN/m at 75 degrees), so the Series Elastic Component's contribution falls as knee\nflexion increases, meaning deeper-flexion landings reduce the \"shock\" character of a\nplyometric exercise. (2) Vertical jump testing found rapid, short-range stretch phases\nwere most beneficial for the vastus medialis (fast-twitch-rich), while knee extensors of\nathletes with more slow-twitch fibres (e.g. distance runners) benefited more from slower,\nlarger-amplitude jumps with a longer transition between stretch and shortening (Bosco et\nal., 1982), attributed to differing cross-bridge lifetimes between fibre types. (PDF pp.\n57-58, printed pp. 46-47.)\n\n**Why it works** — Both findings trace back to the same underlying mechanics: SEC\ncontribution and cross-bridge dynamics vary with joint angle and fibre-type composition,\nso a jump protocol that is optimal for one joint angle or one athlete's fibre profile is\nnot automatically optimal for another.\n\n**When to apply** — The text itself draws the joint-angle application: \"this has profound\nimplications for the prescription of plyometric training, in particular the limiting of\njoint angle to ensure the adequacy of the 'shock' loading on the muscle complex\" (printed\n47). This supports limiting knee flexion depth in depth-jump and shock-method work\nspecifically when the goal is maximal elastic/reactive loading. Inference: deeper flexion\nshifts the exercise toward general strength/power rather than a true \"shock\" stimulus.\n**Inference:** also supports considering an athlete's likely fibre-type profile (position, event, or\ndirect biopsy/proxy data if available) when choosing fast-short vs. slow-larger-amplitude\njump variants; the text states the fibre-type finding but does not itself extend it to a\nprogramming rule.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — **Inference:** fibre-type composition is rarely measured directly\nin a collegiate athletics setting; do not treat sport or position alone as a reliable proxy\nwithout other supporting evidence (this entry's application to specific positions is its\nown inference, not stated in the text). The specific stiffness values (120 kN/m to 30\nkN/m) come from one biomechanics study on a flexible-board bouncing task, not from a\ndepth-jump or standard field-testing setup, so treat them as illustrative of the trend\nrather than as target numbers.\n\n**Example** — **Inference:** a shallow-knee-flexion, low-amplitude rebound drill better\ntargets true reactive/shock adaptation than a deep-squat-style jump, which behaves more\nlike a general power exercise; a distance-oriented athlete may respond better to a slightly\nslower, larger-amplitude jump variant than a power-position athlete.\n\n**Related** — [[principle--short-amortization-preserves-elastic-energy]],\n[[framework--shock-method-and-plyometric-terminology]].\n##### framework--strength-endurance-taxonomy-and-absolute-vs-partial.md\n---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"3.2.2\"\ntopic: strength-endurance classification\ncategory: [strength-endurance, testing, terminology]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-09-26\n---\n\n# Strength-Endurance Splits Two Ways (Dynamic/Static, General/Local), and \"Absolute\" vs \"Partial\" Endurance Explains Contradictory Research\n\n**What it is** — Strength-endurance (sustained muscle tension with minimal efficiency loss,\ne.g. a rower averaging 40-60 kgf per stroke over 230-250 strokes in 6-7 minutes, Samsonov\n1969) is classified along two independent axes. By muscular-work character: **dynamic\nstrength-endurance** (repeated tension without interruption in cyclic sports like running,\nswimming, rowing, or repeated short-rest acyclic work like repeated jumps/throws) versus\n**static strength-endurance** (sustained isometric tension or held posture, e.g. wrestling,\nsailing, shooting, skating). By muscle-group involvement: **general strength-endurance**\n(many muscle groups) versus **local strength-endurance** (individual muscle groups), a\ndistinction the text calls significant for exercise selection, since training should target\nthe specific muscle groups actually driving the sport skill (demonstrated via EMG in rowing,\nLazarov 1967, and via strength-performance correlation in swimming, Shchavelyev 1969).\nSeparately, Zatsiorsky et al (1965) resolve contradictory strength-endurance research\n(some studies found positive correlation with strength, some none, some negative) by\nsplitting endurance into **absolute endurance** (the overall result, ignoring the level of\nunderlying motor abilities) and **partial endurance** (the level of a specific motor ability\nonce the influence of other abilities is excluded, e.g. holding or moving a load at a fixed\n*percentage* of one's own max, which typically shows no or negative correlation with max\nstrength, versus absolute endurance at a fixed *absolute* load, which correlates positively\nwith max strength). For athletes who lift the same absolute load, distinguishing between\nthem requires calculating relative (bodymass-adjusted) static or dynamic strength-endurance;\nthis is stated for that specific comparison case, not as a general requirement for every\npartial-endurance comparison. Elsewhere, in Chapter 2 (printed 108-109), the text names a\nthree-way classification: static, dynamic, and *explosive* strength-endurance (repetitively\nexecuting explosive effort); this section (3.2.2) instead splits strength-endurance into\njust static and dynamic, with no mention of an explosive form. The text does not reconcile\nthese two classifications, and this entry does not either. Strength-endurance's specificity\nis stated as lower than speed's, \"although its carry-over from one type of activity to\nanother is greater\" (printed 143). (PDF pp. 153-155, printed pp. 142-144, \"3.2.2\nStrength-Endurance,\" Fig 3.12-3.13.)\n\n**Why it works** — The authors state only that different studies measured different\nendurance types (printed 143), not a mechanism for why that produces opposite-signed\ncorrelations. **Inference:** an absolute-load endurance test is partly re-measuring maximal\nstrength (a stronger athlete simply has more headroom under any fixed absolute load), while\na relative-load (percentage-of-own-max) test removes that confound and isolates the\nendurance-specific quality; this mechanism is not stated in the text.\n\n**When to apply** — Use the absolute/partial distinction whenever comparing\nstrength-endurance findings (in this book or elsewhere) that seem contradictory: check\nwhether the test load was a fixed absolute weight or a fixed percentage of each athlete's\nown max before concluding the results actually disagree. **Eric's baseline:** the\ndepartment's own testing (`method-baseline.md`, \"Testing and assessment\") already defaults\nnovices and most women's-sport testing to repetition-max protocols rather than 1RM.\n**Inference:** this framework implies that comparing rep-max results *across* athletes as a proxy for\nrelative strength-endurance requires normalizing to each athlete's own max (partial\nendurance), not comparing raw rep counts at a shared absolute load, if the goal is to\nisolate the endurance quality itself rather than re-measuring who is stronger.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The text is explicit that \"an objective, reliable means of\nevaluating strength-endurance in sporting activity has not yet been devised\" and that\n\"specialists frequently use inadequate tests\": this is a classification scheme for\nthinking about strength-endurance clearly, not a validated measurement protocol. The\narcher example (Fig 3.13) shows that as athletes become more skilled, the correlation\nbetween strength-endurance and competitive results actually *decreases* (control of\nmuscular contraction matters more than raw endurance), so this taxonomy should not be\nread as implying strength-endurance always predicts sport performance.\n\n**Example** — **Inference:** a quarter-squat-hold endurance test at a fixed 100 kg for a\nwhole roster measures absolute strength-endurance (confounded with who is strongest); the\nsame test at 60% of each athlete's own back-squat max measures partial (dynamic/static)\nstrength-endurance and is the more isolated endurance metric. This example is not in the\nsource. The text's own guidance is that the best way to develop strength-endurance is to\nexecute the competition exercises themselves under demanding conditions or in a large\nvolume, without excluding special strength exercises (printed 144).\n\n**Related** — [[claim--continuous-vs-interval-circuit-training-for-concurrent-goals]] (Ch1,\nadjacent programming content for endurance-strength concurrent goals).","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"6ca1742a-cd67-4ea3-89b1-7dc7fa83d0e9","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:44:58.929Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"##### claim--optimal-depth-jump-drop-height-for-power-vs-force.md\n---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: claim\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"4.2.3\"\ntopic: optimal depth-jump drop height\ncategory: [plyometrics, testing, programming]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-09-27\n---\n\n# The Optimal Depth-Jump Drop Height Is 0.75-1.15 Metres: Maximal Power at the Low End, Maximal Force at the High End\n\n**What it is** — Fig 4.7 (PDF p. 232, printed p. 221; Verkhoshansky, 1977) plots force, in\nbodyweights, against time for three vertical jumps: (1) jumping from rest from a low squat,\n(2) the usual jump with a dip or amortisation phase, (3) a jump after a depth jump from\n0.4 m. The caption gives jump heights of 0.67 m, 0.74 m and 0.80 m respectively. Verified\nagainst the page image (readings from the figure, approximate): curve 1 peaks early, about\n1.4-1.5 BW at about 0.08-0.1 s, and ends about 0.3 s; curve 2 first drops below bodyweight\n(about 0.6 BW at about 0.15 s), then peaks about 1.4 BW at about 0.5 s and ends about 0.8 s;\ncurve 3 peaks about 3 BW at about 0.1 s and ends about 0.25 s. Curves 1 and 2 have similar\npeaks, and curve 2's is the later; only curve 3 is clearly higher and earlier.\n\nDepth jumps from a range of drop heights (Fig 4.10, PDF p. 234, printed p. 223): the text\nstates that \"maximal force (Fmax) increases up to a height of 1.15 metres, then diminishes\nsharply, while movement time begins by changing insignificantly, then increasing sharply.\nMaximum power and reactivity are achieved using a drop height of approximately 0.75 metres\"\n(PDF p. 233, printed p. 222). Verified against the page image (approximate): Fmax peaks near\n1.1-1.15 m and falls sharply to 1.55 m; the figure also carries a Power curve, not named in\nthe caption, peaking about 0.75 m; RC peaks about 0.8 m; ground contact time t dips slightly\nto a minimum about 0.75 m, then rises sharply. The authors conclude that the optimal range of\ndepth jumping for stimulating muscular activity is 0.75-1.15 metres, \"because at the lower\nend of this range the muscles are working at maximal power and at the upper end they are\nworking at maximal dynamic force\". This finding \"forms the basis for the recommendations for\nusing depth jumps in the training of advanced jumpers (Verkhoshansky 1963, 1964, 1966)\",\nthough it is \"preferable to err on the conservative side of using considerably lower jump\nheights with anyone who is unused to this type of training and to strictly limit the volume\nof this type of 'shock' training\" (p.222).\n\nThe book states the result past 1.15 m two ways. The Fig 4.10 text says Fmax \"diminishes\nsharply\" past 1.15 m; the next paragraph says further increases in height \"significantly\ndecrease the magnitude of the dynamic parameters of the take-off\", support time increases\nquickly because the eccentric-concentric switch is lengthened, and \"the maximum dynamic force\nof take-off and the speed of muscular contraction stabilise and do not increase any further\"\n(p.222). The figure shows a sharp fall. Both are recorded. The contradiction concerns only what\nhappens past the optimum; both readings agree force does not rise past 1.15 m, so it does\nnot dispute the entry's claim (optimal range 0.75-1.15 m), and confidence is `supported`. The take-off comprises amortisation (kinetic energy of the fall\nabsorbed, with increasing depth of knee flexion) and the thrust proper (fairly constant\nspeed of muscle contraction) (p.222).\n\nLoaded depth jumps (Fig 4.9, PDF p. 234, printed p. 223; the caption is on the page image, not\nin the text layer): after an unloaded depth jump (M = mass of athlete) and with an extra 10,\n20, 30 and 40 kg. Verified against the page image: rebound height h2, force F and RC fall and\ncontact time t rises as load increases from M to M+40 kg. The text groups Figs 4.9 and 4.10\nas showing the same tendency as the thrown-load experiment \"under other conditions, although\nthese have their own peculiarities\", and concludes \"it is not beneficial to increase kinetic\nenergy by use of heavier loads\" (p.222; see\n[[claim--thrown-load-height-decreases-with-load-increases-with-drop-height]]). (PDF pp.\n232-234, printed pp. 221-223, \"4.2.3 Kinetic Energy and Strength Processes,\" Figs 4.7, 4.9,\n4.10.)\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' stated mechanism is elastic-energy storage during\namortisation and its release in the thrust (see\n[[framework--kinetic-energy-stimulation-mechanical-basis-and-advantages]]), and for heights\npast the optimum the lengthened switch from eccentric to concentric work (p.222). Inference:\nat greater drop heights more of the force capacity may go to braking, which would fit force\nrising to 1.15 m while power and reactivity peak earlier; the book does not state this.\n\n**When to apply** — For advanced jumpers (Verkhoshansky 1963, 1964, 1966; p.222), use about\n0.75 m when the training goal is maximal power and reactivity, and up to about 1.15 m when\nthe goal is maximal dynamic force, per this protocol. For athletes unused to depth-jump\ntraining, start considerably lower and strictly limit volume, per the authors' caution.\nInference: this gives a citable historical anchor for programming depth-jump height, distinct\nfrom the fixed 0.4 m depth-jump reactivity test already in this library (see\n[[framework--reactivity-coefficient-and-depth-jump-test]]).\n\n**When NOT to apply** — This is single-lab, era-specific data (Verkhoshansky, 1963-1977) on\nan unspecified population (the authors do not state training age, sport or sample size for\nthe drop-height experiment); do not apply 0.75 m and 1.15 m to every athlete without\naccounting for the factors the authors name: whether the athlete is used to this training,\nbodymass, additional load and fall height (p.222). Do\nnot cite the book as saying force merely plateaus past 1.15 m, or merely falls: it says both\n(p.222). Do not conflate this variable drop-height range with the fixed 0.4 m reactivity test\n(see Related).\n\n**Example** — The book's own example is the Fig 4.7, 4.9 and 4.10 data described above; no\nfurther example is added here.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--kinetic-energy-stimulation-mechanical-basis-and-advantages]],\n[[claim--thrown-load-height-decreases-with-load-increases-with-drop-height]],\n[[framework--reactivity-coefficient-and-depth-jump-test]] (Ch3.2.1, a related but distinct\nfixed 0.4 m depth-jump protocol measuring reactive ability rather than optimal training drop\nheight),\n[[framework--shock-method-and-plyometric-terminology]].\n##### heuristic--match-plyometric-prescription-to-joint-angle-and-fiber-type.md\n---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"1.7\"\ntopic: plyometric prescription by joint angle and fibre type\ncategory: [plyometrics, individualization, fiber-type]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-09-26\n---\n\n# Deeper Knee Flexion Reduces \"Shock\" Loading; Fibre-Type Mix Should Guide Jump Tempo\n\n**What it is** — Two related findings on individualizing plyometric prescription.\n(1) Soft-tissue spring stiffness around the knee drops sharply with flexion angle\n(Greene & McMahon, cited PDF p. 58, printed p. 47: roughly 120 kN/m at 0 degrees down to\n30 kN/m at 75 degrees), so the Series Elastic Component's contribution falls as knee\nflexion increases, meaning deeper-flexion landings reduce the \"shock\" character of a\nplyometric exercise. (2) Vertical jump testing found rapid, short-range stretch phases\nwere most beneficial for the vastus medialis (fast-twitch-rich), while knee extensors of\nathletes with more slow-twitch fibres (e.g. distance runners) benefited more from slower,\nlarger-amplitude jumps with a longer transition between stretch and shortening (Bosco et\nal., 1982), attributed to differing cross-bridge lifetimes between fibre types. (PDF pp.\n57-58, printed pp. 46-47.)\n\n**Why it works** — Both findings trace back to the same underlying mechanics: SEC\ncontribution and cross-bridge dynamics vary with joint angle and fibre-type composition,\nso a jump protocol that is optimal for one joint angle or one athlete's fibre profile is\nnot automatically optimal for another.\n\n**When to apply** — The text itself draws the joint-angle application: \"this has profound\nimplications for the prescription of plyometric training, in particular the limiting of\njoint angle to ensure the adequacy of the 'shock' loading on the muscle complex\" (printed\n47). This supports limiting knee flexion depth in depth-jump and shock-method work\nspecifically when the goal is maximal elastic/reactive loading. Inference: deeper flexion\nshifts the exercise toward general strength/power rather than a true \"shock\" stimulus.\n**Inference:** also supports considering an athlete's likely fibre-type profile (position, event, or\ndirect biopsy/proxy data if available) when choosing fast-short vs. slow-larger-amplitude\njump variants; the text states the fibre-type finding but does not itself extend it to a\nprogramming rule.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — **Inference:** fibre-type composition is rarely measured directly\nin a collegiate athletics setting; do not treat sport or position alone as a reliable proxy\nwithout other supporting evidence (this entry's application to specific positions is its\nown inference, not stated in the text). The specific stiffness values (120 kN/m to 30\nkN/m) come from one biomechanics study on a flexible-board bouncing task, not from a\ndepth-jump or standard field-testing setup, so treat them as illustrative of the trend\nrather than as target numbers.\n\n**Example** — **Inference:** a shallow-knee-flexion, low-amplitude rebound drill better\ntargets true reactive/shock adaptation than a deep-squat-style jump, which behaves more\nlike a general power exercise; a distance-oriented athlete may respond better to a slightly\nslower, larger-amplitude jump variant than a power-position athlete.\n\n**Related** — [[principle--short-amortization-preserves-elastic-energy]],\n[[framework--shock-method-and-plyometric-terminology]].\n##### framework--strength-endurance-taxonomy-and-absolute-vs-partial.md\n---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"3.2.2\"\ntopic: strength-endurance classification\ncategory: [strength-endurance, testing, terminology]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-09-26\n---\n\n# Strength-Endurance Splits Two Ways (Dynamic/Static, General/Local), and \"Absolute\" vs \"Partial\" Endurance Explains Contradictory Research\n\n**What it is** — Strength-endurance (sustained muscle tension with minimal efficiency loss,\ne.g. a rower averaging 40-60 kgf per stroke over 230-250 strokes in 6-7 minutes, Samsonov\n1969) is classified along two independent axes. By muscular-work character: **dynamic\nstrength-endurance** (repeated tension without interruption in cyclic sports like running,\nswimming, rowing, or repeated short-rest acyclic work like repeated jumps/throws) versus\n**static strength-endurance** (sustained isometric tension or held posture, e.g. wrestling,\nsailing, shooting, skating). By muscle-group involvement: **general strength-endurance**\n(many muscle groups) versus **local strength-endurance** (individual muscle groups), a\ndistinction the text calls significant for exercise selection, since training should target\nthe specific muscle groups actually driving the sport skill (demonstrated via EMG in rowing,\nLazarov 1967, and via strength-performance correlation in swimming, Shchavelyev 1969).\nSeparately, Zatsiorsky et al (1965) resolve contradictory strength-endurance research\n(some studies found positive correlation with strength, some none, some negative) by\nsplitting endurance into **absolute endurance** (the overall result, ignoring the level of\nunderlying motor abilities) and **partial endurance** (the level of a specific motor ability\nonce the influence of other abilities is excluded, e.g. holding or moving a load at a fixed\n*percentage* of one's own max, which typically shows no or negative correlation with max\nstrength, versus absolute endurance at a fixed *absolute* load, which correlates positively\nwith max strength). For athletes who lift the same absolute load, distinguishing between\nthem requires calculating relative (bodymass-adjusted) static or dynamic strength-endurance;\nthis is stated for that specific comparison case, not as a general requirement for every\npartial-endurance comparison. Elsewhere, in Chapter 2 (printed 108-109), the text names a\nthree-way classification: static, dynamic, and *explosive* strength-endurance (repetitively\nexecuting explosive effort); this section (3.2.2) instead splits strength-endurance into\njust static and dynamic, with no mention of an explosive form. The text does not reconcile\nthese two classifications, and this entry does not either. Strength-endurance's specificity\nis stated as lower than speed's, \"although its carry-over from one type of activity to\nanother is greater\" (printed 143). (PDF pp. 153-155, printed pp. 142-144, \"3.2.2\nStrength-Endurance,\" Fig 3.12-3.13.)\n\n**Why it works** — The authors state only that different studies measured different\nendurance types (printed 143), not a mechanism for why that produces opposite-signed\ncorrelations. **Inference:** an absolute-load endurance test is partly re-measuring maximal\nstrength (a stronger athlete simply has more headroom under any fixed absolute load), while\na relative-load (percentage-of-own-max) test removes that confound and isolates the\nendurance-specific quality; this mechanism is not stated in the text.\n\n**When to apply** — Use the absolute/partial distinction whenever comparing\nstrength-endurance findings (in this book or elsewhere) that seem contradictory: check\nwhether the test load was a fixed absolute weight or a fixed percentage of each athlete's\nown max before concluding the results actually disagree. **Eric's baseline:** the\ndepartment's own testing (`method-baseline.md`, \"Testing and assessment\") already defaults\nnovices and most women's-sport testing to repetition-max protocols rather than 1RM.\n**Inference:** this framework implies that comparing rep-max results *across* athletes as a proxy for\nrelative strength-endurance requires normalizing to each athlete's own max (partial\nendurance), not comparing raw rep counts at a shared absolute load, if the goal is to\nisolate the endurance quality itself rather than re-measuring who is stronger.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The text is explicit that \"an objective, reliable means of\nevaluating strength-endurance in sporting activity has not yet been devised\" and that\n\"specialists frequently use inadequate tests\": this is a classification scheme for\nthinking about strength-endurance clearly, not a validated measurement protocol. The\narcher example (Fig 3.13) shows that as athletes become more skilled, the correlation\nbetween strength-endurance and competitive results actually *decreases* (control of\nmuscular contraction matters more than raw endurance), so this taxonomy should not be\nread as implying strength-endurance always predicts sport performance.\n\n**Example** — **Inference:** a quarter-squat-hold endurance test at a fixed 100 kg for a\nwhole roster measures absolute strength-endurance (confounded with who is strongest); the\nsame test at 60% of each athlete's own back-squat max measures partial (dynamic/static)\nstrength-endurance and is the more isolated endurance metric. This example is not in the\nsource. The text's own guidance is that the best way to develop strength-endurance is to\nexecute the competition exercises themselves under demanding conditions or in a large\nvolume, without excluding special strength exercises (printed 144).\n\n**Related** — [[claim--continuous-vs-interval-circuit-training-for-concurrent-goals]] (Ch1,\nadjacent programming content for endurance-strength concurrent goals).","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"da819662-a296-43c4-a6d4-e564c328d10c","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
