{"parentUuid":"a24959fd-c9f2-45e5-b07d-50420fe0b494","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","attachment":{"type":"hook_additional_context","content":["<context_guidance>\n  <tip>\n    Reading to Edit the file? Read is correct — Edit needs the exact bytes in your conversation to match against.\n    Reading to analyze, summarize, or extract from the file? Use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file(path, language, code) — the bytes stay in the sandbox and only what your code prints enters your conversation.\n  </tip>\n</context_guidance>"],"hookName":"PreToolUse:Read","toolUseID":"toolu_01GJDpycaukJUC66C4ifCGJL","hookEvent":"PreToolUse"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"d22d0609-63ef-4398-873d-3e6bcf9aa7bf","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:45:08.609Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nPreToolUse:Read hook additional context: <context_guidance>\n  <tip>\n    Reading to Edit the file? Read is correct — Edit needs the exact bytes in your conversation to match against.\n    Reading to analyze, summarize, or extract from the file? Use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file(path, language, code) — the bytes stay in the sandbox and only what your code prints enters your conversation.\n  </tip>\n</context_guidance>\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"d72b81b2-50fc-4524-87a0-f4fa878fabcc","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"624a8cec-33fa-4c0a-a445-c72ce718e6ab","agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GJDpycaukJUC66C4ifCGJL","type":"tool_result","content":"819\taerobic plyometrics, but the latter, unlike the Ericson movements, in no way comply with the definition of \n820\tclassical plyomctrics. It is apparent that the aerobics community, as well as strength conditioning coaches, have a \n821\tgreat deal to leam from an innovative non-impact plyometric system developed by a creative athlete in a \n822\twheelchair.\n823\tNon-Impact Plyometrics in Sports Training\n824\tAlthough sports involving explosive non-impact plyometric actions are commonplace in international sport, these \n825\tactions had not been used systematically as plyometric training drills prior to the fundamental research conducted \n826\tin this field by the author in 1994 (Fig 5.7 was derived from some of this work on upper-body non-impact \n827\tplyometrics). \n828\tEvery Western training book on plyometrics to date seems to have been devoted exclusively to \n829\timpact plyometrics using high inertia systems such as the human body, weighted medicine balls or other heavy \n830\tobjects. This is entirely a consequence of the West, in its quest for 'Russian training secrets', simply applying the \n831\tpioneering scientific work earned out by Verkhoshansky to devise an ever expanding repertoire of'plyometric \n832\tdrills’ and market various high inertia systems for explosive training.\n833\tIn this endeavour, a vast encyclopaedia of non-impact actions from boxing and the martial arts, in particular, \n834\tlay largely unutilised. To this we may now add many of the movements developed by Ericson in her SMART \n835\tseated fitness system. Low impact plyometric methods using very light objects for activities such as throwing or \n836\tkicking may also be included in this low inertia approach to plyometrics. Interestingly, many of the movements \n837\tderived from all of these systems share a common denominator in PNF (Proprioceptive Neuromuscular \n838\tFacilitation), in that they naturally follow spiral and diagonal paths which move both ways across the midline of \n839\tthe body. Deliberate deviation from the classical PNF patterns is also encouraged in applying non-impact \n840\tplyometrics, since this develops a wider range of more demanding functional motor abilities that typify sport.\n841\tThus, from the realm of martial arts alone, one may borrow thrust punches, rising blocks, downward blocks, \n842\tknife-hand thrusts, palm-heel strikes, front kicks, side snap kicks, side thrust kicks, front kicks and many other \n843\tstandard forms in a wide variety of full or limited range movements to offer non-impact plyometric drills. These \n844\tactions may be performed solo in a static stance with some of the classical pre-stretching techniques, with \n845\texplosive punches terminating in a brief isometrically-hcld end position or with rapid recoil actions which snap \n846\tthe extremities back to their starting positions.\n847\tThe same movements may be done in fixed routines between opponents standing facing one another, in the \n848\tform of kata (prescribed sequences o f movements) or in freestyle sparring (no body contact). These offer a \n849\trepertoire, not only of non-impact plyometric drills, but also of many other methods for enhancing explosive \n850\tstrength, movement speed, quickness, rapid decision-making skill, reaction time, kinaesthetic awareness and other \n851\tqualities which are important in sports demanding rapidity of action. The use of martial arts, boxing and Ericson's \n852\tSMART methods in explosive sports training was described and demonstrated at the 1994 NSCA (National \n853\tStrength & Conditioning Association) Conference by Siff and Ericson (Myths and Facts o f Speed-Strength, \n854\tExplosive Power and Plyometric Training June 1994). The advisability of integrating such methods into a \n855\tperiodised weight training scheme was emphasized to enhance acquisition of the necessary explosive qualities and \n856\tminimise the possibility of injury, as was the role of cognitive plyometric processes and asymmetric plyometric \n857\ttraining in ensuring that sport specific motor qualities are developed.\n858\tResisted and Water Plyometric Training\n859\tPlyometric drills may be offered with some form of resistance being imposed during the amortisation phase, \n860\tconcentric rebound phase or both of these phases. Thus, one may jump and land with light weights or against \n861\tresistance offered by elastic cords attached to a harness located near the waist or hips. Although both of these \n862\tmethods increase the landing impact and ostensibly increase the strength of reflex muscle contraction, they also \n863\tincrease the risk of impact and overload injuries and should be applied occasionally with great caution. Even \n864\tnormal running or hurdling with very light weights constitutes a form of loaded plyometric training. No resistance\n865\t281\n866\t\n867\t\n868\t######## PDF 293 / printed 282\n869\tSUPERTRAINING\n870\tmethods should not use heavy weights or stiff elastic cords during depth jumps, since this increases the coupl’ \n871\ttime and imposes unnecessarily large loads on the spine and the tendons of the lower extremities, especia \n872\tduring the landing or contact phase.\n873\tA far safer resisted alternative is water or aqua plyometrics, a method used for many decades to decrease the \n874\tlanding force and increase the resistance during the recoil phase. Although this variant does not offer maximal cf \n875\tshock method plyometrics, it can serve as a usual form of preparatory or submaximal plyometrics, especially for \n876\tone-legged drills. The depth of water determines the level of resistance, with chest or shoulder high water offering \n877\tgreater resistance during the landing and take-off phases, less intense eccentric muscic activity, smaller impact \n878\tforces and enhanced safety. Repeated aqua jumps for developing rebound explosive muscle endurance with one or \n879\tboth legs are considerably safer than their land-based equivalents, though they should not completely replace the \n880\tlatter, since they do not adequately develop the specific neuromuscular patterns or functional needs of explosive \n881\tsports. Upper body aqua plyomctrics may be offered in varying depths of water by performing suitable non­\n882\timpact plyometric actions derived from the martial arts or SMART fitness systems, as discussed above.\n883\tPopular Inform ation on Plyom etrics\n884\tMost of the popular plyometric textbooks in the West (e.g. Chu, 1992; Radcliffe & Farantinos, 1985) present an \n885\tinteresting array of jumping and rebound activities, many of which do not constitute true plyometric training as \n886\toriginally conceived by Russian scientists. Such exercises are often supplementary or preparatory movements \n887\twhich lead up to or accompany plyomctric training, but they are not really ‘shock method’ plyometric drills. \n888\tMost books classify extended range swinging, passing or catching of mcdicine balls or other objects as \n889\t'plyometric’, although the amortisation phase is too long to produce classical plyometrics. This is not to say that \n890\tthese exerciscs are not useful: they can play a useful role in enhancing qualities such as eye-hand coordination, \n891\teccentric strength and starting strength, but they arc not strictly plyometric.\n892\tAlthough some of these books, often advertised to be the most complete ever written on plyometrics, claim \n893\tto dispel the myths and misinformation about plyometrics, the contents do not confirm these sentiments, for they \n894\toften fail to reveal an thorough understanding of the scientific model upon which the original Russian shock \n895\tmethod (“udamiye metod”) is based.\n896\tThis method was refined by identifying many different strength qualities such as strength-speed, speed- \n897\tstrength, explosive strength, acceleration strength, maximal strength, absolute strength and starting strength. In \n898\tparticular, it relies on the production of a very brief explosive-isometric and eccentric-isometric phase which \n899\tprecedes the release of the elastic energy stored in the tendons and other components of the series elastic \n900\tcomponent o f the muscle complex during the eccentric deceleration phase. Although most books pay detailed \n901\tattention to the myotatic reflex, which comprises a tonic, longer duration reflex and a phasic, rapid reflex, they do \n902\tnot discuss their relationship to the different types o f plyometric drill. The relevance of positive supportive \n903\treaction, the crossed extensor reflex and other important reflexes is also not covered. The role of correctly phased \n904\tbreathing, which is vital to maximal plyomctrics and efficient stabilisation of the body, is not mentioned in any \n905\tbook.\n906\tDistinction is seldom made between maximal and submaximal plyometrics (and how they are produced), nor \n907\tis the concurrent and sequential prescription of plyometric and other forms of training discussed in any depth. \n908\tFor example, several books advocate sessions with over 100 repetitions of plyomctric movements, sometimes \n909\tcombined with resistance training. This volume of maximal plyometrics (unlike sub-maximal plyometrics) is \n910\tneither productive nor safe for any athlete. Maximal plyometrics, like maximal weight lifting, involves single all- \n911\tout repetitions separated by a few minutes of rest. It is extremely demanding on the central nervous system and \n912\timposes great mechanical strain on the tendons, in particular.\n913\tTo ascertain whether an athlete needs plyometric or heavy resistance training depends on measuring the \n914\tstrength deficit (see Ch 1), prescription of plyometric training remains haphazard, yet plyometric books rarely \n915\tmention this concept. The opposition to plyometric training (powermetrics) by some experts is often the result \n916\tof this type of guesswork training, which can hinder performance and increase the risk of injury.\n917\tSome authors rightly criticize the recommendation sometimes put forward that athletes should be able to \n918\tsquat 1.5-2 times bodymass in order to use certain plyomctric drills, sincc they consider that this criterion is not \n919\tbased on research evidence and does not apply to all plyometric exercises. This recommendation is a safety \n920\tmeasure based on force plate analysis of maximal plyometrics in the form of depth jum ps, where the ground \n921\treaction force on the lower extremity can easily exceed 6 times bodyweight. This guideline was never intended to\n922\t282\n923\t\n924\t\n925\t######## PDF 294 / printed 283\n926\tSUPERTRAINING\n927\tapply religiously to submaximal plyometrics using small drops, skipping drills or less demanding actions. This \n928\tmisunderstanding of Russian guidelines is largely a result of an incomplete grasp of the scientific principles on \n929\twhich the original shock method was based.\n930\tCoaches would appreciate more what plyometrics really is if they were introduced to some of the facilitation \n931\ttechniques used in physiotherapeutic PNF (see 7.2). The physical therapist sometimes imposes a short, sharp \n932\tjerk on an eccentrically or isometrically contracting muscle to produce strong muscle contraction in a weakened \n933\tmusclc. Relatively prolonged movements such as many of the drills recommended in most books are used for \n934\tother purposes, including the reduction of spasm or increasing functional range of movement. These principles \n935\thave preceded sporting plyometrics by several decades, so it is surprising that so much confusion still abounds \n936\tover plyomctrics. Certainly, the so-called plyometric drills in current Western texts are known to enhance \n937\tperformance, but it is not predominantly due to plyometric training, but more to the increased loading o f eccentric \n938\tcontraction, the conditioning o f connective tissue, the improvement o f voluntary starting strength from the quasi- \n939\tisometric state, the use ofprestretch, and enhancement o f acceleration strength by increasing the velocity of \n940\tprojectiles such as medicine balls.\n941\tPlyometrics and the Brain\n942\tRccent brain research reveals that piyomctric action is not simply a phenomenon that can be adequately explained \n943\ton the basis of stored elastic energy and the stretch shortening cycle. The phenomenon of plyometrics involves fa r \n944\tmore intricate processes than reflexive muscle activities that operate at the level of the spinal column and bypass the \n945\tbrain. Many of the subtleties and complexities o f plyometrics can be missed if one is preoccupied with the \n946\tprescription of so-called piyomctric drills or with laboratory measurement of the stretch-shortening cyclc as a local \n947\tmusculotendinous phenomenon. In both cases, it is easy to overlook the possibility that activities such as depth- \n948\tjumping or medicinc ball throwing may involve non-local events, particularly the enormously complicated computer \n949\tprograms in the brain.\n950\tWhile it is accurate to state that plyometric actions arc extremely rapid and therefore occur at the level of \n951\tspecific spinal nerves without involving conscious thought processes, it must be remembered that the preparatory \n952\tperiod prior to the piyomctric action can involve higher order mental processing of exceptional intricacy (sec Fig \n953\t3.11). In fact, the period prior to execution of any skilled movement involves running mental programmes that have \n954\tbeen stored and refined by repeated rehearsal in the brain of the well-trained athlete.\n955\tOne can distinguish further between a plyometric action produced under conditions in which the athlete \n956\tanticipates a particular course of action (cognitive plyometrics) and a plyometric action produced as a purely \n957\treflexive response under unexpected circumstances (non-cognitive plyometrics). The latter type of plyometrics \n958\toccur primarily under survival or self-preservation conditions intended to protect a person from injury or death. \n959\tThe rapidity of the event does not permit any forethought or planning, but relies on primitive reflexes that generally \n960\tlend to bypass the brain by acting at the more spontaneous level of the spinal cord. Cognitive plyometrics, though \n961\talso a very rapid phenomenon, is preceded by a phase of thinking that prepares the person for a specific course of \n962\taction to achieve a definite goal.\n963\tOne should not conclude simplistically that non-cognitivc plyometrics are purely instinctive or automatic \n964\tactions which involve no cognitive or learning processes. It should never be forgotten that efficient execution of \n965\tpiyomctric actions and drills only takes place after a considerable amount o f practice using cognitive processes. In \n966\tfact, most athletes do not manage to perfect plyometric skills even after many weeks of practice. The use of \n967\tnumerous sequential repetitions to imprint these skills may constitute the inappropriate strategy because classical \n968\tplyometrics are 1 RM movements which require as much as several minutes of rest before recovery is complete. \n969\tSubmaximal plyometrics and pseudo-plyometrics may be performed in a state of advancing fatigue, unlike classical \n970\tshock method' plyometrics. The neural and reflexive processes are quite different in each case.\n971\tEven then, what appears to be quite reflexive still involves a certain degree of cognitive preparation and control. \n972\tThe lack of adequate mention o f the importance of cognitive and learning processes in plyometrics by all popular \n973\ttexts on the subject constitutes a major deficit in the field. Reflexes are casually referred to as entirely robotic \n974\tactions that elicit or inhibit musclc contraction, but little is mentioned of the fact that reflexes can be trained and \n975\tmodified, something that the renowned Russian physiologist, Pavlov, had proved some 70 years ago in the form of \n976\this'conditioned reflexes'. Later, Skinner (1938) distinguished between two types of conditioning: which he called \n977\trespondent conditioning, in which the response is modified by a preceding stimulus, and operant conditioning, in \n978\twhich the response is strengthened or weakened by events that follow the response.\n979\t283\n980\t\n981\t\n982\t######## PDF 295 / printed 284\n983\tSUPERTRAINING\n984\tIn the plyometric situation, this implies that a stimulus such as an emotion (e.g. fear, excitement) or a men \n985\timage (or visualisation) one entertains before an action like a depth jump can shape the subsequent neuromuscJ \n986\treflexes. Likewise, any event (such as the jarring of the body, post-exercise fatigue or an injury) following \n987\tplyometric action can modify these reflexes. In fact, it is common for plyometric activity to be associated w' \n988\tboth respondent and operant conditioning.\n989\tIt has also been shown that if the reflex learning proccss is carried out with the subject in a parti \n990\tphysiological or psychological state, then the conditioned reflexes are exhibited only when the same state is pre \n991\tbut not when the state is different. This phenomenon is known as state-dependent learning. Its existence imp!’ \n992\tthat plyometric proficiency or deficiency learned in a specific training situation may not be reproduced \n993\tcompetitive circumstances which differ from those of the learning situation.\n994\tWhat the preceding few paragraphs imply is that it is only partially correct to regard plyometrics as a pr \n995\tin which basic reflexes automatically produce a powerful muscle contraction which is accompanicd by elastic energy \n996\treleased after the muscle has been stretched eccentrically. It is vital to note that the entire plyometric process \n997\tinvolves complex state-dependent learning events which modify the pattern o f reflex activity according to how the \n998\tgiven plyometric drills are executed. This may be one of the reasons why some researchers have carried out \n999\texperiments which apparently have shown plyometric training to produce little or no improvement in speed- \n1000\tstrength or sporting performance. One cannot ignore the neural factors involved in studying the effects of any \n1001\tneuromuscular phenomena: study of muscular output alone is incomplete and lack of control over essential neural \n1002\tfactors affecting the experiment can easily distort the results.\n1003\tIt should be more obvious now that plyometric training is not simply a matter o f dropping from a height or \n1004\tthrowing an object for a given number of repetitions to improve fitness qualities such as strength-speed or explosive \n1005\tstrength. \n1006\tSport usually involves the reproduction of a wide variety of technical skills under expected and \n1007\tunexpected conditions, so that feedforward training and other 'visualisation' (mental and kinaesthetic imaging) \n1008\ttechniques must be used to enable the athlete not only to increase explosive strength and strength-specd, but also to \n1009\tefficiently manage any plyometric actions under real sporting conditions.\n1010\tLest the connection between visualization and movement be oversimplified by sports psychologists, \n1011\tneuroscientist. Dr Kensall Wise of the University of Michigan, points out that the part o f the brain which can \n1012\tvisualise a given action is totally separate from those parts which initiate the muscle contractions to carry out the \n1013\taction (Scientific American, Oct 1988: 23). Although fairly modem research has identified some of the connections \n1014\tbetween some regions of the brain and functions such as sight, hearing and motor output, researchers still have only \n1015\ta vague idea of how the nervous system initiates and manages any given actions.\n1016\tThe importance of ballistic activity to humankind recently has been shown to extend far beyond the realms of \n1017\tsport. Neurophysiologist, William Calvin, has proposed the fascinating hypothesis that the brain's planning of \n1018\tballistic movements may have played a major role in the development of language, music and intelligence over the \n1019\tages (Scientific American, Oct 1994). He makes this proposal, since ballistic movements and language processes \n1020\tinvolve some of the same regions of the brain, in particular those associated with sequencing and planning. In \n1021\treaching this conclusion, he emphasizes that ballistic movements, unlike cocontractive slower movements, require a \n1022\tgreat amount of planning and problem solving. Slow movements may be corrected readily by ongoing feedback \n1023\tinformation, but ballistic movements require the brain to determine every detail of the action in advance by mentally \n1024\tplanning the exact sequence of neural activation for numerous individual muscles.\n1025\tApparently, parts of the language cortex of the brain serve a far more generalised function than previously \n1026\tsuspected. It is implicated in the production of novel sequences of sensations and movements for both the hands \n1027\tand mouth, so that ballistic arm actions may play a role in mental development. Calvin adds that improvements \n1028\tin language skills might improve dexterity and vice versa. The emphasis placed by Russian coachcs on athletes \n1029\tbeing able to accurately describe, draw and visualise sporting movements would appear to correlate with this \n1030\tproposal. Instead of simply executing entertaining plyometric drills like biological robots, athletes would be well \n1031\tadvised to integrate cognitive processes more actively into the training programme.\n1032\tVarious Shock M ethods\n1033\tPlyometrics should not be thought of as the only type of'shock training', even though the term 'shock method' \n1034\twas the preferred name given to the plyometric method by Verkhoshansky when he first formalised this category \n1035\tof impulsive training. Training plyometrics may be regarded as one of several shock methods that may be used in \n1036\tspecial strength training to provide intense stimulation to compel the body to attain a higher level of functional\n1037\t284\n1038\t\n1039\t\n1040\t######## PDF 296 / printed 285\n1041\tSUPERTRAINING\n1042\tadaptation. The principle of gradual progressive overloading may, after a prolonged period of unvaried use, \n1043\tsometimes fail to improve strength or performance, as is commonly the case among elite athletes. \n1044\tIn this \n1045\tsituation, it may be necessary to disrupt the persistent state of stagnation by use of one of the following shock \n1046\tmethods (see 7.1):\n1047\t• plyometrics \n1048\t• forced repetitions\n1049\t• supramaximal methods \n1050\t• electrical stimulation (macrostimulation)\n1051\t• repeated single I RM training \n1052\t• restricted range maxima\n1053\t• contrast methods \n1054\t• maximal eccentrics\n1055\tThe appropriate method, its timing and the duration of its use must be chosen with great care, since lack of \n1056\tprogress may be due to overtraining rather than long-term stagnation. The use of any shock method under such \n1057\tcircumstances is potentially harmful and definitely detrimental to performance. These methods arc generally \n1058\tunsuitable for novices and should only be employed by experienced athletes with a solid background of special \n1059\tstrength training.\n1060\t5.2.5 The D evelopm ent of Strength-Endurance\n1061\tStrength-endurance involves the production of muscular tension without a decrease in efficiency over a long \n1062\tperiod. Strength-endurance, like explosive strength, has several forms, depending upon the motor pattern of the \n1063\tathletic activity, in particular the categories of dynamic and static endurance.\n1064\tDynamic strength-endurance is typical of exercises involving repetitive and significant muscular tension at a \n1065\trelatively slow speed of movement, as well as repeated cyclic or acyclic exercises requiring speed-strength. In the \n1066\tlatter case we are referring to the specific endurance to execute special speed-strength and explosive work without \n1067\tadecrease in efficiency over a relatively long period. Static strength-endurance is typical of activities associated \n1068\twith the maintenance of maximal and near-maximal tension, as well as the moderate tension necessary for holding \n1069\ta specific posture (for example, in shooting or speed-skating).\n1070\tThe development of strength-endurance is an inherent characteristic and a fundamental principle of training \n1071\tfor dev eloping general endurance. Training for strength-endurance is determined predominantly by:\n1072\t1. The magnitude of the load\n1073\t2. The tempo of movement\n1074\t3. The duration of each movement and each phase of the movements\n1075\t4. The rest interval between training sessions\n1076\t5. The length of the training period\n1077\t6. The initial levels of strength-cndurance of the athlete\n1078\t7. The kinesiological structure of the movement\n1079\t8. The relative involvement of static and dynamic strength-endurance.\n1080\tRepetition work with weights 25-50% of IRM at a moderate tempo (from 60-120 repetitions/minute) is used for \n1081\tdeveloping strength-endurance. The effectiveness of strength-endurance development, using equivalent weights \n1082\tand movement tempos, will be higher if the work is executed to complete fatigue, although work of shorter \n1083\tduration (60% of the maximal work capacity time) also will yield satisfactory results (Agolinsky, 1953; \n1084\tKorobkov, 1953; Monogarov, 1958; Diachkov, 1961).\n1085\tStrength-endurance, like the other characteristics of muscular activity, is specific. However, the specificity \n1086\tof strength-endurance is expressed to a lesser degree than the specificity of speed, for example, while its carry­\n1087\tover from one activity to another is greater. When the activity involves repetitively overcoming significant \n1088\tresistance (exceeding 75-80% of IRM), one cannot enhance endurance, since one becomes constrained to \n1089\tdeveloping strength predominantly (Zatsiorsky, 1966).\n1090\tThis study by Zatsiorsky showed that isometric endurance increased 84% as a result of exercising 10 times a \n1091\tday for 5 weeks with the elbow flexors holding a 60% maximal contraction for as long as possible. Dynamic \n1092\tauiurance increased by 93% as a result of performing these arm curls with a weight of 60% of 1 RM at a rate of \n1093\t28repetitions per minute until it was impossible to increase the tempo further. At the same time, the ability to \n1094\texecute repetitive isometric contractions (60% of maximal strength for 5 seconds of tension and 2 seconds of rest) \n1095\tto failure increased by 219%. A similar experiment with 10 dynamic movements executed daily with maximal \n1096\tresistance for 5 weeks resulted in increases in both dynamic and static strength, but dynamic and static endurance\n1097\t285\n1098\t\n1099\t\n1100\t######## PDF 297 / printed 286\n1101\tSUPERTRAINING\n1102\tremained almost unchanged (Hansen, 1963). This indicates that it is necessary to optimise the volume of work \n1103\trequired to develop strength-endurance.\n1104\tIt has been established that an increase in strength-endurance contributes to improving endurance in distance \n1105\trunners, skiers and skaters (Popov, 1968; Nabatnikova, 1972; Mikhailov & Panov, 1975). However, it must be \n1106\temphasized that the magnitude of the strength load used is different if, on the one hand, the key quality required \n1107\tis general endurance and, on the other hand, it is strength-endurance.\n1108\tIt should be noted that certain popular strength exercises such as running with a sack of sand weighing 25% of \n1109\tthe subject's bodyweight, barbell pressing, squats, 'good mornings', and twisting with a barbell not only do not \n1110\tenhance, but to a certain degree, will even hinder the development of endurance in cyclic type exercises \n1111\t(Maisuradze, 1960). It some instances the increase in strength is associated with excessive muscle hypertrophy, \n1112\twhich is a by-product of strength training. Endurance and hypertrophy do not increase simultaneously and there \n1113\tis no correlation between them (Maison & Broekcr, 1941; McMorris & Elkins, 1954).\n1114\tThe lack of a definite correlation between special strength and muscle mass in different sports where the key \n1115\tquality is endurance has been observed by several authors (Chistyakov, 1965; Chcmyaev, 1965; Nabatnikova, \n1116\t1972). This conccms primarily cyclic sports (middle distance running, skating and skiing). In other cases, for \n1117\texample, with gymnasts, a positive linear correlation (R = 0.77) is noticed between strength-endurance and \n1118\trelative strength (Zhalei, 1964). Therefore, in those cases where endurance is associated with the display of \n1119\tsignificant strength, some muscular hypertrophy does not negatively influence training results.\n1120\tThe general principles concerning the development of strength-endurance, which were discusscd earlier, arc \n1121\tapplied differently in each specific case, depending on the characteristics of the sport, for example:\n1122\t• During the preparatory period, skiers should exercise with weights which are up to 65% of 1RM, in \n1123\tcombination with simulation exercises on a flat surface, as well as with movements on roller-skis \n1124\t(Chistyakov, 1965).\n1125\t• Middle and long distance runners obtain good results from lifting a barbell of 55-60% of bodymass up to head \n1126\tlevel 8-10 times rhythmically, clean-and-jerks with various weights (60-85% of bodymass); jumps with \n1127\tkettlebells from a squat position (using about one-third of bodymass for 18-20 repetitions) and squats with \n1128\t55-60% of bodymass until complete fatigue (Likachycvskaya & Kovalchuk, 1963). Exercises with weights \n1129\tof 60-80% of 1RM for multiple repetitions, jumping and hard running are also recommended (Popov, 1966).\n1130\t• Exercises with heavy weights (80-85% of 1RM), as well as medium and small weights are recommended for \n1131\tdeveloping strength-endurance in skaters. Exercises with heavy loads are executed for several sets at a \n1132\tmoderate tempo for 4-12 repetitions per set until the point of complete fatigue, with 2-4 minutes of rest \n1133\tbetween sets. Exercises with light and moderate weights are executed as rapidly as possible for 15-25 \n1134\trepetitions per set, for 3-5 sets with rest intervals of 5-8 minutes (Mikhailov & Panov, 1975).\n1135\t• Male rowers obtain good results with weights of 50-80% of 1 RM and female rowers with loads of 30-40% of \n1136\t1RM (Ulrich ct al, 1966). Significant improvement (up to 40% from initial levels) in strength-endurancehzs \n1137\tbeen noted in women rowers working with light weights (18-20kg) for many repetitions (Chumakova, 1964). \n1138\tImprovement is greater with these weights than with larger loads (35-50 kg) executed in sets of 2-3 \n1139\trepetitions, and it is even greater (by 28%) than that produced by a combination of both methods.\n1140\tIt is necessary to increase the amount of weight and the number of repetitions as strength-endurance increases. \n1141\tThe following method is recommended for skaters to develop strength-endurance in the legs: increasing the \n1142\tnumber of barbell squats by 20-30 each week (beginning with 50 squats per set and over a period of 2-3 months \n1143\tincreasing this to 200-300 repetitions), then increasing the amount of weight and starting a new cycle of strength \n1144\ttraining, but beginning with a smaller number of squats (Mikhailov & Panov, 1975).\n1145\tFor developing strength-endurance it is always appropriate to strive to execute the work under demanding \n1146\tconditions, though with movements whose coordination and structure in space and time are close to those of the \n1147\tspecial exercise. For instance, a runner may run with a small parachute resisting him or pull a loaded sled in the \n1148\tpreparatory phases (Popov, 1966). Similarly, gymnasts may use a heavy belt and vest (Zhilei, 1964) and rowers \n1149\ta spccial water-resistancc dcvice attached to the hull of the boat (Chuprun, 1966). In the case of the rowers. \n1150\tstrength-endurance as measured by the number of pull-ups, the number of arm flexions in the prone position and \n1151\tthe number of barbell presses in 30 seconds increased by 57.1%, 27.8% and 9.1%, respectively.\n1152\t286\n1153\t\n1154\t\n1155\t######## PDF 298 / printed 287\n1156\tSUPERTRAINING\n1157\tThus, multiple repetition exercises with various weights should be considered the fundamental method for \n1158\tdeveloping strength-endurance, with the load being determined by the dynamics of the given exercise. In cases \n1159\trequiring intense effort, an optimally heavy weight should be used together with light weights or with exercises \n1160\tsimulating the given sport activity. If the special exercise is associated with the prolonged production of moderate \n1161\tforce, then it is useful to work with light weights in repeated sets to the point of fatigue or failure.\n1162\tStrength training should not result in increased muscle mass in those types of sport where the key quality is \n1163\tendurance of moderate work output. If strength-endurance is required, especially in those cases where it is \n1164\tnecessary to overcome a large resistance, a small increase in muscle mass is permissible. In no cases, should the \n1165\ttraining ever produce a decrease in relative strength of any muscle groups in the body.\n1166\t5.3 Application of Special S trength Training Means\n1167\tThe value of systematic application of the training means has been investigated repeatedly, but the preliminary \n1168\tconclusions still need to be extended. Consequently, there have been attempts to devise a rational combination of \n1169\tstrength, speed and endurance exercises to:\n1170\t• raise the body's general work capacity (Zimkin, Yakovlev, Korobkov, Zatsiorsky )\n1171\t• investigate the effectiveness of sequential and concurrent (parallel or complcx) use of the means for \n1172\tdeveloping endurance and speed-strength (Ozolin, Diachkov, Mikhailov, Volkov ct al)\n1173\t• study the effect of the sequential use of aerobic and anaerobic exercise on the development of endurance \n1174\t(Volkov ct al, Gordon et al)\n1175\t• investigate the sequential use of strength and speed-strength exercises as one of the schcmes for \n1176\tsystematically developing explosive strength (Ozolin, Diachkov, Vorobyev, Filin, Vcrkhoshansky).\n1177\tHowever, research in the area of the systematic use of means is limited and no definitive practical \n1178\trecommendations have yet emerged.\n1179\tThe systematic use of means of special strength training over a given period produces a cumulative effect \n1180\twhich is significantly greater than the separate non-sequential application of these same means. In principle, there \n1181\tat two possible variants for the systematic application of means: sequential and concurrent, as mentioned above:\n1182\t1. The first variant (sequential means) involves the strictly determined, successive introduction of different \n1183\tmeans, each with a higher training effect, into the annual or multi-annual cyclc. The theoretical basis for this \n1184\tvariant is based upon two observations:\n1185\t• the training effect of any means diminishes as the body adapts to its influence\n1186\t• the training effect must be maintained by using a complcx of means to steadily increase the body's special \n1187\twork capacity.\n1188\tIn addition, experience and research indicate that the training regime designed to develop a particular motor ability \n1189\tis determined largely by the degree of continuity in sequentially changing means with different training effects. \n1190\tBecause of this, functional changes produced by earlier means should create favourable conditions for achieving \n1191\tthe training effect of subsequent means. To devise a systematic sequence of the means of special strength \n1192\ttraining, one must:\n1193\t• assess the quantitative and qualitative components of the training effect\n1194\t• categorise the means relative to the level of the athlete's special fitness.\n1195\tThis, in effect, is the subject of research aimed at identifying the principles for devising logical systems for \n1196\tapplying the different means of special strength training effectively. As an example, the sequential development of \n1197\tendurance begins by training aerobic (cardiorespiratory) processes, then non-oxidative glycolysis and finally high \n1198\tenergy phosphagen processes. This sequential introduction of'anaerobic' work creates favourable conditions for \n1199\tthe training effect of'aerobic'work (Ozolin, 1959, 1970; Volkov, 1963; Yakovlev, 1957; Borisov, 1979).\n1200\t2. The second variant (concurrent means) involves the parallel use of means (over an extended period) that are \n1201\tclose to the body’s work regime, but which produce a largely different training effect. This method is based on \n1202\texperimental evidence that adaptation elicited by the separate components of the complex of training work is not \n1203\tamply a summation of the separate effects, but the synergistic result of the interaction of the effects at each stage \n1204\tof training. This interaction produces a new physical state with a significantly greater work potential than would\n1205\t287\n1206\t"}]},"uuid":"f14a22f6-c74d-4e88-ad4b-2bcad2482baa","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:45:08.842Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/_tmp/ch5b_text.txt","content":"aerobic plyometrics, but the latter, unlike the Ericson movements, in no way comply with the definition of \nclassical plyomctrics. It is apparent that the aerobics community, as well as strength conditioning coaches, have a \ngreat deal to leam from an innovative non-impact plyometric system developed by a creative athlete in a \nwheelchair.\nNon-Impact Plyometrics in Sports Training\nAlthough sports involving explosive non-impact plyometric actions are commonplace in international sport, these \nactions had not been used systematically as plyometric training drills prior to the fundamental research conducted \nin this field by the author in 1994 (Fig 5.7 was derived from some of this work on upper-body non-impact \nplyometrics). \nEvery Western training book on plyometrics to date seems to have been devoted exclusively to \nimpact plyometrics using high inertia systems such as the human body, weighted medicine balls or other heavy \nobjects. This is entirely a consequence of the West, in its quest for 'Russian training secrets', simply applying the \npioneering scientific work earned out by Verkhoshansky to devise an ever expanding repertoire of'plyometric \ndrills’ and market various high inertia systems for explosive training.\nIn this endeavour, a vast encyclopaedia of non-impact actions from boxing and the martial arts, in particular, \nlay largely unutilised. To this we may now add many of the movements developed by Ericson in her SMART \nseated fitness system. Low impact plyometric methods using very light objects for activities such as throwing or \nkicking may also be included in this low inertia approach to plyometrics. Interestingly, many of the movements \nderived from all of these systems share a common denominator in PNF (Proprioceptive Neuromuscular \nFacilitation), in that they naturally follow spiral and diagonal paths which move both ways across the midline of \nthe body. Deliberate deviation from the classical PNF patterns is also encouraged in applying non-impact \nplyometrics, since this develops a wider range of more demanding functional motor abilities that typify sport.\nThus, from the realm of martial arts alone, one may borrow thrust punches, rising blocks, downward blocks, \nknife-hand thrusts, palm-heel strikes, front kicks, side snap kicks, side thrust kicks, front kicks and many other \nstandard forms in a wide variety of full or limited range movements to offer non-impact plyometric drills. These \nactions may be performed solo in a static stance with some of the classical pre-stretching techniques, with \nexplosive punches terminating in a brief isometrically-hcld end position or with rapid recoil actions which snap \nthe extremities back to their starting positions.\nThe same movements may be done in fixed routines between opponents standing facing one another, in the \nform of kata (prescribed sequences o f movements) or in freestyle sparring (no body contact). These offer a \nrepertoire, not only of non-impact plyometric drills, but also of many other methods for enhancing explosive \nstrength, movement speed, quickness, rapid decision-making skill, reaction time, kinaesthetic awareness and other \nqualities which are important in sports demanding rapidity of action. The use of martial arts, boxing and Ericson's \nSMART methods in explosive sports training was described and demonstrated at the 1994 NSCA (National \nStrength & Conditioning Association) Conference by Siff and Ericson (Myths and Facts o f Speed-Strength, \nExplosive Power and Plyometric Training June 1994). The advisability of integrating such methods into a \nperiodised weight training scheme was emphasized to enhance acquisition of the necessary explosive qualities and \nminimise the possibility of injury, as was the role of cognitive plyometric processes and asymmetric plyometric \ntraining in ensuring that sport specific motor qualities are developed.\nResisted and Water Plyometric Training\nPlyometric drills may be offered with some form of resistance being imposed during the amortisation phase, \nconcentric rebound phase or both of these phases. Thus, one may jump and land with light weights or against \nresistance offered by elastic cords attached to a harness located near the waist or hips. Although both of these \nmethods increase the landing impact and ostensibly increase the strength of reflex muscle contraction, they also \nincrease the risk of impact and overload injuries and should be applied occasionally with great caution. Even \nnormal running or hurdling with very light weights constitutes a form of loaded plyometric training. No resistance\n281\n\n\n######## PDF 293 / printed 282\nSUPERTRAINING\nmethods should not use heavy weights or stiff elastic cords during depth jumps, since this increases the coupl’ \ntime and imposes unnecessarily large loads on the spine and the tendons of the lower extremities, especia \nduring the landing or contact phase.\nA far safer resisted alternative is water or aqua plyometrics, a method used for many decades to decrease the \nlanding force and increase the resistance during the recoil phase. Although this variant does not offer maximal cf \nshock method plyometrics, it can serve as a usual form of preparatory or submaximal plyometrics, especially for \none-legged drills. The depth of water determines the level of resistance, with chest or shoulder high water offering \ngreater resistance during the landing and take-off phases, less intense eccentric muscic activity, smaller impact \nforces and enhanced safety. Repeated aqua jumps for developing rebound explosive muscle endurance with one or \nboth legs are considerably safer than their land-based equivalents, though they should not completely replace the \nlatter, since they do not adequately develop the specific neuromuscular patterns or functional needs of explosive \nsports. Upper body aqua plyomctrics may be offered in varying depths of water by performing suitable non­\nimpact plyometric actions derived from the martial arts or SMART fitness systems, as discussed above.\nPopular Inform ation on Plyom etrics\nMost of the popular plyometric textbooks in the West (e.g. Chu, 1992; Radcliffe & Farantinos, 1985) present an \ninteresting array of jumping and rebound activities, many of which do not constitute true plyometric training as \noriginally conceived by Russian scientists. Such exercises are often supplementary or preparatory movements \nwhich lead up to or accompany plyomctric training, but they are not really ‘shock method’ plyometric drills. \nMost books classify extended range swinging, passing or catching of mcdicine balls or other objects as \n'plyometric’, although the amortisation phase is too long to produce classical plyometrics. This is not to say that \nthese exerciscs are not useful: they can play a useful role in enhancing qualities such as eye-hand coordination, \neccentric strength and starting strength, but they arc not strictly plyometric.\nAlthough some of these books, often advertised to be the most complete ever written on plyometrics, claim \nto dispel the myths and misinformation about plyometrics, the contents do not confirm these sentiments, for they \noften fail to reveal an thorough understanding of the scientific model upon which the original Russian shock \nmethod (“udamiye metod”) is based.\nThis method was refined by identifying many different strength qualities such as strength-speed, speed- \nstrength, explosive strength, acceleration strength, maximal strength, absolute strength and starting strength. In \nparticular, it relies on the production of a very brief explosive-isometric and eccentric-isometric phase which \nprecedes the release of the elastic energy stored in the tendons and other components of the series elastic \ncomponent o f the muscle complex during the eccentric deceleration phase. Although most books pay detailed \nattention to the myotatic reflex, which comprises a tonic, longer duration reflex and a phasic, rapid reflex, they do \nnot discuss their relationship to the different types o f plyometric drill. The relevance of positive supportive \nreaction, the crossed extensor reflex and other important reflexes is also not covered. The role of correctly phased \nbreathing, which is vital to maximal plyomctrics and efficient stabilisation of the body, is not mentioned in any \nbook.\nDistinction is seldom made between maximal and submaximal plyometrics (and how they are produced), nor \nis the concurrent and sequential prescription of plyometric and other forms of training discussed in any depth. \nFor example, several books advocate sessions with over 100 repetitions of plyomctric movements, sometimes \ncombined with resistance training. This volume of maximal plyometrics (unlike sub-maximal plyometrics) is \nneither productive nor safe for any athlete. Maximal plyometrics, like maximal weight lifting, involves single all- \nout repetitions separated by a few minutes of rest. It is extremely demanding on the central nervous system and \nimposes great mechanical strain on the tendons, in particular.\nTo ascertain whether an athlete needs plyometric or heavy resistance training depends on measuring the \nstrength deficit (see Ch 1), prescription of plyometric training remains haphazard, yet plyometric books rarely \nmention this concept. The opposition to plyometric training (powermetrics) by some experts is often the result \nof this type of guesswork training, which can hinder performance and increase the risk of injury.\nSome authors rightly criticize the recommendation sometimes put forward that athletes should be able to \nsquat 1.5-2 times bodymass in order to use certain plyomctric drills, sincc they consider that this criterion is not \nbased on research evidence and does not apply to all plyometric exercises. This recommendation is a safety \nmeasure based on force plate analysis of maximal plyometrics in the form of depth jum ps, where the ground \nreaction force on the lower extremity can easily exceed 6 times bodyweight. This guideline was never intended to\n282\n\n\n######## PDF 294 / printed 283\nSUPERTRAINING\napply religiously to submaximal plyometrics using small drops, skipping drills or less demanding actions. This \nmisunderstanding of Russian guidelines is largely a result of an incomplete grasp of the scientific principles on \nwhich the original shock method was based.\nCoaches would appreciate more what plyometrics really is if they were introduced to some of the facilitation \ntechniques used in physiotherapeutic PNF (see 7.2). The physical therapist sometimes imposes a short, sharp \njerk on an eccentrically or isometrically contracting muscle to produce strong muscle contraction in a weakened \nmusclc. Relatively prolonged movements such as many of the drills recommended in most books are used for \nother purposes, including the reduction of spasm or increasing functional range of movement. These principles \nhave preceded sporting plyometrics by several decades, so it is surprising that so much confusion still abounds \nover plyomctrics. Certainly, the so-called plyometric drills in current Western texts are known to enhance \nperformance, but it is not predominantly due to plyometric training, but more to the increased loading o f eccentric \ncontraction, the conditioning o f connective tissue, the improvement o f voluntary starting strength from the quasi- \nisometric state, the use ofprestretch, and enhancement o f acceleration strength by increasing the velocity of \nprojectiles such as medicine balls.\nPlyometrics and the Brain\nRccent brain research reveals that piyomctric action is not simply a phenomenon that can be adequately explained \non the basis of stored elastic energy and the stretch shortening cycle. The phenomenon of plyometrics involves fa r \nmore intricate processes than reflexive muscle activities that operate at the level of the spinal column and bypass the \nbrain. Many of the subtleties and complexities o f plyometrics can be missed if one is preoccupied with the \nprescription of so-called piyomctric drills or with laboratory measurement of the stretch-shortening cyclc as a local \nmusculotendinous phenomenon. In both cases, it is easy to overlook the possibility that activities such as depth- \njumping or medicinc ball throwing may involve non-local events, particularly the enormously complicated computer \nprograms in the brain.\nWhile it is accurate to state that plyometric actions arc extremely rapid and therefore occur at the level of \nspecific spinal nerves without involving conscious thought processes, it must be remembered that the preparatory \nperiod prior to the piyomctric action can involve higher order mental processing of exceptional intricacy (sec Fig \n3.11). In fact, the period prior to execution of any skilled movement involves running mental programmes that have \nbeen stored and refined by repeated rehearsal in the brain of the well-trained athlete.\nOne can distinguish further between a plyometric action produced under conditions in which the athlete \nanticipates a particular course of action (cognitive plyometrics) and a plyometric action produced as a purely \nreflexive response under unexpected circumstances (non-cognitive plyometrics). The latter type of plyometrics \noccur primarily under survival or self-preservation conditions intended to protect a person from injury or death. \nThe rapidity of the event does not permit any forethought or planning, but relies on primitive reflexes that generally \nlend to bypass the brain by acting at the more spontaneous level of the spinal cord. Cognitive plyometrics, though \nalso a very rapid phenomenon, is preceded by a phase of thinking that prepares the person for a specific course of \naction to achieve a definite goal.\nOne should not conclude simplistically that non-cognitivc plyometrics are purely instinctive or automatic \nactions which involve no cognitive or learning processes. It should never be forgotten that efficient execution of \npiyomctric actions and drills only takes place after a considerable amount o f practice using cognitive processes. In \nfact, most athletes do not manage to perfect plyometric skills even after many weeks of practice. The use of \nnumerous sequential repetitions to imprint these skills may constitute the inappropriate strategy because classical \nplyometrics are 1 RM movements which require as much as several minutes of rest before recovery is complete. \nSubmaximal plyometrics and pseudo-plyometrics may be performed in a state of advancing fatigue, unlike classical \nshock method' plyometrics. The neural and reflexive processes are quite different in each case.\nEven then, what appears to be quite reflexive still involves a certain degree of cognitive preparation and control. \nThe lack of adequate mention o f the importance of cognitive and learning processes in plyometrics by all popular \ntexts on the subject constitutes a major deficit in the field. Reflexes are casually referred to as entirely robotic \nactions that elicit or inhibit musclc contraction, but little is mentioned of the fact that reflexes can be trained and \nmodified, something that the renowned Russian physiologist, Pavlov, had proved some 70 years ago in the form of \nhis'conditioned reflexes'. Later, Skinner (1938) distinguished between two types of conditioning: which he called \nrespondent conditioning, in which the response is modified by a preceding stimulus, and operant conditioning, in \nwhich the response is strengthened or weakened by events that follow the response.\n283\n\n\n######## PDF 295 / printed 284\nSUPERTRAINING\nIn the plyometric situation, this implies that a stimulus such as an emotion (e.g. fear, excitement) or a men \nimage (or visualisation) one entertains before an action like a depth jump can shape the subsequent neuromuscJ \nreflexes. Likewise, any event (such as the jarring of the body, post-exercise fatigue or an injury) following \nplyometric action can modify these reflexes. In fact, it is common for plyometric activity to be associated w' \nboth respondent and operant conditioning.\nIt has also been shown that if the reflex learning proccss is carried out with the subject in a parti \nphysiological or psychological state, then the conditioned reflexes are exhibited only when the same state is pre \nbut not when the state is different. This phenomenon is known as state-dependent learning. Its existence imp!’ \nthat plyometric proficiency or deficiency learned in a specific training situation may not be reproduced \ncompetitive circumstances which differ from those of the learning situation.\nWhat the preceding few paragraphs imply is that it is only partially correct to regard plyometrics as a pr \nin which basic reflexes automatically produce a powerful muscle contraction which is accompanicd by elastic energy \nreleased after the muscle has been stretched eccentrically. It is vital to note that the entire plyometric process \ninvolves complex state-dependent learning events which modify the pattern o f reflex activity according to how the \ngiven plyometric drills are executed. This may be one of the reasons why some researchers have carried out \nexperiments which apparently have shown plyometric training to produce little or no improvement in speed- \nstrength or sporting performance. One cannot ignore the neural factors involved in studying the effects of any \nneuromuscular phenomena: study of muscular output alone is incomplete and lack of control over essential neural \nfactors affecting the experiment can easily distort the results.\nIt should be more obvious now that plyometric training is not simply a matter o f dropping from a height or \nthrowing an object for a given number of repetitions to improve fitness qualities such as strength-speed or explosive \nstrength. \nSport usually involves the reproduction of a wide variety of technical skills under expected and \nunexpected conditions, so that feedforward training and other 'visualisation' (mental and kinaesthetic imaging) \ntechniques must be used to enable the athlete not only to increase explosive strength and strength-specd, but also to \nefficiently manage any plyometric actions under real sporting conditions.\nLest the connection between visualization and movement be oversimplified by sports psychologists, \nneuroscientist. Dr Kensall Wise of the University of Michigan, points out that the part o f the brain which can \nvisualise a given action is totally separate from those parts which initiate the muscle contractions to carry out the \naction (Scientific American, Oct 1988: 23). Although fairly modem research has identified some of the connections \nbetween some regions of the brain and functions such as sight, hearing and motor output, researchers still have only \na vague idea of how the nervous system initiates and manages any given actions.\nThe importance of ballistic activity to humankind recently has been shown to extend far beyond the realms of \nsport. Neurophysiologist, William Calvin, has proposed the fascinating hypothesis that the brain's planning of \nballistic movements may have played a major role in the development of language, music and intelligence over the \nages (Scientific American, Oct 1994). He makes this proposal, since ballistic movements and language processes \ninvolve some of the same regions of the brain, in particular those associated with sequencing and planning. In \nreaching this conclusion, he emphasizes that ballistic movements, unlike cocontractive slower movements, require a \ngreat amount of planning and problem solving. Slow movements may be corrected readily by ongoing feedback \ninformation, but ballistic movements require the brain to determine every detail of the action in advance by mentally \nplanning the exact sequence of neural activation for numerous individual muscles.\nApparently, parts of the language cortex of the brain serve a far more generalised function than previously \nsuspected. It is implicated in the production of novel sequences of sensations and movements for both the hands \nand mouth, so that ballistic arm actions may play a role in mental development. Calvin adds that improvements \nin language skills might improve dexterity and vice versa. The emphasis placed by Russian coachcs on athletes \nbeing able to accurately describe, draw and visualise sporting movements would appear to correlate with this \nproposal. Instead of simply executing entertaining plyometric drills like biological robots, athletes would be well \nadvised to integrate cognitive processes more actively into the training programme.\nVarious Shock M ethods\nPlyometrics should not be thought of as the only type of'shock training', even though the term 'shock method' \nwas the preferred name given to the plyometric method by Verkhoshansky when he first formalised this category \nof impulsive training. Training plyometrics may be regarded as one of several shock methods that may be used in \nspecial strength training to provide intense stimulation to compel the body to attain a higher level of functional\n284\n\n\n######## PDF 296 / printed 285\nSUPERTRAINING\nadaptation. The principle of gradual progressive overloading may, after a prolonged period of unvaried use, \nsometimes fail to improve strength or performance, as is commonly the case among elite athletes. \nIn this \nsituation, it may be necessary to disrupt the persistent state of stagnation by use of one of the following shock \nmethods (see 7.1):\n• plyometrics \n• forced repetitions\n• supramaximal methods \n• electrical stimulation (macrostimulation)\n• repeated single I RM training \n• restricted range maxima\n• contrast methods \n• maximal eccentrics\nThe appropriate method, its timing and the duration of its use must be chosen with great care, since lack of \nprogress may be due to overtraining rather than long-term stagnation. The use of any shock method under such \ncircumstances is potentially harmful and definitely detrimental to performance. These methods arc generally \nunsuitable for novices and should only be employed by experienced athletes with a solid background of special \nstrength training.\n5.2.5 The D evelopm ent of Strength-Endurance\nStrength-endurance involves the production of muscular tension without a decrease in efficiency over a long \nperiod. Strength-endurance, like explosive strength, has several forms, depending upon the motor pattern of the \nathletic activity, in particular the categories of dynamic and static endurance.\nDynamic strength-endurance is typical of exercises involving repetitive and significant muscular tension at a \nrelatively slow speed of movement, as well as repeated cyclic or acyclic exercises requiring speed-strength. In the \nlatter case we are referring to the specific endurance to execute special speed-strength and explosive work without \nadecrease in efficiency over a relatively long period. Static strength-endurance is typical of activities associated \nwith the maintenance of maximal and near-maximal tension, as well as the moderate tension necessary for holding \na specific posture (for example, in shooting or speed-skating).\nThe development of strength-endurance is an inherent characteristic and a fundamental principle of training \nfor dev eloping general endurance. Training for strength-endurance is determined predominantly by:\n1. The magnitude of the load\n2. The tempo of movement\n3. The duration of each movement and each phase of the movements\n4. The rest interval between training sessions\n5. The length of the training period\n6. The initial levels of strength-cndurance of the athlete\n7. The kinesiological structure of the movement\n8. The relative involvement of static and dynamic strength-endurance.\nRepetition work with weights 25-50% of IRM at a moderate tempo (from 60-120 repetitions/minute) is used for \ndeveloping strength-endurance. The effectiveness of strength-endurance development, using equivalent weights \nand movement tempos, will be higher if the work is executed to complete fatigue, although work of shorter \nduration (60% of the maximal work capacity time) also will yield satisfactory results (Agolinsky, 1953; \nKorobkov, 1953; Monogarov, 1958; Diachkov, 1961).\nStrength-endurance, like the other characteristics of muscular activity, is specific. However, the specificity \nof strength-endurance is expressed to a lesser degree than the specificity of speed, for example, while its carry­\nover from one activity to another is greater. When the activity involves repetitively overcoming significant \nresistance (exceeding 75-80% of IRM), one cannot enhance endurance, since one becomes constrained to \ndeveloping strength predominantly (Zatsiorsky, 1966).\nThis study by Zatsiorsky showed that isometric endurance increased 84% as a result of exercising 10 times a \nday for 5 weeks with the elbow flexors holding a 60% maximal contraction for as long as possible. Dynamic \nauiurance increased by 93% as a result of performing these arm curls with a weight of 60% of 1 RM at a rate of \n28repetitions per minute until it was impossible to increase the tempo further. At the same time, the ability to \nexecute repetitive isometric contractions (60% of maximal strength for 5 seconds of tension and 2 seconds of rest) \nto failure increased by 219%. A similar experiment with 10 dynamic movements executed daily with maximal \nresistance for 5 weeks resulted in increases in both dynamic and static strength, but dynamic and static endurance\n285\n\n\n######## PDF 297 / printed 286\nSUPERTRAINING\nremained almost unchanged (Hansen, 1963). This indicates that it is necessary to optimise the volume of work \nrequired to develop strength-endurance.\nIt has been established that an increase in strength-endurance contributes to improving endurance in distance \nrunners, skiers and skaters (Popov, 1968; Nabatnikova, 1972; Mikhailov & Panov, 1975). However, it must be \nemphasized that the magnitude of the strength load used is different if, on the one hand, the key quality required \nis general endurance and, on the other hand, it is strength-endurance.\nIt should be noted that certain popular strength exercises such as running with a sack of sand weighing 25% of \nthe subject's bodyweight, barbell pressing, squats, 'good mornings', and twisting with a barbell not only do not \nenhance, but to a certain degree, will even hinder the development of endurance in cyclic type exercises \n(Maisuradze, 1960). It some instances the increase in strength is associated with excessive muscle hypertrophy, \nwhich is a by-product of strength training. Endurance and hypertrophy do not increase simultaneously and there \nis no correlation between them (Maison & Broekcr, 1941; McMorris & Elkins, 1954).\nThe lack of a definite correlation between special strength and muscle mass in different sports where the key \nquality is endurance has been observed by several authors (Chistyakov, 1965; Chcmyaev, 1965; Nabatnikova, \n1972). This conccms primarily cyclic sports (middle distance running, skating and skiing). In other cases, for \nexample, with gymnasts, a positive linear correlation (R = 0.77) is noticed between strength-endurance and \nrelative strength (Zhalei, 1964). Therefore, in those cases where endurance is associated with the display of \nsignificant strength, some muscular hypertrophy does not negatively influence training results.\nThe general principles concerning the development of strength-endurance, which were discusscd earlier, arc \napplied differently in each specific case, depending on the characteristics of the sport, for example:\n• During the preparatory period, skiers should exercise with weights which are up to 65% of 1RM, in \ncombination with simulation exercises on a flat surface, as well as with movements on roller-skis \n(Chistyakov, 1965).\n• Middle and long distance runners obtain good results from lifting a barbell of 55-60% of bodymass up to head \nlevel 8-10 times rhythmically, clean-and-jerks with various weights (60-85% of bodymass); jumps with \nkettlebells from a squat position (using about one-third of bodymass for 18-20 repetitions) and squats with \n55-60% of bodymass until complete fatigue (Likachycvskaya & Kovalchuk, 1963). Exercises with weights \nof 60-80% of 1RM for multiple repetitions, jumping and hard running are also recommended (Popov, 1966).\n• Exercises with heavy weights (80-85% of 1RM), as well as medium and small weights are recommended for \ndeveloping strength-endurance in skaters. Exercises with heavy loads are executed for several sets at a \nmoderate tempo for 4-12 repetitions per set until the point of complete fatigue, with 2-4 minutes of rest \nbetween sets. Exercises with light and moderate weights are executed as rapidly as possible for 15-25 \nrepetitions per set, for 3-5 sets with rest intervals of 5-8 minutes (Mikhailov & Panov, 1975).\n• Male rowers obtain good results with weights of 50-80% of 1 RM and female rowers with loads of 30-40% of \n1RM (Ulrich ct al, 1966). Significant improvement (up to 40% from initial levels) in strength-endurancehzs \nbeen noted in women rowers working with light weights (18-20kg) for many repetitions (Chumakova, 1964). \nImprovement is greater with these weights than with larger loads (35-50 kg) executed in sets of 2-3 \nrepetitions, and it is even greater (by 28%) than that produced by a combination of both methods.\nIt is necessary to increase the amount of weight and the number of repetitions as strength-endurance increases. \nThe following method is recommended for skaters to develop strength-endurance in the legs: increasing the \nnumber of barbell squats by 20-30 each week (beginning with 50 squats per set and over a period of 2-3 months \nincreasing this to 200-300 repetitions), then increasing the amount of weight and starting a new cycle of strength \ntraining, but beginning with a smaller number of squats (Mikhailov & Panov, 1975).\nFor developing strength-endurance it is always appropriate to strive to execute the work under demanding \nconditions, though with movements whose coordination and structure in space and time are close to those of the \nspecial exercise. For instance, a runner may run with a small parachute resisting him or pull a loaded sled in the \npreparatory phases (Popov, 1966). Similarly, gymnasts may use a heavy belt and vest (Zhilei, 1964) and rowers \na spccial water-resistancc dcvice attached to the hull of the boat (Chuprun, 1966). In the case of the rowers. \nstrength-endurance as measured by the number of pull-ups, the number of arm flexions in the prone position and \nthe number of barbell presses in 30 seconds increased by 57.1%, 27.8% and 9.1%, respectively.\n286\n\n\n######## PDF 298 / printed 287\nSUPERTRAINING\nThus, multiple repetition exercises with various weights should be considered the fundamental method for \ndeveloping strength-endurance, with the load being determined by the dynamics of the given exercise. In cases \nrequiring intense effort, an optimally heavy weight should be used together with light weights or with exercises \nsimulating the given sport activity. If the special exercise is associated with the prolonged production of moderate \nforce, then it is useful to work with light weights in repeated sets to the point of fatigue or failure.\nStrength training should not result in increased muscle mass in those types of sport where the key quality is \nendurance of moderate work output. If strength-endurance is required, especially in those cases where it is \nnecessary to overcome a large resistance, a small increase in muscle mass is permissible. In no cases, should the \ntraining ever produce a decrease in relative strength of any muscle groups in the body.\n5.3 Application of Special S trength Training Means\nThe value of systematic application of the training means has been investigated repeatedly, but the preliminary \nconclusions still need to be extended. Consequently, there have been attempts to devise a rational combination of \nstrength, speed and endurance exercises to:\n• raise the body's general work capacity (Zimkin, Yakovlev, Korobkov, Zatsiorsky )\n• investigate the effectiveness of sequential and concurrent (parallel or complcx) use of the means for \ndeveloping endurance and speed-strength (Ozolin, Diachkov, Mikhailov, Volkov ct al)\n• study the effect of the sequential use of aerobic and anaerobic exercise on the development of endurance \n(Volkov ct al, Gordon et al)\n• investigate the sequential use of strength and speed-strength exercises as one of the schcmes for \nsystematically developing explosive strength (Ozolin, Diachkov, Vorobyev, Filin, Vcrkhoshansky).\nHowever, research in the area of the systematic use of means is limited and no definitive practical \nrecommendations have yet emerged.\nThe systematic use of means of special strength training over a given period produces a cumulative effect \nwhich is significantly greater than the separate non-sequential application of these same means. In principle, there \nat two possible variants for the systematic application of means: sequential and concurrent, as mentioned above:\n1. The first variant (sequential means) involves the strictly determined, successive introduction of different \nmeans, each with a higher training effect, into the annual or multi-annual cyclc. The theoretical basis for this \nvariant is based upon two observations:\n• the training effect of any means diminishes as the body adapts to its influence\n• the training effect must be maintained by using a complcx of means to steadily increase the body's special \nwork capacity.\nIn addition, experience and research indicate that the training regime designed to develop a particular motor ability \nis determined largely by the degree of continuity in sequentially changing means with different training effects. \nBecause of this, functional changes produced by earlier means should create favourable conditions for achieving \nthe training effect of subsequent means. To devise a systematic sequence of the means of special strength \ntraining, one must:\n• assess the quantitative and qualitative components of the training effect\n• categorise the means relative to the level of the athlete's special fitness.\nThis, in effect, is the subject of research aimed at identifying the principles for devising logical systems for \napplying the different means of special strength training effectively. As an example, the sequential development of \nendurance begins by training aerobic (cardiorespiratory) processes, then non-oxidative glycolysis and finally high \nenergy phosphagen processes. This sequential introduction of'anaerobic' work creates favourable conditions for \nthe training effect of'aerobic'work (Ozolin, 1959, 1970; Volkov, 1963; Yakovlev, 1957; Borisov, 1979).\n2. The second variant (concurrent means) involves the parallel use of means (over an extended period) that are \nclose to the body’s work regime, but which produce a largely different training effect. This method is based on \nexperimental evidence that adaptation elicited by the separate components of the complex of training work is not \namply a summation of the separate effects, but the synergistic result of the interaction of the effects at each stage \nof training. This interaction produces a new physical state with a significantly greater work potential than would\n287\n","numLines":388,"startLine":819,"totalLines":1206}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"d72b81b2-50fc-4524-87a0-f4fa878fabcc","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
