{"parentUuid":"32ca6e2c-ccc5-43e7-a2f2-1d3c1abcd536","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffZfVFBKmmk1NvQ37RJE","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01HSmqXS3J5QgvmADkypn2JK","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning; sed -n 1,40p supertraining--verkhoshansky/principle--short-amortization-preserves-elastic-energy.md; echo; sed -n 12,30p supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--strength-deficit-sets-training-method-choice.md; echo; sed -n 12,25p science-and-practice-of-strength-training--zatsiorsky/claim--reversible-muscular-action-is-a-trainable-skill-not-automatic.md; echo; sed -n 12,24p supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--resistance-training-prescription-by-goal.md; ls /Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Knowledge/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/ | wc -l","description":"Read overlapping entries briefly"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4240,"cache_read_input_tokens":189302,"output_tokens":372,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4240},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":372,"cache_read_input_tokens":189302,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4240,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4240,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01HSmqXS3J5QgvmADkypn2JK":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning; sed -n 1,40p supertraining--verkhoshansky/principle--short-amortization-preserves-elastic-energy.md; echo; sed -n 12,30p supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--strength-deficit-sets-training-method-choice.md; echo; sed -n 12,25p science-and-practice-of-strength-training--zatsiorsky/claim--reversible-muscular-action-is-a-trainable-skill-not-automatic.md; echo; sed -n 12,24p supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--resistance-training-prescription-by-goal.md; ls /Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Knowledge/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/ | wc -l","description":"Read overlapping entries briefly"}},"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CffZfLU5b2Uo9o93XukN7","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"ae79a57b-a6df-45cf-b347-ffe7b4d891c2","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:46:23.840Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"ae79a57b-a6df-45cf-b347-ffe7b4d891c2","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"624a8cec-33fa-4c0a-a445-c72ce718e6ab","agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HSmqXS3J5QgvmADkypn2JK","type":"tool_result","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: principle\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"1.7\"\ntopic: amortization phase and elastic energy\ncategory: [plyometrics, stretch-shortening-cycle, reactive-strength]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-09-26\n---\n\n# A Short Transition (Amortization) Between Stretch and Shortening Preserves Stored Elastic Energy\n\n**What it is** — \"The lifetime of the cross-bridges between actin and myosin strands is\nlimited, varying from 15 to 120 milliseconds\" (printed 47, no citation given in the text\nfor this figure). For stored elastic energy from a stretch to be used in the following\nconcentric contraction, the transition period between the end of the eccentric (stretch)\nphase and the start of the concentric (shortening) phase must be brief; a longer delay lets\ncross-bridges detach (\"a more prolonged delay will allow fewer cross-bridges to remain\nattached after the stretch,\" Edman et al., 1978, printed 45) and the elastic potential\ndissipates as slippage rather than being used for work. A greater velocity of stretch\nduring the eccentric phase also increases elastic energy storage (Rack & Westbury, 1974,\nprinted 45). Much of the extra positive work seen in rapid eccentric-concentric\n(plyometric) actions is attributed to this stored elastic energy plus a separate\npre-loading/pre-stretch effect (tension at the start of the concentric phase is already\nelevated by the preceding stretch). Tendon-based elastic energy storage during locomotion\nlargely happens via near-isometric muscle contraction with minimal muscle fibre length\nchange, which is also more metabolically efficient than dynamic contraction (printed 45).\n(PDF pp. 56-58, printed pp. 45-47.)\n\n**Why it works** — The cross-bridge attachment window is a hard physiological time\nconstraint; the mechanics of elastic energy return depend on catching that window, which\nis why a fast stretch-to-shorten transition (a short \"amortization phase,\" in later\nplyometrics terminology) is functionally different from a slow, paused transition even if\nthe stretch magnitude is identical.\n\n**When to apply** — **Inference:** this is the underlying mechanism behind coaching cues\nfor minimizing ground-contact/amortization time in plyometric and reactive-strength work\n\n---\n\n# Strength Deficit Size Points to a Different Training Method\n\n**What it is** — If an athlete's strength deficit (see\n[[framework--strength-deficit-jump-comparison-estimate]]) is large for a given muscle\ngroup, the \"In general\" rule on printed 10 recommends near-maximal neuromuscular\nstimulation (weightlifting or plyometric methods). If the deficit is small, it recommends\nsubmaximal hypertrophy work (bodybuilding-style loading) followed by maximal efforts\nagainst heavy loads. Note: the jump paragraph on the same page gives the opposite pairing\nfor the jump-test version of the deficit (large jump-test difference means 5-8RM\nstrength/hypertrophy work with CAT; small difference means shock/ballistic methods); the\nsource is internally inconsistent on this point (see\n[[framework--strength-deficit-jump-comparison-estimate]] for both statements with pages).\nThe book expands CAT two ways: \"Compensatory Activation Training\" with 5-8RM loads\n(printed 10) and, in the separate CAT technique discussion, \"Compensatory Acceleration\nTraining\" for voluntarily managing bar speed, where \"Compensatory Action Training\" is also\nproposed as a broader name (printed 21). The book does not say whether these are the same\nmethod. Verkhoshansky's research, as reported by the authors, found the strength deficit\n\n---\n\n# Reversible Muscular Action (SSC Skill) Must Be Specifically Trained, Not Assumed From Strength\n\n**What it is** — Reversible muscular action (using the stretch-shortening cycle\neffectively) is innate to some movements (e.g. running takeoffs, \"a spring in the leg\")\nbut must be learned for others, such as throwing. Because many sport movements are complex\nand executed briefly, \"even some elite athletes fail to perform this reversible muscular\naction correctly\" (printed p. 35). The authors state that before 1960, SSC training was\naccidental: improvement was a byproduct of other exercises; only since drop jumps and\nsimilar exercises were incorporated into training has SSC capacity been trained\ndeliberately (they note the training method \"has been erroneously called plyometrics by\nsome,\" since the reversible, not strictly eccentric, muscular action is the actual training\nobjective). The direct claim: \"maximal muscular strength (Fmm) and forces produced in fast\n\n---\n\n# Table 1.1: Resistance Training Prescription by Goal (Strength, Power, Hypertrophy, Endurance)\n\n**What it is** — A dynamic (non-isometric) resistance-training prescription chart by\ngoal (PDF p. 22, printed p. 11):\n\n| Variable | Strength | Power | Hypertrophy | Endurance |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Load (% 1RM) | 80-100 | 70-100 | 60-80 | 40-60 |\n| Reps/set | 1-5 | 1-5 | 8-15 | 25-60 |\n| Sets/exercise | 4-7 | 3-5 | 4-8 | 2-4 |\n| Rest between sets | 2-6 min | 2-6 min | 2-5 min | 1-2 min |\n     178","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"f85e2937-6b09-4b11-b357-5469fe5ae09b","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:46:24.060Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: principle\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"1.7\"\ntopic: amortization phase and elastic energy\ncategory: [plyometrics, stretch-shortening-cycle, reactive-strength]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-09-26\n---\n\n# A Short Transition (Amortization) Between Stretch and Shortening Preserves Stored Elastic Energy\n\n**What it is** — \"The lifetime of the cross-bridges between actin and myosin strands is\nlimited, varying from 15 to 120 milliseconds\" (printed 47, no citation given in the text\nfor this figure). For stored elastic energy from a stretch to be used in the following\nconcentric contraction, the transition period between the end of the eccentric (stretch)\nphase and the start of the concentric (shortening) phase must be brief; a longer delay lets\ncross-bridges detach (\"a more prolonged delay will allow fewer cross-bridges to remain\nattached after the stretch,\" Edman et al., 1978, printed 45) and the elastic potential\ndissipates as slippage rather than being used for work. A greater velocity of stretch\nduring the eccentric phase also increases elastic energy storage (Rack & Westbury, 1974,\nprinted 45). Much of the extra positive work seen in rapid eccentric-concentric\n(plyometric) actions is attributed to this stored elastic energy plus a separate\npre-loading/pre-stretch effect (tension at the start of the concentric phase is already\nelevated by the preceding stretch). Tendon-based elastic energy storage during locomotion\nlargely happens via near-isometric muscle contraction with minimal muscle fibre length\nchange, which is also more metabolically efficient than dynamic contraction (printed 45).\n(PDF pp. 56-58, printed pp. 45-47.)\n\n**Why it works** — The cross-bridge attachment window is a hard physiological time\nconstraint; the mechanics of elastic energy return depend on catching that window, which\nis why a fast stretch-to-shorten transition (a short \"amortization phase,\" in later\nplyometrics terminology) is functionally different from a slow, paused transition even if\nthe stretch magnitude is identical.\n\n**When to apply** — **Inference:** this is the underlying mechanism behind coaching cues\nfor minimizing ground-contact/amortization time in plyometric and reactive-strength work\n\n---\n\n# Strength Deficit Size Points to a Different Training Method\n\n**What it is** — If an athlete's strength deficit (see\n[[framework--strength-deficit-jump-comparison-estimate]]) is large for a given muscle\ngroup, the \"In general\" rule on printed 10 recommends near-maximal neuromuscular\nstimulation (weightlifting or plyometric methods). If the deficit is small, it recommends\nsubmaximal hypertrophy work (bodybuilding-style loading) followed by maximal efforts\nagainst heavy loads. Note: the jump paragraph on the same page gives the opposite pairing\nfor the jump-test version of the deficit (large jump-test difference means 5-8RM\nstrength/hypertrophy work with CAT; small difference means shock/ballistic methods); the\nsource is internally inconsistent on this point (see\n[[framework--strength-deficit-jump-comparison-estimate]] for both statements with pages).\nThe book expands CAT two ways: \"Compensatory Activation Training\" with 5-8RM loads\n(printed 10) and, in the separate CAT technique discussion, \"Compensatory Acceleration\nTraining\" for voluntarily managing bar speed, where \"Compensatory Action Training\" is also\nproposed as a broader name (printed 21). The book does not say whether these are the same\nmethod. Verkhoshansky's research, as reported by the authors, found the strength deficit\n\n---\n\n# Reversible Muscular Action (SSC Skill) Must Be Specifically Trained, Not Assumed From Strength\n\n**What it is** — Reversible muscular action (using the stretch-shortening cycle\neffectively) is innate to some movements (e.g. running takeoffs, \"a spring in the leg\")\nbut must be learned for others, such as throwing. Because many sport movements are complex\nand executed briefly, \"even some elite athletes fail to perform this reversible muscular\naction correctly\" (printed p. 35). The authors state that before 1960, SSC training was\naccidental: improvement was a byproduct of other exercises; only since drop jumps and\nsimilar exercises were incorporated into training has SSC capacity been trained\ndeliberately (they note the training method \"has been erroneously called plyometrics by\nsome,\" since the reversible, not strictly eccentric, muscular action is the actual training\nobjective). The direct claim: \"maximal muscular strength (Fmm) and forces produced in fast\n\n---\n\n# Table 1.1: Resistance Training Prescription by Goal (Strength, Power, Hypertrophy, Endurance)\n\n**What it is** — A dynamic (non-isometric) resistance-training prescription chart by\ngoal (PDF p. 22, printed p. 11):\n\n| Variable | Strength | Power | Hypertrophy | Endurance |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Load (% 1RM) | 80-100 | 70-100 | 60-80 | 40-60 |\n| Reps/set | 1-5 | 1-5 | 8-15 | 25-60 |\n| Sets/exercise | 4-7 | 3-5 | 4-8 | 2-4 |\n| Rest between sets | 2-6 min | 2-6 min | 2-5 min | 1-2 min |\n     178","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"ae79a57b-a6df-45cf-b347-ffe7b4d891c2","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
