{"parentUuid":"e0c5cc8e-0447-49cd-9e01-97af9ea918e9","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"624a8cec-33fa-4c0a-a445-c72ce718e6ab","agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019WacA6nGhPVRzMoExoGTpv","type":"tool_result","content":"# The Same Force (F = ma) Produced Different Ways Trains Differently\n\n**What it is** — Newton's second law (F = ma) means the same force can be produced with a\nlarge mass and small acceleration (powerlifting-style, heavy load), a small mass and large\nacceleration (many track and field actions), a moderate mass and moderate acceleration\n(bodybuilding-style hypertrophy training, roughly 8-12 reps), or near-zero acceleration\nunder isometric/isokinetic conditions. Despite equal force output, these methods are\nargued and shown in practice not to produce the same training effect, because different\nneural, muscular, and metabolic processes are engaged in each case. (PDF pp. 32-33,\n----\n_book.md\nclaim--eccentric-vs-concentric-training-adaptation-contradictory-findings.md\nclaim--exercise-economy-improves-with-skill-and-interval-style-costs-more.md\nframework--work-capacity-fitness-preparedness.md\n----\n   1 4.3.3\n   2 4.3.4\n   3 4.3.5\n   1 4.3.6\n  10 4.4\nbut must be learned for others, such as throwing. Because many sport movements are complex\nand executed briefly, \"even some elite athletes fail to perform this reversible muscular\naction correctly\" (printed p. 35). The authors state that before 1960, SSC training was\n21:accidental: improvement was a byproduct of other exercises; only since drop jumps and\n28:cannot compete with triple jumpers in drop jumping\" because, absent specific training, the\n30:capping how much force they can sustain without a compensating drop in output; specific\n32:a force decrease, which is also why dropping height can then be increased. In beginners,\n34:weightlifting; in qualified athletes, \"this skill is very specific\" and drop-jump\n37:drop-jump height by dropping height: Hakkinen & Komi 1985). (PDF pp. 44,\n42:muscle-tendon unit; specific SSC/drop-jump training desensitizes or inhibits that reflex\n44:drop. This is the authors' own stated mechanism, not this entry's inference.\n46:**When to apply** — As direct justification for programming drop jumps, depth jumps, or\n63:training does not improve drop-jump performance in qualified athletes\" as settled beyond","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"87d0902f-ca29-4abc-b07a-a697ee0ea3fc","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:47:26.016Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"# The Same Force (F = ma) Produced Different Ways Trains Differently\n\n**What it is** — Newton's second law (F = ma) means the same force can be produced with a\nlarge mass and small acceleration (powerlifting-style, heavy load), a small mass and large\nacceleration (many track and field actions), a moderate mass and moderate acceleration\n(bodybuilding-style hypertrophy training, roughly 8-12 reps), or near-zero acceleration\nunder isometric/isokinetic conditions. Despite equal force output, these methods are\nargued and shown in practice not to produce the same training effect, because different\nneural, muscular, and metabolic processes are engaged in each case. (PDF pp. 32-33,\n----\n_book.md\nclaim--eccentric-vs-concentric-training-adaptation-contradictory-findings.md\nclaim--exercise-economy-improves-with-skill-and-interval-style-costs-more.md\nframework--work-capacity-fitness-preparedness.md\n----\n   1 4.3.3\n   2 4.3.4\n   3 4.3.5\n   1 4.3.6\n  10 4.4\nbut must be learned for others, such as throwing. Because many sport movements are complex\nand executed briefly, \"even some elite athletes fail to perform this reversible muscular\naction correctly\" (printed p. 35). The authors state that before 1960, SSC training was\n21:accidental: improvement was a byproduct of other exercises; only since drop jumps and\n28:cannot compete with triple jumpers in drop jumping\" because, absent specific training, the\n30:capping how much force they can sustain without a compensating drop in output; specific\n32:a force decrease, which is also why dropping height can then be increased. In beginners,\n34:weightlifting; in qualified athletes, \"this skill is very specific\" and drop-jump\n37:drop-jump height by dropping height: Hakkinen & Komi 1985). (PDF pp. 44,\n42:muscle-tendon unit; specific SSC/drop-jump training desensitizes or inhibits that reflex\n44:drop. This is the authors' own stated mechanism, not this entry's inference.\n46:**When to apply** — As direct justification for programming drop jumps, depth jumps, or\n63:training does not improve drop-jump performance in qualified athletes\" as settled beyond","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"bashEditDiff":{"files":[],"moreFiles":0,"shared":true}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"e0c5cc8e-0447-49cd-9e01-97af9ea918e9","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
