{"parentUuid":"2f653dad-f186-44e5-b2db-6ec37da70e4f","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffZkWid96M46mbkRAg2g","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_014Evr3mPCaZbedJQJVd5u8a","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cat \"/Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Knowledge/library/strength-and-conditioning/fundamentals-of-sprinting--moyer/case-study--isometric-sprinter-and-hurdler.md\"; ls /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/_tmp/ | head; ls /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/_scope* ","description":"Read style reference and list tmp/scope files"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6944,"cache_read_input_tokens":195815,"output_tokens":298,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":80},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6944},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":298,"cache_read_input_tokens":195815,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6944,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6944,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_014Evr3mPCaZbedJQJVd5u8a":{"command":"cat \"/Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Knowledge/library/strength-and-conditioning/fundamentals-of-sprinting--moyer/case-study--isometric-sprinter-and-hurdler.md\"; ls /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/_tmp/ | head; ls /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/_scope* ","description":"Read style reference and list tmp/scope files"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffZkLyUjiTuo1AvVDzo3","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"00afabbf-674b-4fbe-8a3f-aa93aa573b63","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:47:31.722Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"00afabbf-674b-4fbe-8a3f-aa93aa573b63","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"624a8cec-33fa-4c0a-a445-c72ce718e6ab","agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014Evr3mPCaZbedJQJVd5u8a","type":"tool_result","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: case-study\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: fundamentals-of-sprinting--moyer\nchapter: \"16 (guest: Alex Natera)\"\ntopic: RSI athlete cases\ncategory: [isometrics, case-study, reactive-strength]\nconfidence: untested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-09-25\n---\n\n# Case Study: An Elite Sprinter and a Hurdler on Run-Specific Isometrics\n\n**What it is** — Natera presents two athletes (~93:07–101:49).\n\n**Elite male sprinter.** He was a strong, \"power-hungry\" athlete: a good accelerator but poorly elastic, with a weak max velocity.\n- **Baseline:** back squat about **2.65× BW**; isometric mid-thigh pull **5.78× BW**. It is unclear whether that was single- or double-leg; the pull came before Natera moved to iso squats. CMJ about **59–62 cm**.\n- **GPP:** eccentric calf work, then hip ISO holds progressing to switches.\n- **SPP:** ankle and hip ISO catches, plus \"ISO stadium descents,\" a loaded, cyclical isometric step-down that comes after the continuum.\n- **Loads cited:** ankle ISO catch \"up to 160 kg\" (the transcript says \"leaving up to 160 kilo\"), which is implausible against his own 60 kg example and needs verifying on video. Hip ISO catch about 35 kg.\n- **Reported changes:** CMJ +3 cm; CMJ power (~6,000 W) +200–300 W; takeoff velocity up; **RSI** \"improved significantly,\" which he calls the main win; shorter contact times at about 30–40 m; longer acceleration and higher max velocity (around 11 m/s).\n\n**Female 100 m hurdler.** She was coached by a Polish coach whose name the ASR renders \"Peter Marazu ski\" (name unverified), with Natera collaborating. Her times stagnated in 2016–2017. Run-specific isometrics (RSI) were added in 2018 through mid-2019 alongside \"track training\" and traditional squats, deadlifts and Olympic lifts. She used **holds and pushes only**, and progressed past targets such as a **2× BW ankle hold**. Afterwards her hurdle times and speed improved to \"elite level.\" Her quote to a reporter: \"I don't lift heavy weights. I just hold them.\"\n\n*(Every number is single-source ASR from slides; the before/after charts are visual only. Verify at ~95:05–100:58.)*\n\n**Why it works** — Natera's interpretation: isometrics raised reactive strength without extra plyometric volume, and position-specific strength carried over into running. Neither interpretation can be separated from other causes in these data.\n\n**When to apply** — Use the sprinter as a **profile template**, not as evidence: high max strength (squat ≥2.5× BW) plus a low RSI and poor max velocity is the athlete Natera would target. In football that is the big, strong accelerator (RB/LB type) whose drop-jump RSI on ForceDecks is low for his strength. Track RSI and single-leg iso push together to see whether one moves without the other.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — **Confidence is untested.** Each case is n = 1 with concurrent training: sprint coaching, traditional lifting, technical work and maturation. There is no control, and the selection is plainly of successes. The hurdler also had a new collaborating coach, \"great coaching and great technical training,\" per Natera himself. Elsewhere (~34:19–35:15) he reports programs where RSI was \"the only change,\" and he concedes that is \"not exactly a controlled scientific experiment.\" Do not cite these cases as evidence that RSI improves sprint speed. The 160 kg ankle catch must not become a target.\n\n**Example** — Screening question for a strong but non-reactive athlete: \"Is this athlete's RSI low relative to his iso push ×BW?\" If yes, he fits the sprinter case, and Natera would add ankle switches and catches after an eccentric calf base.\n\n**Related** — [[principle--isometric-overload-for-upright-running]] — the rationale these cases are offered to illustrate.\nch5b_text.txt\nch5c_text.txt\nlinks_mac.py\nlint_mac.py\nmemsearch-index-2026-10-03.log\npromote_mac.py\nsync_mac.py\n/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/_scope_issurin_ch3.txt\n/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/_scope_issurin_ch4.txt\n/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/_scope_supertraining_ch1.txt\n/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/_scope_supertraining_ch2-3a.txt\n/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/_scope_supertraining_ch3b.txt\n/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/_scope_supertraining_ch4-1.txt\n/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/_scope_supertraining_ch4-2a.txt\n/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/_scope_supertraining_ch4-2b.txt\n/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/_scope_supertraining_ch4-3-4.txt","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"42bdbc92-7f69-4b5f-a280-e795083dc9ea","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:47:31.915Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: case-study\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: fundamentals-of-sprinting--moyer\nchapter: \"16 (guest: Alex Natera)\"\ntopic: RSI athlete cases\ncategory: [isometrics, case-study, reactive-strength]\nconfidence: untested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-09-25\n---\n\n# Case Study: An Elite Sprinter and a Hurdler on Run-Specific Isometrics\n\n**What it is** — Natera presents two athletes (~93:07–101:49).\n\n**Elite male sprinter.** He was a strong, \"power-hungry\" athlete: a good accelerator but poorly elastic, with a weak max velocity.\n- **Baseline:** back squat about **2.65× BW**; isometric mid-thigh pull **5.78× BW**. It is unclear whether that was single- or double-leg; the pull came before Natera moved to iso squats. CMJ about **59–62 cm**.\n- **GPP:** eccentric calf work, then hip ISO holds progressing to switches.\n- **SPP:** ankle and hip ISO catches, plus \"ISO stadium descents,\" a loaded, cyclical isometric step-down that comes after the continuum.\n- **Loads cited:** ankle ISO catch \"up to 160 kg\" (the transcript says \"leaving up to 160 kilo\"), which is implausible against his own 60 kg example and needs verifying on video. Hip ISO catch about 35 kg.\n- **Reported changes:** CMJ +3 cm; CMJ power (~6,000 W) +200–300 W; takeoff velocity up; **RSI** \"improved significantly,\" which he calls the main win; shorter contact times at about 30–40 m; longer acceleration and higher max velocity (around 11 m/s).\n\n**Female 100 m hurdler.** She was coached by a Polish coach whose name the ASR renders \"Peter Marazu ski\" (name unverified), with Natera collaborating. Her times stagnated in 2016–2017. Run-specific isometrics (RSI) were added in 2018 through mid-2019 alongside \"track training\" and traditional squats, deadlifts and Olympic lifts. She used **holds and pushes only**, and progressed past targets such as a **2× BW ankle hold**. Afterwards her hurdle times and speed improved to \"elite level.\" Her quote to a reporter: \"I don't lift heavy weights. I just hold them.\"\n\n*(Every number is single-source ASR from slides; the before/after charts are visual only. Verify at ~95:05–100:58.)*\n\n**Why it works** — Natera's interpretation: isometrics raised reactive strength without extra plyometric volume, and position-specific strength carried over into running. Neither interpretation can be separated from other causes in these data.\n\n**When to apply** — Use the sprinter as a **profile template**, not as evidence: high max strength (squat ≥2.5× BW) plus a low RSI and poor max velocity is the athlete Natera would target. In football that is the big, strong accelerator (RB/LB type) whose drop-jump RSI on ForceDecks is low for his strength. Track RSI and single-leg iso push together to see whether one moves without the other.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — **Confidence is untested.** Each case is n = 1 with concurrent training: sprint coaching, traditional lifting, technical work and maturation. There is no control, and the selection is plainly of successes. The hurdler also had a new collaborating coach, \"great coaching and great technical training,\" per Natera himself. Elsewhere (~34:19–35:15) he reports programs where RSI was \"the only change,\" and he concedes that is \"not exactly a controlled scientific experiment.\" Do not cite these cases as evidence that RSI improves sprint speed. The 160 kg ankle catch must not become a target.\n\n**Example** — Screening question for a strong but non-reactive athlete: \"Is this athlete's RSI low relative to his iso push ×BW?\" If yes, he fits the sprinter case, and Natera would add ankle switches and catches after an eccentric calf base.\n\n**Related** — [[principle--isometric-overload-for-upright-running]] — the rationale these cases are offered to illustrate.\nch5b_text.txt\nch5c_text.txt\nlinks_mac.py\nlint_mac.py\nmemsearch-index-2026-10-03.log\npromote_mac.py\nsync_mac.py\n/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/_scope_issurin_ch3.txt\n/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/_scope_issurin_ch4.txt\n/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/_scope_supertraining_ch1.txt\n/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/_scope_supertraining_ch2-3a.txt\n/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/_scope_supertraining_ch3b.txt\n/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/_scope_supertraining_ch4-1.txt\n/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/_scope_supertraining_ch4-2a.txt\n/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/_scope_supertraining_ch4-2b.txt\n/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/_scope_supertraining_ch4-3-4.txt","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"00afabbf-674b-4fbe-8a3f-aa93aa573b63","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
