{"parentUuid":"11019268-31d0-42d3-9d14-76c5d9d0a95b","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffZw3ZnLq8iVXVMAYiQB","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7548,"cache_read_input_tokens":220656,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":7548,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":25483,"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CffZvq1UmoCuZTDJ9zAtM","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"d6ff8bfa-4bd7-42fc-a44a-1e06854f5293","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:50:18.339Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"d6ff8bfa-4bd7-42fc-a44a-1e06854f5293","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffZw3ZnLq8iVXVMAYiQB","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Pages verified. Writing entries now, starting with the depth-jump cluster."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7548,"cache_read_input_tokens":220656,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":7548,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffZvq1UmoCuZTDJ9zAtM","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"490d5878-03ab-4979-91b5-21266c274d9a","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:50:18.340Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"490d5878-03ab-4979-91b5-21266c274d9a","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffZw3ZnLq8iVXVMAYiQB","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Mqp5ZsRyrZ96JwcZEGiLo2","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--heavy-squats-and-light-fast-lifts-miss-the-eccentric-concentric-switch.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: claim\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.4\"\ntopic: limits of barbell means for explosive strength\ncategory: [explosive-strength, reactive-ability, exercise-selection]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Heavy Squats Build Strength Potential and Light Fast Lifts Build Speed, but Neither Trains the Rapid Eccentric-to-Concentric Switch\n\n**What it is** — The authors open 5.2.4 by analysing the traditional speed-strength means. In heavy barbell squats \"the muscles work very slowly (quasi-isometrically) with a relatively constant tension produced by the load. Consequently, the muscles primarily develop isometric strength\". Raising the barbell weight, \"frequently considered a basic indicator of special strength fitness, places a large and generally unnecessary load on the spine.\" For speed-strength, athletes use smaller loads; their example is \"squat thrusts with a 60kg barbell on the shoulders\", which \"produces a large dynamic maximum force\". So \"exercises with heavy weights tend to increase the strength potential of the muscles, while fast exercises with light weights improve speed.\" Neither adequately enhances \"specific components of explosive movements such as the rapid excitation of muscles and the rapid changing from eccentric to concentric work.\" These skills need \"a specific training regime which is impossible to imitate with only one resistance exercise\": slow resistance movements, and lifting a barbell in preparation for squatting or jumping with it, remove \"the possibility of controlling the mechanisms which are crucial to the rapid switching of muscles to the active state\", while lowering the resistance \"diminishes the dynamic effort required and creates a vicious cycle from which there is no apparent escape.\" This analysis is the authors' stated route to the shock (plyometric) method on the same page. (PDF p. 278 / printed p. 267, verified against the page image.)\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reason is the one quoted: the barbell means do not reproduce rapid excitation or the fast eccentric-concentric switch. They give no further physiology here. Inference: the \"vicious cycle\" is that heavy loads slow the movement and light loads drop the effort, so no single barbell load gives both high effort and a fast switch.\n\n**When to apply** — When a program relies on heavy squats plus light speed squats to develop jumping or cutting explosiveness, this is the authors' argument for adding a means that trains the switch itself (see [[framework--shock-method-and-plyometric-terminology]]). Eric's baseline: \"Training time is deliberately weighted toward high motor-unit-recruitment core/ballistic movements (squats, cleans, jumps, throws, sprints)\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 104). Inference: this entry is the authors' case for why the jumps in that list are not interchangeable with the squats.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The authors do not say heavy squats are useless for explosive athletes: \"this does not mean that it is impossible for them to execute fast dynamic contractions\", and heavy work raises \"strength potential\". Later in the same section they prefer heavy \"brief maximal tension\" work where explosive force must overcome large resistance (see [[heuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size]]). The 60 kg squat-thrust load is given without athlete bodymass or level, so it is not a prescription.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own example: heavy squats (slow, quasi-isometric) versus squat thrusts with a 60 kg barbell (large dynamic maximum force), with neither training the rapid switch (PDF p. 278 / printed p. 267).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--shock-method-and-plyometric-terminology]]; [[principle--equal-force-different-loading-methods]]; [[heuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size]]; [[principle--rate-of-force-development-as-independent-limiter]]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7548,"cache_read_input_tokens":220656,"output_tokens":4335,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":2787},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":7548},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":4335,"cache_read_input_tokens":220656,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7548,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":7548,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01Mqp5ZsRyrZ96JwcZEGiLo2":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--heavy-squats-and-light-fast-lifts-miss-the-eccentric-concentric-switch.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: claim\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.4\"\ntopic: limits of barbell means for explosive strength\ncategory: [explosive-strength, reactive-ability, exercise-selection]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Heavy Squats Build Strength Potential and Light Fast Lifts Build Speed, but Neither Trains the Rapid Eccentric-to-Concentric Switch\n\n**What it is** — The authors open 5.2.4 by analysing the traditional speed-strength means. In heavy barbell squats \"the muscles work very slowly (quasi-isometrically) with a relatively constant tension produced by the load. Consequently, the muscles primarily develop isometric strength\". Raising the barbell weight, \"frequently considered a basic indicator of special strength fitness, places a large and generally unnecessary load on the spine.\" For speed-strength, athletes use smaller loads; their example is \"squat thrusts with a 60kg barbell on the shoulders\", which \"produces a large dynamic maximum force\". So \"exercises with heavy weights tend to increase the strength potential of the muscles, while fast exercises with light weights improve speed.\" Neither adequately enhances \"specific components of explosive movements such as the rapid excitation of muscles and the rapid changing from eccentric to concentric work.\" These skills need \"a specific training regime which is impossible to imitate with only one resistance exercise\": slow resistance movements, and lifting a barbell in preparation for squatting or jumping with it, remove \"the possibility of controlling the mechanisms which are crucial to the rapid switching of muscles to the active state\", while lowering the resistance \"diminishes the dynamic effort required and creates a vicious cycle from which there is no apparent escape.\" This analysis is the authors' stated route to the shock (plyometric) method on the same page. (PDF p. 278 / printed p. 267, verified against the page image.)\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reason is the one quoted: the barbell means do not reproduce rapid excitation or the fast eccentric-concentric switch. They give no further physiology here. Inference: the \"vicious cycle\" is that heavy loads slow the movement and light loads drop the effort, so no single barbell load gives both high effort and a fast switch.\n\n**When to apply** — When a program relies on heavy squats plus light speed squats to develop jumping or cutting explosiveness, this is the authors' argument for adding a means that trains the switch itself (see [[framework--shock-method-and-plyometric-terminology]]). Eric's baseline: \"Training time is deliberately weighted toward high motor-unit-recruitment core/ballistic movements (squats, cleans, jumps, throws, sprints)\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 104). Inference: this entry is the authors' case for why the jumps in that list are not interchangeable with the squats.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The authors do not say heavy squats are useless for explosive athletes: \"this does not mean that it is impossible for them to execute fast dynamic contractions\", and heavy work raises \"strength potential\". Later in the same section they prefer heavy \"brief maximal tension\" work where explosive force must overcome large resistance (see [[heuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size]]). The 60 kg squat-thrust load is given without athlete bodymass or level, so it is not a prescription.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own example: heavy squats (slow, quasi-isometric) versus squat thrusts with a 60 kg barbell (large dynamic maximum force), with neither training the rapid switch (PDF p. 278 / printed p. 267).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--shock-method-and-plyometric-terminology]]; [[principle--equal-force-different-loading-methods]]; [[heuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size]]; [[principle--rate-of-force-development-as-independent-limiter]]\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011CffZvq1UmoCuZTDJ9zAtM","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"c9c6ba41-891a-41bc-a48d-c74b6b40d414","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:50:30.531Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
