avy weights tend to increase the strength potential of the muscles, while fast exercises with light weights improve speed.\" Neither adequately enhances \"specific components of explosive movements such as the rapid excitation of muscles and the rapid changing from eccentric to concentric work.\" These skills need \"a specific training regime which is impossible to imitate with only one resistance exercise\": slow resistance movements, and lifting a barbell in preparation for squatting or jumping with it, remove \"the possibility of controlling the mechanisms which are crucial to the rapid switching of muscles to the active state\", while lowering the resistance \"diminishes the dynamic effort required and creates a vicious cycle from which there is no apparent escape.\" This analysis is the authors' stated route to the shock (plyometric) method on the same page. (PDF p. 278 / printed p. 267, verified against the page image.)\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reason is the one quoted: the barbell means do not reproduce rapid excitation or the fast eccentric-concentric switch. They give no further physiology here. Inference: the \"vicious cycle\" is that heavy loads slow the movement and light loads drop the effort, so no single barbell load gives both high effort and a fast switch.\n\n**When to apply** — When a program relies on heavy squats plus light speed squats to develop jumping or cutting explosiveness, this is the authors' argument for adding a means that trains the switch itself (see [[framework--shock-method-and-plyometric-terminology]]). Eric's baseline: \"Training time is deliberately weighted toward high motor-unit-recruitment core/ballistic movements (squats, cleans, jumps, throws, sprints)\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 104). Inference: this entry is the authors' case for why the jumps in that list are not interchangeable with the squats.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The authors do not say heavy squats are useless for explosive athletes: \"this does not mean that it is impossible for them to execute fast dynamic contractions\", and heavy work raises \"strength potential\". Later in the same section they prefer heavy \"brief maximal tension\" work where explosive force must overcome large resistance (see [[heuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size]]). The 60 kg squat-thrust load is given without athlete bodymass or level, so it is not a prescription.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own example: heavy squats (slow, quasi-isometric) versus squat thrusts with a 60 kg barbell (large dynamic maximum force), with neither training the rapid switch (PDF p. 278 / printed p. 267).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--shock-method-and-plyometric-terminology]]; [[principle--equal-force-different-loading-methods]]; [[heuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size]]; [[principle--rate-of-force-development-as-independent-limiter]]\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"c9c6ba41-891a-41bc-a48d-c74b6b40d414","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"8698d533-52c6-4e5b-9c37-0ede0b2f0317","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","attachment":{"type":"total_tokens_reminder","text":"<total_tokens>14767459 tokens left</total_tokens>"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"4a8cb652-638f-4288-a92d-179ec76787a3","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:50:30.654Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\n<total_tokens>14767459 tokens left</total_tokens>\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"4a8cb652-638f-4288-a92d-179ec76787a3","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffZzSJLt1sG4UQpLSoYQ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4473,"cache_read_input_tokens":228204,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4473,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":466,"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CffZyxMpw1ac4c3FFebM8","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"7ae91a5e-7601-4f86-9f7f-a30abd143a49","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:50:38.626Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"7ae91a5e-7601-4f86-9f7f-a30abd143a49","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffZzSJLt1sG4UQpLSoYQ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01ETvhJsXXZNHv9oRi8Ucwx4","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--plyometric-training-coupling-under-0-15-s-feedforward-and-force-time-pattern.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.4\"\ntopic: what counts as plyometric training\ncategory: [plyometrics, stretch-shortening-cycle, coupling-time, terminology]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Classical Plyometric Training: Coupling Under About 0.15 s, Feedforward Control, and a Shock Force-Time Pattern, Not Coupling Time Alone\n\n**What it is** — The authors give three tests for whether a drill is classical \"training plyometrics\" rather than ordinary jumping:\n1. **Coupling time.** The method \"is characterised by impulsive action of minimal duration between the end of the eccentric braking phase and initiation of the concentric acceleration phase\". \"If the transition phase (or, coupling phase) is prolonged by more than about 0.15 second, the action may be considered to constitute ordinary jumping and not classical training plyometrics.\" The coaching image: imagine the contact surface \"is red hot, so that any prolonged contact would be dangerous.\" (PDF p. 279 / printed p. 268, verified against the page image.)\n2. **Feedforward control.** \"The activity is not accurately plyometric if the athlete relies upon ongoing feedback processes to control the isometric and concentric actions instead of upon feedforward programmes established before any movement begins\"; true plyometric training \"usually involves ballistic rather than cocontraction processes\" (same page).\n3. **Force-time pattern.** Later the 0.15 s rule \"requires some qualification\" (PDF p. 281 / printed p. 270). \"One cannot simply distinguish between plyometric and non-plyometric solely on the basis of coupling times, otherwise one would have to classify jogging or even brisk walking as classical plyometrics, because the time taken for the ground reaction force to reach a maximum can be less than 0.15 second. One also has to take the force-time pattern and the rate of force development (RFD) into account\" (PDF p. 283 / printed p. 272, verified against the page image).\n\nThe same pages separate *plyometric actions*, which \"occur as part of many running, jumping, hurdling, striking and other rebounding movements in sport\", from *plyometric training*, \"which applies plyometric actions as a distinct training modality according to a definite methodology\" (PDF p. 279 / printed p. 268). Siff writes this passage (it names Verkhoshansky in the third person: \"Verkhoshansky has always consistently favoured the use of the term 'shock method'\"). He suggests calling the action \"stretch-shortening action\" and the training system \"powermetrics\", and dates the earliest published use of \"plyometrics\" to a Soviet publication (Zanon, 1966). He then concludes that arguing whether an action is \"classically plyometric or not becomes complex and academic, with no real benefit to the prescription of plyometric training\", and that it is better to recognise types of plyometric training (PDF p. 283 / printed p. 272; see [[framework--plyometric-training-classification-impact-intensity-and-function]]).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' mechanism: the method \"relies on the production of a very brief explosive-isometric and eccentric-isometric phase which precedes the release of the elastic energy stored in the tendons and other components of the series elastic component\" (PDF p. 279 / printed p. 268). For why the time limit varies, see [[claim--prestretch-benefit-decay-time-differs-by-task]] (tonic versus phasic reflexes, fibre type). Inference: jogging passes the 0.15 s test but has a low, unhurried force-time curve, which is why the authors add the force-time and RFD test.\n\n**When to apply** — Auditing a \"plyo\" block: check (1) ground or implement contact, eccentric to concentric, short enough to meet the about 0.15 s guide, (2) a pre-planned ballistic action rather than a corrected one, and (3) a sharp force rise. Eric's baseline: speed is taught \"as the quality with the shortest ground-contact-time demand (~0.08s male, ~0.10s female at max velocity)\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 96). Inference: those sprint contacts sit under the authors' 0.15 s coupling guide, though contact time and coupling time are not the same interval (coupling is only the eccentric-to-concentric transition); a force plate contact time over 0.15 s does not by itself show a coupling over 0.15 s.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The 0.15 s figure is \"about\" and is qualified by the authors themselves; it is not a hard cut-off. Do not use coupling time alone (the jogging counter-example). Do not read \"not classical plyometrics\" as \"not useful\": the authors say longer-coupling drills train other qualities (see [[claim--popular-plyometric-drills-work-through-other-mechanisms]]). The authors give no method for measuring coupling time in the field.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: jogging and brisk walking reach peak ground reaction force in under 0.15 s yet are not classical plyometrics (PDF p. 283 / printed p. 272). The \"red hot\" surface is their cue (PDF p. 279 / printed p. 268).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--shock-method-and-plyometric-terminology]] (Ch1, the original definition and \"powermetrics\"); [[principle--short-amortization-preserves-elastic-energy]]; [[framework--five-phases-of-a-plyometric-action]]; [[claim--prestretch-benefit-decay-time-differs-by-task]]; [[framework--plyometric-training-classification-impact-intensity-and-function]]; [[science-and-practice-of-strength-training--zatsiorsky/claim--reversible-muscular-action-is-a-trainable-skill-not-automatic]]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4473,"cache_read_input_tokens":228204,"output_tokens":2134,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":14},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4473},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2134,"cache_read_input_tokens":228204,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4473,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4473,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01ETvhJsXXZNHv9oRi8Ucwx4":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--plyometric-training-coupling-under-0-15-s-feedforward-and-force-time-pattern.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.4\"\ntopic: what counts as plyometric training\ncategory: [plyometrics, stretch-shortening-cycle, coupling-time, terminology]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Classical Plyometric Training: Coupling Under About 0.15 s, Feedforward Control, and a Shock Force-Time Pattern, Not Coupling Time Alone\n\n**What it is** — The authors give three tests for whether a drill is classical \"training plyometrics\" rather than ordinary jumping:\n1. **Coupling time.** The method \"is characterised by impulsive action of minimal duration between the end of the eccentric braking phase and initiation of the concentric acceleration phase\". \"If the transition phase (or, coupling phase) is prolonged by more than about 0.15 second, the action may be considered to constitute ordinary jumping and not classical training plyometrics.\" The coaching image: imagine the contact surface \"is red hot, so that any prolonged contact would be dangerous.\" (PDF p. 279 / printed p. 268, verified against the page image.)\n2. **Feedforward control.** \"The activity is not accurately plyometric if the athlete relies upon ongoing feedback processes to control the isometric and concentric actions instead of upon feedforward programmes established before any movement begins\"; true plyometric training \"usually involves ballistic rather than cocontraction processes\" (same page).\n3. **Force-time pattern.** Later the 0.15 s rule \"requires some qualification\" (PDF p. 281 / printed p. 270). \"One cannot simply distinguish between plyometric and non-plyometric solely on the basis of coupling times, otherwise one would have to classify jogging or even brisk walking as classical plyometrics, because the time taken for the ground reaction force to reach a maximum can be less than 0.15 second. One also has to take the force-time pattern and the rate of force development (RFD) into account\" (PDF p. 283 / printed p. 272, verified against the page image).\n\nThe same pages separate *plyometric actions*, which \"occur as part of many running, jumping, hurdling, striking and other rebounding movements in sport\", from *plyometric training*, \"which applies plyometric actions as a distinct training modality according to a definite methodology\" (PDF p. 279 / printed p. 268). Siff writes this passage (it names Verkhoshansky in the third person: \"Verkhoshansky has always consistently favoured the use of the term 'shock method'\"). He suggests calling the action \"stretch-shortening action\" and the training system \"powermetrics\", and dates the earliest published use of \"plyometrics\" to a Soviet publication (Zanon, 1966). He then concludes that arguing whether an action is \"classically plyometric or not becomes complex and academic, with no real benefit to the prescription of plyometric training\", and that it is better to recognise types of plyometric training (PDF p. 283 / printed p. 272; see [[framework--plyometric-training-classification-impact-intensity-and-function]]).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' mechanism: the method \"relies on the production of a very brief explosive-isometric and eccentric-isometric phase which precedes the release of the elastic energy stored in the tendons and other components of the series elastic component\" (PDF p. 279 / printed p. 268). For why the time limit varies, see [[claim--prestretch-benefit-decay-time-differs-by-task]] (tonic versus phasic reflexes, fibre type). Inference: jogging passes the 0.15 s test but has a low, unhurried force-time curve, which is why the authors add the force-time and RFD test.\n\n**When to apply** — Auditing a \"plyo\" block: check (1) ground or implement contact, eccentric to concentric, short enough to meet the about 0.15 s guide, (2) a pre-planned ballistic action rather than a corrected one, and (3) a sharp force rise. Eric's baseline: speed is taught \"as the quality with the shortest ground-contact-time demand (~0.08s male, ~0.10s female at max velocity)\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 96). Inference: those sprint contacts sit under the authors' 0.15 s coupling guide, though contact time and coupling time are not the same interval (coupling is only the eccentric-to-concentric transition); a force plate contact time over 0.15 s does not by itself show a coupling over 0.15 s.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The 0.15 s figure is \"about\" and is qualified by the authors themselves; it is not a hard cut-off. Do not use coupling time alone (the jogging counter-example). Do not read \"not classical plyometrics\" as \"not useful\": the authors say longer-coupling drills train other qualities (see [[claim--popular-plyometric-drills-work-through-other-mechanisms]]). The authors give no method for measuring coupling time in the field.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: jogging and brisk walking reach peak ground reaction force in under 0.15 s yet are not classical plyometrics (PDF p. 283 / printed p. 272). The \"red hot\" surface is their cue (PDF p. 279 / printed p. 268).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--shock-method-and-plyometric-terminology]] (Ch1, the original definition and \"powermetrics\"); [[principle--short-amortization-preserves-elastic-energy]]; [[framework--five-phases-of-a-plyometric-action]]; [[claim--prestretch-benefit-decay-time-differs-by-task]]; [[framework--plyometric-training-classification-impact-intensity-and-function]]; [[science-and-practice-of-strength-training--zatsiorsky/claim--reversible-muscular-action-is-a-trainable-skill-not-automatic]]\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffZyxMpw1ac4c3FFebM8","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"705d4a03-18ac-4d55-88f1-c310347c3477","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:50:55.211Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"705d4a03-18ac-4d55-88f1-c310347c3477","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"624a8cec-33fa-4c0a-a445-c72ce718e6ab","agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01ETvhJsXXZNHv9oRi8Ucwx4","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--plyometric-training-coupling-under-0-15-s-feedforward-and-force-time-pattern.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"ea937613-f8c0-4f1e-8892-34c084211b99","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:50:55.287Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"create","filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--plyometric-training-coupling-under-0-15-s-feedforward-and-force-time-pattern.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.4\"\ntopic: what counts as plyometric training\ncategory: [plyometrics, stretch-shortening-cycle, coupling-time, terminology]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Classical Plyometric Training: Coupling Under About 0.15 s, Feedforward Control, and a Shock Force-Time Pattern, Not Coupling Time Alone\n\n**What it is** — The authors give three tests for whether a drill is classical \"training plyometrics\" rather than ordinary jumping:\n1. **Coupling time.** The method \"is characterised by impulsive action of minimal duration between the end of the eccentric braking phase and initiation of the concentric acceleration phase\". \"If the transition phase (or, coupling phase) is prolonged by more than about 0.15 second, the action may be considered to constitute ordinary jumping and not classical training plyometrics.\" The coaching image: imagine the contact surface \"is red hot, so that any prolonged contact would be dangerous.\" (PDF p. 279 / printed p. 268, verified against the page image.)\n2. **Feedforward control.** \"The activity is not accurately plyometric if the athlete relies upon ongoing feedback processes to control the isometric and concentric actions instead of upon feedforward programmes established before any movement begins\"; true plyometric training \"usually involves ballistic rather than cocontraction processes\" (same page).\n3. **Force-time pattern.** Later the 0.15 s rule \"requires some qualification\" (PDF p. 281 / printed p. 270). \"One cannot simply distinguish between plyometric and non-plyometric solely on the basis of coupling times, otherwise one would have to classify jogging or even brisk walking as classical plyometrics, because the time taken for the ground reaction force to reach a maximum can be less than 0.15 second. One also has to take the force-time pattern and the rate of force development (RFD) into account\" (PDF p. 283 / printed p. 272, verified against the page image).\n\nThe same pages separate *plyometric actions*, which \"occur as part of many running, jumping, hurdling, striking and other rebounding movements in sport\", from *plyometric training*, \"which applies plyometric actions as a distinct training modality according to a definite methodology\" (PDF p. 279 / printed p. 268). Siff writes this passage (it names Verkhoshansky in the third person: \"Verkhoshansky has always consistently favoured the use of the term 'shock method'\"). He suggests calling the action \"stretch-shortening action\" and the training system \"powermetrics\", and dates the earliest published use of \"plyometrics\" to a Soviet publication (Zanon, 1966). He then concludes that arguing whether an action is \"classically plyometric or not becomes complex and academic, with no real benefit to the prescription of plyometric training\", and that it is better to recognise types of plyometric training (PDF p. 283 / printed p. 272; see [[framework--plyometric-training-classification-impact-intensity-and-function]]).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' mechanism: the method \"relies on the production of a very brief explosive-isometric and eccentric-isometric phase which precedes the release of the elastic energy stored in the tendons and other components of the series elastic component\" (PDF p. 279 / printed p. 268). For why the time limit varies, see [[claim--prestretch-benefit-decay-time-differs-by-task]] (tonic versus phasic reflexes, fibre type). Inference: jogging passes the 0.15 s test but has a low, unhurried force-time curve, which is why the authors add the force-time and RFD test.\n\n**When to apply** — Auditing a \"plyo\" block: check (1) ground or implement contact, eccentric to concentric, short enough to meet the about 0.15 s guide, (2) a pre-planned ballistic action rather than a corrected one, and (3) a sharp force rise. Eric's baseline: speed is taught \"as the quality with the shortest ground-contact-time demand (~0.08s male, ~0.10s female at max velocity)\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 96). Inference: those sprint contacts sit under the authors' 0.15 s coupling guide, though contact time and coupling time are not the same interval (coupling is only the eccentric-to-concentric transition); a force plate contact time over 0.15 s does not by itself show a coupling over 0.15 s.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The 0.15 s figure is \"about\" and is qualified by the authors themselves; it is not a hard cut-off. Do not use coupling time alone (the jogging counter-example). Do not read \"not classical plyometrics\" as \"not useful\": the authors say longer-coupling drills train other qualities (see [[claim--popular-plyometric-drills-work-through-other-mechanisms]]). The authors give no method for measuring coupling time in the field.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: jogging and brisk walking reach peak ground reaction force in under 0.15 s yet are not classical plyometrics (PDF p. 283 / printed p. 272). The \"red hot\" surface is their cue (PDF p. 279 / printed p. 268).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--shock-method-and-plyometric-terminology]] (Ch1, the original definition and \"powermetrics\"); [[principle--short-amortization-preserves-elastic-energy]]; [[framework--five-phases-of-a-plyometric-action]]; [[claim--prestretch-benefit-decay-time-differs-by-task]]; [[framework--plyometric-training-classification-impact-intensity-and-function]]; [[science-and-practice-of-strength-training--zatsiorsky/claim--reversible-muscular-action-is-a-trainable-skill-not-automatic]]\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"705d4a03-18ac-4d55-88f1-c310347c3477","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"ea937613-f8c0-4f1e-8892-34c084211b99","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","attachment":{"type":"total_tokens_reminder","text":"<total_tokens>14765187 tokens left</total_tokens>"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"05dbf271-f714-422e-9e93-257088d35689","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:50:55.295Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\n<total_tokens>14765187 tokens left</total_tokens>\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"05dbf271-f714-422e-9e93-257088d35689","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cffa2CFKHDUDz9HSRCAkP","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2273,"cache_read_input_tokens":232677,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2273,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":521,"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011Cffa1mcV9MDziusPBYrqU","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"c332bb40-9bfd-45ca-b3af-0f21a46a23d1","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:51:02.835Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"c332bb40-9bfd-45ca-b3af-0f21a46a23d1","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cffa2CFKHDUDz9HSRCAkP","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01TiGXEkN3MHrXzdi56wXDks","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--five-phases-of-a-plyometric-action.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.4\"\ntopic: phases of a plyometric action\ncategory: [plyometrics, stretch-shortening-cycle, biomechanics]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# The Five Phases of a Plyometric Action (Fig 5.5), With Coupling Time as the Explosive-Isometric Interval\n\n**What it is** — Under \"The Fundamental Theory of Plyometrics\" the authors list five phases (PDF p. 281 / printed p. 270; Fig 5.5 verified against the page image):\n1. **Initial momentum phase:** the body or limb moves \"because of kinetic energy (KE) it has accumulated from a preceding action.\"\n2. **Electromechanical delay phase:** contact with a surface stops the limb and \"provokes the muscles to contract\"; the delay is \"the time elapsing between the onset of the action potential in the motor nerves and the onset of the muscle contraction.\" The authors note some workers include lengthening of the SEC (series elastic component) in this phase, but \"electromechanical\" was coined for the electrical-to-mechanical interval, \"which does not include the passive stretching of connective tissue.\"\n3. **Amortisation phase:** the KE \"produces a powerful myotatic stretch reflex which leads to eccentric muscle contraction accompanied by explosive isometric contraction\" and stretching of the SEC (e.g. tendons). \"The explosive isometric phase between the end of the eccentric action and the beginning of the concentric action lasts for a period known as the *coupling time*.\"\n4. **Rebound phase:** release of elastic energy from the SEC, with involuntary concentric contraction evoked by the stretch reflex, \"as well as possible local change in the muscle fibre mechanics\"; it \"may include the contribution added by voluntary concentric contraction.\"\n5. **Final momentum phase:** after the concentric contraction, the body or limb continues on the KE imparted by contraction and elastic recoil.\n\nFig 5.5, as drawn: boxes for initial momentum, amortisation, rebound and final momentum phases; between them, bars for \"EM delay\", \"eccentric contraction\" and \"concentric contraction\", joined at the bottom by a box \"explosive isometrics\" labelled \"Coupling Time\".\n\nThe caption defines EM delay differently from the numbered text: \"Electromechanical delay between signal to terminate initial momentum phase and instant when eccentric contraction begins\" (caption), against \"between the onset of the action potential in the motor nerves and the onset of the muscle contraction\" (text item 2). Both are on PDF p. 281 / printed p. 270; the book does not reconcile them.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' mechanism is in the phase descriptions: stretch reflex plus SEC elastic energy, released in the rebound. Elsewhere they add that the process is not only spinal reflex and elastic recoil (see [[claim--plyometric-skill-is-cognitive-and-state-dependent]]).\n\n**When to apply** — As a shared vocabulary for coaching and for reading a force-time trace of a depth jump or rebound: the coupling interval is the part the authors' 0.15 s guide applies to (see [[heuristic--plyometric-training-coupling-under-0-15-s-feedforward-and-force-time-pattern]]). Inference: on a force plate, the eccentric (braking) and concentric (propulsive) phases of a drop jump map onto phases 3 and 4; the authors do not give a force-plate method for isolating the isometric coupling interval.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The phase list is a descriptive model, not measured timing; the book gives no durations for phases 1, 2, 4 or 5. Do not quote the EM delay definition without noting the caption and text differ. The rebound phase's \"possible local change in the muscle fibre mechanics\" is stated as possible, not shown.\n\n**Example** — Inference: in a depth jump from a box, phase 1 is the fall, phases 2-3 are the first instants of ground contact through the lowest point, phase 4 is the push-off, and phase 5 is the flight. The authors' own illustration is Fig 5.5.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--series-and-parallel-elastic-components]]; [[principle--triphasic-nature-of-muscle-action]]; [[principle--short-amortization-preserves-elastic-energy]]; [[heuristic--plyometric-training-coupling-under-0-15-s-feedforward-and-force-time-pattern]]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2273,"cache_read_input_tokens":232677,"output_tokens":1734,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":52},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2273},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1734,"cache_read_input_tokens":232677,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2273,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2273,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01TiGXEkN3MHrXzdi56wXDks":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--five-phases-of-a-plyometric-action.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.4\"\ntopic: phases of a plyometric action\ncategory: [plyometrics, stretch-shortening-cycle, biomechanics]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# The Five Phases of a Plyometric Action (Fig 5.5), With Coupling Time as the Explosive-Isometric Interval\n\n**What it is** — Under \"The Fundamental Theory of Plyometrics\" the authors list five phases (PDF p. 281 / printed p. 270; Fig 5.5 verified against the page image):\n1. **Initial momentum phase:** the body or limb moves \"because of kinetic energy (KE) it has accumulated from a preceding action.\"\n2. **Electromechanical delay phase:** contact with a surface stops the limb and \"provokes the muscles to contract\"; the delay is \"the time elapsing between the onset of the action potential in the motor nerves and the onset of the muscle contraction.\" The authors note some workers include lengthening of the SEC (series elastic component) in this phase, but \"electromechanical\" was coined for the electrical-to-mechanical interval, \"which does not include the passive stretching of connective tissue.\"\n3. **Amortisation phase:** the KE \"produces a powerful myotatic stretch reflex which leads to eccentric muscle contraction accompanied by explosive isometric contraction\" and stretching of the SEC (e.g. tendons). \"The explosive isometric phase between the end of the eccentric action and the beginning of the concentric action lasts for a period known as the *coupling time*.\"\n4. **Rebound phase:** release of elastic energy from the SEC, with involuntary concentric contraction evoked by the stretch reflex, \"as well as possible local change in the muscle fibre mechanics\"; it \"may include the contribution added by voluntary concentric contraction.\"\n5. **Final momentum phase:** after the concentric contraction, the body or limb continues on the KE imparted by contraction and elastic recoil.\n\nFig 5.5, as drawn: boxes for initial momentum, amortisation, rebound and final momentum phases; between them, bars for \"EM delay\", \"eccentric contraction\" and \"concentric contraction\", joined at the bottom by a box \"explosive isometrics\" labelled \"Coupling Time\".\n\nThe caption defines EM delay differently from the numbered text: \"Electromechanical delay between signal to terminate initial momentum phase and instant when eccentric contraction begins\" (caption), against \"between the onset of the action potential in the motor nerves and the onset of the muscle contraction\" (text item 2). Both are on PDF p. 281 / printed p. 270; the book does not reconcile them.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' mechanism is in the phase descriptions: stretch reflex plus SEC elastic energy, released in the rebound. Elsewhere they add that the process is not only spinal reflex and elastic recoil (see [[claim--plyometric-skill-is-cognitive-and-state-dependent]]).\n\n**When to apply** — As a shared vocabulary for coaching and for reading a force-time trace of a depth jump or rebound: the coupling interval is the part the authors' 0.15 s guide applies to (see [[heuristic--plyometric-training-coupling-under-0-15-s-feedforward-and-force-time-pattern]]). Inference: on a force plate, the eccentric (braking) and concentric (propulsive) phases of a drop jump map onto phases 3 and 4; the authors do not give a force-plate method for isolating the isometric coupling interval.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The phase list is a descriptive model, not measured timing; the book gives no durations for phases 1, 2, 4 or 5. Do not quote the EM delay definition without noting the caption and text differ. The rebound phase's \"possible local change in the muscle fibre mechanics\" is stated as possible, not shown.\n\n**Example** — Inference: in a depth jump from a box, phase 1 is the fall, phases 2-3 are the first instants of ground contact through the lowest point, phase 4 is the push-off, and phase 5 is the flight. The authors' own illustration is Fig 5.5.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--series-and-parallel-elastic-components]]; [[principle--triphasic-nature-of-muscle-action]]; [[principle--short-amortization-preserves-elastic-energy]]; [[heuristic--plyometric-training-coupling-under-0-15-s-feedforward-and-force-time-pattern]]\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011Cffa1mcV9MDziusPBYrqU","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"7b05ce24-6e63-4dd2-a48a-2a2efb229c83","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:51:16.230Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"7b05ce24-6e63-4dd2-a48a-2a2efb229c83","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"624a8cec-33fa-4c0a-a445-c72ce718e6ab","agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TiGXEkN3MHrXzdi56wXDks","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--five-phases-of-a-plyometric-action.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"a1378e2c-cfb2-49e3-ac2e-5035961f363d","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:51:16.332Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"create","filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--five-phases-of-a-plyometric-action.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.4\"\ntopic: phases of a plyometric action\ncategory: [plyometrics, stretch-shortening-cycle, biomechanics]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# The Five Phases of a Plyometric Action (Fig 5.5), With Coupling Time as the Explosive-Isometric Interval\n\n**What it is** — Under \"The Fundamental Theory of Plyometrics\" the authors list five phases (PDF p. 281 / printed p. 270; Fig 5.5 verified against the page image):\n1. **Initial momentum phase:** the body or limb moves \"because of kinetic energy (KE) it has accumulated from a preceding action.\"\n2. **Electromechanical delay phase:** contact with a surface stops the limb and \"provokes the muscles to contract\"; the delay is \"the time elapsing between the onset of the action potential in the motor nerves and the onset of the muscle contraction.\" The authors note some workers include lengthening of the SEC (series elastic component) in this phase, but \"electromechanical\" was coined for the electrical-to-mechanical interval, \"which does not include the passive stretching of connective tissue.\"\n3. **Amortisation phase:** the KE \"produces a powerful myotatic stretch reflex which leads to eccentric muscle contraction accompanied by explosive isometric contraction\" and stretching of the SEC (e.g. tendons). \"The explosive isometric phase between the end of the eccentric action and the beginning of the concentric action lasts for a period known as the *coupling time*.\"\n4. **Rebound phase:** release of elastic energy from the SEC, with involuntary concentric contraction evoked by the stretch reflex, \"as well as possible local change in the muscle fibre mechanics\"; it \"may include the contribution added by voluntary concentric contraction.\"\n5. **Final momentum phase:** after the concentric contraction, the body or limb continues on the KE imparted by contraction and elastic recoil.\n\nFig 5.5, as drawn: boxes for initial momentum, amortisation, rebound and final momentum phases; between them, bars for \"EM delay\", \"eccentric contraction\" and \"concentric contraction\", joined at the bottom by a box \"explosive isometrics\" labelled \"Coupling Time\".\n\nThe caption defines EM delay differently from the numbered text: \"Electromechanical delay between signal to terminate initial momentum phase and instant when eccentric contraction begins\" (caption), against \"between the onset of the action potential in the motor nerves and the onset of the muscle contraction\" (text item 2). Both are on PDF p. 281 / printed p. 270; the book does not reconcile them.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' mechanism is in the phase descriptions: stretch reflex plus SEC elastic energy, released in the rebound. Elsewhere they add that the process is not only spinal reflex and elastic recoil (see [[claim--plyometric-skill-is-cognitive-and-state-dependent]]).\n\n**When to apply** — As a shared vocabulary for coaching and for reading a force-time trace of a depth jump or rebound: the coupling interval is the part the authors' 0.15 s guide applies to (see [[heuristic--plyometric-training-coupling-under-0-15-s-feedforward-and-force-time-pattern]]). Inference: on a force plate, the eccentric (braking) and concentric (propulsive) phases of a drop jump map onto phases 3 and 4; the authors do not give a force-plate method for isolating the isometric coupling interval.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The phase list is a descriptive model, not measured timing; the book gives no durations for phases 1, 2, 4 or 5. Do not quote the EM delay definition without noting the caption and text differ. The rebound phase's \"possible local change in the muscle fibre mechanics\" is stated as possible, not shown.\n\n**Example** — Inference: in a depth jump from a box, phase 1 is the fall, phases 2-3 are the first instants of ground contact through the lowest point, phase 4 is the push-off, and phase 5 is the flight. The authors' own illustration is Fig 5.5.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--series-and-parallel-elastic-components]]; [[principle--triphasic-nature-of-muscle-action]]; [[principle--short-amortization-preserves-elastic-energy]]; [[heuristic--plyometric-training-coupling-under-0-15-s-feedforward-and-force-time-pattern]]\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"7b05ce24-6e63-4dd2-a48a-2a2efb229c83","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
