{"parentUuid":"6c5ea5f2-b328-42a1-a4cd-e6bff8edddba","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cffa3otD1tBed4E4dv9Ra","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1853,"cache_read_input_tokens":234950,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1853,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":473,"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011Cffa3Ki3xhrjLiXMPivw5","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"24824358-a512-4878-9bc5-6530193f6a69","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:51:24.637Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"24824358-a512-4878-9bc5-6530193f6a69","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cffa3otD1tBed4E4dv9Ra","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01SSCzKUmxev3fthVunZ8Buf","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--prestretch-benefit-decay-time-differs-by-task.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: claim\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.4\"\ntopic: decay of the prestretch benefit\ncategory: [stretch-shortening-cycle, coupling-time, research-conflict]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# How Long a Prestretch Helps Varies From About 0.25 s to About 4 s by Task, and the Book's Own Figure Attribution Conflicts\n\n**What it is** — After stating the about 0.15 s coupling guide, the authors say it \"requires some qualification\" and list conflicting results (PDF pp. 281-282 / printed pp. 270-271; Figs 5.6 and 5.7 verified against the page image):\n- Wilson et al (1990), bench press: \"the benefits of prior stretch may endure for as long as 4 seconds, at which stage it is suggested that all stored elastic energy is lost (see Fig 5.6).\"\n- Chapman and Caldwell (1985), forearm movement: benefits \"dissipated within 0.25 second, a figure which agrees with Siff's analysis of explosive rebound elbow flexion without additional loading (Fig 5.7).\"\n- Wilson et al (1991), rebound action of the chest and arms: \"no benefits of prior stretching are evident after 0.37 second.\"\n\nSummary in the text: \"delays of as long as a second or two can still produce significant augmentation of the subsequent concentric phase for some activities, but delays as short as 0.2 second are sufficient to dissipate the benefits of prior stretch during other activities.\"\n\nFigure readings (approximate, from the image). Fig 5.6, \"% decay\" against delay 0-5 s: rises steeply from 0 near 0.1-0.2 s, roughly half decayed near 1 s, about 80% by 2 s, near 100% at about 4 s. Fig 5.7, unloaded elbow flexion, delay 0.1-0.25 s: close to linear from 0% at 0.1 s to 100% at 0.25 s.\n\n**Internal conflict, recorded:** the Fig 5.6 caption reads \"based on the data of Wilson et al, 1991\", but the text gives the 4 s bench-press result to Wilson et al (1990) and the 0.37 s chest/arms result to Wilson et al (1991) (PDF pp. 281-282 / printed pp. 270-271). The curve's near-total decay at about 4 s matches the text's 1990 sentence, not the 0.37 s result. Which year is right is not resolvable from the book.\n\nThe authors' partial resolutions:\n- Fibre type (Bosco et al, 1982a): high-FT individuals show the maximum plyometric effect with a short eccentric phase, small range and brief coupling; high-ST individuals jump best with a longer eccentric phase, greater range and longer coupling, \"since the actin-myosin cross-bridging attachment time is of greater duration\" (PDF p. 282 / printed p. 271).\n- Joint-specific maximum delays \"probably\" exist, but the body has many reflexes acting \"at different rates\"; tonic (static) and phasic (dynamic) stretch reflexes differ (PDF p. 282 / printed p. 271).\n- The weightlifters' \"pre-stretch principle\" (hold an intense stretch \"for a couple of seconds and then thrust\") \"would seem\" to involve the more tonic reflex with longer coupling, and \"could certainly not be called plyometric\", while phasic reflexes are \"more likely\" behind classical plyometrics (PDF p. 283 / printed p. 272, verified against the page image).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors offer fibre type and tonic versus phasic reflexes as explanations, with hedges (\"partial solution\", \"would seem\", \"more likely\"). They do not test either against the conflicting delay data.\n\n**When to apply** — When someone quotes one fixed \"the stretch benefit is gone after X ms\" number: the book shows the window depends on the task (unloaded elbow about 0.25 s; loaded bench press up to about 4 s). Inference: for loaded lifts a paused rep still keeps some prestretch benefit for a second or more, per Fig 5.6, while for unloaded fast rebounds the benefit is gone within a few tenths of a second, per Fig 5.7.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Do not cite Fig 5.6 under a single Wilson year without noting the caption-text conflict. The figure values are read from small plots and are approximate. Fig 5.7 is Siff's own unpublished analysis; the book gives no subject numbers. The fibre-type and reflex explanations are proposals, not demonstrated here.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: unloaded explosive elbow flexion loses its prestretch benefit over 0.1-0.25 s (Fig 5.7), while the bench-press benefit decays over about 4 s (Fig 5.6).\n\n**Related** — [[heuristic--match-plyometric-prescription-to-joint-angle-and-fiber-type]] (Ch1.7, the same Bosco et al 1982 fibre-type finding); [[principle--short-amortization-preserves-elastic-energy]]; [[framework--stretch-reflex-types-and-neuromuscular-control-of-flexibility]]; [[heuristic--plyometric-training-coupling-under-0-15-s-feedforward-and-force-time-pattern]]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1853,"cache_read_input_tokens":234950,"output_tokens":1975,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":11},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1853},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1975,"cache_read_input_tokens":234950,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1853,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1853,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01SSCzKUmxev3fthVunZ8Buf":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--prestretch-benefit-decay-time-differs-by-task.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: claim\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.4\"\ntopic: decay of the prestretch benefit\ncategory: [stretch-shortening-cycle, coupling-time, research-conflict]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# How Long a Prestretch Helps Varies From About 0.25 s to About 4 s by Task, and the Book's Own Figure Attribution Conflicts\n\n**What it is** — After stating the about 0.15 s coupling guide, the authors say it \"requires some qualification\" and list conflicting results (PDF pp. 281-282 / printed pp. 270-271; Figs 5.6 and 5.7 verified against the page image):\n- Wilson et al (1990), bench press: \"the benefits of prior stretch may endure for as long as 4 seconds, at which stage it is suggested that all stored elastic energy is lost (see Fig 5.6).\"\n- Chapman and Caldwell (1985), forearm movement: benefits \"dissipated within 0.25 second, a figure which agrees with Siff's analysis of explosive rebound elbow flexion without additional loading (Fig 5.7).\"\n- Wilson et al (1991), rebound action of the chest and arms: \"no benefits of prior stretching are evident after 0.37 second.\"\n\nSummary in the text: \"delays of as long as a second or two can still produce significant augmentation of the subsequent concentric phase for some activities, but delays as short as 0.2 second are sufficient to dissipate the benefits of prior stretch during other activities.\"\n\nFigure readings (approximate, from the image). Fig 5.6, \"% decay\" against delay 0-5 s: rises steeply from 0 near 0.1-0.2 s, roughly half decayed near 1 s, about 80% by 2 s, near 100% at about 4 s. Fig 5.7, unloaded elbow flexion, delay 0.1-0.25 s: close to linear from 0% at 0.1 s to 100% at 0.25 s.\n\n**Internal conflict, recorded:** the Fig 5.6 caption reads \"based on the data of Wilson et al, 1991\", but the text gives the 4 s bench-press result to Wilson et al (1990) and the 0.37 s chest/arms result to Wilson et al (1991) (PDF pp. 281-282 / printed pp. 270-271). The curve's near-total decay at about 4 s matches the text's 1990 sentence, not the 0.37 s result. Which year is right is not resolvable from the book.\n\nThe authors' partial resolutions:\n- Fibre type (Bosco et al, 1982a): high-FT individuals show the maximum plyometric effect with a short eccentric phase, small range and brief coupling; high-ST individuals jump best with a longer eccentric phase, greater range and longer coupling, \"since the actin-myosin cross-bridging attachment time is of greater duration\" (PDF p. 282 / printed p. 271).\n- Joint-specific maximum delays \"probably\" exist, but the body has many reflexes acting \"at different rates\"; tonic (static) and phasic (dynamic) stretch reflexes differ (PDF p. 282 / printed p. 271).\n- The weightlifters' \"pre-stretch principle\" (hold an intense stretch \"for a couple of seconds and then thrust\") \"would seem\" to involve the more tonic reflex with longer coupling, and \"could certainly not be called plyometric\", while phasic reflexes are \"more likely\" behind classical plyometrics (PDF p. 283 / printed p. 272, verified against the page image).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors offer fibre type and tonic versus phasic reflexes as explanations, with hedges (\"partial solution\", \"would seem\", \"more likely\"). They do not test either against the conflicting delay data.\n\n**When to apply** — When someone quotes one fixed \"the stretch benefit is gone after X ms\" number: the book shows the window depends on the task (unloaded elbow about 0.25 s; loaded bench press up to about 4 s). Inference: for loaded lifts a paused rep still keeps some prestretch benefit for a second or more, per Fig 5.6, while for unloaded fast rebounds the benefit is gone within a few tenths of a second, per Fig 5.7.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Do not cite Fig 5.6 under a single Wilson year without noting the caption-text conflict. The figure values are read from small plots and are approximate. Fig 5.7 is Siff's own unpublished analysis; the book gives no subject numbers. The fibre-type and reflex explanations are proposals, not demonstrated here.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: unloaded explosive elbow flexion loses its prestretch benefit over 0.1-0.25 s (Fig 5.7), while the bench-press benefit decays over about 4 s (Fig 5.6).\n\n**Related** — [[heuristic--match-plyometric-prescription-to-joint-angle-and-fiber-type]] (Ch1.7, the same Bosco et al 1982 fibre-type finding); [[principle--short-amortization-preserves-elastic-energy]]; [[framework--stretch-reflex-types-and-neuromuscular-control-of-flexibility]]; [[heuristic--plyometric-training-coupling-under-0-15-s-feedforward-and-force-time-pattern]]\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011Cffa3Ki3xhrjLiXMPivw5","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"ffe33652-cfbd-434d-b734-bc3ca836e54e","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:51:40.661Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
