{"parentUuid":"e7bbd01b-a771-4214-a425-2ab2fd80c68f","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cffa5LcBvugaVHounLQWP","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2099,"cache_read_input_tokens":236803,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2099,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":450,"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011Cffa585sKsviBQsAtppC7","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"06a266a3-3562-49ea-a075-9fa128f53b62","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:51:45.084Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"06a266a3-3562-49ea-a075-9fa128f53b62","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cffa5LcBvugaVHounLQWP","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01LpWoqe3hLL2DuFVVsaAQyT","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--plyometric-training-classification-impact-intensity-and-function.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.4\"\ntopic: classes of plyometric training\ncategory: [plyometrics, classification, exercise-selection]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Classes of Plyometric (Powermetric) Training: Impact or Non-Impact, Maximal to Non-Plyometric, Functional or Non-Functional (Fig 5.8)\n\n**What it is** — The authors replace the \"classically plyometric or not\" argument with a classification (PDF pp. 283-284 / printed pp. 272-273; Fig 5.8 verified against the page image):\n- **Impact versus non-impact.** In impact plyometrics \"the rebound is stimulated by contact with a surface or object\"; in non-impact plyometrics the recoil \"is mediated by explosive eccentric action of the muscles that produce a movement not terminated by contact with a surface.\" Depth jumps are impact; \"snap kicks or rapidly-retracted blows in boxing or the martial arts\" are non-impact, which \"may also be maximal or submaximal.\"\n- **Maximal, submaximal, non-plyometric.** *Maximal plyometrics* are \"low-repetition activities where the intensity of the depth jump or rebound exercise is such that maximal or near-maximal rebound tension is produced.\" *Sub-maximal plyometrics* \"do not produce maximal muscular tension, although they are still performed with a minimally long contact phase\": \"lower box heights are used for depth jumps and the athlete does not strive to reach maximal rebound height\"; \"rapid double-footed bouncing or low skipping on the spot\" are other examples. The three are drawn in one box \"as a reminder that there is really a continuum of intensities.\"\n- **Classical, supplementary, preparatory.** Classical plyometric exercises are *functional* (\"matched as closely as possible to the specific explosive actions of the particular sport with respect to movement pattern, muscle involvement, timing and direction of motion\") or *non-functional* (\"general training of the explosive qualities\"). Functional examples: forward bounding for long jump, backward rebounds for shot put, one-legged forward hopping for jumping, and for basketball \"both short and relatively long amortisation phases in vertical rebounds.\" Preparatory or supplementary drills are weight training for muscular strength (\"especially eccentric strength\") and connective tissue, plus jumping, swinging, throwing and catching drills \"with longer duration transition phases\"; \"Most of the drills described in popular books on plyometrics in the West are actually supplementary exercises.\"\n- **Pseudo-plyometrics.** Drills \"incorrectly labelled as plyometric because they include a sudden stop or some form of jump\", e.g. \"'power moves' (involving quick landings and delayed jumps in a semi-squat position)\" and many \"'water plyos'\" in aerobics classes; lacking \"an explosive rebound phase with a short coupling time\", they serve as \"preparatory non-plyometrics.\"\n\nFig 5.8 as drawn: Plyometric training splits into impact and non-impact; below these sit maximal plyometrics, submaximal plyometrics and non-plyometrics; under maximal sits \"classical plyometrics\" (split into functional and non-functional); under submaximal sits \"supplementary plyometrics\"; under non-plyometrics sits \"preparatory/supplementary activities\" (split into weight training and \"jumps throws etc\"). The text lists classical, supplementary and preparatory as three flat categories; the figure ties each to one intensity level.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reason for classifying rather than arguing definitions: the definitional debate \"becomes complex and academic, with no real benefit to the prescription of plyometric training\" (PDF p. 283 / printed p. 272). Intensity is graded by the momentum at contact (see [[heuristic--rate-plyometric-intensity-by-contact-momentum-from-drop-height]]).\n\n**When to apply** — Labelling every jump, bound, hop and med-ball drill in a plan by class, so volume and rest rules are applied to the right class: the authors' low-rep, long-rest rules are for maximal plyometrics, while submaximal and preparatory drills tolerate more volume (see [[heuristic--maximal-plyometric-and-depth-jump-volume-and-rest]]). Inference: in football, a vertical depth jump is classical non-functional, while a bound or single-leg hop that matches a cut or drive phase in direction and timing would be the authors' \"functional\" class.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The text and figure place \"supplementary\" differently (a flat list of three versus a level under submaximal; and \"preparatory or supplementary drills\" in the text against \"supplementary plyometrics\" as its own box in the figure). The class boundaries are a continuum by the authors' own statement, so the labels are not sharp cut-offs.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: depth jumps (impact, maximal when performed for maximal rebound), low box depth jumps without striving for height (submaximal), karate snap kicks (non-impact), \"power moves\" in aerobics (pseudo-plyometric).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--shock-method-and-plyometric-terminology]]; [[heuristic--plyometric-training-coupling-under-0-15-s-feedforward-and-force-time-pattern]]; [[framework--non-impact-plyometrics-ericson-and-martial-arts]]; [[claim--popular-plyometric-drills-work-through-other-mechanisms]]; [[heuristic--maximal-plyometric-and-depth-jump-volume-and-rest]]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2099,"cache_read_input_tokens":236803,"output_tokens":2057,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":9},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2099},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2057,"cache_read_input_tokens":236803,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2099,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2099,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01LpWoqe3hLL2DuFVVsaAQyT":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--plyometric-training-classification-impact-intensity-and-function.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.4\"\ntopic: classes of plyometric training\ncategory: [plyometrics, classification, exercise-selection]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Classes of Plyometric (Powermetric) Training: Impact or Non-Impact, Maximal to Non-Plyometric, Functional or Non-Functional (Fig 5.8)\n\n**What it is** — The authors replace the \"classically plyometric or not\" argument with a classification (PDF pp. 283-284 / printed pp. 272-273; Fig 5.8 verified against the page image):\n- **Impact versus non-impact.** In impact plyometrics \"the rebound is stimulated by contact with a surface or object\"; in non-impact plyometrics the recoil \"is mediated by explosive eccentric action of the muscles that produce a movement not terminated by contact with a surface.\" Depth jumps are impact; \"snap kicks or rapidly-retracted blows in boxing or the martial arts\" are non-impact, which \"may also be maximal or submaximal.\"\n- **Maximal, submaximal, non-plyometric.** *Maximal plyometrics* are \"low-repetition activities where the intensity of the depth jump or rebound exercise is such that maximal or near-maximal rebound tension is produced.\" *Sub-maximal plyometrics* \"do not produce maximal muscular tension, although they are still performed with a minimally long contact phase\": \"lower box heights are used for depth jumps and the athlete does not strive to reach maximal rebound height\"; \"rapid double-footed bouncing or low skipping on the spot\" are other examples. The three are drawn in one box \"as a reminder that there is really a continuum of intensities.\"\n- **Classical, supplementary, preparatory.** Classical plyometric exercises are *functional* (\"matched as closely as possible to the specific explosive actions of the particular sport with respect to movement pattern, muscle involvement, timing and direction of motion\") or *non-functional* (\"general training of the explosive qualities\"). Functional examples: forward bounding for long jump, backward rebounds for shot put, one-legged forward hopping for jumping, and for basketball \"both short and relatively long amortisation phases in vertical rebounds.\" Preparatory or supplementary drills are weight training for muscular strength (\"especially eccentric strength\") and connective tissue, plus jumping, swinging, throwing and catching drills \"with longer duration transition phases\"; \"Most of the drills described in popular books on plyometrics in the West are actually supplementary exercises.\"\n- **Pseudo-plyometrics.** Drills \"incorrectly labelled as plyometric because they include a sudden stop or some form of jump\", e.g. \"'power moves' (involving quick landings and delayed jumps in a semi-squat position)\" and many \"'water plyos'\" in aerobics classes; lacking \"an explosive rebound phase with a short coupling time\", they serve as \"preparatory non-plyometrics.\"\n\nFig 5.8 as drawn: Plyometric training splits into impact and non-impact; below these sit maximal plyometrics, submaximal plyometrics and non-plyometrics; under maximal sits \"classical plyometrics\" (split into functional and non-functional); under submaximal sits \"supplementary plyometrics\"; under non-plyometrics sits \"preparatory/supplementary activities\" (split into weight training and \"jumps throws etc\"). The text lists classical, supplementary and preparatory as three flat categories; the figure ties each to one intensity level.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reason for classifying rather than arguing definitions: the definitional debate \"becomes complex and academic, with no real benefit to the prescription of plyometric training\" (PDF p. 283 / printed p. 272). Intensity is graded by the momentum at contact (see [[heuristic--rate-plyometric-intensity-by-contact-momentum-from-drop-height]]).\n\n**When to apply** — Labelling every jump, bound, hop and med-ball drill in a plan by class, so volume and rest rules are applied to the right class: the authors' low-rep, long-rest rules are for maximal plyometrics, while submaximal and preparatory drills tolerate more volume (see [[heuristic--maximal-plyometric-and-depth-jump-volume-and-rest]]). Inference: in football, a vertical depth jump is classical non-functional, while a bound or single-leg hop that matches a cut or drive phase in direction and timing would be the authors' \"functional\" class.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The text and figure place \"supplementary\" differently (a flat list of three versus a level under submaximal; and \"preparatory or supplementary drills\" in the text against \"supplementary plyometrics\" as its own box in the figure). The class boundaries are a continuum by the authors' own statement, so the labels are not sharp cut-offs.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: depth jumps (impact, maximal when performed for maximal rebound), low box depth jumps without striving for height (submaximal), karate snap kicks (non-impact), \"power moves\" in aerobics (pseudo-plyometric).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--shock-method-and-plyometric-terminology]]; [[heuristic--plyometric-training-coupling-under-0-15-s-feedforward-and-force-time-pattern]]; [[framework--non-impact-plyometrics-ericson-and-martial-arts]]; [[claim--popular-plyometric-drills-work-through-other-mechanisms]]; [[heuristic--maximal-plyometric-and-depth-jump-volume-and-rest]]\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011Cffa585sKsviBQsAtppC7","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"35736cb0-5894-49bd-b740-d770cd561cd4","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:52:01.034Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"35736cb0-5894-49bd-b740-d770cd561cd4","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"624a8cec-33fa-4c0a-a445-c72ce718e6ab","agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LpWoqe3hLL2DuFVVsaAQyT","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--plyometric-training-classification-impact-intensity-and-function.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"7ab0f90e-64f8-4009-9c2e-215b40bd2b7c","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:52:01.138Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"create","filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--plyometric-training-classification-impact-intensity-and-function.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.4\"\ntopic: classes of plyometric training\ncategory: [plyometrics, classification, exercise-selection]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Classes of Plyometric (Powermetric) Training: Impact or Non-Impact, Maximal to Non-Plyometric, Functional or Non-Functional (Fig 5.8)\n\n**What it is** — The authors replace the \"classically plyometric or not\" argument with a classification (PDF pp. 283-284 / printed pp. 272-273; Fig 5.8 verified against the page image):\n- **Impact versus non-impact.** In impact plyometrics \"the rebound is stimulated by contact with a surface or object\"; in non-impact plyometrics the recoil \"is mediated by explosive eccentric action of the muscles that produce a movement not terminated by contact with a surface.\" Depth jumps are impact; \"snap kicks or rapidly-retracted blows in boxing or the martial arts\" are non-impact, which \"may also be maximal or submaximal.\"\n- **Maximal, submaximal, non-plyometric.** *Maximal plyometrics* are \"low-repetition activities where the intensity of the depth jump or rebound exercise is such that maximal or near-maximal rebound tension is produced.\" *Sub-maximal plyometrics* \"do not produce maximal muscular tension, although they are still performed with a minimally long contact phase\": \"lower box heights are used for depth jumps and the athlete does not strive to reach maximal rebound height\"; \"rapid double-footed bouncing or low skipping on the spot\" are other examples. The three are drawn in one box \"as a reminder that there is really a continuum of intensities.\"\n- **Classical, supplementary, preparatory.** Classical plyometric exercises are *functional* (\"matched as closely as possible to the specific explosive actions of the particular sport with respect to movement pattern, muscle involvement, timing and direction of motion\") or *non-functional* (\"general training of the explosive qualities\"). Functional examples: forward bounding for long jump, backward rebounds for shot put, one-legged forward hopping for jumping, and for basketball \"both short and relatively long amortisation phases in vertical rebounds.\" Preparatory or supplementary drills are weight training for muscular strength (\"especially eccentric strength\") and connective tissue, plus jumping, swinging, throwing and catching drills \"with longer duration transition phases\"; \"Most of the drills described in popular books on plyometrics in the West are actually supplementary exercises.\"\n- **Pseudo-plyometrics.** Drills \"incorrectly labelled as plyometric because they include a sudden stop or some form of jump\", e.g. \"'power moves' (involving quick landings and delayed jumps in a semi-squat position)\" and many \"'water plyos'\" in aerobics classes; lacking \"an explosive rebound phase with a short coupling time\", they serve as \"preparatory non-plyometrics.\"\n\nFig 5.8 as drawn: Plyometric training splits into impact and non-impact; below these sit maximal plyometrics, submaximal plyometrics and non-plyometrics; under maximal sits \"classical plyometrics\" (split into functional and non-functional); under submaximal sits \"supplementary plyometrics\"; under non-plyometrics sits \"preparatory/supplementary activities\" (split into weight training and \"jumps throws etc\"). The text lists classical, supplementary and preparatory as three flat categories; the figure ties each to one intensity level.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reason for classifying rather than arguing definitions: the definitional debate \"becomes complex and academic, with no real benefit to the prescription of plyometric training\" (PDF p. 283 / printed p. 272). Intensity is graded by the momentum at contact (see [[heuristic--rate-plyometric-intensity-by-contact-momentum-from-drop-height]]).\n\n**When to apply** — Labelling every jump, bound, hop and med-ball drill in a plan by class, so volume and rest rules are applied to the right class: the authors' low-rep, long-rest rules are for maximal plyometrics, while submaximal and preparatory drills tolerate more volume (see [[heuristic--maximal-plyometric-and-depth-jump-volume-and-rest]]). Inference: in football, a vertical depth jump is classical non-functional, while a bound or single-leg hop that matches a cut or drive phase in direction and timing would be the authors' \"functional\" class.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The text and figure place \"supplementary\" differently (a flat list of three versus a level under submaximal; and \"preparatory or supplementary drills\" in the text against \"supplementary plyometrics\" as its own box in the figure). The class boundaries are a continuum by the authors' own statement, so the labels are not sharp cut-offs.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: depth jumps (impact, maximal when performed for maximal rebound), low box depth jumps without striving for height (submaximal), karate snap kicks (non-impact), \"power moves\" in aerobics (pseudo-plyometric).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--shock-method-and-plyometric-terminology]]; [[heuristic--plyometric-training-coupling-under-0-15-s-feedforward-and-force-time-pattern]]; [[framework--non-impact-plyometrics-ericson-and-martial-arts]]; [[claim--popular-plyometric-drills-work-through-other-mechanisms]]; [[heuristic--maximal-plyometric-and-depth-jump-volume-and-rest]]\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"35736cb0-5894-49bd-b740-d770cd561cd4","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"7ab0f90e-64f8-4009-9c2e-215b40bd2b7c","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","attachment":{"type":"total_tokens_reminder","text":"<total_tokens>14759039 tokens left</total_tokens>"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"10e68f42-bfdc-4bb2-81bc-f73b7f05dbda","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:52:01.146Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\n<total_tokens>14759039 tokens left</total_tokens>\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"10e68f42-bfdc-4bb2-81bc-f73b7f05dbda","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cffa71aWRW9kN7ZhxF4qv","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2181,"cache_read_input_tokens":238902,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2181,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":985,"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011Cffa6dq4aJebmKmbkrdeV","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"956e5815-fb79-4a1f-b036-d832f465b76c","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:52:08.804Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"956e5815-fb79-4a1f-b036-d832f465b76c","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cffa71aWRW9kN7ZhxF4qv","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01B6D34fx9CNBx2wVPZ94u4f","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--rate-plyometric-intensity-by-contact-momentum-from-drop-height.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.4\"\ntopic: plyometric intensity scale\ncategory: [plyometrics, intensity, load-monitoring, biomechanics]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Grade Plyometric Intensity by Momentum at Contact (Set by Drop Height), Since a 1RM Does Not Exist; Prefer Height Over Added Weight\n\n**What it is** — The authors propose \"a *powermetrics intensity scale* similar to that used in weightlifting, where exercises are ranked in terms of percentages of one's 1RM.\" Because \"the concept of 1RMs is meaningless in plyometric training\", one must use estimates of muscle tension or of \"the impulse ... produced during the contact phase\". \"Impulse is equivalent to the change of momentum (m.ΔV) during the amortisation phase, so that its magnitude for a falling body depends on the height from which the body drops. Thus, the momentum just before contact (estimated from height of the drop jump or distance fallen by the object) may serve as a measure of intensity.\" At the top of the scale is maximal plyometrics, \"the equivalent of the emotionally demanding competitive maximum, CFmax\"; at the bottom, preparatory non-impact plyometrics (PDF p. 283 / printed p. 272, verified against the page image).\n\nThe prescription list adds: \"The magnitude of the impulsive load is determined by the weight and the height of its free-fall. The optimal combination is determined empirically in each specific case; however, preference should be given to a large height over a heavy weight. Since the resulting force is governed by Newton's Second Law (F = m.a), a force plate may be used to determine the height of drop to produce the required force\" (PDF p. 285 / printed p. 274, item 1, verified against the page image). Item 4 says rep dosage can be set more precisely \"to calculate the force involved (from bodymass and the drop height) and the athlete's level of special speed-strength\" (same page).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reasoning is the impulse-momentum statement quoted. Inference (physics): for a body falling from rest through height h, speed at contact is √(2gh), so momentum at contact is m·√(2gh). Momentum therefore rises with the square root of drop height, not in proportion to it: doubling the drop height raises contact momentum by a factor of √2, about 41%, and quadrupling it doubles momentum. Inference (physics): the peak force on landing is not fixed by drop height alone; for the same momentum, a shorter, stiffer arrest gives a higher average force (average net force = change in momentum divided by contact time). That is why the authors' force plate step is needed to find the height that gives a target force.\n\n**When to apply** — Progressing depth jumps or rebounds: rank drills by drop height and bodymass rather than counting all \"plyos\" as equal, and raise height before adding external load. Inference: with ForceDecks, a drop jump from a known box gives landing force and contact time for each athlete, which is the measurement the authors' item 1 asks for.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Momentum at contact is an intensity proxy for impact drills with a fall; it does not grade non-impact or ground-based bounding drills where there is no measured drop. The authors give no numeric scale bands (no \"% of CFmax\" per height). Item 1's \"F = m.a\" sentence does not on its own let you compute landing force from height (see the Inference above). Height also has an upper limit: past the optimum the transition slows (see [[heuristic--depth-jump-height-set-by-transition-speed-and-heel-contact]] and [[claim--optimal-depth-jump-drop-height-for-power-vs-force]]).\n\n**Example** — Inference: a 100 kg athlete stepping off a 0.5 m box lands at about √(2 × 9.81 × 0.5) ≈ 3.1 m/s, about 313 kg·m/s of momentum; from a 1.0 m box about 4.4 m/s and about 443 kg·m/s, roughly 41% more, not double. This calculation is not in the source.\n\n**Related** — [[claim--optimal-depth-jump-drop-height-for-power-vs-force]] (Ch4.2.3, the 0.75-1.15 m range); [[framework--kinetic-energy-stimulation-mechanical-basis-and-advantages]]; [[claim--thrown-load-height-decreases-with-load-increases-with-drop-height]] (Ch4.2.3, heavier loads not beneficial); [[framework--plyometric-training-classification-impact-intensity-and-function]]; [[heuristic--rfd-as-correspondence-criterion-and-impulse-cannot-compare-movements]]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2181,"cache_read_input_tokens":238902,"output_tokens":1882,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":118},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2181},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1882,"cache_read_input_tokens":238902,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2181,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2181,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01B6D34fx9CNBx2wVPZ94u4f":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--rate-plyometric-intensity-by-contact-momentum-from-drop-height.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.4\"\ntopic: plyometric intensity scale\ncategory: [plyometrics, intensity, load-monitoring, biomechanics]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Grade Plyometric Intensity by Momentum at Contact (Set by Drop Height), Since a 1RM Does Not Exist; Prefer Height Over Added Weight\n\n**What it is** — The authors propose \"a *powermetrics intensity scale* similar to that used in weightlifting, where exercises are ranked in terms of percentages of one's 1RM.\" Because \"the concept of 1RMs is meaningless in plyometric training\", one must use estimates of muscle tension or of \"the impulse ... produced during the contact phase\". \"Impulse is equivalent to the change of momentum (m.ΔV) during the amortisation phase, so that its magnitude for a falling body depends on the height from which the body drops. Thus, the momentum just before contact (estimated from height of the drop jump or distance fallen by the object) may serve as a measure of intensity.\" At the top of the scale is maximal plyometrics, \"the equivalent of the emotionally demanding competitive maximum, CFmax\"; at the bottom, preparatory non-impact plyometrics (PDF p. 283 / printed p. 272, verified against the page image).\n\nThe prescription list adds: \"The magnitude of the impulsive load is determined by the weight and the height of its free-fall. The optimal combination is determined empirically in each specific case; however, preference should be given to a large height over a heavy weight. Since the resulting force is governed by Newton's Second Law (F = m.a), a force plate may be used to determine the height of drop to produce the required force\" (PDF p. 285 / printed p. 274, item 1, verified against the page image). Item 4 says rep dosage can be set more precisely \"to calculate the force involved (from bodymass and the drop height) and the athlete's level of special speed-strength\" (same page).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reasoning is the impulse-momentum statement quoted. Inference (physics): for a body falling from rest through height h, speed at contact is √(2gh), so momentum at contact is m·√(2gh). Momentum therefore rises with the square root of drop height, not in proportion to it: doubling the drop height raises contact momentum by a factor of √2, about 41%, and quadrupling it doubles momentum. Inference (physics): the peak force on landing is not fixed by drop height alone; for the same momentum, a shorter, stiffer arrest gives a higher average force (average net force = change in momentum divided by contact time). That is why the authors' force plate step is needed to find the height that gives a target force.\n\n**When to apply** — Progressing depth jumps or rebounds: rank drills by drop height and bodymass rather than counting all \"plyos\" as equal, and raise height before adding external load. Inference: with ForceDecks, a drop jump from a known box gives landing force and contact time for each athlete, which is the measurement the authors' item 1 asks for.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Momentum at contact is an intensity proxy for impact drills with a fall; it does not grade non-impact or ground-based bounding drills where there is no measured drop. The authors give no numeric scale bands (no \"% of CFmax\" per height). Item 1's \"F = m.a\" sentence does not on its own let you compute landing force from height (see the Inference above). Height also has an upper limit: past the optimum the transition slows (see [[heuristic--depth-jump-height-set-by-transition-speed-and-heel-contact]] and [[claim--optimal-depth-jump-drop-height-for-power-vs-force]]).\n\n**Example** — Inference: a 100 kg athlete stepping off a 0.5 m box lands at about √(2 × 9.81 × 0.5) ≈ 3.1 m/s, about 313 kg·m/s of momentum; from a 1.0 m box about 4.4 m/s and about 443 kg·m/s, roughly 41% more, not double. This calculation is not in the source.\n\n**Related** — [[claim--optimal-depth-jump-drop-height-for-power-vs-force]] (Ch4.2.3, the 0.75-1.15 m range); [[framework--kinetic-energy-stimulation-mechanical-basis-and-advantages]]; [[claim--thrown-load-height-decreases-with-load-increases-with-drop-height]] (Ch4.2.3, heavier loads not beneficial); [[framework--plyometric-training-classification-impact-intensity-and-function]]; [[heuristic--rfd-as-correspondence-criterion-and-impulse-cannot-compare-movements]]\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011Cffa6dq4aJebmKmbkrdeV","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"d9258e84-480e-49c0-ab5b-8b5b93f3d532","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:52:23.396Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
