{"parentUuid":"8c9d5431-46be-44fb-ab6c-4b9e2a11cf14","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","attachment":{"type":"total_tokens_reminder","text":"<total_tokens>14757033 tokens left</total_tokens>"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"77b4e6ff-e4ce-40fd-adec-661ffcacb4cc","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:52:23.528Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\n<total_tokens>14757033 tokens left</total_tokens>\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"77b4e6ff-e4ce-40fd-adec-661ffcacb4cc","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cffa8nQJH7syhUY6qRENF","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2015,"cache_read_input_tokens":241083,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2015,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":1026,"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011Cffa8Gu28UFtqxXmzsbLW","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"4e705364-cb06-47ee-94a8-e644b040e809","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:52:32.896Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"4e705364-cb06-47ee-94a8-e644b040e809","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cffa8nQJH7syhUY6qRENF","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01ANdq2QkYwid8KVySBBrste","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--maximal-plyometric-and-depth-jump-volume-and-rest.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.4\"\ntopic: depth jump volume and rest\ncategory: [plyometrics, depth-jumps, programming, volume, rest-intervals]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Depth-Jump and Maximal-Plyometric Volume and Rest: The Book Gives Two Incompatible Dosing Models\n\n**What it is** — Section 5.2.4 gives the following dosing statements. They do not agree, so all are listed with locators.\n\n**Model 1: maximal plyometrics dosed like a maximal lift.**\n- \"*Maximal plyometrics* essentially are low-repetition activities ... Just like maximal strength training with weights, these powerful impulses are not meant to be imposed on the musculature every workout, nor are multiple repetitions even possible or advisable. Moreover, a rest interval of 2-4 minutes is recommended between successive repetitions and as much as 10-12 minutes between sets\" (PDF p. 283 / printed p. 272, verified against the page image).\n- Prescription item 6: \"The rest interval between sets of maximal plyometric exercises should be about 10 minutes for power development (strength-speed and speed-strength) and much shorter intervals with submaximal plyometrics to develop explosive-strength endurance\" (PDF p. 285 / printed p. 274, verified).\n- \"several books advocate sessions with over 100 repetitions of plyometric movements ... This volume of *maximal plyometrics* (unlike sub-maximal plyometrics) is neither productive nor safe for any athlete. Maximal plyometrics, like maximal weight lifting, involves single all-out repetitions separated by a few minutes of rest\" (PDF p. 293 / printed p. 282, verified).\n- \"classical plyometrics are 1 RM movements which require as much as several minutes of rest before recovery is complete\" (PDF p. 294 / printed p. 283, text layer).\n\n**Model 2: depth jumps in sets of 5-10 (credited to \"prolific experience\").**\n- Prescription item 4: \"As an initial guideline, the dosage of plyometric exercises should not exceed 5-8 repetitions in one set\" (PDF p. 285 / printed p. 274, verified).\n- Depth-jump item 1: with a complex of vertical jumps (Fig 5.11), \"Each exercise is executed in sets of 10 repetitions, with 1-15 minutes of rest between sets\" (PDF p. 286 / printed p. 275; the page image prints \"1-15\", recorded as printed).\n- Depth-jump item 2: \"The optimal dosage of depth-jumps with an energetic vertical take-off in one training session should not exceed 4 sets of 10 jumps for well-conditioned athletes and 2-3 sets of 5-8 jumps for lesser conditioned athletes. Easy running and relaxation exercises for a period of 10-15 minutes should be done between sets\" (same page, verified).\n- Depth-jump item 3: \"Well-conditioned athletes can execute depth-jumps 3 times per week for 2 sets of 10 jumps at the completion of technical training\" (same page, verified).\n\n**The conflict.** Model 1 says multiple repetitions of maximal plyometrics are not \"possible or advisable\" and separates single reps by 2-4 minutes. Model 2 prescribes sets of 10 energetic depth jumps, up to 40 per session, with rest given only between sets. Model 2 might be read as submaximal work, but depth-jump item 2 specifies \"an energetic vertical take-off\", and the 0.75-1.15 m heights in Ch4 are for advanced jumpers. Rest between sets also varies: about 10 min (p. 274), 10-12 min (p. 272), 10-15 min of easy running (p. 275), and \"1-15 minutes\" (p. 275). The book does not reconcile them.\n\n**Why it works** — Model 1's stated reasons: maximal plyometrics are \"extremely demanding on the central nervous system\" and impose \"great mechanical strain on the tendons, in particular\" (PDF p. 293 / printed p. 282). Model 2's stated basis is \"prolific experience with depth-jumps for developing jumping power\" (PDF p. 286 / printed p. 275). Neither gives fatigue or recovery data.\n\n**When to apply** — Inference: one way to use both is to treat Model 1 as the rule for truly maximal efforts (maximal height, rebound to a marker, near-maximal tension) and Model 2 as an upper volume cap for depth-jump sessions, keeping per-session counts at or under 2-3 × 5-8 for less-conditioned athletes and 4 × 10 for well-conditioned ones. The book does not make this split. For any athlete, the authors' quality rule holds: \"It is the quality of the plyometric exercises, not the quantity, which is important\" (PDF p. 287 / printed p. 276, item 11). Eric's baseline: in-season the stated rule is \"maintain intensity, reduce volume\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 105). Inference: Model 1's low-rep, long-rest structure fits that rule more directly than Model 2's sets of 10.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Do not cite \"4 × 10 depth jumps\" as Supertraining's recommendation without the Model 1 statements beside it, or the reverse. \"1-15 minutes\" may be a misprint (Inference: 1.5 or 10-15 are possible readings), but the page prints 1-15, so do not quote a corrected value. None of these numbers comes with a cited study, population or drop height (except the Ch4 heights for advanced jumpers). The \"over 100 repetitions\" warning targets maximal plyometrics; the authors say submaximal plyometrics differ.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own figures: lesser-conditioned athletes 2-3 sets of 5-8 energetic depth jumps with 10-15 minutes of easy running between sets (PDF p. 286 / printed p. 275), against maximal rebounds as single reps 2-4 minutes apart with 10-12 minutes between sets (PDF p. 283 / printed p. 272).\n\n**Related** — [[heuristic--depth-jump-weekly-frequency-and-seasonal-placement]]; [[claim--optimal-depth-jump-drop-height-for-power-vs-force]]; [[framework--plyometric-training-classification-impact-intensity-and-function]]; [[claim--high-volume-plyometrics-do-not-build-rebound-fatigue-resistance]]; [[framework--resistance-training-prescription-by-goal]]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2015,"cache_read_input_tokens":241083,"output_tokens":2538,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":124},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2015},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2538,"cache_read_input_tokens":241083,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2015,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2015,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01ANdq2QkYwid8KVySBBrste":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--maximal-plyometric-and-depth-jump-volume-and-rest.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.4\"\ntopic: depth jump volume and rest\ncategory: [plyometrics, depth-jumps, programming, volume, rest-intervals]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Depth-Jump and Maximal-Plyometric Volume and Rest: The Book Gives Two Incompatible Dosing Models\n\n**What it is** — Section 5.2.4 gives the following dosing statements. They do not agree, so all are listed with locators.\n\n**Model 1: maximal plyometrics dosed like a maximal lift.**\n- \"*Maximal plyometrics* essentially are low-repetition activities ... Just like maximal strength training with weights, these powerful impulses are not meant to be imposed on the musculature every workout, nor are multiple repetitions even possible or advisable. Moreover, a rest interval of 2-4 minutes is recommended between successive repetitions and as much as 10-12 minutes between sets\" (PDF p. 283 / printed p. 272, verified against the page image).\n- Prescription item 6: \"The rest interval between sets of maximal plyometric exercises should be about 10 minutes for power development (strength-speed and speed-strength) and much shorter intervals with submaximal plyometrics to develop explosive-strength endurance\" (PDF p. 285 / printed p. 274, verified).\n- \"several books advocate sessions with over 100 repetitions of plyometric movements ... This volume of *maximal plyometrics* (unlike sub-maximal plyometrics) is neither productive nor safe for any athlete. Maximal plyometrics, like maximal weight lifting, involves single all-out repetitions separated by a few minutes of rest\" (PDF p. 293 / printed p. 282, verified).\n- \"classical plyometrics are 1 RM movements which require as much as several minutes of rest before recovery is complete\" (PDF p. 294 / printed p. 283, text layer).\n\n**Model 2: depth jumps in sets of 5-10 (credited to \"prolific experience\").**\n- Prescription item 4: \"As an initial guideline, the dosage of plyometric exercises should not exceed 5-8 repetitions in one set\" (PDF p. 285 / printed p. 274, verified).\n- Depth-jump item 1: with a complex of vertical jumps (Fig 5.11), \"Each exercise is executed in sets of 10 repetitions, with 1-15 minutes of rest between sets\" (PDF p. 286 / printed p. 275; the page image prints \"1-15\", recorded as printed).\n- Depth-jump item 2: \"The optimal dosage of depth-jumps with an energetic vertical take-off in one training session should not exceed 4 sets of 10 jumps for well-conditioned athletes and 2-3 sets of 5-8 jumps for lesser conditioned athletes. Easy running and relaxation exercises for a period of 10-15 minutes should be done between sets\" (same page, verified).\n- Depth-jump item 3: \"Well-conditioned athletes can execute depth-jumps 3 times per week for 2 sets of 10 jumps at the completion of technical training\" (same page, verified).\n\n**The conflict.** Model 1 says multiple repetitions of maximal plyometrics are not \"possible or advisable\" and separates single reps by 2-4 minutes. Model 2 prescribes sets of 10 energetic depth jumps, up to 40 per session, with rest given only between sets. Model 2 might be read as submaximal work, but depth-jump item 2 specifies \"an energetic vertical take-off\", and the 0.75-1.15 m heights in Ch4 are for advanced jumpers. Rest between sets also varies: about 10 min (p. 274), 10-12 min (p. 272), 10-15 min of easy running (p. 275), and \"1-15 minutes\" (p. 275). The book does not reconcile them.\n\n**Why it works** — Model 1's stated reasons: maximal plyometrics are \"extremely demanding on the central nervous system\" and impose \"great mechanical strain on the tendons, in particular\" (PDF p. 293 / printed p. 282). Model 2's stated basis is \"prolific experience with depth-jumps for developing jumping power\" (PDF p. 286 / printed p. 275). Neither gives fatigue or recovery data.\n\n**When to apply** — Inference: one way to use both is to treat Model 1 as the rule for truly maximal efforts (maximal height, rebound to a marker, near-maximal tension) and Model 2 as an upper volume cap for depth-jump sessions, keeping per-session counts at or under 2-3 × 5-8 for less-conditioned athletes and 4 × 10 for well-conditioned ones. The book does not make this split. For any athlete, the authors' quality rule holds: \"It is the quality of the plyometric exercises, not the quantity, which is important\" (PDF p. 287 / printed p. 276, item 11). Eric's baseline: in-season the stated rule is \"maintain intensity, reduce volume\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 105). Inference: Model 1's low-rep, long-rest structure fits that rule more directly than Model 2's sets of 10.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Do not cite \"4 × 10 depth jumps\" as Supertraining's recommendation without the Model 1 statements beside it, or the reverse. \"1-15 minutes\" may be a misprint (Inference: 1.5 or 10-15 are possible readings), but the page prints 1-15, so do not quote a corrected value. None of these numbers comes with a cited study, population or drop height (except the Ch4 heights for advanced jumpers). The \"over 100 repetitions\" warning targets maximal plyometrics; the authors say submaximal plyometrics differ.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own figures: lesser-conditioned athletes 2-3 sets of 5-8 energetic depth jumps with 10-15 minutes of easy running between sets (PDF p. 286 / printed p. 275), against maximal rebounds as single reps 2-4 minutes apart with 10-12 minutes between sets (PDF p. 283 / printed p. 272).\n\n**Related** — [[heuristic--depth-jump-weekly-frequency-and-seasonal-placement]]; [[claim--optimal-depth-jump-drop-height-for-power-vs-force]]; [[framework--plyometric-training-classification-impact-intensity-and-function]]; [[claim--high-volume-plyometrics-do-not-build-rebound-fatigue-resistance]]; [[framework--resistance-training-prescription-by-goal]]\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011Cffa8Gu28UFtqxXmzsbLW","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"bb613ce7-a52c-4a7f-935e-79603d1c5671","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:52:51.922Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"bb613ce7-a52c-4a7f-935e-79603d1c5671","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"624a8cec-33fa-4c0a-a445-c72ce718e6ab","agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01ANdq2QkYwid8KVySBBrste","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--maximal-plyometric-and-depth-jump-volume-and-rest.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"873a4a93-3d29-45ff-9f41-d53cace922d6","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:52:52.066Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"create","filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--maximal-plyometric-and-depth-jump-volume-and-rest.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.4\"\ntopic: depth jump volume and rest\ncategory: [plyometrics, depth-jumps, programming, volume, rest-intervals]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Depth-Jump and Maximal-Plyometric Volume and Rest: The Book Gives Two Incompatible Dosing Models\n\n**What it is** — Section 5.2.4 gives the following dosing statements. They do not agree, so all are listed with locators.\n\n**Model 1: maximal plyometrics dosed like a maximal lift.**\n- \"*Maximal plyometrics* essentially are low-repetition activities ... Just like maximal strength training with weights, these powerful impulses are not meant to be imposed on the musculature every workout, nor are multiple repetitions even possible or advisable. Moreover, a rest interval of 2-4 minutes is recommended between successive repetitions and as much as 10-12 minutes between sets\" (PDF p. 283 / printed p. 272, verified against the page image).\n- Prescription item 6: \"The rest interval between sets of maximal plyometric exercises should be about 10 minutes for power development (strength-speed and speed-strength) and much shorter intervals with submaximal plyometrics to develop explosive-strength endurance\" (PDF p. 285 / printed p. 274, verified).\n- \"several books advocate sessions with over 100 repetitions of plyometric movements ... This volume of *maximal plyometrics* (unlike sub-maximal plyometrics) is neither productive nor safe for any athlete. Maximal plyometrics, like maximal weight lifting, involves single all-out repetitions separated by a few minutes of rest\" (PDF p. 293 / printed p. 282, verified).\n- \"classical plyometrics are 1 RM movements which require as much as several minutes of rest before recovery is complete\" (PDF p. 294 / printed p. 283, text layer).\n\n**Model 2: depth jumps in sets of 5-10 (credited to \"prolific experience\").**\n- Prescription item 4: \"As an initial guideline, the dosage of plyometric exercises should not exceed 5-8 repetitions in one set\" (PDF p. 285 / printed p. 274, verified).\n- Depth-jump item 1: with a complex of vertical jumps (Fig 5.11), \"Each exercise is executed in sets of 10 repetitions, with 1-15 minutes of rest between sets\" (PDF p. 286 / printed p. 275; the page image prints \"1-15\", recorded as printed).\n- Depth-jump item 2: \"The optimal dosage of depth-jumps with an energetic vertical take-off in one training session should not exceed 4 sets of 10 jumps for well-conditioned athletes and 2-3 sets of 5-8 jumps for lesser conditioned athletes. Easy running and relaxation exercises for a period of 10-15 minutes should be done between sets\" (same page, verified).\n- Depth-jump item 3: \"Well-conditioned athletes can execute depth-jumps 3 times per week for 2 sets of 10 jumps at the completion of technical training\" (same page, verified).\n\n**The conflict.** Model 1 says multiple repetitions of maximal plyometrics are not \"possible or advisable\" and separates single reps by 2-4 minutes. Model 2 prescribes sets of 10 energetic depth jumps, up to 40 per session, with rest given only between sets. Model 2 might be read as submaximal work, but depth-jump item 2 specifies \"an energetic vertical take-off\", and the 0.75-1.15 m heights in Ch4 are for advanced jumpers. Rest between sets also varies: about 10 min (p. 274), 10-12 min (p. 272), 10-15 min of easy running (p. 275), and \"1-15 minutes\" (p. 275). The book does not reconcile them.\n\n**Why it works** — Model 1's stated reasons: maximal plyometrics are \"extremely demanding on the central nervous system\" and impose \"great mechanical strain on the tendons, in particular\" (PDF p. 293 / printed p. 282). Model 2's stated basis is \"prolific experience with depth-jumps for developing jumping power\" (PDF p. 286 / printed p. 275). Neither gives fatigue or recovery data.\n\n**When to apply** — Inference: one way to use both is to treat Model 1 as the rule for truly maximal efforts (maximal height, rebound to a marker, near-maximal tension) and Model 2 as an upper volume cap for depth-jump sessions, keeping per-session counts at or under 2-3 × 5-8 for less-conditioned athletes and 4 × 10 for well-conditioned ones. The book does not make this split. For any athlete, the authors' quality rule holds: \"It is the quality of the plyometric exercises, not the quantity, which is important\" (PDF p. 287 / printed p. 276, item 11). Eric's baseline: in-season the stated rule is \"maintain intensity, reduce volume\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 105). Inference: Model 1's low-rep, long-rest structure fits that rule more directly than Model 2's sets of 10.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Do not cite \"4 × 10 depth jumps\" as Supertraining's recommendation without the Model 1 statements beside it, or the reverse. \"1-15 minutes\" may be a misprint (Inference: 1.5 or 10-15 are possible readings), but the page prints 1-15, so do not quote a corrected value. None of these numbers comes with a cited study, population or drop height (except the Ch4 heights for advanced jumpers). The \"over 100 repetitions\" warning targets maximal plyometrics; the authors say submaximal plyometrics differ.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own figures: lesser-conditioned athletes 2-3 sets of 5-8 energetic depth jumps with 10-15 minutes of easy running between sets (PDF p. 286 / printed p. 275), against maximal rebounds as single reps 2-4 minutes apart with 10-12 minutes between sets (PDF p. 283 / printed p. 272).\n\n**Related** — [[heuristic--depth-jump-weekly-frequency-and-seasonal-placement]]; [[claim--optimal-depth-jump-drop-height-for-power-vs-force]]; [[framework--plyometric-training-classification-impact-intensity-and-function]]; [[claim--high-volume-plyometrics-do-not-build-rebound-fatigue-resistance]]; [[framework--resistance-training-prescription-by-goal]]\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"bb613ce7-a52c-4a7f-935e-79603d1c5671","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"873a4a93-3d29-45ff-9f41-d53cace922d6","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","attachment":{"type":"total_tokens_reminder","text":"<total_tokens>14754362 tokens left</total_tokens>"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"0c292325-b541-4608-8407-11021738e019","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:52:52.074Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\n<total_tokens>14754362 tokens left</total_tokens>\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"0c292325-b541-4608-8407-11021738e019","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffaAdCTcyPVy9n2f7gw7","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2668,"cache_read_input_tokens":243098,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2668,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":1180,"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CffaAPHZ4m821t8KWySHP","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"d9017eab-46f9-4fac-ad10-ae62d46af700","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:52:57.964Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"d9017eab-46f9-4fac-ad10-ae62d46af700","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffaAdCTcyPVy9n2f7gw7","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01WDQaRe8s8xYMfyJ5dxnxk7","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--depth-jump-weekly-frequency-and-seasonal-placement.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.4\"\ntopic: depth jump frequency and timing\ncategory: [plyometrics, depth-jumps, periodization, in-season]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Depth Jumps: Once or Twice a Week, Kept 3-4 Days From Technique Sessions, Every 10-14 Days In Season and Not Within 10 Days of Competition\n\n**What it is** — From the authors' depth-jump recommendations (PDF pp. 286-287 / printed pp. 275-276, verified against the page images):\n- **Weekly frequency (item 3):** depth jumps \"in the volume indicated should be done once or twice a week in training sessions devoted to special strength training\", which can also include \"localised exercises for other muscle groups and general developmental exercises executed with a small volume.\" But \"Well-conditioned athletes can execute depth-jumps 3 times per week for 2 sets of 10 jumps at the completion of technical training in the particular sport.\"\n- **Spacing from technique (item 4):** \"Depth-jumps strongly excite the nervous system. Therefore, they should be used not less than 3-4 days before a technique training session. Sessions devoted to general physical preparation with a small volume of work should be done after depth-jump workouts.\"\n- **Season (item 5):** \"Depth-jumps occupy a fundamental place in the second half of the preparatory period of the yearly cycle. However, they can function as a means of maintaining the achieved level of special strength fitness in the competition period. During the competition period they should be included once every 10-14 days, but no later than 10 days before a competition.\"\n- **Delayed effect (item 15):** integration \"depends on not disrupting the delayed training effect of prior strength work. It should be noted that the use of depth-jumps as a means of stimulation can delay this effect for 5-6 days.\"\n\n**Internal conflict, recorded:** item 4 keeps depth jumps at least 3-4 days before a technique session, while item 3 allows well-conditioned athletes three depth-jump sessions a week placed \"at the completion of technical training.\" Three sessions a week tied to technical training cannot all sit 3-4 days before the next technique session. Both are on PDF p. 286 / printed p. 275; the book does not reconcile them.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' stated reasons: strong excitation of the nervous system (item 4) and a 5-6 day delay of the training effect of prior strength work (item 15). Neither cites a study. Inference: the in-season 10-day cut-off before competition is consistent with item 15's 5-6 day delayed effect plus a margin; the book does not make that link.\n\n**When to apply** — Placing depth jumps in a football week or year. Eric's baseline: \"In-season, the stated rule is \"maintain intensity, reduce volume\"\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 105). Inference: the authors' in-season rule (one exposure every 10-14 days, none in the 10 days before a game) is far sparser than a weekly game schedule allows; read literally, a team playing every 7 days would have no legal in-season depth-jump day. That is a reason to treat the 10-day rule as written for track-and-field competition calendars, which the book does not say.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — These are experience-based rules with no population, drop height or study given. They are written for an annual cycle with a long preparatory period and spaced competitions (the authors' examples elsewhere in 5.2.4 are track-and-field jumpers). Do not quote the 3-4 day spacing without the item 3 exception. The 5-6 day delay applies to depth jumps used \"as a means of stimulation\"; the book does not say how it scales with volume.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: depth jumps central in the second half of the preparatory period, then once every 10-14 days in the competition period, last one at least 10 days out (PDF p. 286 / printed p. 275).\n\n**Related** — [[heuristic--maximal-plyometric-and-depth-jump-volume-and-rest]]; [[claim--delayed-strength-training-effect-lag-before-gains-appear]] (Ch4.1, the delayed training effect); [[framework--acute-delayed-particular-cumulative-training-effect-taxonomy]]; [[block-periodization--issurin/claim--residual-training-effect-durations-by-motor-ability]]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2668,"cache_read_input_tokens":243098,"output_tokens":1785,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":148},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2668},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1785,"cache_read_input_tokens":243098,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2668,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2668,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01WDQaRe8s8xYMfyJ5dxnxk7":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--depth-jump-weekly-frequency-and-seasonal-placement.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.4\"\ntopic: depth jump frequency and timing\ncategory: [plyometrics, depth-jumps, periodization, in-season]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Depth Jumps: Once or Twice a Week, Kept 3-4 Days From Technique Sessions, Every 10-14 Days In Season and Not Within 10 Days of Competition\n\n**What it is** — From the authors' depth-jump recommendations (PDF pp. 286-287 / printed pp. 275-276, verified against the page images):\n- **Weekly frequency (item 3):** depth jumps \"in the volume indicated should be done once or twice a week in training sessions devoted to special strength training\", which can also include \"localised exercises for other muscle groups and general developmental exercises executed with a small volume.\" But \"Well-conditioned athletes can execute depth-jumps 3 times per week for 2 sets of 10 jumps at the completion of technical training in the particular sport.\"\n- **Spacing from technique (item 4):** \"Depth-jumps strongly excite the nervous system. Therefore, they should be used not less than 3-4 days before a technique training session. Sessions devoted to general physical preparation with a small volume of work should be done after depth-jump workouts.\"\n- **Season (item 5):** \"Depth-jumps occupy a fundamental place in the second half of the preparatory period of the yearly cycle. However, they can function as a means of maintaining the achieved level of special strength fitness in the competition period. During the competition period they should be included once every 10-14 days, but no later than 10 days before a competition.\"\n- **Delayed effect (item 15):** integration \"depends on not disrupting the delayed training effect of prior strength work. It should be noted that the use of depth-jumps as a means of stimulation can delay this effect for 5-6 days.\"\n\n**Internal conflict, recorded:** item 4 keeps depth jumps at least 3-4 days before a technique session, while item 3 allows well-conditioned athletes three depth-jump sessions a week placed \"at the completion of technical training.\" Three sessions a week tied to technical training cannot all sit 3-4 days before the next technique session. Both are on PDF p. 286 / printed p. 275; the book does not reconcile them.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' stated reasons: strong excitation of the nervous system (item 4) and a 5-6 day delay of the training effect of prior strength work (item 15). Neither cites a study. Inference: the in-season 10-day cut-off before competition is consistent with item 15's 5-6 day delayed effect plus a margin; the book does not make that link.\n\n**When to apply** — Placing depth jumps in a football week or year. Eric's baseline: \"In-season, the stated rule is \"maintain intensity, reduce volume\"\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 105). Inference: the authors' in-season rule (one exposure every 10-14 days, none in the 10 days before a game) is far sparser than a weekly game schedule allows; read literally, a team playing every 7 days would have no legal in-season depth-jump day. That is a reason to treat the 10-day rule as written for track-and-field competition calendars, which the book does not say.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — These are experience-based rules with no population, drop height or study given. They are written for an annual cycle with a long preparatory period and spaced competitions (the authors' examples elsewhere in 5.2.4 are track-and-field jumpers). Do not quote the 3-4 day spacing without the item 3 exception. The 5-6 day delay applies to depth jumps used \"as a means of stimulation\"; the book does not say how it scales with volume.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: depth jumps central in the second half of the preparatory period, then once every 10-14 days in the competition period, last one at least 10 days out (PDF p. 286 / printed p. 275).\n\n**Related** — [[heuristic--maximal-plyometric-and-depth-jump-volume-and-rest]]; [[claim--delayed-strength-training-effect-lag-before-gains-appear]] (Ch4.1, the delayed training effect); [[framework--acute-delayed-particular-cumulative-training-effect-taxonomy]]; [[block-periodization--issurin/claim--residual-training-effect-durations-by-motor-ability]]\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffaAPHZ4m821t8KWySHP","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"a27208c6-3474-4316-843d-f5eef2fe821f","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:53:11.078Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"a27208c6-3474-4316-843d-f5eef2fe821f","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"624a8cec-33fa-4c0a-a445-c72ce718e6ab","agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WDQaRe8s8xYMfyJ5dxnxk7","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--depth-jump-weekly-frequency-and-seasonal-placement.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"c0de028d-4e8a-4fdb-a90a-7dc65a5f6cbc","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:53:11.186Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"create","filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--depth-jump-weekly-frequency-and-seasonal-placement.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.4\"\ntopic: depth jump frequency and timing\ncategory: [plyometrics, depth-jumps, periodization, in-season]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Depth Jumps: Once or Twice a Week, Kept 3-4 Days From Technique Sessions, Every 10-14 Days In Season and Not Within 10 Days of Competition\n\n**What it is** — From the authors' depth-jump recommendations (PDF pp. 286-287 / printed pp. 275-276, verified against the page images):\n- **Weekly frequency (item 3):** depth jumps \"in the volume indicated should be done once or twice a week in training sessions devoted to special strength training\", which can also include \"localised exercises for other muscle groups and general developmental exercises executed with a small volume.\" But \"Well-conditioned athletes can execute depth-jumps 3 times per week for 2 sets of 10 jumps at the completion of technical training in the particular sport.\"\n- **Spacing from technique (item 4):** \"Depth-jumps strongly excite the nervous system. Therefore, they should be used not less than 3-4 days before a technique training session. Sessions devoted to general physical preparation with a small volume of work should be done after depth-jump workouts.\"\n- **Season (item 5):** \"Depth-jumps occupy a fundamental place in the second half of the preparatory period of the yearly cycle. However, they can function as a means of maintaining the achieved level of special strength fitness in the competition period. During the competition period they should be included once every 10-14 days, but no later than 10 days before a competition.\"\n- **Delayed effect (item 15):** integration \"depends on not disrupting the delayed training effect of prior strength work. It should be noted that the use of depth-jumps as a means of stimulation can delay this effect for 5-6 days.\"\n\n**Internal conflict, recorded:** item 4 keeps depth jumps at least 3-4 days before a technique session, while item 3 allows well-conditioned athletes three depth-jump sessions a week placed \"at the completion of technical training.\" Three sessions a week tied to technical training cannot all sit 3-4 days before the next technique session. Both are on PDF p. 286 / printed p. 275; the book does not reconcile them.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' stated reasons: strong excitation of the nervous system (item 4) and a 5-6 day delay of the training effect of prior strength work (item 15). Neither cites a study. Inference: the in-season 10-day cut-off before competition is consistent with item 15's 5-6 day delayed effect plus a margin; the book does not make that link.\n\n**When to apply** — Placing depth jumps in a football week or year. Eric's baseline: \"In-season, the stated rule is \"maintain intensity, reduce volume\"\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 105). Inference: the authors' in-season rule (one exposure every 10-14 days, none in the 10 days before a game) is far sparser than a weekly game schedule allows; read literally, a team playing every 7 days would have no legal in-season depth-jump day. That is a reason to treat the 10-day rule as written for track-and-field competition calendars, which the book does not say.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — These are experience-based rules with no population, drop height or study given. They are written for an annual cycle with a long preparatory period and spaced competitions (the authors' examples elsewhere in 5.2.4 are track-and-field jumpers). Do not quote the 3-4 day spacing without the item 3 exception. The 5-6 day delay applies to depth jumps used \"as a means of stimulation\"; the book does not say how it scales with volume.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: depth jumps central in the second half of the preparatory period, then once every 10-14 days in the competition period, last one at least 10 days out (PDF p. 286 / printed p. 275).\n\n**Related** — [[heuristic--maximal-plyometric-and-depth-jump-volume-and-rest]]; [[claim--delayed-strength-training-effect-lag-before-gains-appear]] (Ch4.1, the delayed training effect); [[framework--acute-delayed-particular-cumulative-training-effect-taxonomy]]; [[block-periodization--issurin/claim--residual-training-effect-durations-by-motor-ability]]\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"a27208c6-3474-4316-843d-f5eef2fe821f","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
