ght or take-off height\" (PDF p. 285 / printed p. 274, item 5).\n- \"One should begin at a relatively low height and increase it gradually to the optimal height. It makes sense to begin by jumping upward and forward and only after sufficient training, to jump strictly upward\" (PDF p. 286 / printed p. 275, item 1).\n- Height is preferred over added weight, and a force plate can find the height for a required force (PDF p. 285 / printed p. 274, item 1).\n- For jumping power \"one can work without an additional load and utilise only falling bodymass\" (PDF p. 285 / printed p. 274). Fig 5.10 (Verkhoshansky, 1977) shows three variants: two \"horizontal projection\" depth jumps (jumping forward over obstacles after landing) and one \"vertical projection\" (rebound up to a hanging marker) (PDF p. 286 / printed p. 275, read from the image).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reason is the transition: height is only useful while it raises dynamic force without \"slowing down the transition from eccentric to concentric work\". Heels forced to the ground is their visible sign that momentum has overwhelmed the athlete. Inference: a heel strike means the ankle has been driven through its range, which lengthens the amortisation the authors want kept short; the book states only the rule.\n\n**When to apply** — Inference: with a force plate or contact mat, find each athlete's height by stepping the box up until contact time rises or rebound height falls, and stop below that point; on the field, watch for heels hitting the ground. This individual approach is consistent with the authors' \"determined by the athlete's strength fitness.\" Eric's baseline: the department reads reactive ability through mRSI, which \"was deliberately chosen as the daily athlete-readiness marker\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 74). Inference: a drop-jump RSI (jump height over contact time) taken at stepped heights is the direct test of the authors' \"without slowing down the transition\" rule, but Eric's baseline names CMJ-based mRSI, not drop jumps.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The Ch4.2.3 figures put the optimum for advanced jumpers at 0.75-1.15 m ([[claim--optimal-depth-jump-drop-height-for-power-vs-force]]); the rules here give no number, and the authors stress starting \"considerably lower\" for anyone unused to the training. The heel rule is a coaching sign, not a measured threshold. Upward-and-forward before strictly upward is the authors' order; they give no reason.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: the three Fig 5.10 depth-jump variants, and the progression from upward-and-forward to strictly upward jumps (PDF p. 286 / printed p. 275).\n\n**Related** — [[claim--optimal-depth-jump-drop-height-for-power-vs-force]]; [[framework--reactivity-coefficient-and-depth-jump-test]]; [[heuristic--rate-plyometric-intensity-by-contact-momentum-from-drop-height]]; [[heuristic--depth-jump-execution-cues-landing-gaze-breath]]; [[heuristic--match-plyometric-prescription-to-joint-angle-and-fiber-type]]\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"98804e8c-55a1-4582-82b1-5c7f45e6b109","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
