{"parentUuid":"d185828a-1a38-404b-9eac-702c650ef663","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"624a8cec-33fa-4c0a-a445-c72ce718e6ab","agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018toBMfPTz5PkVdyNbQWng2","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--depth-jump-height-set-by-transition-speed-and-heel-contact.md has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"4b6d161c-858f-4cd8-900e-3891fee9ed0d","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:53:41.195Z","toolUseResult":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--depth-jump-height-set-by-transition-speed-and-heel-contact.md","oldString":"shows three variants: two \"horizontal projection\" depth jumps (jumping forward over obstacles after landing) and one \"vertical projection\" (rebound up to a hanging marker) (PDF p. 286 / printed p. 275, read from the image).","newString":"shows three variants: two under \"Horizontal Projection\" (after the drop, the athlete rebounds forward over low objects drawn on the ground) and one under \"Vertical Projection\" (rebound upward toward a hanging marker) (PDF p. 286 / printed p. 275, read from the image; the drawings are small, so the object details are approximate).","originalFile":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.4\"\ntopic: choosing depth jump height\ncategory: [plyometrics, depth-jumps, progression, coaching-cues]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Set Depth-Jump Height by Strength Fitness: Too High Once the Transition Slows or the Heels Are Driven to the Ground; Raise Speed Before Height\n\n**What it is** — The authors' rules for choosing drop height in training (all on pages verified against the page image):\n- \"The optimal depth of the jump is determined by the athlete's strength fitness, while the athlete should ensure that significant dynamic force is developed without slowing down the transition from eccentric to concentric work by the muscles involved\" (PDF p. 285 / printed p. 274).\n- \"The optimal height of a depth jump does not result in a landing where the heel is forced to the ground by momentum\" (PDF p. 287 / printed p. 276, item 8).\n- \"One should aim to increase the speed and acceleration of movement before increasing the drop height or take-off height\" (PDF p. 285 / printed p. 274, item 5).\n- \"One should begin at a relatively low height and increase it gradually to the optimal height. It makes sense to begin by jumping upward and forward and only after sufficient training, to jump strictly upward\" (PDF p. 286 / printed p. 275, item 1).\n- Height is preferred over added weight, and a force plate can find the height for a required force (PDF p. 285 / printed p. 274, item 1).\n- For jumping power \"one can work without an additional load and utilise only falling bodymass\" (PDF p. 285 / printed p. 274). Fig 5.10 (Verkhoshansky, 1977) shows three variants: two \"horizontal projection\" depth jumps (jumping forward over obstacles after landing) and one \"vertical projection\" (rebound up to a hanging marker) (PDF p. 286 / printed p. 275, read from the image).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reason is the transition: height is only useful while it raises dynamic force without \"slowing down the transition from eccentric to concentric work\". Heels forced to the ground is their visible sign that momentum has overwhelmed the athlete. Inference: a heel strike means the ankle has been driven through its range, which lengthens the amortisation the authors want kept short; the book states only the rule.\n\n**When to apply** — Inference: with a force plate or contact mat, find each athlete's height by stepping the box up until contact time rises or rebound height falls, and stop below that point; on the field, watch for heels hitting the ground. This individual approach is consistent with the authors' \"determined by the athlete's strength fitness.\" Eric's baseline: the department reads reactive ability through mRSI, which \"was deliberately chosen as the daily athlete-readiness marker\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 74). Inference: a drop-jump RSI (jump height over contact time) taken at stepped heights is the direct test of the authors' \"without slowing down the transition\" rule, but Eric's baseline names CMJ-based mRSI, not drop jumps.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The Ch4.2.3 figures put the optimum for advanced jumpers at 0.75-1.15 m ([[claim--optimal-depth-jump-drop-height-for-power-vs-force]]); the rules here give no number, and the authors stress starting \"considerably lower\" for anyone unused to the training. The heel rule is a coaching sign, not a measured threshold. Upward-and-forward before strictly upward is the authors' order; they give no reason.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: the three Fig 5.10 depth-jump variants, and the progression from upward-and-forward to strictly upward jumps (PDF p. 286 / printed p. 275).\n\n**Related** — [[claim--optimal-depth-jump-drop-height-for-power-vs-force]]; [[framework--reactivity-coefficient-and-depth-jump-test]]; [[heuristic--rate-plyometric-intensity-by-contact-momentum-from-drop-height]]; [[heuristic--depth-jump-execution-cues-landing-gaze-breath]]; [[heuristic--match-plyometric-prescription-to-joint-angle-and-fiber-type]]\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":19,"oldLines":7,"newStart":19,"newLines":7,"lines":[" - \"One should aim to increase the speed and acceleration of movement before increasing the drop height or take-off height\" (PDF p. 285 / printed p. 274, item 5)."," - \"One should begin at a relatively low height and increase it gradually to the optimal height. It makes sense to begin by jumping upward and forward and only after sufficient training, to jump strictly upward\" (PDF p. 286 / printed p. 275, item 1)."," - Height is preferred over added weight, and a force plate can find the height for a required force (PDF p. 285 / printed p. 274, item 1).","-- For jumping power \"one can work without an additional load and utilise only falling bodymass\" (PDF p. 285 / printed p. 274). Fig 5.10 (Verkhoshansky, 1977) shows three variants: two \"horizontal projection\" depth jumps (jumping forward over obstacles after landing) and one \"vertical projection\" (rebound up to a hanging marker) (PDF p. 286 / printed p. 275, read from the image).","+- For jumping power \"one can work without an additional load and utilise only falling bodymass\" (PDF p. 285 / printed p. 274). Fig 5.10 (Verkhoshansky, 1977) shows three variants: two under \"Horizontal Projection\" (after the drop, the athlete rebounds forward over low objects drawn on the ground) and one under \"Vertical Projection\" (rebound upward toward a hanging marker) (PDF p. 286 / printed p. 275, read from the image; the drawings are small, so the object details are approximate)."," "," **Why it works** — The authors' reason is the transition: height is only useful while it raises dynamic force without \"slowing down the transition from eccentric to concentric work\". Heels forced to the ground is their visible sign that momentum has overwhelmed the athlete. Inference: a heel strike means the ankle has been driven through its range, which lengthens the amortisation the authors want kept short; the book states only the rule."," "]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"d185828a-1a38-404b-9eac-702c650ef663","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
