{"parentUuid":"41b8c2e4-d3a1-4888-b4bb-858b807c8cee","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","attachment":{"type":"deferred_tools_record","entries":[],"toolInputCopies":[{"id":"toolu_018toBMfPTz5PkVdyNbQWng2","copy":"wire"}]},"type":"attachment","uuid":"918ee44c-ce92-4b3e-b2a5-e0a40d50c166","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:53:49.756Z","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"918ee44c-ce92-4b3e-b2a5-e0a40d50c166","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffaERKwpgKFeLhHsbgwv","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":648,"cache_read_input_tokens":249501,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":648,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":1323,"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CffaE14hYwUGwmB9PKoPC","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"3cb688cf-56b3-4d51-9641-0d20db7c54db","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:53:49.756Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"3cb688cf-56b3-4d51-9641-0d20db7c54db","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffaERKwpgKFeLhHsbgwv","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01SgJMV2s1KbLvx37MkYYAPU","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--depth-jump-execution-cues-landing-gaze-breath.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.4\"\ntopic: depth jump execution technique\ncategory: [plyometrics, depth-jumps, coaching-cues, breathing]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Depth-Jump Execution Cues: Step Off Relaxed, Land on the Balls of the Feet With Shallow Amortisation, Eyes Up, Breath Held Through Contact\n\n**What it is** — The authors' execution rules for depth jumps and plyometric drills (pages verified against the page images unless marked):\n- **Leaving the box:** \"Depth-jumping necessitates falling in a relaxed state, but not jumping, off the box\" (PDF p. 287 / printed p. 276, item 10).\n- **Landing:** \"land with the legs slightly flexed, muscles mildly tensed and on the balls of the feet in order to avoid an excessive shock. The depth of amortisation should not be great and the subsequent take-off needs to be executed very quickly with an energetic throwing upwards of the arms\" (PDF p. 286 / printed p. 275).\n- **Target:** \"strive with the hands or head to reach some height indicated by some marker if the take-off is executed upward, or to land at some point if it is directed upward and forward\"; a gain in height or distance \"clearly indicates improvement in special fitness, which always has a positive effect on the athlete's emotional state\" (same page).\n- **Starting posture:** the amortisation \"should be of minimal duration, but sufficient to create impulsive contraction of the muscles. Therefore, the initial posture with respect to the joint angles should correspond to the same position in which the working movement begins in the sporting exercise\" (PDF p. 285 / printed p. 274, item 2).\n- **Range of motion** in the Fig 5.9 resistance-apparatus exercises \"should be somewhat greater than the required coordination range of the exercise in order to enhance shock absorption by the soft tissues and thereby avoid impact injury\" (PDF p. 285 / printed p. 274).\n- **Head and gaze:** \"Flexing the head downward to look at the floor before take-off may result in spurious muscle contraction or altered timing. Similarly if the head is not thrust upwards during the take-off, maximal height will not be reached\" (PDF p. 287 / printed p. 276, item 9).\n- **Warm-up:** plyometric training \"should be preceded by a good warmup of the intensively involved muscles\" (PDF p. 285 / printed p. 274, item 3).\n- **Breathing:** \"It is vital that the athlete holds the breath during the amortisation and early propulsion phase to stabilise the body, offer pneumatic shock absorption and to increase the rebound force. Forced exhalation may accompany the remainder of the propulsion phase\" (PDF p. 284 / printed p. 273). Item 12: \"the breath should be held and never released during the contact phase or any phase where maximal effort is to be produced. Exhalation during any landing phase reduces overall stability and increases the loading on the spine during depth-jumping\" (PDF p. 287 / printed p. 276).\n\n**Why it works** — Reasons given by the authors are in the quotes: avoiding excessive shock (landing posture), stability and \"pneumatic shock absorption\" (breath), timing (gaze), and shock absorption by soft tissues (range). They cite no study for these cues.\n\n**When to apply** — Coaching any depth-jump or rebound session; the cues are concrete and observable. Inference: the starting-posture rule (item 2) gives a way to make a depth jump more specific to a football stance or a block-start angle by matching the landing knee and hip angle to the sport's start position.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The two breathing statements differ in detail: p. 273 allows \"forced exhalation\" during the remainder of the propulsion phase, which is still ground contact in a rebound, while item 12 says the breath is \"never released during the contact phase.\" Read together, the safe reading is breath held through landing and early push-off. Item 2's \"minimal duration but sufficient\" depth and the Fig 5.9 \"somewhat greater\" range are not quantified. Inference: maximal breath-holding effort raises blood pressure; the book does not discuss this for athletes with cardiovascular conditions.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: jumping up to touch a marker with the hands or head after the depth jump (PDF p. 286 / printed p. 275).\n\n**Related** — [[principle--breath-holding-and-valsalva-support-spinal-stability-in-maximal-lifting]]; [[heuristic--head-and-eye-position-cues-for-barbell-lifts]]; [[heuristic--depth-jump-height-set-by-transition-speed-and-heel-contact]]; [[heuristic--match-plyometric-prescription-to-joint-angle-and-fiber-type]] (Ch1.7, limiting joint angle to keep the \"shock\" loading); [[framework--warmup-mechanisms-and-task-specific-guidelines]]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":648,"cache_read_input_tokens":249501,"output_tokens":2030,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":166},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":648},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2030,"cache_read_input_tokens":249501,"cache_creation_input_tokens":648,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":648,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01SgJMV2s1KbLvx37MkYYAPU":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--depth-jump-execution-cues-landing-gaze-breath.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.4\"\ntopic: depth jump execution technique\ncategory: [plyometrics, depth-jumps, coaching-cues, breathing]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Depth-Jump Execution Cues: Step Off Relaxed, Land on the Balls of the Feet With Shallow Amortisation, Eyes Up, Breath Held Through Contact\n\n**What it is** — The authors' execution rules for depth jumps and plyometric drills (pages verified against the page images unless marked):\n- **Leaving the box:** \"Depth-jumping necessitates falling in a relaxed state, but not jumping, off the box\" (PDF p. 287 / printed p. 276, item 10).\n- **Landing:** \"land with the legs slightly flexed, muscles mildly tensed and on the balls of the feet in order to avoid an excessive shock. The depth of amortisation should not be great and the subsequent take-off needs to be executed very quickly with an energetic throwing upwards of the arms\" (PDF p. 286 / printed p. 275).\n- **Target:** \"strive with the hands or head to reach some height indicated by some marker if the take-off is executed upward, or to land at some point if it is directed upward and forward\"; a gain in height or distance \"clearly indicates improvement in special fitness, which always has a positive effect on the athlete's emotional state\" (same page).\n- **Starting posture:** the amortisation \"should be of minimal duration, but sufficient to create impulsive contraction of the muscles. Therefore, the initial posture with respect to the joint angles should correspond to the same position in which the working movement begins in the sporting exercise\" (PDF p. 285 / printed p. 274, item 2).\n- **Range of motion** in the Fig 5.9 resistance-apparatus exercises \"should be somewhat greater than the required coordination range of the exercise in order to enhance shock absorption by the soft tissues and thereby avoid impact injury\" (PDF p. 285 / printed p. 274).\n- **Head and gaze:** \"Flexing the head downward to look at the floor before take-off may result in spurious muscle contraction or altered timing. Similarly if the head is not thrust upwards during the take-off, maximal height will not be reached\" (PDF p. 287 / printed p. 276, item 9).\n- **Warm-up:** plyometric training \"should be preceded by a good warmup of the intensively involved muscles\" (PDF p. 285 / printed p. 274, item 3).\n- **Breathing:** \"It is vital that the athlete holds the breath during the amortisation and early propulsion phase to stabilise the body, offer pneumatic shock absorption and to increase the rebound force. Forced exhalation may accompany the remainder of the propulsion phase\" (PDF p. 284 / printed p. 273). Item 12: \"the breath should be held and never released during the contact phase or any phase where maximal effort is to be produced. Exhalation during any landing phase reduces overall stability and increases the loading on the spine during depth-jumping\" (PDF p. 287 / printed p. 276).\n\n**Why it works** — Reasons given by the authors are in the quotes: avoiding excessive shock (landing posture), stability and \"pneumatic shock absorption\" (breath), timing (gaze), and shock absorption by soft tissues (range). They cite no study for these cues.\n\n**When to apply** — Coaching any depth-jump or rebound session; the cues are concrete and observable. Inference: the starting-posture rule (item 2) gives a way to make a depth jump more specific to a football stance or a block-start angle by matching the landing knee and hip angle to the sport's start position.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The two breathing statements differ in detail: p. 273 allows \"forced exhalation\" during the remainder of the propulsion phase, which is still ground contact in a rebound, while item 12 says the breath is \"never released during the contact phase.\" Read together, the safe reading is breath held through landing and early push-off. Item 2's \"minimal duration but sufficient\" depth and the Fig 5.9 \"somewhat greater\" range are not quantified. Inference: maximal breath-holding effort raises blood pressure; the book does not discuss this for athletes with cardiovascular conditions.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: jumping up to touch a marker with the hands or head after the depth jump (PDF p. 286 / printed p. 275).\n\n**Related** — [[principle--breath-holding-and-valsalva-support-spinal-stability-in-maximal-lifting]]; [[heuristic--head-and-eye-position-cues-for-barbell-lifts]]; [[heuristic--depth-jump-height-set-by-transition-speed-and-heel-contact]]; [[heuristic--match-plyometric-prescription-to-joint-angle-and-fiber-type]] (Ch1.7, limiting joint angle to keep the \"shock\" loading); [[framework--warmup-mechanisms-and-task-specific-guidelines]]\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffaE14hYwUGwmB9PKoPC","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"9f66d840-8768-43e9-8379-5bbbb14ad16e","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:54:04.025Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"9f66d840-8768-43e9-8379-5bbbb14ad16e","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"624a8cec-33fa-4c0a-a445-c72ce718e6ab","agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SgJMV2s1KbLvx37MkYYAPU","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--depth-jump-execution-cues-landing-gaze-breath.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"036403f6-fd9f-4cd0-9b7e-c774b3e66f23","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:54:04.129Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"create","filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--depth-jump-execution-cues-landing-gaze-breath.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.4\"\ntopic: depth jump execution technique\ncategory: [plyometrics, depth-jumps, coaching-cues, breathing]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Depth-Jump Execution Cues: Step Off Relaxed, Land on the Balls of the Feet With Shallow Amortisation, Eyes Up, Breath Held Through Contact\n\n**What it is** — The authors' execution rules for depth jumps and plyometric drills (pages verified against the page images unless marked):\n- **Leaving the box:** \"Depth-jumping necessitates falling in a relaxed state, but not jumping, off the box\" (PDF p. 287 / printed p. 276, item 10).\n- **Landing:** \"land with the legs slightly flexed, muscles mildly tensed and on the balls of the feet in order to avoid an excessive shock. The depth of amortisation should not be great and the subsequent take-off needs to be executed very quickly with an energetic throwing upwards of the arms\" (PDF p. 286 / printed p. 275).\n- **Target:** \"strive with the hands or head to reach some height indicated by some marker if the take-off is executed upward, or to land at some point if it is directed upward and forward\"; a gain in height or distance \"clearly indicates improvement in special fitness, which always has a positive effect on the athlete's emotional state\" (same page).\n- **Starting posture:** the amortisation \"should be of minimal duration, but sufficient to create impulsive contraction of the muscles. Therefore, the initial posture with respect to the joint angles should correspond to the same position in which the working movement begins in the sporting exercise\" (PDF p. 285 / printed p. 274, item 2).\n- **Range of motion** in the Fig 5.9 resistance-apparatus exercises \"should be somewhat greater than the required coordination range of the exercise in order to enhance shock absorption by the soft tissues and thereby avoid impact injury\" (PDF p. 285 / printed p. 274).\n- **Head and gaze:** \"Flexing the head downward to look at the floor before take-off may result in spurious muscle contraction or altered timing. Similarly if the head is not thrust upwards during the take-off, maximal height will not be reached\" (PDF p. 287 / printed p. 276, item 9).\n- **Warm-up:** plyometric training \"should be preceded by a good warmup of the intensively involved muscles\" (PDF p. 285 / printed p. 274, item 3).\n- **Breathing:** \"It is vital that the athlete holds the breath during the amortisation and early propulsion phase to stabilise the body, offer pneumatic shock absorption and to increase the rebound force. Forced exhalation may accompany the remainder of the propulsion phase\" (PDF p. 284 / printed p. 273). Item 12: \"the breath should be held and never released during the contact phase or any phase where maximal effort is to be produced. Exhalation during any landing phase reduces overall stability and increases the loading on the spine during depth-jumping\" (PDF p. 287 / printed p. 276).\n\n**Why it works** — Reasons given by the authors are in the quotes: avoiding excessive shock (landing posture), stability and \"pneumatic shock absorption\" (breath), timing (gaze), and shock absorption by soft tissues (range). They cite no study for these cues.\n\n**When to apply** — Coaching any depth-jump or rebound session; the cues are concrete and observable. Inference: the starting-posture rule (item 2) gives a way to make a depth jump more specific to a football stance or a block-start angle by matching the landing knee and hip angle to the sport's start position.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The two breathing statements differ in detail: p. 273 allows \"forced exhalation\" during the remainder of the propulsion phase, which is still ground contact in a rebound, while item 12 says the breath is \"never released during the contact phase.\" Read together, the safe reading is breath held through landing and early push-off. Item 2's \"minimal duration but sufficient\" depth and the Fig 5.9 \"somewhat greater\" range are not quantified. Inference: maximal breath-holding effort raises blood pressure; the book does not discuss this for athletes with cardiovascular conditions.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: jumping up to touch a marker with the hands or head after the depth jump (PDF p. 286 / printed p. 275).\n\n**Related** — [[principle--breath-holding-and-valsalva-support-spinal-stability-in-maximal-lifting]]; [[heuristic--head-and-eye-position-cues-for-barbell-lifts]]; [[heuristic--depth-jump-height-set-by-transition-speed-and-heel-contact]]; [[heuristic--match-plyometric-prescription-to-joint-angle-and-fiber-type]] (Ch1.7, limiting joint angle to keep the \"shock\" loading); [[framework--warmup-mechanisms-and-task-specific-guidelines]]\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"9f66d840-8768-43e9-8379-5bbbb14ad16e","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
