{"parentUuid":"8b19e30a-ade2-42d3-9ecc-c9666f089a3d","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"624a8cec-33fa-4c0a-a445-c72ce718e6ab","agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DLwktBQ7Y72uV38Z2qQmYu","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--depth-jump-execution-cues-landing-gaze-breath.md has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"e3889fbf-53bc-4562-8d9d-7cef8e9726ba","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:54:15.390Z","toolUseResult":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--depth-jump-execution-cues-landing-gaze-breath.md","oldString":"Read together, the safe reading","newString":"Inference: read together, the cautious reading","originalFile":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.4\"\ntopic: depth jump execution technique\ncategory: [plyometrics, depth-jumps, coaching-cues, breathing]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Depth-Jump Execution Cues: Step Off Relaxed, Land on the Balls of the Feet With Shallow Amortisation, Eyes Up, Breath Held Through Contact\n\n**What it is** — The authors' execution rules for depth jumps and plyometric drills (pages verified against the page images unless marked):\n- **Leaving the box:** \"Depth-jumping necessitates falling in a relaxed state, but not jumping, off the box\" (PDF p. 287 / printed p. 276, item 10).\n- **Landing:** \"land with the legs slightly flexed, muscles mildly tensed and on the balls of the feet in order to avoid an excessive shock. The depth of amortisation should not be great and the subsequent take-off needs to be executed very quickly with an energetic throwing upwards of the arms\" (PDF p. 286 / printed p. 275).\n- **Target:** \"strive with the hands or head to reach some height indicated by some marker if the take-off is executed upward, or to land at some point if it is directed upward and forward\"; a gain in height or distance \"clearly indicates improvement in special fitness, which always has a positive effect on the athlete's emotional state\" (same page).\n- **Starting posture:** the amortisation \"should be of minimal duration, but sufficient to create impulsive contraction of the muscles. Therefore, the initial posture with respect to the joint angles should correspond to the same position in which the working movement begins in the sporting exercise\" (PDF p. 285 / printed p. 274, item 2).\n- **Range of motion** in the Fig 5.9 resistance-apparatus exercises \"should be somewhat greater than the required coordination range of the exercise in order to enhance shock absorption by the soft tissues and thereby avoid impact injury\" (PDF p. 285 / printed p. 274).\n- **Head and gaze:** \"Flexing the head downward to look at the floor before take-off may result in spurious muscle contraction or altered timing. Similarly if the head is not thrust upwards during the take-off, maximal height will not be reached\" (PDF p. 287 / printed p. 276, item 9).\n- **Warm-up:** plyometric training \"should be preceded by a good warmup of the intensively involved muscles\" (PDF p. 285 / printed p. 274, item 3).\n- **Breathing:** \"It is vital that the athlete holds the breath during the amortisation and early propulsion phase to stabilise the body, offer pneumatic shock absorption and to increase the rebound force. Forced exhalation may accompany the remainder of the propulsion phase\" (PDF p. 284 / printed p. 273). Item 12: \"the breath should be held and never released during the contact phase or any phase where maximal effort is to be produced. Exhalation during any landing phase reduces overall stability and increases the loading on the spine during depth-jumping\" (PDF p. 287 / printed p. 276).\n\n**Why it works** — Reasons given by the authors are in the quotes: avoiding excessive shock (landing posture), stability and \"pneumatic shock absorption\" (breath), timing (gaze), and shock absorption by soft tissues (range). They cite no study for these cues.\n\n**When to apply** — Coaching any depth-jump or rebound session; the cues are concrete and observable. Inference: the starting-posture rule (item 2) gives a way to make a depth jump more specific to a football stance or a block-start angle by matching the landing knee and hip angle to the sport's start position.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The two breathing statements differ in detail: p. 273 allows \"forced exhalation\" during the remainder of the propulsion phase, which is still ground contact in a rebound, while item 12 says the breath is \"never released during the contact phase.\" Read together, the safe reading is breath held through landing and early push-off. Item 2's \"minimal duration but sufficient\" depth and the Fig 5.9 \"somewhat greater\" range are not quantified. Inference: maximal breath-holding effort raises blood pressure; the book does not discuss this for athletes with cardiovascular conditions.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: jumping up to touch a marker with the hands or head after the depth jump (PDF p. 286 / printed p. 275).\n\n**Related** — [[principle--breath-holding-and-valsalva-support-spinal-stability-in-maximal-lifting]]; [[heuristic--head-and-eye-position-cues-for-barbell-lifts]]; [[heuristic--depth-jump-height-set-by-transition-speed-and-heel-contact]]; [[heuristic--match-plyometric-prescription-to-joint-angle-and-fiber-type]] (Ch1.7, limiting joint angle to keep the \"shock\" loading); [[framework--warmup-mechanisms-and-task-specific-guidelines]]\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":27,"oldLines":7,"newStart":27,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," **When to apply** — Coaching any depth-jump or rebound session; the cues are concrete and observable. Inference: the starting-posture rule (item 2) gives a way to make a depth jump more specific to a football stance or a block-start angle by matching the landing knee and hip angle to the sport's start position."," ","-**When NOT to apply** — The two breathing statements differ in detail: p. 273 allows \"forced exhalation\" during the remainder of the propulsion phase, which is still ground contact in a rebound, while item 12 says the breath is \"never released during the contact phase.\" Read together, the safe reading is breath held through landing and early push-off. Item 2's \"minimal duration but sufficient\" depth and the Fig 5.9 \"somewhat greater\" range are not quantified. Inference: maximal breath-holding effort raises blood pressure; the book does not discuss this for athletes with cardiovascular conditions.","+**When NOT to apply** — The two breathing statements differ in detail: p. 273 allows \"forced exhalation\" during the remainder of the propulsion phase, which is still ground contact in a rebound, while item 12 says the breath is \"never released during the contact phase.\" Inference: read together, the cautious reading is breath held through landing and early push-off. Item 2's \"minimal duration but sufficient\" depth and the Fig 5.9 \"somewhat greater\" range are not quantified. Inference: maximal breath-holding effort raises blood pressure; the book does not discuss this for athletes with cardiovascular conditions."," "," **Example** — The authors' own: jumping up to touch a marker with the hands or head after the depth jump (PDF p. 286 / printed p. 275)."," "]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"8b19e30a-ade2-42d3-9ecc-c9666f089a3d","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"e3889fbf-53bc-4562-8d9d-7cef8e9726ba","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffaGGXfehmci7hqmYqmF","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01CwUe6goPT38LoaLXqU1XsS","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--preparatory-progression-before-depth-jumps.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.4\"\ntopic: readiness and preparation for depth jumps\ncategory: [plyometrics, depth-jumps, progression, readiness, eccentric-strength]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Before Depth Jumps: Months of Barbell, Jumping, Sprint and Skipping Work, Eccentric and Explosive-Isometric Strength, Static-Start Jumps, Then Low Hops\n\n**What it is** — The authors' preparation rules (pages verified against the page images):\n- \"Depth-jumps require special preliminary preparation such as a significant volume of barbell exercises and traditional jumping, short sprints and skipping over a period of several months\" (PDF p. 286 / printed p. 275, item 1). Contra-indications: \"Muscle or tendon fatigue, pain or soreness, as well as incompletely healed injuries\" (same item).\n- \"depth-jumping is not a basic type of training to be indulged in lightly by athletes who are relatively unused to explosive or strongly resisted movements. For anyone who is not accustomed to movements such as weightlifting cleans, jerks, squats or hurdling, it is highly advisable that a preparatory programme using submaximal plyometrics, non-impact and non-plyometrics be followed\" first (PDF p. 287 / printed p. 276).\n- **Eccentric and explosive-isometric strength first:** \"a primary requirement for anyone contemplating the use of classical plyometrics is a high level of eccentric strength and explosive isometric strength. Herein lies the value of small-range squats, power cleans, power snatches, cleans from the hang, snatches from the hang, push presses or jerks, push presses or jerks from racks, and the descent of flights of stairs.\" Front and back squats both, \"since they each model different types of jump (directly upwards versus forwards and upwards).\" \"Slow quasi-isometric squats over a moderate range with heavy weights should later lead to more rapid dips over a shallower range with lighter weights\" (PDF pp. 287-288 / printed pp. 276-277).\n- **Static start before dynamic start:** \"it is initially useful to improve jumping ability from a static start, without exposing oneself to the greater musculotendinous stress of a dynamic start\": standing broad and vertical jumps without a dip, then the vertical jump test \"progressing from a static flexed knee start to a dynamic dip start with arms flung vigorously upwards\" (PDF p. 288 / printed p. 277). Progress can be tracked with the Lewis formula ([[claim--lewis-formula-estimates-jump-power-from-bodymass-and-height]]).\n- **Low hops:** \"hopping on the spot at a frequency which feels most comfortable for the athlete, using a few sets of about 25 repetitions\", then small forward, backward, sideward, forward-backward and zig-zag hops, then vertical hops \"as rapidly as possible, again using a few sets of about 20 repetitions\" (PDF p. 286 / printed p. 275, item 6). Rope skipping is useful, but \"long periods of skipping are not the appropriate form of preparatory drill\" because the aim is speed-strength, not endurance (item 7).\n- **Technique and complexity:** learn normal jumping first, with \"the correct lumbar-pelvic rhythm, as is also used during clean pulls or power cleans from the ground\" (PDF p. 287 / printed p. 276, item 13). \"Great caution\" with depth jumps involving \"one-legged thrusts, changes of direction or complex variations, since the injury risk is significantly higher\" (item 14).\n- Then integrate: \"As eccentric, isometric and concentric strength increases ... the athlete may progress to actions which more rapidly integrate all three aspects of plyometric movement\"; the same principles apply to upper-body work (medicine ball catching and throwing, plyometric press-ups and bench presses) (PDF p. 288 / printed p. 277).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors derive the preparation from the action itself: because plyometric actions involve \"a powerful eccentric amortisation phase terminating in an explosive isometric contraction\", eccentric and explosive-isometric strength come first; because the concentric phase is reflex-assisted, static-start jumping builds the concentric part \"without exposing oneself to the greater musculotendinous stress of a dynamic start\" (PDF pp. 287-288 / printed pp. 276-277).\n\n**When to apply** — Freshmen, return-to-training athletes, or anyone without a lifting and jumping base. Eric's baseline: the Vermeil hierarchy (work capacity, then strength, then power, then speed, then sport skill, \"each level a required foundation for the next\") is the department's programming \"compass\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 101). Inference: the authors' sequence (strength base, static jumps, low hops, then depth jumps) is the same ordering applied inside the power level. Inference: single-leg and change-of-direction depth jumps, the most football-like variants, are the ones the authors flag as highest risk, so they come last.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — \"Several months\" is not quantified, and no strength threshold is given here (for the 1.5-2 × bodymass squat rule, see [[claim--squat-1-5-to-2-bodymass-rule-applies-to-maximal-depth-jumps-only]]). Inference: athletes with a long sporting history of running, jumping and lifting may already meet much of this preparation; the authors also say an athlete's sport may already supply most plyometric loading ([[claim--plyometric-injury-risk-comes-from-prescription-not-the-method]]).\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: hopping on the spot in a few sets of about 25, moving to multi-directional hops, then fast vertical hops in sets of about 20 (PDF p. 286 / printed p. 275).\n\n**Related** — [[heuristic--depth-jump-height-set-by-transition-speed-and-heel-contact]]; [[heuristic--depth-jump-execution-cues-landing-gaze-breath]]; [[framework--plyometric-training-classification-impact-intensity-and-function]]; [[heuristic--eccentric-quasi-isometrics-for-active-flexibility-and-starting-strength]]; [[framework--strength-deficit-jump-comparison-estimate]]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2157,"cache_read_input_tokens":250149,"output_tokens":2592,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":97},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2157},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2592,"cache_read_input_tokens":250149,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2157,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2157,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01CwUe6goPT38LoaLXqU1XsS":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--preparatory-progression-before-depth-jumps.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.4\"\ntopic: readiness and preparation for depth jumps\ncategory: [plyometrics, depth-jumps, progression, readiness, eccentric-strength]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Before Depth Jumps: Months of Barbell, Jumping, Sprint and Skipping Work, Eccentric and Explosive-Isometric Strength, Static-Start Jumps, Then Low Hops\n\n**What it is** — The authors' preparation rules (pages verified against the page images):\n- \"Depth-jumps require special preliminary preparation such as a significant volume of barbell exercises and traditional jumping, short sprints and skipping over a period of several months\" (PDF p. 286 / printed p. 275, item 1). Contra-indications: \"Muscle or tendon fatigue, pain or soreness, as well as incompletely healed injuries\" (same item).\n- \"depth-jumping is not a basic type of training to be indulged in lightly by athletes who are relatively unused to explosive or strongly resisted movements. For anyone who is not accustomed to movements such as weightlifting cleans, jerks, squats or hurdling, it is highly advisable that a preparatory programme using submaximal plyometrics, non-impact and non-plyometrics be followed\" first (PDF p. 287 / printed p. 276).\n- **Eccentric and explosive-isometric strength first:** \"a primary requirement for anyone contemplating the use of classical plyometrics is a high level of eccentric strength and explosive isometric strength. Herein lies the value of small-range squats, power cleans, power snatches, cleans from the hang, snatches from the hang, push presses or jerks, push presses or jerks from racks, and the descent of flights of stairs.\" Front and back squats both, \"since they each model different types of jump (directly upwards versus forwards and upwards).\" \"Slow quasi-isometric squats over a moderate range with heavy weights should later lead to more rapid dips over a shallower range with lighter weights\" (PDF pp. 287-288 / printed pp. 276-277).\n- **Static start before dynamic start:** \"it is initially useful to improve jumping ability from a static start, without exposing oneself to the greater musculotendinous stress of a dynamic start\": standing broad and vertical jumps without a dip, then the vertical jump test \"progressing from a static flexed knee start to a dynamic dip start with arms flung vigorously upwards\" (PDF p. 288 / printed p. 277). Progress can be tracked with the Lewis formula ([[claim--lewis-formula-estimates-jump-power-from-bodymass-and-height]]).\n- **Low hops:** \"hopping on the spot at a frequency which feels most comfortable for the athlete, using a few sets of about 25 repetitions\", then small forward, backward, sideward, forward-backward and zig-zag hops, then vertical hops \"as rapidly as possible, again using a few sets of about 20 repetitions\" (PDF p. 286 / printed p. 275, item 6). Rope skipping is useful, but \"long periods of skipping are not the appropriate form of preparatory drill\" because the aim is speed-strength, not endurance (item 7).\n- **Technique and complexity:** learn normal jumping first, with \"the correct lumbar-pelvic rhythm, as is also used during clean pulls or power cleans from the ground\" (PDF p. 287 / printed p. 276, item 13). \"Great caution\" with depth jumps involving \"one-legged thrusts, changes of direction or complex variations, since the injury risk is significantly higher\" (item 14).\n- Then integrate: \"As eccentric, isometric and concentric strength increases ... the athlete may progress to actions which more rapidly integrate all three aspects of plyometric movement\"; the same principles apply to upper-body work (medicine ball catching and throwing, plyometric press-ups and bench presses) (PDF p. 288 / printed p. 277).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors derive the preparation from the action itself: because plyometric actions involve \"a powerful eccentric amortisation phase terminating in an explosive isometric contraction\", eccentric and explosive-isometric strength come first; because the concentric phase is reflex-assisted, static-start jumping builds the concentric part \"without exposing oneself to the greater musculotendinous stress of a dynamic start\" (PDF pp. 287-288 / printed pp. 276-277).\n\n**When to apply** — Freshmen, return-to-training athletes, or anyone without a lifting and jumping base. Eric's baseline: the Vermeil hierarchy (work capacity, then strength, then power, then speed, then sport skill, \"each level a required foundation for the next\") is the department's programming \"compass\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 101). Inference: the authors' sequence (strength base, static jumps, low hops, then depth jumps) is the same ordering applied inside the power level. Inference: single-leg and change-of-direction depth jumps, the most football-like variants, are the ones the authors flag as highest risk, so they come last.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — \"Several months\" is not quantified, and no strength threshold is given here (for the 1.5-2 × bodymass squat rule, see [[claim--squat-1-5-to-2-bodymass-rule-applies-to-maximal-depth-jumps-only]]). Inference: athletes with a long sporting history of running, jumping and lifting may already meet much of this preparation; the authors also say an athlete's sport may already supply most plyometric loading ([[claim--plyometric-injury-risk-comes-from-prescription-not-the-method]]).\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: hopping on the spot in a few sets of about 25, moving to multi-directional hops, then fast vertical hops in sets of about 20 (PDF p. 286 / printed p. 275).\n\n**Related** — [[heuristic--depth-jump-height-set-by-transition-speed-and-heel-contact]]; [[heuristic--depth-jump-execution-cues-landing-gaze-breath]]; [[framework--plyometric-training-classification-impact-intensity-and-function]]; [[heuristic--eccentric-quasi-isometrics-for-active-flexibility-and-starting-strength]]; [[framework--strength-deficit-jump-comparison-estimate]]\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011CffaFgwacK7fYbHqANNUk","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"92f04473-43fc-4ef1-af47-51c9fc202217","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:54:32.410Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"92f04473-43fc-4ef1-af47-51c9fc202217","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"624a8cec-33fa-4c0a-a445-c72ce718e6ab","agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CwUe6goPT38LoaLXqU1XsS","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--preparatory-progression-before-depth-jumps.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"a61d1967-9f18-41f9-adf7-2cd583b2fb48","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:54:32.538Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"create","filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--preparatory-progression-before-depth-jumps.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.4\"\ntopic: readiness and preparation for depth jumps\ncategory: [plyometrics, depth-jumps, progression, readiness, eccentric-strength]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Before Depth Jumps: Months of Barbell, Jumping, Sprint and Skipping Work, Eccentric and Explosive-Isometric Strength, Static-Start Jumps, Then Low Hops\n\n**What it is** — The authors' preparation rules (pages verified against the page images):\n- \"Depth-jumps require special preliminary preparation such as a significant volume of barbell exercises and traditional jumping, short sprints and skipping over a period of several months\" (PDF p. 286 / printed p. 275, item 1). Contra-indications: \"Muscle or tendon fatigue, pain or soreness, as well as incompletely healed injuries\" (same item).\n- \"depth-jumping is not a basic type of training to be indulged in lightly by athletes who are relatively unused to explosive or strongly resisted movements. For anyone who is not accustomed to movements such as weightlifting cleans, jerks, squats or hurdling, it is highly advisable that a preparatory programme using submaximal plyometrics, non-impact and non-plyometrics be followed\" first (PDF p. 287 / printed p. 276).\n- **Eccentric and explosive-isometric strength first:** \"a primary requirement for anyone contemplating the use of classical plyometrics is a high level of eccentric strength and explosive isometric strength. Herein lies the value of small-range squats, power cleans, power snatches, cleans from the hang, snatches from the hang, push presses or jerks, push presses or jerks from racks, and the descent of flights of stairs.\" Front and back squats both, \"since they each model different types of jump (directly upwards versus forwards and upwards).\" \"Slow quasi-isometric squats over a moderate range with heavy weights should later lead to more rapid dips over a shallower range with lighter weights\" (PDF pp. 287-288 / printed pp. 276-277).\n- **Static start before dynamic start:** \"it is initially useful to improve jumping ability from a static start, without exposing oneself to the greater musculotendinous stress of a dynamic start\": standing broad and vertical jumps without a dip, then the vertical jump test \"progressing from a static flexed knee start to a dynamic dip start with arms flung vigorously upwards\" (PDF p. 288 / printed p. 277). Progress can be tracked with the Lewis formula ([[claim--lewis-formula-estimates-jump-power-from-bodymass-and-height]]).\n- **Low hops:** \"hopping on the spot at a frequency which feels most comfortable for the athlete, using a few sets of about 25 repetitions\", then small forward, backward, sideward, forward-backward and zig-zag hops, then vertical hops \"as rapidly as possible, again using a few sets of about 20 repetitions\" (PDF p. 286 / printed p. 275, item 6). Rope skipping is useful, but \"long periods of skipping are not the appropriate form of preparatory drill\" because the aim is speed-strength, not endurance (item 7).\n- **Technique and complexity:** learn normal jumping first, with \"the correct lumbar-pelvic rhythm, as is also used during clean pulls or power cleans from the ground\" (PDF p. 287 / printed p. 276, item 13). \"Great caution\" with depth jumps involving \"one-legged thrusts, changes of direction or complex variations, since the injury risk is significantly higher\" (item 14).\n- Then integrate: \"As eccentric, isometric and concentric strength increases ... the athlete may progress to actions which more rapidly integrate all three aspects of plyometric movement\"; the same principles apply to upper-body work (medicine ball catching and throwing, plyometric press-ups and bench presses) (PDF p. 288 / printed p. 277).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors derive the preparation from the action itself: because plyometric actions involve \"a powerful eccentric amortisation phase terminating in an explosive isometric contraction\", eccentric and explosive-isometric strength come first; because the concentric phase is reflex-assisted, static-start jumping builds the concentric part \"without exposing oneself to the greater musculotendinous stress of a dynamic start\" (PDF pp. 287-288 / printed pp. 276-277).\n\n**When to apply** — Freshmen, return-to-training athletes, or anyone without a lifting and jumping base. Eric's baseline: the Vermeil hierarchy (work capacity, then strength, then power, then speed, then sport skill, \"each level a required foundation for the next\") is the department's programming \"compass\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 101). Inference: the authors' sequence (strength base, static jumps, low hops, then depth jumps) is the same ordering applied inside the power level. Inference: single-leg and change-of-direction depth jumps, the most football-like variants, are the ones the authors flag as highest risk, so they come last.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — \"Several months\" is not quantified, and no strength threshold is given here (for the 1.5-2 × bodymass squat rule, see [[claim--squat-1-5-to-2-bodymass-rule-applies-to-maximal-depth-jumps-only]]). Inference: athletes with a long sporting history of running, jumping and lifting may already meet much of this preparation; the authors also say an athlete's sport may already supply most plyometric loading ([[claim--plyometric-injury-risk-comes-from-prescription-not-the-method]]).\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: hopping on the spot in a few sets of about 25, moving to multi-directional hops, then fast vertical hops in sets of about 20 (PDF p. 286 / printed p. 275).\n\n**Related** — [[heuristic--depth-jump-height-set-by-transition-speed-and-heel-contact]]; [[heuristic--depth-jump-execution-cues-landing-gaze-breath]]; [[framework--plyometric-training-classification-impact-intensity-and-function]]; [[heuristic--eccentric-quasi-isometrics-for-active-flexibility-and-starting-strength]]; [[framework--strength-deficit-jump-comparison-estimate]]\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"92f04473-43fc-4ef1-af47-51c9fc202217","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
