{"parentUuid":"f3b2e53b-c555-4b08-8f22-fbe465484de5","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffaJ1HCW7FVkJkuckHF6","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2865,"cache_read_input_tokens":252306,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2865,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":849,"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CffaHndwR5Qsc9aJJKQVP","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"ef588af2-0f4e-4d8f-baec-15d78dae43f6","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:54:37.841Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"ef588af2-0f4e-4d8f-baec-15d78dae43f6","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffaJ1HCW7FVkJkuckHF6","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_018oeBtiKua5fi55HXupTF9q","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--squat-1-5-to-2-bodymass-rule-applies-to-maximal-depth-jumps-only.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: claim\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.4\"\ntopic: strength prerequisite for depth jumps\ncategory: [plyometrics, depth-jumps, readiness, safety]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# The \"Squat 1.5-2 Times Bodymass\" Prerequisite Is a Safety Rule for Maximal Depth Jumps, Not for Submaximal Plyometrics\n\n**What it is** — \"Some authors rightly criticize the recommendation sometimes put forward that athletes should be able to squat 1.5 - 2 times bodymass in order to use certain plyometric drills, since they consider that this criterion is not based on research evidence and does not apply to all plyometric exercises. This recommendation is a safety measure based on force plate analysis of *maximal plyometrics* in the form of depth jumps, where the ground reaction force on the lower extremity can easily exceed 6 times bodyweight. *This guideline was never intended to apply religiously to submaximal plyometrics using small drops, skipping drills or less demanding actions.* This misunderstanding of Russian guidelines is largely a result of an incomplete grasp of the scientific principles on which the original shock method was based\" (PDF pp. 293-294 / printed pp. 282-283; p. 282 verified against the page image, p. 283 from the text layer).\n\nBoth halves are the authors': they agree the criterion is not research-based (\"rightly criticize\"), then defend it as a force-plate-based safety measure for maximal depth jumps.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reasoning: maximal depth jumps can produce ground reaction forces that \"easily exceed 6 times bodyweight\", so a strength base is a safety margin. They give no derivation linking a 1.5-2 × bodymass squat to tolerating a 6 × bodyweight landing. Inference: the squat is a slow, bilateral, concentric-eccentric test and the landing is a brief eccentric-isometric impact, so the rule is a proxy, not a matched test.\n\n**When to apply** — Deciding whether an athlete may do maximal depth jumps (high box, maximal rebound). Inference: for football, where most players have a lifting base, the rule mainly screens young or newly enrolled athletes before maximal depth jumps; it should not block hops, skips, low drops or bounds, which the authors explicitly exclude.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Not a gate for submaximal plyometrics, \"small drops, skipping drills or less demanding actions.\" The authors do not say whether the squat is a 1RM, a full or partial squat, or how to test it. The book also says readiness for plyometric versus heavy work should be decided by the strength deficit (see [[heuristic--strength-deficit-sets-training-method-choice]]), which is a different question from this safety threshold.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own framing: the threshold comes from force plate analysis of maximal depth jumps with ground reaction forces over 6 × bodyweight (PDF p. 293 / printed p. 282).\n\n**Related** — [[heuristic--preparatory-progression-before-depth-jumps]]; [[framework--plyometric-training-classification-impact-intensity-and-function]]; [[claim--depth-landings-from-2-to-3-2-m-produce-forces-over-20-times-bodyweight]]; [[heuristic--strength-deficit-sets-training-method-choice]]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2865,"cache_read_input_tokens":252306,"output_tokens":1461,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":125},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2865},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1461,"cache_read_input_tokens":252306,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2865,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2865,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_018oeBtiKua5fi55HXupTF9q":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--squat-1-5-to-2-bodymass-rule-applies-to-maximal-depth-jumps-only.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: claim\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.4\"\ntopic: strength prerequisite for depth jumps\ncategory: [plyometrics, depth-jumps, readiness, safety]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# The \"Squat 1.5-2 Times Bodymass\" Prerequisite Is a Safety Rule for Maximal Depth Jumps, Not for Submaximal Plyometrics\n\n**What it is** — \"Some authors rightly criticize the recommendation sometimes put forward that athletes should be able to squat 1.5 - 2 times bodymass in order to use certain plyometric drills, since they consider that this criterion is not based on research evidence and does not apply to all plyometric exercises. This recommendation is a safety measure based on force plate analysis of *maximal plyometrics* in the form of depth jumps, where the ground reaction force on the lower extremity can easily exceed 6 times bodyweight. *This guideline was never intended to apply religiously to submaximal plyometrics using small drops, skipping drills or less demanding actions.* This misunderstanding of Russian guidelines is largely a result of an incomplete grasp of the scientific principles on which the original shock method was based\" (PDF pp. 293-294 / printed pp. 282-283; p. 282 verified against the page image, p. 283 from the text layer).\n\nBoth halves are the authors': they agree the criterion is not research-based (\"rightly criticize\"), then defend it as a force-plate-based safety measure for maximal depth jumps.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reasoning: maximal depth jumps can produce ground reaction forces that \"easily exceed 6 times bodyweight\", so a strength base is a safety margin. They give no derivation linking a 1.5-2 × bodymass squat to tolerating a 6 × bodyweight landing. Inference: the squat is a slow, bilateral, concentric-eccentric test and the landing is a brief eccentric-isometric impact, so the rule is a proxy, not a matched test.\n\n**When to apply** — Deciding whether an athlete may do maximal depth jumps (high box, maximal rebound). Inference: for football, where most players have a lifting base, the rule mainly screens young or newly enrolled athletes before maximal depth jumps; it should not block hops, skips, low drops or bounds, which the authors explicitly exclude.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Not a gate for submaximal plyometrics, \"small drops, skipping drills or less demanding actions.\" The authors do not say whether the squat is a 1RM, a full or partial squat, or how to test it. The book also says readiness for plyometric versus heavy work should be decided by the strength deficit (see [[heuristic--strength-deficit-sets-training-method-choice]]), which is a different question from this safety threshold.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own framing: the threshold comes from force plate analysis of maximal depth jumps with ground reaction forces over 6 × bodyweight (PDF p. 293 / printed p. 282).\n\n**Related** — [[heuristic--preparatory-progression-before-depth-jumps]]; [[framework--plyometric-training-classification-impact-intensity-and-function]]; [[claim--depth-landings-from-2-to-3-2-m-produce-forces-over-20-times-bodyweight]]; [[heuristic--strength-deficit-sets-training-method-choice]]\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffaHndwR5Qsc9aJJKQVP","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"c9ce9fd4-cbcb-44f8-9ea3-79947c100976","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:54:48.185Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
