rstanding of Russian guidelines is largely a result of an incomplete grasp of the scientific principles on which the original shock method was based\" (PDF pp. 293-294 / printed pp. 282-283; p. 282 verified against the page image, p. 283 from the text layer).\n\nBoth halves are the authors': they agree the criterion is not research-based (\"rightly criticize\"), then defend it as a force-plate-based safety measure for maximal depth jumps.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reasoning: maximal depth jumps can produce ground reaction forces that \"easily exceed 6 times bodyweight\", so a strength base is a safety margin. They give no derivation linking a 1.5-2 × bodymass squat to tolerating a 6 × bodyweight landing. Inference: the squat is a slow, bilateral, concentric-eccentric test and the landing is a brief eccentric-isometric impact, so the rule is a proxy, not a matched test.\n\n**When to apply** — Deciding whether an athlete may do maximal depth jumps (high box, maximal rebound). Inference: for football, where most players have a lifting base, the rule mainly screens young or newly enrolled athletes before maximal depth jumps; it should not block hops, skips, low drops or bounds, which the authors explicitly exclude.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Not a gate for submaximal plyometrics, \"small drops, skipping drills or less demanding actions.\" The authors do not say whether the squat is a 1RM, a full or partial squat, or how to test it. The book also says readiness for plyometric versus heavy work should be decided by the strength deficit (see [[heuristic--strength-deficit-sets-training-method-choice]]), which is a different question from this safety threshold.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own framing: the threshold comes from force plate analysis of maximal depth jumps with ground reaction forces over 6 × bodyweight (PDF p. 293 / printed p. 282).\n\n**Related** — [[heuristic--preparatory-progression-before-depth-jumps]]; [[framework--plyometric-training-classification-impact-intensity-and-function]]; [[claim--depth-landings-from-2-to-3-2-m-produce-forces-over-20-times-bodyweight]]; [[heuristic--strength-deficit-sets-training-method-choice]]\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"c9ce9fd4-cbcb-44f8-9ea3-79947c100976","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"1e008f4d-a591-4d15-b3f8-6d990bf55a08","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","attachment":{"type":"total_tokens_reminder","text":"<total_tokens>14743366 tokens left</total_tokens>"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"fb2b6396-6417-4436-8aec-e05041313d4e","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:54:48.291Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\n<total_tokens>14743366 tokens left</total_tokens>\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"fb2b6396-6417-4436-8aec-e05041313d4e","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffaKAFyUf1JzHp8ZGjzX","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1601,"cache_read_input_tokens":255171,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1601,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":455,"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CffaJwifVrC8w7fs4thNX","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"7f92e4ac-de54-48d8-a5e4-f66a6a103a21","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:54:52.566Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"7f92e4ac-de54-48d8-a5e4-f66a6a103a21","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffaKAFyUf1JzHp8ZGjzX","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01KVfrFh4kC5oWPwqrBSFfFk","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--lewis-formula-estimates-jump-power-from-bodymass-and-height.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: claim\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.4\"\ntopic: Lewis jump power formula\ncategory: [testing, vertical-jump, power, biomechanics]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# The Lewis Formula P = 21.693 x B x √H Tracks Jump Progress, but It Computes a Falling-Body Power, Not Take-Off Power\n\n**What it is** — In the preparatory programme, the authors suggest tracking jump progress this way: \"To gain an idea of individual progress, the power developed may be calculated from the Lewis nomogram or from the formula: Power P = (21.693) B√H watts, where B = bodymass in kg and H = height jumped in metres\" (PDF p. 288 / printed p. 277, formula read from the page image; the text layer garbles it as \"b Vh\").\n\n**Why it works** — The book gives no derivation. Inference (physics): 21.693 = 9.8 × √4.9, so the formula is P = B·g·√(g·H/2) with g = 9.8 m/s². A body falling freely from rest through height H reaches √(2gH); its mean speed during the fall is half that, √(gH/2). So the formula is body weight (B·g) times the mean speed of a body falling through H, in watts. It is a fixed function of bodymass and jump height only.\n\n**When to apply** — As the authors use it: a cheap way to see whether an individual's bodymass-scaled jump is improving over time, when only a jump height and a scale are available. Eric's baseline: jumps are tested on ForceDecks force plates (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 77, the CMJ family protocol). Inference: where force plates are available, measured propulsive power replaces this estimate; the formula is only a fallback.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Inference: because the formula is weight times the mean speed of a free fall through H, it is not the athlete's peak or mean propulsive power during take-off, which depends on how fast force is produced over the push-off distance; two athletes with the same mass and jump height get the same value even if one pushes off much faster. Do not compare its values with force-plate peak power. The book gives no validation data for it.\n\n**Example** — Inference: a 100 kg athlete jumping 0.50 m gives P = 21.693 × 100 × √0.50 ≈ 1,534 W; at 0.55 m, ≈ 1,609 W. This arithmetic is not in the source.\n\n**Related** — [[heuristic--preparatory-progression-before-depth-jumps]]; [[claim--average-or-peak-force-times-velocity-is-not-power]] (Ch4.3, the authors' own warning about crude power estimates); [[framework--strength-deficit-jump-comparison-estimate]]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1601,"cache_read_input_tokens":255171,"output_tokens":1113,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":11},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1601},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1113,"cache_read_input_tokens":255171,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1601,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1601,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01KVfrFh4kC5oWPwqrBSFfFk":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--lewis-formula-estimates-jump-power-from-bodymass-and-height.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: claim\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.4\"\ntopic: Lewis jump power formula\ncategory: [testing, vertical-jump, power, biomechanics]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# The Lewis Formula P = 21.693 x B x √H Tracks Jump Progress, but It Computes a Falling-Body Power, Not Take-Off Power\n\n**What it is** — In the preparatory programme, the authors suggest tracking jump progress this way: \"To gain an idea of individual progress, the power developed may be calculated from the Lewis nomogram or from the formula: Power P = (21.693) B√H watts, where B = bodymass in kg and H = height jumped in metres\" (PDF p. 288 / printed p. 277, formula read from the page image; the text layer garbles it as \"b Vh\").\n\n**Why it works** — The book gives no derivation. Inference (physics): 21.693 = 9.8 × √4.9, so the formula is P = B·g·√(g·H/2) with g = 9.8 m/s². A body falling freely from rest through height H reaches √(2gH); its mean speed during the fall is half that, √(gH/2). So the formula is body weight (B·g) times the mean speed of a body falling through H, in watts. It is a fixed function of bodymass and jump height only.\n\n**When to apply** — As the authors use it: a cheap way to see whether an individual's bodymass-scaled jump is improving over time, when only a jump height and a scale are available. Eric's baseline: jumps are tested on ForceDecks force plates (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 77, the CMJ family protocol). Inference: where force plates are available, measured propulsive power replaces this estimate; the formula is only a fallback.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Inference: because the formula is weight times the mean speed of a free fall through H, it is not the athlete's peak or mean propulsive power during take-off, which depends on how fast force is produced over the push-off distance; two athletes with the same mass and jump height get the same value even if one pushes off much faster. Do not compare its values with force-plate peak power. The book gives no validation data for it.\n\n**Example** — Inference: a 100 kg athlete jumping 0.50 m gives P = 21.693 × 100 × √0.50 ≈ 1,534 W; at 0.55 m, ≈ 1,609 W. This arithmetic is not in the source.\n\n**Related** — [[heuristic--preparatory-progression-before-depth-jumps]]; [[claim--average-or-peak-force-times-velocity-is-not-power]] (Ch4.3, the authors' own warning about crude power estimates); [[framework--strength-deficit-jump-comparison-estimate]]\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffaJwifVrC8w7fs4thNX","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"81fe4ceb-0e3e-42b2-b615-5a3eab42bef5","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:55:01.593Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"81fe4ceb-0e3e-42b2-b615-5a3eab42bef5","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"624a8cec-33fa-4c0a-a445-c72ce718e6ab","agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KVfrFh4kC5oWPwqrBSFfFk","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--lewis-formula-estimates-jump-power-from-bodymass-and-height.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"d6e840f3-908b-4397-9d7a-6cb516b230ae","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:55:01.711Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"create","filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--lewis-formula-estimates-jump-power-from-bodymass-and-height.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: claim\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.4\"\ntopic: Lewis jump power formula\ncategory: [testing, vertical-jump, power, biomechanics]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# The Lewis Formula P = 21.693 x B x √H Tracks Jump Progress, but It Computes a Falling-Body Power, Not Take-Off Power\n\n**What it is** — In the preparatory programme, the authors suggest tracking jump progress this way: \"To gain an idea of individual progress, the power developed may be calculated from the Lewis nomogram or from the formula: Power P = (21.693) B√H watts, where B = bodymass in kg and H = height jumped in metres\" (PDF p. 288 / printed p. 277, formula read from the page image; the text layer garbles it as \"b Vh\").\n\n**Why it works** — The book gives no derivation. Inference (physics): 21.693 = 9.8 × √4.9, so the formula is P = B·g·√(g·H/2) with g = 9.8 m/s². A body falling freely from rest through height H reaches √(2gH); its mean speed during the fall is half that, √(gH/2). So the formula is body weight (B·g) times the mean speed of a body falling through H, in watts. It is a fixed function of bodymass and jump height only.\n\n**When to apply** — As the authors use it: a cheap way to see whether an individual's bodymass-scaled jump is improving over time, when only a jump height and a scale are available. Eric's baseline: jumps are tested on ForceDecks force plates (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 77, the CMJ family protocol). Inference: where force plates are available, measured propulsive power replaces this estimate; the formula is only a fallback.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Inference: because the formula is weight times the mean speed of a free fall through H, it is not the athlete's peak or mean propulsive power during take-off, which depends on how fast force is produced over the push-off distance; two athletes with the same mass and jump height get the same value even if one pushes off much faster. Do not compare its values with force-plate peak power. The book gives no validation data for it.\n\n**Example** — Inference: a 100 kg athlete jumping 0.50 m gives P = 21.693 × 100 × √0.50 ≈ 1,534 W; at 0.55 m, ≈ 1,609 W. This arithmetic is not in the source.\n\n**Related** — [[heuristic--preparatory-progression-before-depth-jumps]]; [[claim--average-or-peak-force-times-velocity-is-not-power]] (Ch4.3, the authors' own warning about crude power estimates); [[framework--strength-deficit-jump-comparison-estimate]]\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"81fe4ceb-0e3e-42b2-b615-5a3eab42bef5","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"d6e840f3-908b-4397-9d7a-6cb516b230ae","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","attachment":{"type":"total_tokens_reminder","text":"<total_tokens>14742113 tokens left</total_tokens>"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"d41c17a2-ffea-44ab-9403-d1267a149f86","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:55:01.719Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\n<total_tokens>14742113 tokens left</total_tokens>\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"d41c17a2-ffea-44ab-9403-d1267a149f86","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffaLJbJmcRN94Bd4tKDj","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1243,"cache_read_input_tokens":256772,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1243,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":1862,"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CffaKwATwVEr8zMURUPv7","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"0ba0f724-6d9f-4678-972d-3b9a0631a5d5","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:55:10.016Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"0ba0f724-6d9f-4678-972d-3b9a0631a5d5","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffaLJbJmcRN94Bd4tKDj","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01EPJ4SwNuZrmHmSsp6jgszx","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--plyometric-injury-risk-comes-from-prescription-not-the-method.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: claim\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.4\"\ntopic: plyometric safety\ncategory: [plyometrics, injury-risk, cartilage, overtraining]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Plyometric Injury Risk Comes From Prescription (Overload and Overuse), Not the Method; Regularly Impact-Loaded Cartilage Is Reported as Healthier\n\n**What it is** — In \"Plyometric Training and Safety\" (PDF pp. 280-281 / printed pp. 269-270; p. 280 verified against the page image) the text argues:\n- The claim that \"plyometric drills on their own are dangerous and useless has not been proved experimentally or clinically\"; the danger is \"inferred from the magnitude of the impulsive peak forces which have been measured via force plate research\", and an experiment isolating plyometric effects with matched controls \"is very difficult to design.\"\n- Opponents imply running and jumping \"in the context of normal sport are perfectly safe, but in the context of special plyometric drills are dangerous\", and assume joints do not follow SAID (Specific Adaptation to Imposed Demands).\n- Cartilage: \"joints subjected to heavy impact are relatively free from osteoarthritis in old age and ... those subjected to much lower loading experience a greater incidence of osteoarthritis and cartilage fibrillation (Bullough et al, 1973; Kempson et al, 1975; Seedhom & Swann, 1985; Seedhom & Wright, 1988; Seedhom et al, 1977).\" Siff's former colleague Mark Swanepoel (University of the Witwatersrand) noted that fibrillation at any age increases going up from ankle to knee, hip and lumbar spine. Regularly impulse-loaded cartilage is \"mechanically much stiffer\", while cartilage \"heavily loaded infrequently softens due to decrease in proteoglycan production\" (Swanepoel, 1998).\n- \"it is not the inherent nature of plyometrics which may produce injury, but the manner in which it is used.\" Western practice earns much of the criticism: \"encyclopaedic compilations of drills offered in extended, isolated and highly repetitive forms\"; drills often prescribed without regard to \"loading imposed by other work or play ... or the use of formal restoration\"; and \"the athlete's sport alone may offer all or most of the plyometric training that is necessary.\"\n- \"overtraining comprises two distinct components: overload (the imposition of too great a force at any given time) and overuse (the imposition of too great a volume of exercise).\" An \"individually appropriate volume and intensity (e.g. via choice of drop height)\" with \"gradual progressive overload\" and \"adequate restoration regimes need pose no greater risks than the naturally occurring plyometric actions found in running or jumping\" (PDF pp. 280-281).\n\nVoice: Siff (the passage names \"Siff's former colleague\").\n\n**Why it works** — The stated mechanism is adaptation (SAID) and the cartilage findings cited. Inference: overload maps to drop height and rebound effort, overuse to contacts per session and week, which gives two separate dials to manage.\n\n**When to apply** — Answering \"are depth jumps dangerous?\" for coaches or medical staff: the text's position is that risk is a dose question. Inference: in football, count the plyometric loading already present in practice (cutting, jumping, sprinting) before adding drills, as the text's \"sport alone may offer all or most\" warning suggests.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The text argues mainly from absence of proof and from cartilage studies of everyday joint loading; it cites no controlled study showing plyometric drills are safe. The \"heavy impact joints are free from osteoarthritis\" sentence compares joints and loading histories, not plyometric training programs, so do not cite it as evidence that depth jumps protect joints. The cited studies were not checked for this entry.\n\n**Example** — The text's own: an Nicol et al (1997) finding cited here, that many repetitions do not improve fatigue resistance in basketball rebounding, as an example of a Western volume habit that is \"untrue\" (PDF p. 280 / printed p. 269; see [[claim--high-volume-plyometrics-do-not-build-rebound-fatigue-resistance]]).\n\n**Related** — [[claim--collagen-loading-curve-and-injury-risk]]; [[claim--differential-tissue-adaptation-rates]]; [[heuristic--maximal-plyometric-and-depth-jump-volume-and-rest]]; [[claim--squat-1-5-to-2-bodymass-rule-applies-to-maximal-depth-jumps-only]]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1243,"cache_read_input_tokens":256772,"output_tokens":1988,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":246},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1243},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1988,"cache_read_input_tokens":256772,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1243,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1243,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01EPJ4SwNuZrmHmSsp6jgszx":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--plyometric-injury-risk-comes-from-prescription-not-the-method.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: claim\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.4\"\ntopic: plyometric safety\ncategory: [plyometrics, injury-risk, cartilage, overtraining]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Plyometric Injury Risk Comes From Prescription (Overload and Overuse), Not the Method; Regularly Impact-Loaded Cartilage Is Reported as Healthier\n\n**What it is** — In \"Plyometric Training and Safety\" (PDF pp. 280-281 / printed pp. 269-270; p. 280 verified against the page image) the text argues:\n- The claim that \"plyometric drills on their own are dangerous and useless has not been proved experimentally or clinically\"; the danger is \"inferred from the magnitude of the impulsive peak forces which have been measured via force plate research\", and an experiment isolating plyometric effects with matched controls \"is very difficult to design.\"\n- Opponents imply running and jumping \"in the context of normal sport are perfectly safe, but in the context of special plyometric drills are dangerous\", and assume joints do not follow SAID (Specific Adaptation to Imposed Demands).\n- Cartilage: \"joints subjected to heavy impact are relatively free from osteoarthritis in old age and ... those subjected to much lower loading experience a greater incidence of osteoarthritis and cartilage fibrillation (Bullough et al, 1973; Kempson et al, 1975; Seedhom & Swann, 1985; Seedhom & Wright, 1988; Seedhom et al, 1977).\" Siff's former colleague Mark Swanepoel (University of the Witwatersrand) noted that fibrillation at any age increases going up from ankle to knee, hip and lumbar spine. Regularly impulse-loaded cartilage is \"mechanically much stiffer\", while cartilage \"heavily loaded infrequently softens due to decrease in proteoglycan production\" (Swanepoel, 1998).\n- \"it is not the inherent nature of plyometrics which may produce injury, but the manner in which it is used.\" Western practice earns much of the criticism: \"encyclopaedic compilations of drills offered in extended, isolated and highly repetitive forms\"; drills often prescribed without regard to \"loading imposed by other work or play ... or the use of formal restoration\"; and \"the athlete's sport alone may offer all or most of the plyometric training that is necessary.\"\n- \"overtraining comprises two distinct components: overload (the imposition of too great a force at any given time) and overuse (the imposition of too great a volume of exercise).\" An \"individually appropriate volume and intensity (e.g. via choice of drop height)\" with \"gradual progressive overload\" and \"adequate restoration regimes need pose no greater risks than the naturally occurring plyometric actions found in running or jumping\" (PDF pp. 280-281).\n\nVoice: Siff (the passage names \"Siff's former colleague\").\n\n**Why it works** — The stated mechanism is adaptation (SAID) and the cartilage findings cited. Inference: overload maps to drop height and rebound effort, overuse to contacts per session and week, which gives two separate dials to manage.\n\n**When to apply** — Answering \"are depth jumps dangerous?\" for coaches or medical staff: the text's position is that risk is a dose question. Inference: in football, count the plyometric loading already present in practice (cutting, jumping, sprinting) before adding drills, as the text's \"sport alone may offer all or most\" warning suggests.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The text argues mainly from absence of proof and from cartilage studies of everyday joint loading; it cites no controlled study showing plyometric drills are safe. The \"heavy impact joints are free from osteoarthritis\" sentence compares joints and loading histories, not plyometric training programs, so do not cite it as evidence that depth jumps protect joints. The cited studies were not checked for this entry.\n\n**Example** — The text's own: an Nicol et al (1997) finding cited here, that many repetitions do not improve fatigue resistance in basketball rebounding, as an example of a Western volume habit that is \"untrue\" (PDF p. 280 / printed p. 269; see [[claim--high-volume-plyometrics-do-not-build-rebound-fatigue-resistance]]).\n\n**Related** — [[claim--collagen-loading-curve-and-injury-risk]]; [[claim--differential-tissue-adaptation-rates]]; [[heuristic--maximal-plyometric-and-depth-jump-volume-and-rest]]; [[claim--squat-1-5-to-2-bodymass-rule-applies-to-maximal-depth-jumps-only]]\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffaKwATwVEr8zMURUPv7","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"bcdc8cae-6961-4842-b5bc-c8d75dfd0575","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:55:24.799Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"bcdc8cae-6961-4842-b5bc-c8d75dfd0575","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"624a8cec-33fa-4c0a-a445-c72ce718e6ab","agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EPJ4SwNuZrmHmSsp6jgszx","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--plyometric-injury-risk-comes-from-prescription-not-the-method.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"ae6d0430-bab1-438f-9665-b390915d3be3","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:55:24.909Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"create","filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--plyometric-injury-risk-comes-from-prescription-not-the-method.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: claim\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.4\"\ntopic: plyometric safety\ncategory: [plyometrics, injury-risk, cartilage, overtraining]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Plyometric Injury Risk Comes From Prescription (Overload and Overuse), Not the Method; Regularly Impact-Loaded Cartilage Is Reported as Healthier\n\n**What it is** — In \"Plyometric Training and Safety\" (PDF pp. 280-281 / printed pp. 269-270; p. 280 verified against the page image) the text argues:\n- The claim that \"plyometric drills on their own are dangerous and useless has not been proved experimentally or clinically\"; the danger is \"inferred from the magnitude of the impulsive peak forces which have been measured via force plate research\", and an experiment isolating plyometric effects with matched controls \"is very difficult to design.\"\n- Opponents imply running and jumping \"in the context of normal sport are perfectly safe, but in the context of special plyometric drills are dangerous\", and assume joints do not follow SAID (Specific Adaptation to Imposed Demands).\n- Cartilage: \"joints subjected to heavy impact are relatively free from osteoarthritis in old age and ... those subjected to much lower loading experience a greater incidence of osteoarthritis and cartilage fibrillation (Bullough et al, 1973; Kempson et al, 1975; Seedhom & Swann, 1985; Seedhom & Wright, 1988; Seedhom et al, 1977).\" Siff's former colleague Mark Swanepoel (University of the Witwatersrand) noted that fibrillation at any age increases going up from ankle to knee, hip and lumbar spine. Regularly impulse-loaded cartilage is \"mechanically much stiffer\", while cartilage \"heavily loaded infrequently softens due to decrease in proteoglycan production\" (Swanepoel, 1998).\n- \"it is not the inherent nature of plyometrics which may produce injury, but the manner in which it is used.\" Western practice earns much of the criticism: \"encyclopaedic compilations of drills offered in extended, isolated and highly repetitive forms\"; drills often prescribed without regard to \"loading imposed by other work or play ... or the use of formal restoration\"; and \"the athlete's sport alone may offer all or most of the plyometric training that is necessary.\"\n- \"overtraining comprises two distinct components: overload (the imposition of too great a force at any given time) and overuse (the imposition of too great a volume of exercise).\" An \"individually appropriate volume and intensity (e.g. via choice of drop height)\" with \"gradual progressive overload\" and \"adequate restoration regimes need pose no greater risks than the naturally occurring plyometric actions found in running or jumping\" (PDF pp. 280-281).\n\nVoice: Siff (the passage names \"Siff's former colleague\").\n\n**Why it works** — The stated mechanism is adaptation (SAID) and the cartilage findings cited. Inference: overload maps to drop height and rebound effort, overuse to contacts per session and week, which gives two separate dials to manage.\n\n**When to apply** — Answering \"are depth jumps dangerous?\" for coaches or medical staff: the text's position is that risk is a dose question. Inference: in football, count the plyometric loading already present in practice (cutting, jumping, sprinting) before adding drills, as the text's \"sport alone may offer all or most\" warning suggests.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The text argues mainly from absence of proof and from cartilage studies of everyday joint loading; it cites no controlled study showing plyometric drills are safe. The \"heavy impact joints are free from osteoarthritis\" sentence compares joints and loading histories, not plyometric training programs, so do not cite it as evidence that depth jumps protect joints. The cited studies were not checked for this entry.\n\n**Example** — The text's own: an Nicol et al (1997) finding cited here, that many repetitions do not improve fatigue resistance in basketball rebounding, as an example of a Western volume habit that is \"untrue\" (PDF p. 280 / printed p. 269; see [[claim--high-volume-plyometrics-do-not-build-rebound-fatigue-resistance]]).\n\n**Related** — [[claim--collagen-loading-curve-and-injury-risk]]; [[claim--differential-tissue-adaptation-rates]]; [[heuristic--maximal-plyometric-and-depth-jump-volume-and-rest]]; [[claim--squat-1-5-to-2-bodymass-rule-applies-to-maximal-depth-jumps-only]]\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"bcdc8cae-6961-4842-b5bc-c8d75dfd0575","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
