mping \"in the context of normal sport are perfectly safe, but in the context of special plyometric drills are dangerous\", and assume joints do not follow SAID (Specific Adaptation to Imposed Demands).\n- Cartilage: \"joints subjected to heavy impact are relatively free from osteoarthritis in old age and ... those subjected to much lower loading experience a greater incidence of osteoarthritis and cartilage fibrillation (Bullough et al, 1973; Kempson et al, 1975; Seedhom & Swann, 1985; Seedhom & Wright, 1988; Seedhom et al, 1977).\" Siff's former colleague Mark Swanepoel (University of the Witwatersrand) noted that fibrillation at any age increases going up from ankle to knee, hip and lumbar spine. Regularly impulse-loaded cartilage is \"mechanically much stiffer\", while cartilage \"heavily loaded infrequently softens due to decrease in proteoglycan production\" (Swanepoel, 1998).\n- \"it is not the inherent nature of plyometrics which may produce injury, but the manner in which it is used.\" Western practice earns much of the criticism: \"encyclopaedic compilations of drills offered in extended, isolated and highly repetitive forms\"; drills often prescribed without regard to \"loading imposed by other work or play ... or the use of formal restoration\"; and \"the athlete's sport alone may offer all or most of the plyometric training that is necessary.\"\n- \"overtraining comprises two distinct components: overload (the imposition of too great a force at any given time) and overuse (the imposition of too great a volume of exercise).\" An \"individually appropriate volume and intensity (e.g. via choice of drop height)\" with \"gradual progressive overload\" and \"adequate restoration regimes need pose no greater risks than the naturally occurring plyometric actions found in running or jumping\" (PDF pp. 280-281).\n\nVoice: Siff (the passage names \"Siff's former colleague\").\n\n**Why it works** — The stated mechanism is adaptation (SAID) and the cartilage findings cited. Inference: overload maps to drop height and rebound effort, overuse to contacts per session and week, which gives two separate dials to manage.\n\n**When to apply** — Answering \"are depth jumps dangerous?\" for coaches or medical staff: the text's position is that risk is a dose question. Inference: in football, count the plyometric loading already present in practice (cutting, jumping, sprinting) before adding drills, as the text's \"sport alone may offer all or most\" warning suggests.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The text argues mainly from absence of proof and from cartilage studies of everyday joint loading; it cites no controlled study showing plyometric drills are safe. The \"heavy impact joints are free from osteoarthritis\" sentence compares joints and loading histories, not plyometric training programs, so do not cite it as evidence that depth jumps protect joints. The cited studies were not checked for this entry.\n\n**Example** — The text's own: an Nicol et al (1997) finding cited here, that many repetitions do not improve fatigue resistance in basketball rebounding, as an example of a Western volume habit that is \"untrue\" (PDF p. 280 / printed p. 269; see [[claim--high-volume-plyometrics-do-not-build-rebound-fatigue-resistance]]).\n\n**Related** — [[claim--collagen-loading-curve-and-injury-risk]]; [[claim--differential-tissue-adaptation-rates]]; [[heuristic--maximal-plyometric-and-depth-jump-volume-and-rest]]; [[claim--squat-1-5-to-2-bodymass-rule-applies-to-maximal-depth-jumps-only]]\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":28,"oldLines":6,"newStart":28,"newLines":6,"lines":[" "," **When NOT to apply** — The text argues mainly from absence of proof and from cartilage studies of everyday joint loading; it cites no controlled study showing plyometric drills are safe. The \"heavy impact joints are free from osteoarthritis\" sentence compares joints and loading histories, not plyometric training programs, so do not cite it as evidence that depth jumps protect joints. The cited studies were not checked for this entry."," ","-**Example** — The text's own: an Nicol et al (1997) finding cited here, that many repetitions do not improve fatigue resistance in basketball rebounding, as an example of a Western volume habit that is \"untrue\" (PDF p. 280 / printed p. 269; see [[claim--high-volume-plyometrics-do-not-build-rebound-fatigue-resistance]]).","+**Example** — The text's own: the Nicol et al (1997) finding, that many repetitions do not improve fatigue resistance in basketball rebounding, as an example of a Western volume habit that is \"untrue\" (PDF p. 280 / printed p. 269; see [[claim--high-volume-plyometrics-do-not-build-rebound-fatigue-resistance]])."," "," **Related** — [[claim--collagen-loading-curve-and-injury-risk]]; [[claim--differential-tissue-adaptation-rates]]; [[heuristic--maximal-plyometric-and-depth-jump-volume-and-rest]]; [[claim--squat-1-5-to-2-bodymass-rule-applies-to-maximal-depth-jumps-only]]"]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"73d59aa3-bdb4-417e-8ab0-2d68f24f8e74","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"c8bd5e43-5a52-4837-aa4d-5a2ef61d52b0","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffaMsspcD2xkcUJd6izz","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_0185zbfWN4PbwVEumCmerqhs","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--depth-jump-training-beat-higher-volume-traditional-training-in-jumpers.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: claim\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.4\"\ntopic: training economy of depth jumps\ncategory: [plyometrics, depth-jumps, reactive-ability, training-economy]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Jumpers Doing 475 Depth Jumps in 12 Weeks Gained More Reactive Ability Than a Group Doing 1472 Barbell Push-Offs and 93 Tonnes\n\n**What it is** — \"The plyometric method of developing explosive strength and reactive ability solves to a significant degree the problem of economising training, since it ensures that a high level of special conditioning is attained in the minimum time. Thus, a comparative experiment revealed that a group of track-and-field jumpers executing primarily depth-jumps (all of them did 475 jumps) over a 12 week period in the preparation phase showed greater improvement in reactive ability than the group which trained with traditional methods and executed 1472 general push-offs (squats, jumping and hopping with a barbell weighing 90-95%, 70-80% and 30-40% of 1RM) and lifted a total volume of 93 tons!\" (PDF p. 288 / printed p. 277, verified against the page image).\n\nTwo sentences around it: \"The training effect of depth-jumps or shock-loading for developing explosive strength is very large, having little equal when compared with the other means of strength training, especially when combined in a complex with other forms of intensive strength training\", corroborated by Semyenov 1971, Tatyan 1974, Dobrovolsky 1972, Savin 1974, Khodykin 1974 and Deniskin 1976 (same page). And: \"Carefully periodised training programmes which combine resistance exercises with plyometrics can develop explosive strength even more effectively than plyometrics alone, provided the resistance movements precede the plyometric activities in individual sessions\" (same page).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors give the result as evidence of \"economising training\"; no mechanism is stated here beyond the shock-method rationale elsewhere in 5.2.4.\n\n**When to apply** — Making the case to coaches that a small, well-placed depth-jump dose can do more for reactive ability than a large barbell volume. Inference: the companion sentence (resistance work before plyometrics in the same session) is a direct sequencing rule for combined sessions.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Unclear points in the source: \"all of them did 475 jumps\" does not say whether 475 is per athlete or the group total; the experiment has no citation, group sizes, statistics or outcome test named beyond \"reactive ability\"; the comparison group's \"1472 general push-offs\" is also unclear per athlete or total. Supported only as a reported Soviet-era comparison. The result is for track-and-field jumpers in a preparation phase.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own experiment as quoted above (PDF p. 288 / printed p. 277).\n\n**Related** — [[heuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size]]; [[heuristic--maximal-plyometric-and-depth-jump-volume-and-rest]]; [[heuristic--structured-load-progression-beats-volume-alone-or-random-combination]]; [[framework--post-activation-potentiation-load-timing-sequencing]]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2117,"cache_read_input_tokens":258015,"output_tokens":1562,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":65},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2117},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1562,"cache_read_input_tokens":258015,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2117,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2117,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_0185zbfWN4PbwVEumCmerqhs":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--depth-jump-training-beat-higher-volume-traditional-training-in-jumpers.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: claim\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.4\"\ntopic: training economy of depth jumps\ncategory: [plyometrics, depth-jumps, reactive-ability, training-economy]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Jumpers Doing 475 Depth Jumps in 12 Weeks Gained More Reactive Ability Than a Group Doing 1472 Barbell Push-Offs and 93 Tonnes\n\n**What it is** — \"The plyometric method of developing explosive strength and reactive ability solves to a significant degree the problem of economising training, since it ensures that a high level of special conditioning is attained in the minimum time. Thus, a comparative experiment revealed that a group of track-and-field jumpers executing primarily depth-jumps (all of them did 475 jumps) over a 12 week period in the preparation phase showed greater improvement in reactive ability than the group which trained with traditional methods and executed 1472 general push-offs (squats, jumping and hopping with a barbell weighing 90-95%, 70-80% and 30-40% of 1RM) and lifted a total volume of 93 tons!\" (PDF p. 288 / printed p. 277, verified against the page image).\n\nTwo sentences around it: \"The training effect of depth-jumps or shock-loading for developing explosive strength is very large, having little equal when compared with the other means of strength training, especially when combined in a complex with other forms of intensive strength training\", corroborated by Semyenov 1971, Tatyan 1974, Dobrovolsky 1972, Savin 1974, Khodykin 1974 and Deniskin 1976 (same page). And: \"Carefully periodised training programmes which combine resistance exercises with plyometrics can develop explosive strength even more effectively than plyometrics alone, provided the resistance movements precede the plyometric activities in individual sessions\" (same page).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors give the result as evidence of \"economising training\"; no mechanism is stated here beyond the shock-method rationale elsewhere in 5.2.4.\n\n**When to apply** — Making the case to coaches that a small, well-placed depth-jump dose can do more for reactive ability than a large barbell volume. Inference: the companion sentence (resistance work before plyometrics in the same session) is a direct sequencing rule for combined sessions.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Unclear points in the source: \"all of them did 475 jumps\" does not say whether 475 is per athlete or the group total; the experiment has no citation, group sizes, statistics or outcome test named beyond \"reactive ability\"; the comparison group's \"1472 general push-offs\" is also unclear per athlete or total. Supported only as a reported Soviet-era comparison. The result is for track-and-field jumpers in a preparation phase.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own experiment as quoted above (PDF p. 288 / printed p. 277).\n\n**Related** — [[heuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size]]; [[heuristic--maximal-plyometric-and-depth-jump-volume-and-rest]]; [[heuristic--structured-load-progression-beats-volume-alone-or-random-combination]]; [[framework--post-activation-potentiation-load-timing-sequencing]]\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011CffaMeQhFuSo9zGYPnebu","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"d0ffa410-c79e-48e8-bb6d-3561b8eef850","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:55:40.927Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
