{"parentUuid":"45066c80-0ab9-4cb6-8b68-08c26845ac76","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffaPG9451eJenhHRn82S","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1848,"cache_read_input_tokens":260132,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1848,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":472,"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CffaNqHa7ev669rtXctTL","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"2c3b1615-72b6-4603-bf33-9ee691c8402a","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:55:48.465Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"2c3b1615-72b6-4603-bf33-9ee691c8402a","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffaPG9451eJenhHRn82S","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01FaE5nJPuvNEZHAahBt3mEG","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--depth-landings-from-2-to-3-2-m-produce-forces-over-20-times-bodyweight.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: claim\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.4\"\ntopic: depth landings without rebound\ncategory: [plyometrics, eccentric-strength, depth-landings, safety]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Depth Landings Without a Rebound Raised Strength in Dursenev and Raevsky (1978): 1500-3500 kgf in 0.028-0.061 s From Drops up to 3.2 m\n\n**What it is** — \"*depth landings* (i.e. depth jumps without the rebound after landing) alone can also have a significant effect on concentric and eccentric strength. This was shown by Dursenev and Raevsky (1978) who investigated the effect of depth landings from heights of 2m or more onto soft gymnastic mats. Under these conditions, the subjects acquired the ability to display brief muscle tension (over periods of between 0.028-0.061 secs) of magnitude 1500-3500 kgf, i.e. over 20 times bodyweight for heights of up to 3.2 metres. The rationale for using this method was that weight training or other similar methods of resistance training cannot produce this intensity of voluntary effort.\" \"the maximum possible height which the athletes could contemplate using produced the greatest increase in strength, although they often hesitated to attempt these extremely stressful jumps\" (PDF p. 288 / printed p. 277, verified against the page image).\n\nThe authors' caveats on the same page: \"the drop heights studied were for experimental purposes alone and it would be most inadvisable to use drops of 3.2 metres for training.\" \"It appeared that the main consideration for enhancing strength is not the type of muscle contraction, but the intensity and speed of contraction elicited by the exercise.\" Increase drop height \"gradually from fairly low levels\", \"limit the number of drops per session\", \"land on suitably firm shock-absorbing surfaces\" and attend to landing technique.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' rationale: intensity of tension that resistance training cannot reach, with intensity and speed of contraction, not contraction type, as \"the main consideration.\" Inference: \"over 20 times bodyweight\" holds for a 75 kg subject at the low end (1500 kgf / 75 kg = 20); the book gives no subject masses, so the ratio is approximate.\n\n**When to apply** — As evidence that the eccentric landing alone is a strong stimulus, which supports using low-height landing drills as a step before rebound work. Inference: for football, landing drills from modest heights are the safe use of this finding; the experimental heights are not.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Never use 2-3.2 m drops in training (the authors' own warning). The study is reported second-hand, with no subject numbers, training duration or strength measure given. It used \"soft gymnastic mats\", which differs from the firm surfaces the authors want for rebound plyometrics (see [[heuristic--plyometric-surface-footwear-and-simple-apparatus]]).\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: drops of 2 m or more onto soft mats, with peak tensions of 1500-3500 kgf over 0.028-0.061 s (PDF p. 288 / printed p. 277).\n\n**Related** — [[claim--squat-1-5-to-2-bodymass-rule-applies-to-maximal-depth-jumps-only]]; [[claim--eccentric-training-damage-adaptation-and-injury-risk-tradeoff]]; [[claim--optimal-depth-jump-drop-height-for-power-vs-force]]; [[heuristic--rate-plyometric-intensity-by-contact-momentum-from-drop-height]]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1848,"cache_read_input_tokens":260132,"output_tokens":1434,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":35},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1848},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1434,"cache_read_input_tokens":260132,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1848,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1848,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01FaE5nJPuvNEZHAahBt3mEG":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--depth-landings-from-2-to-3-2-m-produce-forces-over-20-times-bodyweight.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: claim\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.4\"\ntopic: depth landings without rebound\ncategory: [plyometrics, eccentric-strength, depth-landings, safety]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Depth Landings Without a Rebound Raised Strength in Dursenev and Raevsky (1978): 1500-3500 kgf in 0.028-0.061 s From Drops up to 3.2 m\n\n**What it is** — \"*depth landings* (i.e. depth jumps without the rebound after landing) alone can also have a significant effect on concentric and eccentric strength. This was shown by Dursenev and Raevsky (1978) who investigated the effect of depth landings from heights of 2m or more onto soft gymnastic mats. Under these conditions, the subjects acquired the ability to display brief muscle tension (over periods of between 0.028-0.061 secs) of magnitude 1500-3500 kgf, i.e. over 20 times bodyweight for heights of up to 3.2 metres. The rationale for using this method was that weight training or other similar methods of resistance training cannot produce this intensity of voluntary effort.\" \"the maximum possible height which the athletes could contemplate using produced the greatest increase in strength, although they often hesitated to attempt these extremely stressful jumps\" (PDF p. 288 / printed p. 277, verified against the page image).\n\nThe authors' caveats on the same page: \"the drop heights studied were for experimental purposes alone and it would be most inadvisable to use drops of 3.2 metres for training.\" \"It appeared that the main consideration for enhancing strength is not the type of muscle contraction, but the intensity and speed of contraction elicited by the exercise.\" Increase drop height \"gradually from fairly low levels\", \"limit the number of drops per session\", \"land on suitably firm shock-absorbing surfaces\" and attend to landing technique.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' rationale: intensity of tension that resistance training cannot reach, with intensity and speed of contraction, not contraction type, as \"the main consideration.\" Inference: \"over 20 times bodyweight\" holds for a 75 kg subject at the low end (1500 kgf / 75 kg = 20); the book gives no subject masses, so the ratio is approximate.\n\n**When to apply** — As evidence that the eccentric landing alone is a strong stimulus, which supports using low-height landing drills as a step before rebound work. Inference: for football, landing drills from modest heights are the safe use of this finding; the experimental heights are not.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Never use 2-3.2 m drops in training (the authors' own warning). The study is reported second-hand, with no subject numbers, training duration or strength measure given. It used \"soft gymnastic mats\", which differs from the firm surfaces the authors want for rebound plyometrics (see [[heuristic--plyometric-surface-footwear-and-simple-apparatus]]).\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: drops of 2 m or more onto soft mats, with peak tensions of 1500-3500 kgf over 0.028-0.061 s (PDF p. 288 / printed p. 277).\n\n**Related** — [[claim--squat-1-5-to-2-bodymass-rule-applies-to-maximal-depth-jumps-only]]; [[claim--eccentric-training-damage-adaptation-and-injury-risk-tradeoff]]; [[claim--optimal-depth-jump-drop-height-for-power-vs-force]]; [[heuristic--rate-plyometric-intensity-by-contact-momentum-from-drop-height]]\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffaNqHa7ev669rtXctTL","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"8347b01f-a129-4a77-a3a2-faf1b5ca25e8","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:55:58.490Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
