{"parentUuid":"01f62e89-437d-4fb0-9f5a-4f5f8b44ed62","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffaQNN8WC48HceW7Mp9J","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1573,"cache_read_input_tokens":261980,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1573,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":462,"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CffaQ8Xw2wgCM4cvDiZeV","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"98062ec6-a1cb-4fc5-977c-5c75a8ae0d20","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:56:03.283Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"98062ec6-a1cb-4fc5-977c-5c75a8ae0d20","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffaQNN8WC48HceW7Mp9J","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01BwM4SgzUqSAvhDGWkV4C8u","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.4\"\ntopic: choosing between heavy and plyometric methods\ncategory: [explosive-strength, plyometrics, method-selection, sequencing]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Large Resistance Calls for the Brief Maximal Tension Method, Small Resistance and Reactive Demand Call for Plyometrics; the Book Disagrees With Itself on Whether Plyometrics Should Be the Main Method\n\n**What it is** — The authors' method-selection rules (PDF p. 289 / printed p. 278, verified against the page image unless marked):\n- \"In those cases where explosive force is associated with the overcoming of large resistance, preference should be given to the brief maximal tension method, as used commonly in Olympic weightlifting. In situations where the resistance is small and the working effect depends upon the reactive ability of the muscles, plyometric methods should be given preference.\"\n- Staging: \"Although it should form an integral part of the training programme, plyometrics should not be combined in the beginning stages with resistance exercises such as the brief maximal tension method.\" \"In subsequent stages, plyometrics should be the main method in the training of highly qualified athletes for developing explosive strength and reactive ability.\"\n- Concentric explosive work: \"exercises with maximum effort and mixed regimes using weights equal to 40% of 1RM, or 30% of 1RM (Verkhoshansky, 1963; Papysheva, 1966). Explosive-isometric tension up to 80% of maximum should be used in conjunction with this routine.\"\n- In combined sessions, resistance work goes first: combined programmes beat plyometrics alone \"provided the resistance movements precede the plyometric activities in individual sessions\" (PDF p. 288 / printed p. 277).\n- Testing decides: \"To ascertain whether an athlete needs plyometric or heavy resistance training depends on measuring the *strength deficit* ... Without such testing, prescription of plyometric training tends to remain hit-and-miss\" (PDF p. 284 / printed p. 273; repeated at PDF p. 293 / printed p. 282 in a grammatically broken sentence).\n\n**Internal conflict, recorded:** \"plyometrics should be the main method in the training of highly qualified athletes\" (PDF p. 289 / printed p. 278) against, half a page earlier, \"it is futile to rely predominantly on plyometric drills to enhance sport specific explosive strength and power, because efficiency of motor control is fundamental to all skilled sporting performance\" (PDF pp. 288-289 / printed pp. 277-278). The first reads as the Verkhoshansky shock-method programme; the second follows a Nicol et al (1997) citation in the Siff-voiced material. The book does not reconcile them.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors tie each method to the force demand: heavy brief tension for large resistance, reactive shock loading for small resistance. The strength deficit decides which an individual needs (Ch1; see [[heuristic--strength-deficit-sets-training-method-choice]], which records that the book also states the deficit rule two ways).\n\n**When to apply** — Eric's baseline: \"we incorporate the countermovement jump and the isometric mid-thigh pull to get a dynamic strength index, to determine whether we need more plyometric training or more max-strength training\" (Eric, interview 2026-09-28, Q21; `docs/method-baseline.md` line 76). Inference: DSI plays the role the authors give the strength deficit (both compare a fast expression of force with a maximal one to choose plyometric versus heavy work), but they are different measures; Eric's baseline Tension 17 records that the DSI cutoffs were never validated and that IMTP peak force drives most of the ratio (line 261). Inference: for linemen, whose sport force is against large resistance, the authors' rule favours brief maximal tension; for skill positions, whose force is against bodymass, it favours plyometrics.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Do not cite Supertraining as saying plyometrics should be the main method without the \"futile to rely predominantly\" sentence, or the reverse. \"Beginning stages\" and \"highly qualified\" are not defined. The 40% and 30% of 1RM loads and the 80% explosive-isometric tension are given without sets, reps or rest.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own contrast: Olympic weightlifting (large resistance, brief maximal tension) versus jumping from depth (small resistance, reactive ability) (PDF p. 289 / printed p. 278).\n\n**Related** — [[heuristic--strength-deficit-sets-training-method-choice]]; [[framework--strength-deficit-jump-comparison-estimate]]; [[claim--high-volume-plyometrics-do-not-build-rebound-fatigue-resistance]]; [[claim--depth-jump-training-beat-higher-volume-traditional-training-in-jumpers]]; [[claim--heavy-squats-and-light-fast-lifts-miss-the-eccentric-concentric-switch]]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1573,"cache_read_input_tokens":261980,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1573,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01BwM4SgzUqSAvhDGWkV4C8u":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.4\"\ntopic: choosing between heavy and plyometric methods\ncategory: [explosive-strength, plyometrics, method-selection, sequencing]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Large Resistance Calls for the Brief Maximal Tension Method, Small Resistance and Reactive Demand Call for Plyometrics; the Book Disagrees With Itself on Whether Plyometrics Should Be the Main Method\n\n**What it is** — The authors' method-selection rules (PDF p. 289 / printed p. 278, verified against the page image unless marked):\n- \"In those cases where explosive force is associated with the overcoming of large resistance, preference should be given to the brief maximal tension method, as used commonly in Olympic weightlifting. In situations where the resistance is small and the working effect depends upon the reactive ability of the muscles, plyometric methods should be given preference.\"\n- Staging: \"Although it should form an integral part of the training programme, plyometrics should not be combined in the beginning stages with resistance exercises such as the brief maximal tension method.\" \"In subsequent stages, plyometrics should be the main method in the training of highly qualified athletes for developing explosive strength and reactive ability.\"\n- Concentric explosive work: \"exercises with maximum effort and mixed regimes using weights equal to 40% of 1RM, or 30% of 1RM (Verkhoshansky, 1963; Papysheva, 1966). Explosive-isometric tension up to 80% of maximum should be used in conjunction with this routine.\"\n- In combined sessions, resistance work goes first: combined programmes beat plyometrics alone \"provided the resistance movements precede the plyometric activities in individual sessions\" (PDF p. 288 / printed p. 277).\n- Testing decides: \"To ascertain whether an athlete needs plyometric or heavy resistance training depends on measuring the *strength deficit* ... Without such testing, prescription of plyometric training tends to remain hit-and-miss\" (PDF p. 284 / printed p. 273; repeated at PDF p. 293 / printed p. 282 in a grammatically broken sentence).\n\n**Internal conflict, recorded:** \"plyometrics should be the main method in the training of highly qualified athletes\" (PDF p. 289 / printed p. 278) against, half a page earlier, \"it is futile to rely predominantly on plyometric drills to enhance sport specific explosive strength and power, because efficiency of motor control is fundamental to all skilled sporting performance\" (PDF pp. 288-289 / printed pp. 277-278). The first reads as the Verkhoshansky shock-method programme; the second follows a Nicol et al (1997) citation in the Siff-voiced material. The book does not reconcile them.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors tie each method to the force demand: heavy brief tension for large resistance, reactive shock loading for small resistance. The strength deficit decides which an individual needs (Ch1; see [[heuristic--strength-deficit-sets-training-method-choice]], which records that the book also states the deficit rule two ways).\n\n**When to apply** — Eric's baseline: \"we incorporate the countermovement jump and the isometric mid-thigh pull to get a dynamic strength index, to determine whether we need more plyometric training or more max-strength training\" (Eric, interview 2026-09-28, Q21; `docs/method-baseline.md` line 76). Inference: DSI plays the role the authors give the strength deficit (both compare a fast expression of force with a maximal one to choose plyometric versus heavy work), but they are different measures; Eric's baseline Tension 17 records that the DSI cutoffs were never validated and that IMTP peak force drives most of the ratio (line 261). Inference: for linemen, whose sport force is against large resistance, the authors' rule favours brief maximal tension; for skill positions, whose force is against bodymass, it favours plyometrics.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Do not cite Supertraining as saying plyometrics should be the main method without the \"futile to rely predominantly\" sentence, or the reverse. \"Beginning stages\" and \"highly qualified\" are not defined. The 40% and 30% of 1RM loads and the 80% explosive-isometric tension are given without sets, reps or rest.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own contrast: Olympic weightlifting (large resistance, brief maximal tension) versus jumping from depth (small resistance, reactive ability) (PDF p. 289 / printed p. 278).\n\n**Related** — [[heuristic--strength-deficit-sets-training-method-choice]]; [[framework--strength-deficit-jump-comparison-estimate]]; [[claim--high-volume-plyometrics-do-not-build-rebound-fatigue-resistance]]; [[claim--depth-jump-training-beat-higher-volume-traditional-training-in-jumpers]]; [[claim--heavy-squats-and-light-fast-lifts-miss-the-eccentric-concentric-switch]]\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffaQ8Xw2wgCM4cvDiZeV","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"1a3bb309-0b31-4398-bfd6-629b11f2d848","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:56:18.078Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"1a3bb309-0b31-4398-bfd6-629b11f2d848","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"624a8cec-33fa-4c0a-a445-c72ce718e6ab","agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BwM4SgzUqSAvhDGWkV4C8u","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"d2632190-30bd-4a34-8ce8-99e95e08a94e","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:56:18.167Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"create","filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.4\"\ntopic: choosing between heavy and plyometric methods\ncategory: [explosive-strength, plyometrics, method-selection, sequencing]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Large Resistance Calls for the Brief Maximal Tension Method, Small Resistance and Reactive Demand Call for Plyometrics; the Book Disagrees With Itself on Whether Plyometrics Should Be the Main Method\n\n**What it is** — The authors' method-selection rules (PDF p. 289 / printed p. 278, verified against the page image unless marked):\n- \"In those cases where explosive force is associated with the overcoming of large resistance, preference should be given to the brief maximal tension method, as used commonly in Olympic weightlifting. In situations where the resistance is small and the working effect depends upon the reactive ability of the muscles, plyometric methods should be given preference.\"\n- Staging: \"Although it should form an integral part of the training programme, plyometrics should not be combined in the beginning stages with resistance exercises such as the brief maximal tension method.\" \"In subsequent stages, plyometrics should be the main method in the training of highly qualified athletes for developing explosive strength and reactive ability.\"\n- Concentric explosive work: \"exercises with maximum effort and mixed regimes using weights equal to 40% of 1RM, or 30% of 1RM (Verkhoshansky, 1963; Papysheva, 1966). Explosive-isometric tension up to 80% of maximum should be used in conjunction with this routine.\"\n- In combined sessions, resistance work goes first: combined programmes beat plyometrics alone \"provided the resistance movements precede the plyometric activities in individual sessions\" (PDF p. 288 / printed p. 277).\n- Testing decides: \"To ascertain whether an athlete needs plyometric or heavy resistance training depends on measuring the *strength deficit* ... Without such testing, prescription of plyometric training tends to remain hit-and-miss\" (PDF p. 284 / printed p. 273; repeated at PDF p. 293 / printed p. 282 in a grammatically broken sentence).\n\n**Internal conflict, recorded:** \"plyometrics should be the main method in the training of highly qualified athletes\" (PDF p. 289 / printed p. 278) against, half a page earlier, \"it is futile to rely predominantly on plyometric drills to enhance sport specific explosive strength and power, because efficiency of motor control is fundamental to all skilled sporting performance\" (PDF pp. 288-289 / printed pp. 277-278). The first reads as the Verkhoshansky shock-method programme; the second follows a Nicol et al (1997) citation in the Siff-voiced material. The book does not reconcile them.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors tie each method to the force demand: heavy brief tension for large resistance, reactive shock loading for small resistance. The strength deficit decides which an individual needs (Ch1; see [[heuristic--strength-deficit-sets-training-method-choice]], which records that the book also states the deficit rule two ways).\n\n**When to apply** — Eric's baseline: \"we incorporate the countermovement jump and the isometric mid-thigh pull to get a dynamic strength index, to determine whether we need more plyometric training or more max-strength training\" (Eric, interview 2026-09-28, Q21; `docs/method-baseline.md` line 76). Inference: DSI plays the role the authors give the strength deficit (both compare a fast expression of force with a maximal one to choose plyometric versus heavy work), but they are different measures; Eric's baseline Tension 17 records that the DSI cutoffs were never validated and that IMTP peak force drives most of the ratio (line 261). Inference: for linemen, whose sport force is against large resistance, the authors' rule favours brief maximal tension; for skill positions, whose force is against bodymass, it favours plyometrics.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Do not cite Supertraining as saying plyometrics should be the main method without the \"futile to rely predominantly\" sentence, or the reverse. \"Beginning stages\" and \"highly qualified\" are not defined. The 40% and 30% of 1RM loads and the 80% explosive-isometric tension are given without sets, reps or rest.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own contrast: Olympic weightlifting (large resistance, brief maximal tension) versus jumping from depth (small resistance, reactive ability) (PDF p. 289 / printed p. 278).\n\n**Related** — [[heuristic--strength-deficit-sets-training-method-choice]]; [[framework--strength-deficit-jump-comparison-estimate]]; [[claim--high-volume-plyometrics-do-not-build-rebound-fatigue-resistance]]; [[claim--depth-jump-training-beat-higher-volume-traditional-training-in-jumpers]]; [[claim--heavy-squats-and-light-fast-lifts-miss-the-eccentric-concentric-switch]]\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"1a3bb309-0b31-4398-bfd6-629b11f2d848","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"d2632190-30bd-4a34-8ce8-99e95e08a94e","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffaQNN8WC48HceW7Mp9J","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_017nFNPSVSwNvx8qCShsrMNa","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--high-volume-plyometrics-do-not-build-rebound-fatigue-resistance.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: claim\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.4\"\ntopic: plyometric volume and fatigue resistance\ncategory: [plyometrics, volume, fatigue, motor-control]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Many Sets and Reps of Plyometrics Do Not Build Fatigue Resistance for Repeated Rebounding; Movement Efficiency Does (Nicol et al, 1997)\n\n**What it is** — Stated twice in 5.2.4:\n- \"Many repetitions of plyometric drills frequently are prescribed for some sports because of assumptions that this sort of training will improve fatigue resistance in repeated actions such as rebounding in basketball, yet research has shown this to be untrue (Nicol et al, 1997)\" (PDF p. 280 / printed p. 269, verified against the page image).\n- \"The use of many repetitions and sets of plyometric drills in an attempt to facilitate adaptation to cope with exhaustive bouts of stretch-shortening activities such as rebounding in basketball is not supported by research (Nicol et al, 1997). Instead any improvement in such situations is better mediated by the use of training to enhance movement efficiency. This is a very important point which concerns all explosive and ballistic activities: it is futile to rely predominantly on plyometric drills to enhance sport specific explosive strength and power, because efficiency of motor control is fundamental to all skilled sporting performance\" (PDF pp. 288-289 / printed pp. 277-278, verified against the page images).\n\n**Internal conflict, recorded:** the \"futile to rely predominantly\" sentence is followed on PDF p. 289 / printed p. 278 by \"plyometrics should be the main method in the training of highly qualified athletes for developing explosive strength and reactive ability.\" See [[heuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size]].\n\n**Why it works** — The text's stated reason is \"efficiency of motor control is fundamental to all skilled sporting performance.\" It does not describe the Nicol et al study design or measures. Inference: Nicol, Komi and colleagues published on stretch-shortening-cycle fatigue in the 1990s; the exact 1997 paper was not checked for this entry, so what it measured is unverified here.\n\n**When to apply** — When someone proposes high-rep plyometric circuits to make athletes \"fresher\" in the fourth quarter. Eric's baseline: \"Everything we do is some form of conditioning\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 122). Inference: the text's alternative for repeat-effort tolerance is better movement efficiency in the sport skill itself, not more drill volume; the book also allows \"much shorter intervals with submaximal plyometrics to develop explosive-strength endurance\" (PDF p. 285 / printed p. 274, item 6), so submaximal plyometric endurance work is not ruled out.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Not evidence against plyometrics for explosive strength; the same section calls the shock method's training effect \"very large.\" The claim rests on one citation the book does not describe.\n\n**Example** — The text's own: basketball rebounding as the repeated stretch-shortening task that high plyometric volume was assumed, wrongly, to prepare (PDF p. 280 / printed p. 269).\n\n**Related** — [[heuristic--maximal-plyometric-and-depth-jump-volume-and-rest]]; [[claim--plyometric-injury-risk-comes-from-prescription-not-the-method]]; [[heuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size]]; [[claim--exercise-economy-improves-with-skill-and-interval-style-costs-more]]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1573,"cache_read_input_tokens":261980,"output_tokens":3334,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":20},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1573},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":3334,"cache_read_input_tokens":261980,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1573,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1573,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_017nFNPSVSwNvx8qCShsrMNa":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--high-volume-plyometrics-do-not-build-rebound-fatigue-resistance.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: claim\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.4\"\ntopic: plyometric volume and fatigue resistance\ncategory: [plyometrics, volume, fatigue, motor-control]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Many Sets and Reps of Plyometrics Do Not Build Fatigue Resistance for Repeated Rebounding; Movement Efficiency Does (Nicol et al, 1997)\n\n**What it is** — Stated twice in 5.2.4:\n- \"Many repetitions of plyometric drills frequently are prescribed for some sports because of assumptions that this sort of training will improve fatigue resistance in repeated actions such as rebounding in basketball, yet research has shown this to be untrue (Nicol et al, 1997)\" (PDF p. 280 / printed p. 269, verified against the page image).\n- \"The use of many repetitions and sets of plyometric drills in an attempt to facilitate adaptation to cope with exhaustive bouts of stretch-shortening activities such as rebounding in basketball is not supported by research (Nicol et al, 1997). Instead any improvement in such situations is better mediated by the use of training to enhance movement efficiency. This is a very important point which concerns all explosive and ballistic activities: it is futile to rely predominantly on plyometric drills to enhance sport specific explosive strength and power, because efficiency of motor control is fundamental to all skilled sporting performance\" (PDF pp. 288-289 / printed pp. 277-278, verified against the page images).\n\n**Internal conflict, recorded:** the \"futile to rely predominantly\" sentence is followed on PDF p. 289 / printed p. 278 by \"plyometrics should be the main method in the training of highly qualified athletes for developing explosive strength and reactive ability.\" See [[heuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size]].\n\n**Why it works** — The text's stated reason is \"efficiency of motor control is fundamental to all skilled sporting performance.\" It does not describe the Nicol et al study design or measures. Inference: Nicol, Komi and colleagues published on stretch-shortening-cycle fatigue in the 1990s; the exact 1997 paper was not checked for this entry, so what it measured is unverified here.\n\n**When to apply** — When someone proposes high-rep plyometric circuits to make athletes \"fresher\" in the fourth quarter. Eric's baseline: \"Everything we do is some form of conditioning\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 122). Inference: the text's alternative for repeat-effort tolerance is better movement efficiency in the sport skill itself, not more drill volume; the book also allows \"much shorter intervals with submaximal plyometrics to develop explosive-strength endurance\" (PDF p. 285 / printed p. 274, item 6), so submaximal plyometric endurance work is not ruled out.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Not evidence against plyometrics for explosive strength; the same section calls the shock method's training effect \"very large.\" The claim rests on one citation the book does not describe.\n\n**Example** — The text's own: basketball rebounding as the repeated stretch-shortening task that high plyometric volume was assumed, wrongly, to prepare (PDF p. 280 / printed p. 269).\n\n**Related** — [[heuristic--maximal-plyometric-and-depth-jump-volume-and-rest]]; [[claim--plyometric-injury-risk-comes-from-prescription-not-the-method]]; [[heuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size]]; [[claim--exercise-economy-improves-with-skill-and-interval-style-costs-more]]\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011CffaQ8Xw2wgCM4cvDiZeV","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"17636a12-630a-434e-a05c-8b6490390d1f","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:56:29.721Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"17636a12-630a-434e-a05c-8b6490390d1f","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"624a8cec-33fa-4c0a-a445-c72ce718e6ab","agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017nFNPSVSwNvx8qCShsrMNa","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--high-volume-plyometrics-do-not-build-rebound-fatigue-resistance.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"e3b9f652-02ee-48e9-8510-06337f0b614f","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:56:29.845Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"create","filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--high-volume-plyometrics-do-not-build-rebound-fatigue-resistance.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: claim\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.4\"\ntopic: plyometric volume and fatigue resistance\ncategory: [plyometrics, volume, fatigue, motor-control]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Many Sets and Reps of Plyometrics Do Not Build Fatigue Resistance for Repeated Rebounding; Movement Efficiency Does (Nicol et al, 1997)\n\n**What it is** — Stated twice in 5.2.4:\n- \"Many repetitions of plyometric drills frequently are prescribed for some sports because of assumptions that this sort of training will improve fatigue resistance in repeated actions such as rebounding in basketball, yet research has shown this to be untrue (Nicol et al, 1997)\" (PDF p. 280 / printed p. 269, verified against the page image).\n- \"The use of many repetitions and sets of plyometric drills in an attempt to facilitate adaptation to cope with exhaustive bouts of stretch-shortening activities such as rebounding in basketball is not supported by research (Nicol et al, 1997). Instead any improvement in such situations is better mediated by the use of training to enhance movement efficiency. This is a very important point which concerns all explosive and ballistic activities: it is futile to rely predominantly on plyometric drills to enhance sport specific explosive strength and power, because efficiency of motor control is fundamental to all skilled sporting performance\" (PDF pp. 288-289 / printed pp. 277-278, verified against the page images).\n\n**Internal conflict, recorded:** the \"futile to rely predominantly\" sentence is followed on PDF p. 289 / printed p. 278 by \"plyometrics should be the main method in the training of highly qualified athletes for developing explosive strength and reactive ability.\" See [[heuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size]].\n\n**Why it works** — The text's stated reason is \"efficiency of motor control is fundamental to all skilled sporting performance.\" It does not describe the Nicol et al study design or measures. Inference: Nicol, Komi and colleagues published on stretch-shortening-cycle fatigue in the 1990s; the exact 1997 paper was not checked for this entry, so what it measured is unverified here.\n\n**When to apply** — When someone proposes high-rep plyometric circuits to make athletes \"fresher\" in the fourth quarter. Eric's baseline: \"Everything we do is some form of conditioning\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 122). Inference: the text's alternative for repeat-effort tolerance is better movement efficiency in the sport skill itself, not more drill volume; the book also allows \"much shorter intervals with submaximal plyometrics to develop explosive-strength endurance\" (PDF p. 285 / printed p. 274, item 6), so submaximal plyometric endurance work is not ruled out.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Not evidence against plyometrics for explosive strength; the same section calls the shock method's training effect \"very large.\" The claim rests on one citation the book does not describe.\n\n**Example** — The text's own: basketball rebounding as the repeated stretch-shortening task that high plyometric volume was assumed, wrongly, to prepare (PDF p. 280 / printed p. 269).\n\n**Related** — [[heuristic--maximal-plyometric-and-depth-jump-volume-and-rest]]; [[claim--plyometric-injury-risk-comes-from-prescription-not-the-method]]; [[heuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size]]; [[claim--exercise-economy-improves-with-skill-and-interval-style-costs-more]]\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"17636a12-630a-434e-a05c-8b6490390d1f","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
