allows \"much shorter intervals with submaximal plyometrics to develop explosive-strength endurance\" (PDF p. 285 / printed p. 274, item 6), so submaximal plyometric endurance work is not ruled out.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Not evidence against plyometrics for explosive strength; the same section calls the shock method's training effect \"very large.\" The claim rests on one citation the book does not describe.\n\n**Example** — The text's own: basketball rebounding as the repeated stretch-shortening task that high plyometric volume was assumed, wrongly, to prepare (PDF p. 280 / printed p. 269).\n\n**Related** — [[heuristic--maximal-plyometric-and-depth-jump-volume-and-rest]]; [[claim--plyometric-injury-risk-comes-from-prescription-not-the-method]]; [[heuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size]]; [[claim--exercise-economy-improves-with-skill-and-interval-style-costs-more]]\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":19,"oldLines":7,"newStart":19,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," **Internal conflict, recorded:** the \"futile to rely predominantly\" sentence is followed on PDF p. 289 / printed p. 278 by \"plyometrics should be the main method in the training of highly qualified athletes for developing explosive strength and reactive ability.\" See [[heuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size]]."," ","-**Why it works** — The text's stated reason is \"efficiency of motor control is fundamental to all skilled sporting performance.\" It does not describe the Nicol et al study design or measures. Inference: Nicol, Komi and colleagues published on stretch-shortening-cycle fatigue in the 1990s; the exact 1997 paper was not checked for this entry, so what it measured is unverified here.","+**Why it works** — The text's stated reason is \"efficiency of motor control is fundamental to all skilled sporting performance.\" It does not describe the Nicol et al (1997) study design or measures, and the paper was not checked for this entry, so what it 