{"parentUuid":"3f0c4fa6-b359-4817-8bab-b98f83c3fbd9","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffaSwVk9AmKnNRTockT4","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01QUFvX8TZNAenHYiRPDfkgE","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size.md","old_string":"The first reads as the Verkhoshansky shock-method programme; the second follows a Nicol et al (1997) citation in the Siff-voiced material. The book does not reconcile them.","new_string":"The second follows a Nicol et al (1997) citation. Inference: the first reads like the original shock-method programme and the second like a later addition, but the book marks neither passage by author. The book does not reconcile them."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3619,"cache_read_input_tokens":263553,"output_tokens":771,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":171},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3619},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":771,"cache_read_input_tokens":263553,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3619,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3619,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01QUFvX8TZNAenHYiRPDfkgE":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size.md","old_string":"The first reads as the Verkhoshansky shock-method programme; the second follows a Nicol et al (1997) citation in the Siff-voiced material. The book does not reconcile them.","new_string":"The second follows a Nicol et al (1997) citation. Inference: the first reads like the original shock-method programme and the second like a later addition, but the book marks neither passage by author. The book does not reconcile them."}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011CffaSSPXw9SSbtfeMB8pr","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"3e223f28-9c80-4b4d-b3dd-3e7fab8188e9","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:56:43.938Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"3e223f28-9c80-4b4d-b3dd-3e7fab8188e9","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"624a8cec-33fa-4c0a-a445-c72ce718e6ab","agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QUFvX8TZNAenHYiRPDfkgE","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size.md has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"10f096bd-9e45-41d0-9f6a-ab54ebc1f22a","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:56:44.050Z","toolUseResult":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size.md","oldString":"The first reads as the Verkhoshansky shock-method programme; the second follows a Nicol et al (1997) citation in the Siff-voiced material. The book does not reconcile them.","newString":"The second follows a Nicol et al (1997) citation. Inference: the first reads like the original shock-method programme and the second like a later addition, but the book marks neither passage by author. The book does not reconcile them.","originalFile":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.4\"\ntopic: choosing between heavy and plyometric methods\ncategory: [explosive-strength, plyometrics, method-selection, sequencing]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Large Resistance Calls for the Brief Maximal Tension Method, Small Resistance and Reactive Demand Call for Plyometrics; the Book Disagrees With Itself on Whether Plyometrics Should Be the Main Method\n\n**What it is** — The authors' method-selection rules (PDF p. 289 / printed p. 278, verified against the page image unless marked):\n- \"In those cases where explosive force is associated with the overcoming of large resistance, preference should be given to the brief maximal tension method, as used commonly in Olympic weightlifting. In situations where the resistance is small and the working effect depends upon the reactive ability of the muscles, plyometric methods should be given preference.\"\n- Staging: \"Although it should form an integral part of the training programme, plyometrics should not be combined in the beginning stages with resistance exercises such as the brief maximal tension method.\" \"In subsequent stages, plyometrics should be the main method in the training of highly qualified athletes for developing explosive strength and reactive ability.\"\n- Concentric explosive work: \"exercises with maximum effort and mixed regimes using weights equal to 40% of 1RM, or 30% of 1RM (Verkhoshansky, 1963; Papysheva, 1966). Explosive-isometric tension up to 80% of maximum should be used in conjunction with this routine.\"\n- In combined sessions, resistance work goes first: combined programmes beat plyometrics alone \"provided the resistance movements precede the plyometric activities in individual sessions\" (PDF p. 288 / printed p. 277).\n- Testing decides: \"To ascertain whether an athlete needs plyometric or heavy resistance training depends on measuring the *strength deficit* ... Without such testing, prescription of plyometric training tends to remain hit-and-miss\" (PDF p. 284 / printed p. 273; repeated at PDF p. 293 / printed p. 282 in a grammatically broken sentence).\n\n**Internal conflict, recorded:** \"plyometrics should be the main method in the training of highly qualified athletes\" (PDF p. 289 / printed p. 278) against, half a page earlier, \"it is futile to rely predominantly on plyometric drills to enhance sport specific explosive strength and power, because efficiency of motor control is fundamental to all skilled sporting performance\" (PDF pp. 288-289 / printed pp. 277-278). The first reads as the Verkhoshansky shock-method programme; the second follows a Nicol et al (1997) citation in the Siff-voiced material. The book does not reconcile them.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors tie each method to the force demand: heavy brief tension for large resistance, reactive shock loading for small resistance. The strength deficit decides which an individual needs (Ch1; see [[heuristic--strength-deficit-sets-training-method-choice]], which records that the book also states the deficit rule two ways).\n\n**When to apply** — Eric's baseline: \"we incorporate the countermovement jump and the isometric mid-thigh pull to get a dynamic strength index, to determine whether we need more plyometric training or more max-strength training\" (Eric, interview 2026-09-28, Q21; `docs/method-baseline.md` line 76). Inference: DSI plays the role the authors give the strength deficit (both compare a fast expression of force with a maximal one to choose plyometric versus heavy work), but they are different measures; Eric's baseline Tension 17 records that the DSI cutoffs were never validated and that IMTP peak force drives most of the ratio (line 261). Inference: for linemen, whose sport force is against large resistance, the authors' rule favours brief maximal tension; for skill positions, whose force is against bodymass, it favours plyometrics.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Do not cite Supertraining as saying plyometrics should be the main method without the \"futile to rely predominantly\" sentence, or the reverse. \"Beginning stages\" and \"highly qualified\" are not defined. The 40% and 30% of 1RM loads and the 80% explosive-isometric tension are given without sets, reps or rest.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own contrast: Olympic weightlifting (large resistance, brief maximal tension) versus jumping from depth (small resistance, reactive ability) (PDF p. 289 / printed p. 278).\n\n**Related** — [[heuristic--strength-deficit-sets-training-method-choice]]; [[framework--strength-deficit-jump-comparison-estimate]]; [[claim--high-volume-plyometrics-do-not-build-rebound-fatigue-resistance]]; [[claim--depth-jump-training-beat-higher-volume-traditional-training-in-jumpers]]; [[claim--heavy-squats-and-light-fast-lifts-miss-the-eccentric-concentric-switch]]\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":20,"oldLines":7,"newStart":20,"newLines":7,"lines":[" - In combined sessions, resistance work goes first: combined programmes beat plyometrics alone \"provided the resistance movements precede the plyometric activities in individual sessions\" (PDF p. 288 / printed p. 277)."," - Testing decides: \"To ascertain whether an athlete needs plyometric or heavy resistance training depends on measuring the *strength deficit* ... Without such testing, prescription of plyometric training tends to remain hit-and-miss\" (PDF p. 284 / printed p. 273; repeated at PDF p. 293 / printed p. 282 in a grammatically broken sentence)."," ","-**Internal conflict, recorded:** \"plyometrics should be the main method in the training of highly qualified athletes\" (PDF p. 289 / printed p. 278) against, half a page earlier, \"it is futile to rely predominantly on plyometric drills to enhance sport specific explosive strength and power, because efficiency of motor control is fundamental to all skilled sporting performance\" (PDF pp. 288-289 / printed pp. 277-278). The first reads as the Verkhoshansky shock-method programme; the second follows a Nicol et al (1997) citation in the Siff-voiced material. The book does not reconcile them.","+**Internal conflict, recorded:** \"plyometrics should be the main method in the training of highly qualified athletes\" (PDF p. 289 / printed p. 278) against, half a page earlier, \"it is futile to rely predominantly on plyometric drills to enhance sport specific explosive strength and power, because efficiency of motor control is fundamental to all skilled sporting performance\" (PDF pp. 288-289 / printed pp. 277-278). The second follows a Nicol et al (1997) citation. Inference: the first reads like the original shock-method programme and the second like a later addition, but the book marks neither passage by author. The book does not reconcile them."," "," **Why it works** — The authors tie each method to the force demand: heavy brief tension for large resistance, reactive shock loading for small resistance. The strength deficit decides which an individual needs (Ch1; see [[heuristic--strength-deficit-sets-training-method-choice]], which records that the book also states the deficit rule two ways)."," "]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"3e223f28-9c80-4b4d-b3dd-3e7fab8188e9","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"10f096bd-9e45-41d0-9f6a-ab54ebc1f22a","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","attachment":{"type":"total_tokens_reminder","text":"<total_tokens>14732055 tokens left</total_tokens>"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"6e0f4f1f-b0e9-4ac0-8edc-e951d0715cca","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:56:44.061Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\n<total_tokens>14732055 tokens left</total_tokens>\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"6e0f4f1f-b0e9-4ac0-8edc-e951d0715cca","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","attachment":{"type":"deferred_tools_record","entries":[],"toolInputCopies":[{"id":"toolu_01N4bBkqfqM1tFAa83JXTfhK","copy":"wire"},{"id":"toolu_01QUFvX8TZNAenHYiRPDfkgE","copy":"wire"}]},"type":"attachment","uuid":"37250e8d-5ac2-4e40-8086-22d943ebb90d","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:56:54.040Z","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"37250e8d-5ac2-4e40-8086-22d943ebb90d","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffaU5guBwTFakAC3W8wc","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1064,"cache_read_input_tokens":267172,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1064,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":540,"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CffaTUzL4YpDuWwLwdFvn","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"e15aafb4-f1c0-4a74-bee6-778327feeffb","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:56:54.040Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"e15aafb4-f1c0-4a74-bee6-778327feeffb","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffaU5guBwTFakAC3W8wc","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01KaGiWxMSB8WjeHGEqNJx4G","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--plyometric-surface-footwear-and-simple-apparatus.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.4\"\ntopic: plyometric surfaces and apparatus\ncategory: [plyometrics, surfaces, footwear, equipment]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Plyometric Surfaces and Footwear: Not Too Soft for Rebounds, Yet Sand and Soft Mats Have Their Uses; Swings and Pogo Sticks as Cheap Powermetric Tools\n\n**What it is** — Surface and apparatus statements in 5.2.4:\n- \"Footwear or the floor covering should not be very soft or excessively shock absorbent, since this may impair ankle stability, diminish the storage of elastic energy in the SEC (e.g. tendons) and delay the triggering of the positive supportive reaction of the foot (see 6.1.2) when contacting the floor\" (PDF p. 284 / printed p. 273, verified against the page image).\n- The surface \"can also have a significant effect on the outcome of the exercise, as alluded to earlier in comments made about the alterations in motor skill and proprioceptive sensitivity associated with the use of soft-soled shoes and very resilient jumping surfaces. A little known finding is that plyometric and jumping drills performed according to carefully planned progressive patterns in sand can significantly enhance jumping skills in a short period, even as short as 5 days in duration (Montagni & Cardinale, 1996)\" (PDF p. 289 / printed p. 278, verified).\n- For depth landings: Dursenev and Raevsky used \"soft gymnastic mats\", and the authors advise landing \"on suitably firm shock-absorbing surfaces\" (PDF p. 288 / printed p. 277, verified).\n- Simple apparatus: a children's swing near a wall, where the feet push off the wall after swinging from \"a suitable height\", progressed by raising the backswing; longer ropes give a slower repetition rate; uprights in front allow upper-body rebounds. The \"Pogo-Stick\", with intensity \"determined by the height of rebound\", and boxers' skipping are also offered as \"powermetric\" tools (PDF p. 289 / printed p. 278, verified).\n\n**Internal tension, recorded:** p. 273 warns against very soft, shock-absorbent surfaces for plyometrics, while p. 278 reports fast jumping-skill gains from progressive drills in sand, and p. 277 pairs a soft-mat landing study with advice to land on \"suitably firm shock-absorbing surfaces.\" The book does not reconcile these. Inference: they may refer to different aims (sand for skill and lower impact; firm surfaces for maximal shock rebounds), but the book does not say so.\n\n**Why it works** — The p. 273 reasons are stated (ankle stability, SEC elastic storage, the foot's positive supportive reaction). No mechanism is given for the sand result. The swing physics is stated by the authors as rope length setting the oscillation time.\n\n**When to apply** — Choosing where to run maximal rebound work: a firm, not very soft surface and non-cushioned footwear, per p. 273. Inference: sand is an option for early-phase or high-volume submaximal jumping, consistent with the p. 278 finding, but not for maximal depth jumps.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The Montagni & Cardinale (1996) result is cited without design or measures; \"jumping skills\" is not defined. The swing and pogo stick suggestions are the text's ideas, with no data.\n\n**Example** — The text's own: beach volleyball and beach handball as examples of the value of sand training (PDF p. 289 / printed p. 278).\n\n**Related** — [[heuristic--depth-jump-execution-cues-landing-gaze-breath]]; [[claim--depth-landings-from-2-to-3-2-m-produce-forces-over-20-times-bodyweight]]; [[heuristic--resisted-and-water-plyometrics]]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1064,"cache_read_input_tokens":267172,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1064,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01KaGiWxMSB8WjeHGEqNJx4G":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--plyometric-surface-footwear-and-simple-apparatus.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.4\"\ntopic: plyometric surfaces and apparatus\ncategory: [plyometrics, surfaces, footwear, equipment]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Plyometric Surfaces and Footwear: Not Too Soft for Rebounds, Yet Sand and Soft Mats Have Their Uses; Swings and Pogo Sticks as Cheap Powermetric Tools\n\n**What it is** — Surface and apparatus statements in 5.2.4:\n- \"Footwear or the floor covering should not be very soft or excessively shock absorbent, since this may impair ankle stability, diminish the storage of elastic energy in the SEC (e.g. tendons) and delay the triggering of the positive supportive reaction of the foot (see 6.1.2) when contacting the floor\" (PDF p. 284 / printed p. 273, verified against the page image).\n- The surface \"can also have a significant effect on the outcome of the exercise, as alluded to earlier in comments made about the alterations in motor skill and proprioceptive sensitivity associated with the use of soft-soled shoes and very resilient jumping surfaces. A little known finding is that plyometric and jumping drills performed according to carefully planned progressive patterns in sand can significantly enhance jumping skills in a short period, even as short as 5 days in duration (Montagni & Cardinale, 1996)\" (PDF p. 289 / printed p. 278, verified).\n- For depth landings: Dursenev and Raevsky used \"soft gymnastic mats\", and the authors advise landing \"on suitably firm shock-absorbing surfaces\" (PDF p. 288 / printed p. 277, verified).\n- Simple apparatus: a children's swing near a wall, where the feet push off the wall after swinging from \"a suitable height\", progressed by raising the backswing; longer ropes give a slower repetition rate; uprights in front allow upper-body rebounds. The \"Pogo-Stick\", with intensity \"determined by the height of rebound\", and boxers' skipping are also offered as \"powermetric\" tools (PDF p. 289 / printed p. 278, verified).\n\n**Internal tension, recorded:** p. 273 warns against very soft, shock-absorbent surfaces for plyometrics, while p. 278 reports fast jumping-skill gains from progressive drills in sand, and p. 277 pairs a soft-mat landing study with advice to land on \"suitably firm shock-absorbing surfaces.\" The book does not reconcile these. Inference: they may refer to different aims (sand for skill and lower impact; firm surfaces for maximal shock rebounds), but the book does not say so.\n\n**Why it works** — The p. 273 reasons are stated (ankle stability, SEC elastic storage, the foot's positive supportive reaction). No mechanism is given for the sand result. The swing physics is stated by the authors as rope length setting the oscillation time.\n\n**When to apply** — Choosing where to run maximal rebound work: a firm, not very soft surface and non-cushioned footwear, per p. 273. Inference: sand is an option for early-phase or high-volume submaximal jumping, consistent with the p. 278 finding, but not for maximal depth jumps.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The Montagni & Cardinale (1996) result is cited without design or measures; \"jumping skills\" is not defined. The swing and pogo stick suggestions are the text's ideas, with no data.\n\n**Example** — The text's own: beach volleyball and beach handball as examples of the value of sand training (PDF p. 289 / printed p. 278).\n\n**Related** — [[heuristic--depth-jump-execution-cues-landing-gaze-breath]]; [[claim--depth-landings-from-2-to-3-2-m-produce-forces-over-20-times-bodyweight]]; [[heuristic--resisted-and-water-plyometrics]]\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffaTUzL4YpDuWwLwdFvn","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"8b10bc55-01c4-4efb-9179-249b38e5f0a2","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:57:07.179Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"8b10bc55-01c4-4efb-9179-249b38e5f0a2","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"624a8cec-33fa-4c0a-a445-c72ce718e6ab","agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KaGiWxMSB8WjeHGEqNJx4G","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--plyometric-surface-footwear-and-simple-apparatus.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"cafa448f-40c8-4d7f-b52e-60ece271595c","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:57:07.273Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"create","filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--plyometric-surface-footwear-and-simple-apparatus.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.4\"\ntopic: plyometric surfaces and apparatus\ncategory: [plyometrics, surfaces, footwear, equipment]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Plyometric Surfaces and Footwear: Not Too Soft for Rebounds, Yet Sand and Soft Mats Have Their Uses; Swings and Pogo Sticks as Cheap Powermetric Tools\n\n**What it is** — Surface and apparatus statements in 5.2.4:\n- \"Footwear or the floor covering should not be very soft or excessively shock absorbent, since this may impair ankle stability, diminish the storage of elastic energy in the SEC (e.g. tendons) and delay the triggering of the positive supportive reaction of the foot (see 6.1.2) when contacting the floor\" (PDF p. 284 / printed p. 273, verified against the page image).\n- The surface \"can also have a significant effect on the outcome of the exercise, as alluded to earlier in comments made about the alterations in motor skill and proprioceptive sensitivity associated with the use of soft-soled shoes and very resilient jumping surfaces. A little known finding is that plyometric and jumping drills performed according to carefully planned progressive patterns in sand can significantly enhance jumping skills in a short period, even as short as 5 days in duration (Montagni & Cardinale, 1996)\" (PDF p. 289 / printed p. 278, verified).\n- For depth landings: Dursenev and Raevsky used \"soft gymnastic mats\", and the authors advise landing \"on suitably firm shock-absorbing surfaces\" (PDF p. 288 / printed p. 277, verified).\n- Simple apparatus: a children's swing near a wall, where the feet push off the wall after swinging from \"a suitable height\", progressed by raising the backswing; longer ropes give a slower repetition rate; uprights in front allow upper-body rebounds. The \"Pogo-Stick\", with intensity \"determined by the height of rebound\", and boxers' skipping are also offered as \"powermetric\" tools (PDF p. 289 / printed p. 278, verified).\n\n**Internal tension, recorded:** p. 273 warns against very soft, shock-absorbent surfaces for plyometrics, while p. 278 reports fast jumping-skill gains from progressive drills in sand, and p. 277 pairs a soft-mat landing study with advice to land on \"suitably firm shock-absorbing surfaces.\" The book does not reconcile these. Inference: they may refer to different aims (sand for skill and lower impact; firm surfaces for maximal shock rebounds), but the book does not say so.\n\n**Why it works** — The p. 273 reasons are stated (ankle stability, SEC elastic storage, the foot's positive supportive reaction). No mechanism is given for the sand result. The swing physics is stated by the authors as rope length setting the oscillation time.\n\n**When to apply** — Choosing where to run maximal rebound work: a firm, not very soft surface and non-cushioned footwear, per p. 273. Inference: sand is an option for early-phase or high-volume submaximal jumping, consistent with the p. 278 finding, but not for maximal depth jumps.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The Montagni & Cardinale (1996) result is cited without design or measures; \"jumping skills\" is not defined. The swing and pogo stick suggestions are the text's ideas, with no data.\n\n**Example** — The text's own: beach volleyball and beach handball as examples of the value of sand training (PDF p. 289 / printed p. 278).\n\n**Related** — [[heuristic--depth-jump-execution-cues-landing-gaze-breath]]; [[claim--depth-landings-from-2-to-3-2-m-produce-forces-over-20-times-bodyweight]]; [[heuristic--resisted-and-water-plyometrics]]\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"8b10bc55-01c4-4efb-9179-249b38e5f0a2","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"cafa448f-40c8-4d7f-b52e-60ece271595c","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffaU5guBwTFakAC3W8wc","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01JrQw6nyFtEF8UL3UWw2R1G","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--resisted-and-water-plyometrics.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.4\"\ntopic: resisted and aquatic plyometrics\ncategory: [plyometrics, resisted-training, aquatic-training, safety]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Resisted Plyometrics Only Occasionally and Never With Heavy Weights or Stiff Cords in Depth Jumps; Water Plyometrics Are a Safer Submaximal Option\n\n**What it is** — Under \"Resisted and Water Plyometric Training\" (PDF pp. 292-293 / printed pp. 281-282; p. 293 verified against the page image, p. 292 from the text layer):\n- Resistance can be imposed in the amortisation phase, the concentric rebound or both: light weights, or \"elastic cords attached to a harness located near the waist or hips.\" Both \"increase the landing impact and ostensibly increase the strength of reflex muscle contraction\", but \"also increase the risk of impact and overload injuries and should be applied occasionally with great caution. Even normal running or hurdling with very light weights constitutes a form of loaded plyometric training.\"\n- Resistance methods \"should not use heavy weights or stiff elastic cords during depth jumps, since this increases the coupling time and imposes unnecessarily large loads on the spine and the tendons of the lower extremities, especially during the landing or contact phase.\"\n- Water or \"aqua plyometrics\" decrease landing force and increase resistance in the recoil. They do \"not offer maximal or shock method plyometrics\" but serve as preparatory or submaximal plyometrics, \"especially for one-legged drills.\" Water depth sets the resistance: \"chest or shoulder high water offering greater resistance during the landing and take-off phases, less intense eccentric muscle activity, smaller impact forces and enhanced safety.\" Repeated aqua jumps for \"rebound explosive muscle endurance\" are \"considerably safer than their land-based equivalents, though they should not completely replace the latter, since they do not adequately develop the specific neuromuscular patterns or functional needs of explosive sports.\"\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reasons are stated: heavy load lengthens the coupling time (taking the drill out of classical plyometrics) and loads spine and tendons; water reduces impact and eccentric intensity.\n\n**When to apply** — Inference: aqua jumps suit early return-to-training or single-leg plyometric progressions where impact must be limited, consistent with the authors' \"especially for one-legged drills.\" Light loads during running or hurdling are already loaded plyometrics, per the authors, so they belong in the plyometric count.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Not a replacement for land plyometrics for explosive sport (the authors' own limit). \"Light\" and \"heavy\" weights and \"stiff\" cords are not quantified. The \"water plyos\" in aerobics classes the authors call pseudo-plyometric (PDF p. 284 / printed p. 273) differ from these structured aqua drills; do not conflate them.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: chest- or shoulder-high water for one-legged preparatory jumps (PDF p. 293 / printed p. 282).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--plyometric-training-classification-impact-intensity-and-function]]; [[claim--thrown-load-height-decreases-with-load-increases-with-drop-height]] (Ch4.2.3, heavier loads not beneficial for kinetic-energy work); [[heuristic--plyometric-surface-footwear-and-simple-apparatus]]; [[heuristic--plyometric-training-coupling-under-0-15-s-feedforward-and-force-time-pattern]]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1064,"cache_read_input_tokens":267172,"output_tokens":2979,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":84},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1064},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2979,"cache_read_input_tokens":267172,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1064,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1064,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01JrQw6nyFtEF8UL3UWw2R1G":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--resisted-and-water-plyometrics.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.4\"\ntopic: resisted and aquatic plyometrics\ncategory: [plyometrics, resisted-training, aquatic-training, safety]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Resisted Plyometrics Only Occasionally and Never With Heavy Weights or Stiff Cords in Depth Jumps; Water Plyometrics Are a Safer Submaximal Option\n\n**What it is** — Under \"Resisted and Water Plyometric Training\" (PDF pp. 292-293 / printed pp. 281-282; p. 293 verified against the page image, p. 292 from the text layer):\n- Resistance can be imposed in the amortisation phase, the concentric rebound or both: light weights, or \"elastic cords attached to a harness located near the waist or hips.\" Both \"increase the landing impact and ostensibly increase the strength of reflex muscle contraction\", but \"also increase the risk of impact and overload injuries and should be applied occasionally with great caution. Even normal running or hurdling with very light weights constitutes a form of loaded plyometric training.\"\n- Resistance methods \"should not use heavy weights or stiff elastic cords during depth jumps, since this increases the coupling time and imposes unnecessarily large loads on the spine and the tendons of the lower extremities, especially during the landing or contact phase.\"\n- Water or \"aqua plyometrics\" decrease landing force and increase resistance in the recoil. They do \"not offer maximal or shock method plyometrics\" but serve as preparatory or submaximal plyometrics, \"especially for one-legged drills.\" Water depth sets the resistance: \"chest or shoulder high water offering greater resistance during the landing and take-off phases, less intense eccentric muscle activity, smaller impact forces and enhanced safety.\" Repeated aqua jumps for \"rebound explosive muscle endurance\" are \"considerably safer than their land-based equivalents, though they should not completely replace the latter, since they do not adequately develop the specific neuromuscular patterns or functional needs of explosive sports.\"\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reasons are stated: heavy load lengthens the coupling time (taking the drill out of classical plyometrics) and loads spine and tendons; water reduces impact and eccentric intensity.\n\n**When to apply** — Inference: aqua jumps suit early return-to-training or single-leg plyometric progressions where impact must be limited, consistent with the authors' \"especially for one-legged drills.\" Light loads during running or hurdling are already loaded plyometrics, per the authors, so they belong in the plyometric count.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Not a replacement for land plyometrics for explosive sport (the authors' own limit). \"Light\" and \"heavy\" weights and \"stiff\" cords are not quantified. The \"water plyos\" in aerobics classes the authors call pseudo-plyometric (PDF p. 284 / printed p. 273) differ from these structured aqua drills; do not conflate them.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: chest- or shoulder-high water for one-legged preparatory jumps (PDF p. 293 / printed p. 282).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--plyometric-training-classification-impact-intensity-and-function]]; [[claim--thrown-load-height-decreases-with-load-increases-with-drop-height]] (Ch4.2.3, heavier loads not beneficial for kinetic-energy work); [[heuristic--plyometric-surface-footwear-and-simple-apparatus]]; [[heuristic--plyometric-training-coupling-under-0-15-s-feedforward-and-force-time-pattern]]\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011CffaTUzL4YpDuWwLwdFvn","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"7b9448ed-9e0b-4ebb-8034-0848228cecb3","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:57:18.095Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
