not use heavy weights or stiff elastic cords during depth jumps, since this increases the coupling time and imposes unnecessarily large loads on the spine and the tendons of the lower extremities, especially during the landing or contact phase.\"\n- Water or \"aqua plyometrics\" decrease landing force and increase resistance in the recoil. They do \"not offer maximal or shock method plyometrics\" but serve as preparatory or submaximal plyometrics, \"especially for one-legged drills.\" Water depth sets the resistance: \"chest or shoulder high water offering greater resistance during the landing and take-off phases, less intense eccentric muscle activity, smaller impact forces and enhanced safety.\" Repeated aqua jumps for \"rebound explosive muscle endurance\" are \"considerably safer than their land-based equivalents, though they should not completely replace the latter, since they do not adequately develop the specific neuromuscular patterns or functional needs of explosive sports.\"\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reasons are stated: heavy load lengthens the coupling time (taking the drill out of classical plyometrics) and loads spine and tendons; water reduces impact and eccentric intensity.\n\n**When to apply** — Inference: aqua jumps suit early return-to-training or single-leg plyometric progressions where impact must be limited, consistent with the authors' \"especially for one-legged drills.\" Light loads during running or hurdling are already loaded plyometrics, per the authors, so they belong in the plyometric count.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Not a replacement for land plyometrics for explosive sport (the authors' own limit). \"Light\" and \"heavy\" weights and \"stiff\" cords are not quantified. The \"water plyos\" in aerobics classes the authors call pseudo-plyometric (PDF p. 284 / printed p. 273) differ from these structured aqua drills; do not conflate them.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: chest- or shoulder-high water for one-legged preparatory jumps (PDF p. 293 / printed p. 282).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--plyometric-training-classification-impact-intensity-and-function]]; [[claim--thrown-load-height-decreases-with-load-increases-with-drop-height]] (Ch4.2.3, heavier loads not beneficial for kinetic-energy work); [[heuristic--plyometric-surface-footwear-and-simple-apparatus]]; [[heuristic--plyometric-training-coupling-under-0-15-s-feedforward-and-force-time-pattern]]\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"7b9448ed-9e0b-4ebb-8034-0848228cecb3","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"c8f2a8bd-66ee-492a-9744-45e753c5e0ce","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","attachment":{"type":"total_tokens_reminder","text":"<total_tokens>14728783 tokens left</total_tokens>"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"b171c07e-d4fa-46a1-a51e-e52c19f412d0","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:57:18.207Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\n<total_tokens>14728783 tokens left</total_tokens>\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"b171c07e-d4fa-46a1-a51e-e52c19f412d0","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffaWEKs29bpRthUafuc5","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3245,"cache_read_input_tokens":268236,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3245,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":796,"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CffaVzmnCuC9knPuksQNu","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"f84e49ef-da5b-4ea8-b00d-0beedffd0e53","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:57:23.589Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"f84e49ef-da5b-4ea8-b00d-0beedffd0e53","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffaWEKs29bpRthUafuc5","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01NrFwoYE2KWRuRxGCHdAd7H","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--match-plyometric-drills-to-limb-symmetry-and-rotational-dominance.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.4\"\ntopic: asymmetric plyometrics\ncategory: [plyometrics, asymmetry, specificity, exercise-selection]\nconfidence: untested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Match Plyometric Drills to the Sport's Symmetry, Limb Dominance and Rotational Dominance (Asymmetric Plyometrics)\n\n**What it is** — Under \"Asymmetric Plyometrics\" (PDF pp. 289-290 / printed pp. 278-279; p. 289 verified against the page image, p. 290 from the text layer):\n- Bilateral depth jumps and medicine ball drills \"may be valuable for sports which require this type of limb action, but are far less beneficial for sports which involve alternate action of the limbs in different patterns of movement.\"\n- \"before selecting plyometric drills, it is important to categorize sporting movements as cyclic, acyclic, unilateral, bilateral, symmetric, asymmetric or as various combinations\", and distinguish \"dominant and non-dominant limbs, as well as driving leg and swing leg in running, since the efficiency of movement depends primarily on central nervous factors.\" \"all activity of the legs in sport is related in specific spatio-temporal patterns to motion in the arms.\"\n- Rotational dominance: \"a right-handed baseballer, golfer or shotputter displays anti-clockwise rotational dominance, whereas the left-hander displays clockwise rotational dominance\", which bears on medicine ball catching and throwing drills.\n- \"Research has shown that the transfer of strength developed in bilateral training (e.g. using squats or power cleans) offers specific improvement in performance in bilateral events such as the squat clean and snatch in weightlifting, while unilateral training (e.g. with dumbbells or split cleans) enhances performances more effectively in unilateral activity such as running, jumping or karate\" (no citation given).\n- Named methods: the *alternate-asymmetric method* (\"strength and jumping exercises alternately with each leg, rather than with both legs simultaneously\") and the *limb-synchronisation method* (each leg synchronised with the opposite arm or both arms, \"depending on the given activity (e.g. sprinting vs volleyball, basketball or trampolining)\").\n- Siff's own laboratory work at the University of the Witwatersrand (\"research by the author\", Siff writing): drill time, peak force, RFD and distance from a dominant versus non-dominant limb start \"reveal that it may be relevant to individualise plyometric drills to match the functional asymmetry and limb dominance characteristics of the athlete.\"\n\n**Why it works** — The stated reason is central nervous control: \"motor performance is determined by how competently one programs the central nervous computer of the body, taking functional asymmetry into careful consideration.\" No data are given.\n\n**When to apply** — Inference: football movements are mostly unilateral and alternating (sprinting, cutting, a lineman's drive step), so by this rule bilateral depth jumps would be general (non-functional) work and alternate-leg bounds and hops the more specific choice; for rotational throwing (quarterbacks), medicine ball work would account for the thrower's rotational dominance.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — `untested`: the bilateral-unilateral transfer claim has no citation in the passage, and the Witwatersrand study gives no numbers or subjects (\"may be relevant\"). The book's other position on symmetry warns against chasing perfect bilateral symmetry (see [[claim--pursuing-perfect-bilateral-symmetry-can-be-counterproductive]]); this entry is about matching drills to a sport's asymmetry, not correcting it.\n\n**Example** — The text's own: a right-handed shot putter's anti-clockwise rotational dominance, and sprinting (leg with opposite arm) versus volleyball or basketball (leg with both arms) for limb synchronisation (PDF p. 290 / printed p. 279).\n\n**Related** — [[claim--bilateral-deficit-and-limb-training-specificity]]; [[claim--pursuing-perfect-bilateral-symmetry-can-be-counterproductive]]; [[framework--plyometric-training-classification-impact-intensity-and-function]]; [[framework--five-criteria-of-dynamic-correspondence]]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3245,"cache_read_input_tokens":268236,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3245,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01NrFwoYE2KWRuRxGCHdAd7H":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--match-plyometric-drills-to-limb-symmetry-and-rotational-dominance.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.4\"\ntopic: asymmetric plyometrics\ncategory: [plyometrics, asymmetry, specificity, exercise-selection]\nconfidence: untested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Match Plyometric Drills to the Sport's Symmetry, Limb Dominance and Rotational Dominance (Asymmetric Plyometrics)\n\n**What it is** — Under \"Asymmetric Plyometrics\" (PDF pp. 289-290 / printed pp. 278-279; p. 289 verified against the page image, p. 290 from the text layer):\n- Bilateral depth jumps and medicine ball drills \"may be valuable for sports which require this type of limb action, but are far less beneficial for sports which involve alternate action of the limbs in different patterns of movement.\"\n- \"before selecting plyometric drills, it is important to categorize sporting movements as cyclic, acyclic, unilateral, bilateral, symmetric, asymmetric or as various combinations\", and distinguish \"dominant and non-dominant limbs, as well as driving leg and swing leg in running, since the efficiency of movement depends primarily on central nervous factors.\" \"all activity of the legs in sport is related in specific spatio-temporal patterns to motion in the arms.\"\n- Rotational dominance: \"a right-handed baseballer, golfer or shotputter displays anti-clockwise rotational dominance, whereas the left-hander displays clockwise rotational dominance\", which bears on medicine ball catching and throwing drills.\n- \"Research has shown that the transfer of strength developed in bilateral training (e.g. using squats or power cleans) offers specific improvement in performance in bilateral events such as the squat clean and snatch in weightlifting, while unilateral training (e.g. with dumbbells or split cleans) enhances performances more effectively in unilateral activity such as running, jumping or karate\" (no citation given).\n- Named methods: the *alternate-asymmetric method* (\"strength and jumping exercises alternately with each leg, rather than with both legs simultaneously\") and the *limb-synchronisation method* (each leg synchronised with the opposite arm or both arms, \"depending on the given activity (e.g. sprinting vs volleyball, basketball or trampolining)\").\n- Siff's own laboratory work at the University of the Witwatersrand (\"research by the author\", Siff writing): drill time, peak force, RFD and distance from a dominant versus non-dominant limb start \"reveal that it may be relevant to individualise plyometric drills to match the functional asymmetry and limb dominance characteristics of the athlete.\"\n\n**Why it works** — The stated reason is central nervous control: \"motor performance is determined by how competently one programs the central nervous computer of the body, taking functional asymmetry into careful consideration.\" No data are given.\n\n**When to apply** — Inference: football movements are mostly unilateral and alternating (sprinting, cutting, a lineman's drive step), so by this rule bilateral depth jumps would be general (non-functional) work and alternate-leg bounds and hops the more specific choice; for rotational throwing (quarterbacks), medicine ball work would account for the thrower's rotational dominance.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — `untested`: the bilateral-unilateral transfer claim has no citation in the passage, and the Witwatersrand study gives no numbers or subjects (\"may be relevant\"). The book's other position on symmetry warns against chasing perfect bilateral symmetry (see [[claim--pursuing-perfect-bilateral-symmetry-can-be-counterproductive]]); this entry is about matching drills to a sport's asymmetry, not correcting it.\n\n**Example** — The text's own: a right-handed shot putter's anti-clockwise rotational dominance, and sprinting (leg with opposite arm) versus volleyball or basketball (leg with both arms) for limb synchronisation (PDF p. 290 / printed p. 279).\n\n**Related** — [[claim--bilateral-deficit-and-limb-training-specificity]]; [[claim--pursuing-perfect-bilateral-symmetry-can-be-counterproductive]]; [[framework--plyometric-training-classification-impact-intensity-and-function]]; [[framework--five-criteria-of-dynamic-correspondence]]\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffaVzmnCuC9knPuksQNu","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"005f7c18-b306-4334-9885-4b62218bc26b","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:57:36.240Z","advisorModel":"clau