e the efficiency of movement depends primarily on central nervous factors.\" \"all activity of the legs in sport is related in specific spatio-temporal patterns to motion in the arms.\"\n- Rotational dominance: \"a right-handed baseballer, golfer or shotputter displays anti-clockwise rotational dominance, whereas the left-hander displays clockwise rotational dominance\", which bears on medicine ball catching and throwing drills.\n- \"Research has shown that the transfer of strength developed in bilateral training (e.g. using squats or power cleans) offers specific improvement in performance in bilateral events such as the squat clean and snatch in weightlifting, while unilateral training (e.g. with dumbbells or split cleans) enhances performances more effectively in unilateral activity such as running, jumping or karate\" (no citation given).\n- Named methods: the *alternate-asymmetric method* (\"strength and jumping exercises alternately with each leg, rather than with both legs simultaneously\") and the *limb-synchronisation method* (each leg synchronised with the opposite arm or both arms, \"depending on the given activity (e.g. sprinting vs volleyball, basketball or trampolining)\").\n- Siff's own laboratory work at the University of the Witwatersrand (\"research by the author\", Siff writing): drill time, peak force, RFD and distance from a dominant versus non-dominant limb start \"reveal that it may be relevant to individualise plyometric drills to match the functional asymmetry and limb dominance characteristics of the athlete.\"\n\n**Why it works** — The stated reason is central nervous control: \"motor performance is determined by how competently one programs the central nervous computer of the body, taking functional asymmetry into careful consideration.\" No data are given.\n\n**When to apply** — Inference: football movements are mostly unilateral and alternating (sprinting, cutting, a lineman's drive step), so by this rule bilateral depth jumps would be general (non-functional) work and alternate-leg bounds and hops the more specific choice; for rotational throwing (quarterbacks), medicine ball work would account for the thrower's rotational dominance.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — `untested`: the bilateral-unilateral transfer claim has no citation in the passage, and the Witwatersrand study gives no numbers or subjects (\"may be relevant\"). The book's other position on symmetry warns against chasing perfect bilateral symmetry (see [[claim--pursuing-perfect-bilateral-symmetry-can-be-counterproductive]]); this entry is about matching drills to a sport's asymmetry, not correcting it.\n\n**Example** — The text's own: a right-handed shot putter's anti-clockwise rotational dominance, and sprinting (leg with opposite arm) versus volleyball or basketball (leg with both arms) for limb synchronisation (PDF p. 290 / printed p. 279).\n\n**Related** — [[claim--bilateral-deficit-and-limb-training-specificity]]; [[claim--pursuing-perfect-bilateral-symmetry-can-be-counterproductive]]; [[framework--plyometric-training-classification-impact-intensity-and-function]]; [[framework--five-criteria-of-dynamic-correspondence]]\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"005f7c18-b306-4334-9885-4b62218bc26b","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"e491b84c-e4a6-4693-bafa-9e4b6e72b107","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffaWEKs29bpRthUafuc5","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Q9htfeCKJcyzUX7qDa2nXd","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--non-impact-plyometrics-ericson-and-martial-arts.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.4\"\ntopic: non-impact plyometrics\ncategory: [plyometrics, non-impact, upper-body, rehabilitation]\nconfidence: untested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Non-Impact Plyometrics: Explosive Eccentric Recoil Without Surface Contact (Ericson's Seated System, Martial Arts Snaps)\n\n**What it is** — Siff's section (he refers to \"the author\" doing force-plate analysis and to \"his wife, Lisa Ericson\") argues that plyometric action does not need impact (PDF pp. 290-292 / printed pp. 279-281, text layer):\n- Western and even Russian practice treats \"shock contact of parts of the body with some surface\" as the common denominator of plyometrics, but \"this is not the only way of producing plyometric action.\" The non-impact variety was recognised when Siff analysed martial arts movements and Ericson's seated fitness system (Supersitting, later SMART).\n- Ericson's system: \"very rapid wide-range movements choreographed at rates exceeding 240 beats per minute, interspersed with rhythmic transitional PNF-type patterns\", over sessions of about 60 minutes, movements \"initiated and terminated explosively in sets of uninterrupted sequences\" that change form and direction \"to avoid excessive fatigue, habituation or injury.\"\n- It counts as non-impact plyometrics \"because it obeys all the requirements of plyometrics as originally conceived\": \"a ballistic action which is rapidly terminated by eccentric and explosive isometric muscle contraction, followed immediately by a powerful concentric recoil mediated by recruitment of the myotatic stretch reflex and augmented by the release of elastic energy.\"\n- Comparison with karate: karate uses few repetitions with brief intervals, and the punch often contacts its target briefly, \"sufficient to dissipate much of the elastic energy\"; Ericson's transition is \"usually shorter\" with many continuous repetitions, which \"probably explains the differences in functional and structural development.\"\n- Intensity: most class movements are submaximal plyometrics; \"Performed in the form of all-out single movements, they become maximal non-impact plyometrics.\" \"maximal impact plyometrics will generally increase muscle tension to a greater extent than the equivalent non-impact movements for the leg and hip extensors (e.g. the karate thrust-kick).\"\n- Uses: maximal non-impact plyometrics, like submaximal impact plyometrics, \"can serve as a preparatory tool for maximal impact plyometrics\", for other qualities (\"strength, muscle hypertrophy, muscle endurance and speed-strength endurance\"), or to train selected muscle groups \"instead of unnecessarily stressing the entire musculature.\"\n- Sport sources (PNF-like spiral and diagonal paths are a common feature): thrust punches, blocks, knife-hand thrusts, palm-heel strikes, front, side snap and side thrust kicks, done solo with pre-stretch, ending in \"a brief isometrically-held end position\" or \"rapid recoil actions\", in kata, or in non-contact sparring. Low-impact throwing or kicking of very light objects is included as \"low inertia\" plyometrics.\n\n**Why it works** — The stated mechanism is the same reflex-plus-elastic recoil as impact plyometrics, produced by the muscles' own explosive eccentric braking. Evidence offered: Siff's force-plate analysis (no data shown), changes in Ericson's wheelchair students, and \"preliminary studies executed in 1995 by University of Pretoria physiotherapy students supervised by Siff and Ericson\" that \"warrant more detailed expansion.\"\n\n**When to apply** — Inference: upper-body rebound work for linemen's hand strikes, or plyometric exposure for an athlete who cannot yet tolerate landing impact, are the obvious fits; the authors name \"athletes who require high levels of upper body power and endurance\" and arm rehabilitation.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — `untested`: no data are reported, the source is the author's own and his wife's system, and the text says research so far has studied speed-strength, not these other qualities. Siff himself says maximal impact plyometrics produce more leg and hip extensor tension than non-impact equivalents, so non-impact work does not replace depth jumps for lower-body reactive strength.\n\n**Example** — The text's own: a karate snap kick, thrust out and whipped back (non-impact), compared with a punch that briefly hits its target (PDF p. 291 / printed p. 280).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--plyometric-training-classification-impact-intensity-and-function]]; [[heuristic--resisted-and-water-plyometrics]] (upper-body aqua plyometrics use the same actions); [[framework--shock-method-and-plyometric-terminology]]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3245,"cache_read_input_tokens":268236,"output_tokens":3531,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":92},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3245},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":3531,"cache_read_input_tokens":268236,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3245,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3245,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01Q9htfeCKJcyzUX7qDa2nXd":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--non-impact-plyometrics-ericson-and-martial-arts.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.4\"\ntopic: non-impact plyometrics\ncategory: [plyometrics, non-impact, upper-body, rehabilitation]\nconfidence: untested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Non-Impact Plyometrics: Explosive Eccentric Recoil Without Surface Contact (Ericson's Seated System, Martial Arts Snaps)\n\n**What it is** — Siff's section (he refers to \"the author\" doing force-plate analysis and to \"his wife, Lisa Ericson\") argues that plyometric action does not need impact (PDF pp. 290-292 / printed pp. 279-281, text layer):\n- Western and even Russian practice treats \"shock contact of parts of the body with some surface\" as the common denominator of plyometrics, but \"this is not the only way of producing plyometric action.\" The non-impact variety was recognised when Siff analysed martial arts movements and Ericson's seated fitness system (Supersitting, later SMART).\n- Ericson's system: \"very rapid wide-range movements choreographed at rates exceeding 240 beats per minute, interspersed with rhythmic transitional PNF-type patterns\", over sessions of about 60 minutes, movements \"initiated and terminated explosively in sets of uninterrupted sequences\" that change form and direction \"to avoid excessive fatigue, habituation or injury.\"\n- It counts as non-impact plyometrics \"because it obeys all the requirements of plyometrics as originally conceived\": \"a ballistic action which is rapidly terminated by eccentric and explosive isometric muscle contraction, followed immediately by a powerful concentric recoil mediated by recruitment of the myotatic stretch reflex and augmented by the release of elastic energy.\"\n- Comparison with karate: karate uses few repetitions with brief intervals, and the punch often contacts its target briefly, \"sufficient to dissipate much of the elastic energy\"; Ericson's transition is \"usually shorter\" with many continuous repetitions, which \"probably explains the differences in functional and structural development.\"\n- Intensity: most class movements are submaximal plyometrics; \"Performed in the form of all-out single movements, they become maximal non-impact plyometrics.\" \"maximal impact plyometrics will generally increase muscle tension to a greater extent than the equivalent non-impact movements for the leg and hip extensors (e.g. the karate thrust-kick).\"\n- Uses: maximal non-impact plyometrics, like submaximal impact plyometrics, \"can serve as a preparatory tool for maximal impact plyometrics\", for other qualities (\"strength, muscle hypertrophy, muscle endurance and speed-strength endurance\"), or to train selected muscle groups \"instead of unnecessarily stressing the entire musculature.\"\n- Sport sources (PNF-like spiral and diagonal paths are a common feature): thrust punches, blocks, knife-hand thrusts, palm-heel strikes, front, side snap and side thrust kicks, done solo with pre-stretch, ending in \"a brief isometrically-held end position\" or \"rapid recoil actions\", in kata, or in non-contact sparring. Low-impact throwing or kicking of very light objects is included as \"low inertia\" plyometrics.\n\n**Why it works** — The stated mechanism is the same reflex-plus-elastic recoil as impact plyometrics, produced by the muscles' own explosive eccentric braking. Evidence offered: Siff's force-plate analysis (no data shown), changes in Ericson's wheelchair students, and \"preliminary studies executed in 1995 by University of Pretoria physiotherapy students supervised by Siff and Ericson\" that \"warrant more detailed expansion.\"\n\n**When to apply** — Inference: upper-body rebound work for linemen's hand strikes, or plyometric exposure for an athlete who cannot yet tolerate landing impact, are the obvious fits; the authors name \"athletes who require high levels of upper body power and endurance\" and arm rehabilitation.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — `untested`: no data are reported, the source is the author's own and his wife's system, and the text says research so far has studied speed-strength, not these other qualities. Siff himself says maximal impact plyometrics produce more leg and hip extensor tension than non-impact equivalents, so non-impact work does not replace depth jumps for lower-body reactive strength.\n\n**Example** — The text's own: a karate snap kick, thrust out and whipped back (non-impact), compared with a punch that briefly hits its target (PDF p. 291 / printed p. 280).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--plyometric-training-classification-impact-intensity-and-function]]; [[heuristic--resisted-and-water-plyometrics]] (upper-body aqua plyometrics use the same actions); [[framework--shock-method-and-plyometric-terminology]]\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011CffaVzmnCuC9knPuksQNu","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"ba971ef4-b61c-4f15-b41e-6e68c124fe4f","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:57:52.230Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
