{"parentUuid":"e9c0f812-771d-425c-a904-a1c2405cd36c","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffaYjaoHMDnLyHCpiaMD","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01YbMymUPspAkwiij9zoRPJN","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--non-impact-plyometrics-ericson-and-martial-arts.md","old_string":"(Supersitting, later SMART)","new_string":"(called Supersitting, and on p. 281 the \"SMART seated fitness system\")"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3812,"cache_read_input_tokens":271481,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3812,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01YbMymUPspAkwiij9zoRPJN":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--non-impact-plyometrics-ericson-and-martial-arts.md","old_string":"(Supersitting, later SMART)","new_string":"(called Supersitting, and on p. 281 the \"SMART seated fitness system\")"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffaYWnN3CWreouPBxmND","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"e28eddc6-2f23-4891-8218-ece4acfb92e9","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:57:59.115Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"e28eddc6-2f23-4891-8218-ece4acfb92e9","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"624a8cec-33fa-4c0a-a445-c72ce718e6ab","agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01YbMymUPspAkwiij9zoRPJN","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--non-impact-plyometrics-ericson-and-martial-arts.md has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"e8993151-4bde-4b42-93e0-581b1789f9f3","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:57:59.202Z","toolUseResult":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--non-impact-plyometrics-ericson-and-martial-arts.md","oldString":"(Supersitting, later SMART)","newString":"(called Supersitting, and on p. 281 the \"SMART seated fitness system\")","originalFile":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.4\"\ntopic: non-impact plyometrics\ncategory: [plyometrics, non-impact, upper-body, rehabilitation]\nconfidence: untested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Non-Impact Plyometrics: Explosive Eccentric Recoil Without Surface Contact (Ericson's Seated System, Martial Arts Snaps)\n\n**What it is** — Siff's section (he refers to \"the author\" doing force-plate analysis and to \"his wife, Lisa Ericson\") argues that plyometric action does not need impact (PDF pp. 290-292 / printed pp. 279-281, text layer):\n- Western and even Russian practice treats \"shock contact of parts of the body with some surface\" as the common denominator of plyometrics, but \"this is not the only way of producing plyometric action.\" The non-impact variety was recognised when Siff analysed martial arts movements and Ericson's seated fitness system (Supersitting, later SMART).\n- Ericson's system: \"very rapid wide-range movements choreographed at rates exceeding 240 beats per minute, interspersed with rhythmic transitional PNF-type patterns\", over sessions of about 60 minutes, movements \"initiated and terminated explosively in sets of uninterrupted sequences\" that change form and direction \"to avoid excessive fatigue, habituation or injury.\"\n- It counts as non-impact plyometrics \"because it obeys all the requirements of plyometrics as originally conceived\": \"a ballistic action which is rapidly terminated by eccentric and explosive isometric muscle contraction, followed immediately by a powerful concentric recoil mediated by recruitment of the myotatic stretch reflex and augmented by the release of elastic energy.\"\n- Comparison with karate: karate uses few repetitions with brief intervals, and the punch often contacts its target briefly, \"sufficient to dissipate much of the elastic energy\"; Ericson's transition is \"usually shorter\" with many continuous repetitions, which \"probably explains the differences in functional and structural development.\"\n- Intensity: most class movements are submaximal plyometrics; \"Performed in the form of all-out single movements, they become maximal non-impact plyometrics.\" \"maximal impact plyometrics will generally increase muscle tension to a greater extent than the equivalent non-impact movements for the leg and hip extensors (e.g. the karate thrust-kick).\"\n- Uses: maximal non-impact plyometrics, like submaximal impact plyometrics, \"can serve as a preparatory tool for maximal impact plyometrics\", for other qualities (\"strength, muscle hypertrophy, muscle endurance and speed-strength endurance\"), or to train selected muscle groups \"instead of unnecessarily stressing the entire musculature.\"\n- Sport sources (PNF-like spiral and diagonal paths are a common feature): thrust punches, blocks, knife-hand thrusts, palm-heel strikes, front, side snap and side thrust kicks, done solo with pre-stretch, ending in \"a brief isometrically-held end position\" or \"rapid recoil actions\", in kata, or in non-contact sparring. Low-impact throwing or kicking of very light objects is included as \"low inertia\" plyometrics.\n\n**Why it works** — The stated mechanism is the same reflex-plus-elastic recoil as impact plyometrics, produced by the muscles' own explosive eccentric braking. Evidence offered: Siff's force-plate analysis (no data shown), changes in Ericson's wheelchair students, and \"preliminary studies executed in 1995 by University of Pretoria physiotherapy students supervised by Siff and Ericson\" that \"warrant more detailed expansion.\"\n\n**When to apply** — Inference: upper-body rebound work for linemen's hand strikes, or plyometric exposure for an athlete who cannot yet tolerate landing impact, are the obvious fits; the authors name \"athletes who require high levels of upper body power and endurance\" and arm rehabilitation.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — `untested`: no data are reported, the source is the author's own and his wife's system, and the text says research so far has studied speed-strength, not these other qualities. Siff himself says maximal impact plyometrics produce more leg and hip extensor tension than non-impact equivalents, so non-impact work does not replace depth jumps for lower-body reactive strength.\n\n**Example** — The text's own: a karate snap kick, thrust out and whipped back (non-impact), compared with a punch that briefly hits its target (PDF p. 291 / printed p. 280).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--plyometric-training-classification-impact-intensity-and-function]]; [[heuristic--resisted-and-water-plyometrics]] (upper-body aqua plyometrics use the same actions); [[framework--shock-method-and-plyometric-terminology]]\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":14,"oldLines":7,"newStart":14,"newLines":7,"lines":[" # Non-Impact Plyometrics: Explosive Eccentric Recoil Without Surface Contact (Ericson's Seated System, Martial Arts Snaps)"," "," **What it is** — Siff's section (he refers to \"the author\" doing force-plate analysis and to \"his wife, Lisa Ericson\") argues that plyometric action does not need impact (PDF pp. 290-292 / printed pp. 279-281, text layer):","-- Western and even Russian practice treats \"shock contact of parts of the body with some surface\" as the common denominator of plyometrics, but \"this is not the only way of producing plyometric action.\" The non-impact variety was recognised when Siff analysed martial arts movements and Ericson's seated fitness system (Supersitting, later SMART).","+- Western and even Russian practice treats \"shock contact of parts of the body with some surface\" as the common denominator of plyometrics, but \"this is not the only way of producing plyometric action.\" The non-impact variety was recognised when Siff analysed martial arts movements and Ericson's seated fitness system (called Supersitting, and on p. 281 the \"SMART seated fitness system\")."," - Ericson's system: \"very rapid wide-range movements choreographed at rates exceeding 240 beats per minute, interspersed with rhythmic transitional PNF-type patterns\", over sessions of about 60 minutes, movements \"initiated and terminated explosively in sets of uninterrupted sequences\" that change form and direction \"to avoid excessive fatigue, habituation or injury.\""," - It counts as non-impact plyometrics \"because it obeys all the requirements of plyometrics as originally conceived\": \"a ballistic action which is rapidly terminated by eccentric and explosive isometric muscle contraction, followed immediately by a powerful concentric recoil mediated by recruitment of the myotatic stretch reflex and augmented by the release of elastic energy.\""," - Comparison with karate: karate uses few repetitions with brief intervals, and the punch often contacts its target briefly, \"sufficient to dissipate much of the elastic energy\"; Ericson's transition is \"usually shorter\" with many continuous repetitions, which \"probably explains the differences in functional and structural development.\""]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"e28eddc6-2f23-4891-8218-ece4acfb92e9","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"e8993151-4bde-4b42-93e0-581b1789f9f3","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffaYjaoHMDnLyHCpiaMD","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_015t6Rht4CJ8XvQ4mh2wzya7","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--popular-plyometric-drills-work-through-other-mechanisms.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: claim\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.4\"\ntopic: popular plyometric drills\ncategory: [plyometrics, exercise-selection, terminology]\nconfidence: untested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Most \"Plyometric\" Drills in Popular Western Books Work Through Eccentric Loading, Connective Tissue, Starting Strength and Prestretch, Not the Shock Mechanism\n\n**What it is** — Under \"Popular Information on Plyometrics\" (PDF pp. 293-294 / printed pp. 282-283; p. 293 verified against the page image, p. 294 from the text layer):\n- Popular Western texts (\"e.g. Chu, 1992; Radcliffe & Farantinos, 1985\") present jumping and rebound activities \"many of which do not constitute true plyometric training as originally conceived by Russian scientists\"; they are \"often supplementary or preparatory movements.\"\n- \"Most books classify extended range swinging, passing or catching of medicine balls or other objects as 'plyometric', although the amortisation phase is too long to produce classical plyometrics.\" These drills still help \"eye-hand coordination, eccentric strength and starting strength, but they are not strictly plyometric.\"\n- Books omit the tonic versus phasic myotatic reflex in relation to drill types, the positive supportive reaction, the crossed extensor reflex, phased breathing, the maximal-submaximal distinction, and concurrent and sequential prescription.\n- \"the so-called plyometric drills in current Western texts are known to enhance performance, but it is not predominantly due to plyometric training, but more to the increased loading of eccentric contraction, the conditioning of connective tissue, the improvement of voluntary starting strength from the quasi-isometric state, the use of prestretch, and enhancement of acceleration strength by increasing the velocity of projectiles such as medicine balls\" (PDF p. 294 / printed p. 283).\n- The physiotherapy link: in PNF a therapist \"sometimes imposes a short, sharp jerk on an eccentrically or isometrically contracting muscle to produce strong muscle contraction in a weakened muscle\", principles that \"preceded sporting plyometrics by several decades\" (same page).\n- Related, from the classification passage: *pseudo-plyometrics* such as aerobics \"power moves\" and many \"water plyos\" lack \"an explosive rebound phase with a short coupling time\" (PDF p. 284 / printed p. 273, verified).\n\n**Why it works** — The text's argument is definitional: long amortisation phases fall outside the shock method, so the benefit must come from the other listed qualities. It gives no study separating these mechanisms.\n\n**When to apply** — Labelling a program's med-ball and jump drills honestly: Inference: a long-amortisation med-ball chest pass or rotational throw is a starting-strength and eccentric drill in the authors' terms, and should not be counted as shock-method volume or dosed with shock-method rest.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — `untested`: the list of \"other mechanisms\" is the text's assertion, with no data. The criticism of specific books is the author's opinion and was not checked against those books. Not a reason to drop these drills: the text says they \"are known to enhance performance.\"\n\n**Example** — The text's own: extended-range medicine ball swinging and catching, called plyometric in popular books, as an example of a drill whose amortisation is too long for classical plyometrics (PDF p. 293 / printed p. 282).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--plyometric-training-classification-impact-intensity-and-function]]; [[heuristic--plyometric-training-coupling-under-0-15-s-feedforward-and-force-time-pattern]]; [[framework--shock-method-and-plyometric-terminology]]; [[heuristic--reflex-based-techniques-for-flexibility-training]]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3812,"cache_read_input_tokens":271481,"output_tokens":1753,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":85},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3812},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1753,"cache_read_input_tokens":271481,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3812,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3812,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_015t6Rht4CJ8XvQ4mh2wzya7":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--popular-plyometric-drills-work-through-other-mechanisms.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: claim\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.4\"\ntopic: popular plyometric drills\ncategory: [plyometrics, exercise-selection, terminology]\nconfidence: untested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Most \"Plyometric\" Drills in Popular Western Books Work Through Eccentric Loading, Connective Tissue, Starting Strength and Prestretch, Not the Shock Mechanism\n\n**What it is** — Under \"Popular Information on Plyometrics\" (PDF pp. 293-294 / printed pp. 282-283; p. 293 verified against the page image, p. 294 from the text layer):\n- Popular Western texts (\"e.g. Chu, 1992; Radcliffe & Farantinos, 1985\") present jumping and rebound activities \"many of which do not constitute true plyometric training as originally conceived by Russian scientists\"; they are \"often supplementary or preparatory movements.\"\n- \"Most books classify extended range swinging, passing or catching of medicine balls or other objects as 'plyometric', although the amortisation phase is too long to produce classical plyometrics.\" These drills still help \"eye-hand coordination, eccentric strength and starting strength, but they are not strictly plyometric.\"\n- Books omit the tonic versus phasic myotatic reflex in relation to drill types, the positive supportive reaction, the crossed extensor reflex, phased breathing, the maximal-submaximal distinction, and concurrent and sequential prescription.\n- \"the so-called plyometric drills in current Western texts are known to enhance performance, but it is not predominantly due to plyometric training, but more to the increased loading of eccentric contraction, the conditioning of connective tissue, the improvement of voluntary starting strength from the quasi-isometric state, the use of prestretch, and enhancement of acceleration strength by increasing the velocity of projectiles such as medicine balls\" (PDF p. 294 / printed p. 283).\n- The physiotherapy link: in PNF a therapist \"sometimes imposes a short, sharp jerk on an eccentrically or isometrically contracting muscle to produce strong muscle contraction in a weakened muscle\", principles that \"preceded sporting plyometrics by several decades\" (same page).\n- Related, from the classification passage: *pseudo-plyometrics* such as aerobics \"power moves\" and many \"water plyos\" lack \"an explosive rebound phase with a short coupling time\" (PDF p. 284 / printed p. 273, verified).\n\n**Why it works** — The text's argument is definitional: long amortisation phases fall outside the shock method, so the benefit must come from the other listed qualities. It gives no study separating these mechanisms.\n\n**When to apply** — Labelling a program's med-ball and jump drills honestly: Inference: a long-amortisation med-ball chest pass or rotational throw is a starting-strength and eccentric drill in the authors' terms, and should not be counted as shock-method volume or dosed with shock-method rest.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — `untested`: the list of \"other mechanisms\" is the text's assertion, with no data. The criticism of specific books is the author's opinion and was not checked against those books. Not a reason to drop these drills: the text says they \"are known to enhance performance.\"\n\n**Example** — The text's own: extended-range medicine ball swinging and catching, called plyometric in popular books, as an example of a drill whose amortisation is too long for classical plyometrics (PDF p. 293 / printed p. 282).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--plyometric-training-classification-impact-intensity-and-function]]; [[heuristic--plyometric-training-coupling-under-0-15-s-feedforward-and-force-time-pattern]]; [[framework--shock-method-and-plyometric-terminology]]; [[heuristic--reflex-based-techniques-for-flexibility-training]]\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011CffaYWnN3CWreouPBxmND","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"130fb0eb-1c52-4274-87b0-7df7a184ae67","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:58:11.132Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
