{"parentUuid":"08cdea7e-6455-4fa6-8585-02ff0005a27f","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffaaAiBk8a1v4ceFC89u","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2029,"cache_read_input_tokens":275293,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2029,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":395,"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CffaZuabUNdZH7brLaMyk","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"454de8ba-b720-47c1-ac40-8fd3e33211b4","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:58:16.604Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"454de8ba-b720-47c1-ac40-8fd3e33211b4","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffaaAiBk8a1v4ceFC89u","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01FRdbz56xoH39ncs6cYxZDC","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--plyometric-skill-is-cognitive-and-state-dependent.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: claim\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.4\"\ntopic: plyometrics and the brain\ncategory: [plyometrics, motor-learning, reflexes, psychology]\nconfidence: untested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Plyometric Action Is Not Only Spinal Reflex and Elastic Recoil: It Is Planned, Conditionable and State-Dependent\n\n**What it is** — Under \"Plyometrics and the Brain\" (PDF pp. 294-295 / printed pp. 283-284, text layer):\n- \"plyometric action is not simply a phenomenon that can be adequately explained on the basis of stored elastic energy and the stretch shortening cycle.\" Although the action itself is fast and spinal, \"the preparatory period prior to the plyometric action can involve higher order mental processing of exceptional intricacy.\"\n- **Cognitive versus non-cognitive plyometrics:** a plyometric action \"under conditions in which the athlete anticipates a particular course of action (*cognitive plyometrics*)\" versus \"a purely reflexive response under unexpected circumstances (*non-cognitive plyometrics*)\", the latter mainly in self-protection.\n- **Practice:** \"most athletes do not manage to perfect plyometric skills even after many weeks of practice. The use of numerous sequential repetitions to imprint these skills may constitute the inappropriate strategy because classical plyometrics are 1 RM movements which require as much as several minutes of rest before recovery is complete. Submaximal plyometrics and pseudo-plyometrics may be performed in a state of advancing fatigue, unlike classical 'shock method' plyometrics.\"\n- **Conditioning:** reflexes \"can be trained and modified\" (Pavlov's conditioned reflexes; Skinner, 1938, respondent and operant conditioning). An emotion such as fear or excitement, or a mental image, before a depth jump \"can shape the subsequent neuromuscular reflexes\"; events after it (\"the jarring of the body, post-exercise fatigue or an injury\") can modify them too.\n- **State-dependent learning:** conditioned reflexes learned in a particular physiological or psychological state \"are exhibited only when the same state is present\", so \"plyometric proficiency or deficiency learned in a specific training situation may not be reproduced in competitive circumstances.\" The text suggests this \"may be one of the reasons\" some experiments found little or no benefit from plyometric training.\n- Visualisation caveat: Kensall Wise (Scientific American, Oct 1988) is cited that the brain region that visualises an action is separate from those that initiate it. Calvin's hypothesis (Scientific American, Oct 1994) links planning of ballistic movement to language and intelligence.\n\nVoice: the passage cites no author by name; it reads as Siff's (it continues the Siff-voiced material on either side), but the book does not mark it, so \"the text\" is used here.\n\n**Why it works** — The text's mechanism is that ballistic movements must be fully planned in advance (\"ballistic movements require the brain to determine every detail of the action in advance\"), so the planning state, emotions and the learning context become part of the skill.\n\n**When to apply** — Inference: run plyometrics in states closer to competition (with reactive cues, under some arousal, sometimes unplanned) if the aim is transfer to football, since the text warns that drills learned in one state may not reproduce in another. The text recommends feedforward and visualisation techniques \"to efficiently manage any plyometric actions under real sporting conditions.\"\n\n**When NOT to apply** — `untested`: no plyometric study is cited for state-dependent learning or for the conditioning claims; the supports are general psychology and two popular-science articles. Do not cite it as evidence that a given plyometric study failed for this reason (the text says only \"may be\").\n\n**Example** — The text's own: fear or excitement before a depth jump shaping the reflexes, and a jarring landing afterwards modifying them (PDF p. 295 / printed p. 284).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--kinaesthetic-system-and-feedforward-visualization]]; [[heuristic--plyometric-training-coupling-under-0-15-s-feedforward-and-force-time-pattern]]; [[claim--hypnosis-and-social-presence-alter-strength-output]]; [[science-and-practice-of-strength-training--zatsiorsky/claim--reversible-muscular-action-is-a-trainable-skill-not-automatic]]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2029,"cache_read_input_tokens":275293,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2029,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01FRdbz56xoH39ncs6cYxZDC":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--plyometric-skill-is-cognitive-and-state-dependent.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: claim\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.4\"\ntopic: plyometrics and the brain\ncategory: [plyometrics, motor-learning, reflexes, psychology]\nconfidence: untested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Plyometric Action Is Not Only Spinal Reflex and Elastic Recoil: It Is Planned, Conditionable and State-Dependent\n\n**What it is** — Under \"Plyometrics and the Brain\" (PDF pp. 294-295 / printed pp. 283-284, text layer):\n- \"plyometric action is not simply a phenomenon that can be adequately explained on the basis of stored elastic energy and the stretch shortening cycle.\" Although the action itself is fast and spinal, \"the preparatory period prior to the plyometric action can involve higher order mental processing of exceptional intricacy.\"\n- **Cognitive versus non-cognitive plyometrics:** a plyometric action \"under conditions in which the athlete anticipates a particular course of action (*cognitive plyometrics*)\" versus \"a purely reflexive response under unexpected circumstances (*non-cognitive plyometrics*)\", the latter mainly in self-protection.\n- **Practice:** \"most athletes do not manage to perfect plyometric skills even after many weeks of practice. The use of numerous sequential repetitions to imprint these skills may constitute the inappropriate strategy because classical plyometrics are 1 RM movements which require as much as several minutes of rest before recovery is complete. Submaximal plyometrics and pseudo-plyometrics may be performed in a state of advancing fatigue, unlike classical 'shock method' plyometrics.\"\n- **Conditioning:** reflexes \"can be trained and modified\" (Pavlov's conditioned reflexes; Skinner, 1938, respondent and operant conditioning). An emotion such as fear or excitement, or a mental image, before a depth jump \"can shape the subsequent neuromuscular reflexes\"; events after it (\"the jarring of the body, post-exercise fatigue or an injury\") can modify them too.\n- **State-dependent learning:** conditioned reflexes learned in a particular physiological or psychological state \"are exhibited only when the same state is present\", so \"plyometric proficiency or deficiency learned in a specific training situation may not be reproduced in competitive circumstances.\" The text suggests this \"may be one of the reasons\" some experiments found little or no benefit from plyometric training.\n- Visualisation caveat: Kensall Wise (Scientific American, Oct 1988) is cited that the brain region that visualises an action is separate from those that initiate it. Calvin's hypothesis (Scientific American, Oct 1994) links planning of ballistic movement to language and intelligence.\n\nVoice: the passage cites no author by name; it reads as Siff's (it continues the Siff-voiced material on either side), but the book does not mark it, so \"the text\" is used here.\n\n**Why it works** — The text's mechanism is that ballistic movements must be fully planned in advance (\"ballistic movements require the brain to determine every detail of the action in advance\"), so the planning state, emotions and the learning context become part of the skill.\n\n**When to apply** — Inference: run plyometrics in states closer to competition (with reactive cues, under some arousal, sometimes unplanned) if the aim is transfer to football, since the text warns that drills learned in one state may not reproduce in another. The text recommends feedforward and visualisation techniques \"to efficiently manage any plyometric actions under real sporting conditions.\"\n\n**When NOT to apply** — `untested`: no plyometric study is cited for state-dependent learning or for the conditioning claims; the supports are general psychology and two popular-science articles. Do not cite it as evidence that a given plyometric study failed for this reason (the text says only \"may be\").\n\n**Example** — The text's own: fear or excitement before a depth jump shaping the reflexes, and a jarring landing afterwards modifying them (PDF p. 295 / printed p. 284).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--kinaesthetic-system-and-feedforward-visualization]]; [[heuristic--plyometric-training-coupling-under-0-15-s-feedforward-and-force-time-pattern]]; [[claim--hypnosis-and-social-presence-alter-strength-output]]; [[science-and-practice-of-strength-training--zatsiorsky/claim--reversible-muscular-action-is-a-trainable-skill-not-automatic]]\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffaZuabUNdZH7brLaMyk","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"53f837e0-92c1-4c1b-a842-71ae841a73af","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:58:30.252Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"53f837e0-92c1-4c1b-a842-71ae841a73af","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"624a8cec-33fa-4c0a-a445-c72ce718e6ab","agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FRdbz56xoH39ncs6cYxZDC","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--plyometric-skill-is-cognitive-and-state-dependent.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"da027e1b-84f0-4607-aeff-3ff0132d3de0","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:58:30.342Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"create","filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--plyometric-skill-is-cognitive-and-state-dependent.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: claim\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.4\"\ntopic: plyometrics and the brain\ncategory: [plyometrics, motor-learning, reflexes, psychology]\nconfidence: untested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Plyometric Action Is Not Only Spinal Reflex and Elastic Recoil: It Is Planned, Conditionable and State-Dependent\n\n**What it is** — Under \"Plyometrics and the Brain\" (PDF pp. 294-295 / printed pp. 283-284, text layer):\n- \"plyometric action is not simply a phenomenon that can be adequately explained on the basis of stored elastic energy and the stretch shortening cycle.\" Although the action itself is fast and spinal, \"the preparatory period prior to the plyometric action can involve higher order mental processing of exceptional intricacy.\"\n- **Cognitive versus non-cognitive plyometrics:** a plyometric action \"under conditions in which the athlete anticipates a particular course of action (*cognitive plyometrics*)\" versus \"a purely reflexive response under unexpected circumstances (*non-cognitive plyometrics*)\", the latter mainly in self-protection.\n- **Practice:** \"most athletes do not manage to perfect plyometric skills even after many weeks of practice. The use of numerous sequential repetitions to imprint these skills may constitute the inappropriate strategy because classical plyometrics are 1 RM movements which require as much as several minutes of rest before recovery is complete. Submaximal plyometrics and pseudo-plyometrics may be performed in a state of advancing fatigue, unlike classical 'shock method' plyometrics.\"\n- **Conditioning:** reflexes \"can be trained and modified\" (Pavlov's conditioned reflexes; Skinner, 1938, respondent and operant conditioning). An emotion such as fear or excitement, or a mental image, before a depth jump \"can shape the subsequent neuromuscular reflexes\"; events after it (\"the jarring of the body, post-exercise fatigue or an injury\") can modify them too.\n- **State-dependent learning:** conditioned reflexes learned in a particular physiological or psychological state \"are exhibited only when the same state is present\", so \"plyometric proficiency or deficiency learned in a specific training situation may not be reproduced in competitive circumstances.\" The text suggests this \"may be one of the reasons\" some experiments found little or no benefit from plyometric training.\n- Visualisation caveat: Kensall Wise (Scientific American, Oct 1988) is cited that the brain region that visualises an action is separate from those that initiate it. Calvin's hypothesis (Scientific American, Oct 1994) links planning of ballistic movement to language and intelligence.\n\nVoice: the passage cites no author by name; it reads as Siff's (it continues the Siff-voiced material on either side), but the book does not mark it, so \"the text\" is used here.\n\n**Why it works** — The text's mechanism is that ballistic movements must be fully planned in advance (\"ballistic movements require the brain to determine every detail of the action in advance\"), so the planning state, emotions and the learning context become part of the skill.\n\n**When to apply** — Inference: run plyometrics in states closer to competition (with reactive cues, under some arousal, sometimes unplanned) if the aim is transfer to football, since the text warns that drills learned in one state may not reproduce in another. The text recommends feedforward and visualisation techniques \"to efficiently manage any plyometric actions under real sporting conditions.\"\n\n**When NOT to apply** — `untested`: no plyometric study is cited for state-dependent learning or for the conditioning claims; the supports are general psychology and two popular-science articles. Do not cite it as evidence that a given plyometric study failed for this reason (the text says only \"may be\").\n\n**Example** — The text's own: fear or excitement before a depth jump shaping the reflexes, and a jarring landing afterwards modifying them (PDF p. 295 / printed p. 284).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--kinaesthetic-system-and-feedforward-visualization]]; [[heuristic--plyometric-training-coupling-under-0-15-s-feedforward-and-force-time-pattern]]; [[claim--hypnosis-and-social-presence-alter-strength-output]]; [[science-and-practice-of-strength-training--zatsiorsky/claim--reversible-muscular-action-is-a-trainable-skill-not-automatic]]\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"53f837e0-92c1-4c1b-a842-71ae841a73af","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"da027e1b-84f0-4607-aeff-3ff0132d3de0","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffaaAiBk8a1v4ceFC89u","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Vrwmmo4LhKZ4ALxsfozcmX","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--shock-methods-to-break-training-stagnation.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.4\"\ntopic: shock methods for plateaus\ncategory: [programming, plateaus, overtraining, advanced-athletes]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Eight \"Shock Methods\" for Breaking Long-Term Stagnation in Experienced Athletes, Used Only After Ruling Out Overtraining\n\n**What it is** — Under \"Various Shock Methods\" (PDF pp. 295-296 / printed pp. 284-285; p. 296 verified against the page image) the text widens \"shock\" beyond plyometrics: \"Training plyometrics may be regarded as one of several shock methods that may be used in special strength training to provide intense stimulation to compel the body to attain a higher level of functional adaptation.\" Because \"The principle of gradual progressive overloading may, after a prolonged period of unvaried use, sometimes fail to improve strength or performance, as is commonly the case among elite athletes\", it \"may be necessary to disrupt the persistent state of stagnation\" with one of:\n- plyometrics\n- forced repetitions\n- supramaximal methods\n- electrical stimulation (macrostimulation)\n- repeated single 1RM training\n- restricted range maxima\n- contrast methods\n- maximal eccentrics\n\nCautions: \"The appropriate method, its timing and the duration of its use must be chosen with great care, since lack of progress may be due to overtraining rather than long-term stagnation. The use of any shock method under such circumstances is potentially harmful and definitely detrimental to performance. These methods are generally unsuitable for novices and should only be employed by experienced athletes with a solid background of special strength training.\" The text notes \"shock method\" was Verkhoshansky's name for the plyometric method when he first formalised it.\n\n**Why it works** — The stated rationale is \"intense stimulation to compel the body to attain a higher level of functional adaptation\" after adaptation to unvaried progressive overload stalls. No study is cited here; the text refers to 7.1 (not yet ingested).\n\n**When to apply** — An experienced athlete whose strength or power has stalled for a long period despite progressive loading, after overtraining has been excluded. Inference: in a collegiate setting, the check for \"overtraining rather than long-term stagnation\" would draw on the readiness data already collected (for Eric, mRSI trends, `docs/method-baseline.md` lines 74-75) before choosing a shock method.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Not for novices, and not when the plateau may be fatigue (the text says a shock method is then \"potentially harmful and definitely detrimental\"). The list gives no dosing; the methods are described elsewhere (7.1). The text gives no rule for distinguishing stagnation from overtraining.\n\n**Example** — Inference: a senior lifter whose squat has not moved for months on steady linear loading, with stable readiness markers, might get a short block of maximal eccentrics or contrast work from this list. The text gives no worked example.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--shock-method-and-plyometric-terminology]]; [[heuristic--plan-variation-to-avoid-restrictive-neural-patterns]]; [[framework--fitness-fatigue-modeling-and-taper-timing]]; [[framework--clinical-physiological-effects-of-electrostimulation]]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2029,"cache_read_input_tokens":275293,"output_tokens":3016,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":63},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2029},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":3016,"cache_read_input_tokens":275293,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2029,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2029,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01Vrwmmo4LhKZ4ALxsfozcmX":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--shock-methods-to-break-training-stagnation.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.4\"\ntopic: shock methods for plateaus\ncategory: [programming, plateaus, overtraining, advanced-athletes]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Eight \"Shock Methods\" for Breaking Long-Term Stagnation in Experienced Athletes, Used Only After Ruling Out Overtraining\n\n**What it is** — Under \"Various Shock Methods\" (PDF pp. 295-296 / printed pp. 284-285; p. 296 verified against the page image) the text widens \"shock\" beyond plyometrics: \"Training plyometrics may be regarded as one of several shock methods that may be used in special strength training to provide intense stimulation to compel the body to attain a higher level of functional adaptation.\" Because \"The principle of gradual progressive overloading may, after a prolonged period of unvaried use, sometimes fail to improve strength or performance, as is commonly the case among elite athletes\", it \"may be necessary to disrupt the persistent state of stagnation\" with one of:\n- plyometrics\n- forced repetitions\n- supramaximal methods\n- electrical stimulation (macrostimulation)\n- repeated single 1RM training\n- restricted range maxima\n- contrast methods\n- maximal eccentrics\n\nCautions: \"The appropriate method, its timing and the duration of its use must be chosen with great care, since lack of progress may be due to overtraining rather than long-term stagnation. The use of any shock method under such circumstances is potentially harmful and definitely detrimental to performance. These methods are generally unsuitable for novices and should only be employed by experienced athletes with a solid background of special strength training.\" The text notes \"shock method\" was Verkhoshansky's name for the plyometric method when he first formalised it.\n\n**Why it works** — The stated rationale is \"intense stimulation to compel the body to attain a higher level of functional adaptation\" after adaptation to unvaried progressive overload stalls. No study is cited here; the text refers to 7.1 (not yet ingested).\n\n**When to apply** — An experienced athlete whose strength or power has stalled for a long period despite progressive loading, after overtraining has been excluded. Inference: in a collegiate setting, the check for \"overtraining rather than long-term stagnation\" would draw on the readiness data already collected (for Eric, mRSI trends, `docs/method-baseline.md` lines 74-75) before choosing a shock method.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Not for novices, and not when the plateau may be fatigue (the text says a shock method is then \"potentially harmful and definitely detrimental\"). The list gives no dosing; the methods are described elsewhere (7.1). The text gives no rule for distinguishing stagnation from overtraining.\n\n**Example** — Inference: a senior lifter whose squat has not moved for months on steady linear loading, with stable readiness markers, might get a short block of maximal eccentrics or contrast work from this list. The text gives no worked example.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--shock-method-and-plyometric-terminology]]; [[heuristic--plan-variation-to-avoid-restrictive-neural-patterns]]; [[framework--fitness-fatigue-modeling-and-taper-timing]]; [[framework--clinical-physiological-effects-of-electrostimulation]]\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011CffaZuabUNdZH7brLaMyk","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"d113ecc6-b243-4ce4-b726-da178d00ce75","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:58:41.186Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"d113ecc6-b243-4ce4-b726-da178d00ce75","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"624a8cec-33fa-4c0a-a445-c72ce718e6ab","agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Vrwmmo4LhKZ4ALxsfozcmX","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--shock-methods-to-break-training-stagnation.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"d8e3ec2b-80fc-4019-9a68-045440632c23","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:58:41.318Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"create","filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--shock-methods-to-break-training-stagnation.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.4\"\ntopic: shock methods for plateaus\ncategory: [programming, plateaus, overtraining, advanced-athletes]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Eight \"Shock Methods\" for Breaking Long-Term Stagnation in Experienced Athletes, Used Only After Ruling Out Overtraining\n\n**What it is** — Under \"Various Shock Methods\" (PDF pp. 295-296 / printed pp. 284-285; p. 296 verified against the page image) the text widens \"shock\" beyond plyometrics: \"Training plyometrics may be regarded as one of several shock methods that may be used in special strength training to provide intense stimulation to compel the body to attain a higher level of functional adaptation.\" Because \"The principle of gradual progressive overloading may, after a prolonged period of unvaried use, sometimes fail to improve strength or performance, as is commonly the case among elite athletes\", it \"may be necessary to disrupt the persistent state of stagnation\" with one of:\n- plyometrics\n- forced repetitions\n- supramaximal methods\n- electrical stimulation (macrostimulation)\n- repeated single 1RM training\n- restricted range maxima\n- contrast methods\n- maximal eccentrics\n\nCautions: \"The appropriate method, its timing and the duration of its use must be chosen with great care, since lack of progress may be due to overtraining rather than long-term stagnation. The use of any shock method under such circumstances is potentially harmful and definitely detrimental to performance. These methods are generally unsuitable for novices and should only be employed by experienced athletes with a solid background of special strength training.\" The text notes \"shock method\" was Verkhoshansky's name for the plyometric method when he first formalised it.\n\n**Why it works** — The stated rationale is \"intense stimulation to compel the body to attain a higher level of functional adaptation\" after adaptation to unvaried progressive overload stalls. No study is cited here; the text refers to 7.1 (not yet ingested).\n\n**When to apply** — An experienced athlete whose strength or power has stalled for a long period despite progressive loading, after overtraining has been excluded. Inference: in a collegiate setting, the check for \"overtraining rather than long-term stagnation\" would draw on the readiness data already collected (for Eric, mRSI trends, `docs/method-baseline.md` lines 74-75) before choosing a shock method.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Not for novices, and not when the plateau may be fatigue (the text says a shock method is then \"potentially harmful and definitely detrimental\"). The list gives no dosing; the methods are described elsewhere (7.1). The text gives no rule for distinguishing stagnation from overtraining.\n\n**Example** — Inference: a senior lifter whose squat has not moved for months on steady linear loading, with stable readiness markers, might get a short block of maximal eccentrics or contrast work from this list. The text gives no worked example.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--shock-method-and-plyometric-terminology]]; [[heuristic--plan-variation-to-avoid-restrictive-neural-patterns]]; [[framework--fitness-fatigue-modeling-and-taper-timing]]; [[framework--clinical-physiological-effects-of-electrostimulation]]\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"d113ecc6-b243-4ce4-b726-da178d00ce75","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
