{"parentUuid":"ef51cd08-2567-4cbb-9b41-77cc35ef466e","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffaccSYpd11vgCgRMLtm","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3282,"cache_read_input_tokens":277322,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3282,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":1220,"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011Cffac8Cg3EswqWQz45rea","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"9a248c55-8dfc-4b88-86d8-612684b196e9","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:58:50.529Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"9a248c55-8dfc-4b88-86d8-612684b196e9","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffaccSYpd11vgCgRMLtm","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01XsrER9YjvKxXiryT5TmeGp","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--strength-endurance-load-tempo-and-the-75-80-percent-ceiling.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.5\"\ntopic: strength-endurance loading\ncategory: [strength-endurance, programming, load, tempo]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Strength-Endurance: 25-50% 1RM at 60-120 Reps per Minute, Ideally to Fatigue; Above 75-80% 1RM Trains Strength Instead (but the Book Also Prescribes 80-85%)\n\n**What it is** — Section 5.2.5 (PDF pp. 296-298 / printed pp. 285-287; pp. 296-298 verified against the page images):\n- **Eight determinants.** Training for strength-endurance \"is determined predominantly by: 1. The magnitude of the load 2. The tempo of movement 3. The duration of each movement and each phase of the movements 4. The rest interval between training sessions 5. The length of the training period 6. The initial levels of strength-endurance of the athlete 7. The kinesiological structure of the movement 8. The relative involvement of static and dynamic strength-endurance\" (PDF p. 296 / printed p. 285).\n- **Basic prescription.** \"Repetition work with weights 25-50% of 1RM at a moderate tempo (from 60-120 repetitions/minute) is used for developing strength-endurance. The effectiveness ... will be higher if the work is executed to complete fatigue, although work of shorter duration (60% of the maximal work capacity time) also will yield satisfactory results (Agolinsky, 1953; Korobkov, 1953; Monogarov, 1958; Diachkov, 1961)\" (same page).\n- **Ceiling.** \"When the activity involves repetitively overcoming significant resistance (exceeding 75-80% of 1RM), one cannot enhance endurance, since one becomes constrained to developing strength predominantly (Zatsiorsky, 1966)\" (same page).\n- **Progression.** \"It is necessary to increase the amount of weight and the number of repetitions as strength-endurance increases\" (PDF p. 297 / printed p. 286).\n- **Summary rule.** \"multiple repetition exercises with various weights should be considered the fundamental method for developing strength-endurance, with the load being determined by the dynamics of the given exercise. In cases requiring intense effort, an optimally heavy weight should be used together with light weights or with exercises simulating the given sport activity. If the special exercise is associated with the prolonged production of moderate force, then it is useful to work with light weights in repeated sets to the point of fatigue or failure\" (PDF p. 298 / printed p. 287).\n\n**Internal conflict, recorded:** the 75-80% ceiling (PDF p. 296 / printed p. 285) conflicts with the sport examples a page later: \"Exercises with heavy weights (80-85% of 1RM) ... are recommended for developing strength-endurance in skaters\", 4-12 reps per set to complete fatigue with 2-4 min rest (Mikhailov & Panov, 1975), and \"Male rowers obtain good results with weights of 50-80% of 1RM\" (Ulrich et al, 1966) (PDF p. 297 / printed p. 286). The summary rule on p. 287 (\"an optimally heavy weight ... together with light weights\" where intense effort is needed) sits closer to the examples than to the ceiling. The book does not reconcile them.\n\n**Why it works** — The ceiling's stated reason: above 75-80% the work is \"constrained to developing strength predominantly.\" The load-to-fatigue advantage is reported from the cited Soviet studies, without a stated mechanism.\n\n**When to apply** — Inference: for football, where strength-endurance means repeated forceful efforts (blocking, pushing a sled), the summary rule fits better than the 25-50% band: a heavy-enough load mixed with lighter or sport-simulating work. The 60-120 reps per minute tempo describes cyclic-sport endurance work more than gridiron work. Eric's baseline: for football the priority stack is alactic and aerobic capacity and power, with glycolytic work \"a \"worst case scenario\"\" absorbed through camp and practice (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 116). Inference: to-fatigue strength-endurance sets would add glycolytic load the department deliberately does not program.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Do not quote the 75-80% ceiling as a firm rule without the 80-85% skater and 50-80% rower examples. The determinants list has no weightings. \"60% of the maximal work capacity time\" is not defined further (60% of the time to failure is the plain reading).\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: 25-50% 1RM at 60-120 reps per minute to fatigue, or to 60% of the maximal work time (PDF p. 296 / printed p. 285).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--strength-endurance-taxonomy-and-absolute-vs-partial]] (Ch3.2.2, the dynamic/static and absolute/partial distinctions this section restates); [[claim--zatsiorsky-and-hansen-show-strength-endurance-training-specificity]]; [[case-study--soviet-sport-specific-strength-endurance-prescriptions]]; [[framework--resistance-training-prescription-by-goal]] (Ch1 Table 1.1, endurance at 40-60% 1RM)\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3282,"cache_read_input_tokens":277322,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3282,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01XsrER9YjvKxXiryT5TmeGp":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--strength-endurance-load-tempo-and-the-75-80-percent-ceiling.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.5\"\ntopic: strength-endurance loading\ncategory: [strength-endurance, programming, load, tempo]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Strength-Endurance: 25-50% 1RM at 60-120 Reps per Minute, Ideally to Fatigue; Above 75-80% 1RM Trains Strength Instead (but the Book Also Prescribes 80-85%)\n\n**What it is** — Section 5.2.5 (PDF pp. 296-298 / printed pp. 285-287; pp. 296-298 verified against the page images):\n- **Eight determinants.** Training for strength-endurance \"is determined predominantly by: 1. The magnitude of the load 2. The tempo of movement 3. The duration of each movement and each phase of the movements 4. The rest interval between training sessions 5. The length of the training period 6. The initial levels of strength-endurance of the athlete 7. The kinesiological structure of the movement 8. The relative involvement of static and dynamic strength-endurance\" (PDF p. 296 / printed p. 285).\n- **Basic prescription.** \"Repetition work with weights 25-50% of 1RM at a moderate tempo (from 60-120 repetitions/minute) is used for developing strength-endurance. The effectiveness ... will be higher if the work is executed to complete fatigue, although work of shorter duration (60% of the maximal work capacity time) also will yield satisfactory results (Agolinsky, 1953; Korobkov, 1953; Monogarov, 1958; Diachkov, 1961)\" (same page).\n- **Ceiling.** \"When the activity involves repetitively overcoming significant resistance (exceeding 75-80% of 1RM), one cannot enhance endurance, since one becomes constrained to developing strength predominantly (Zatsiorsky, 1966)\" (same page).\n- **Progression.** \"It is necessary to increase the amount of weight and the number of repetitions as strength-endurance increases\" (PDF p. 297 / printed p. 286).\n- **Summary rule.** \"multiple repetition exercises with various weights should be considered the fundamental method for developing strength-endurance, with the load being determined by the dynamics of the given exercise. In cases requiring intense effort, an optimally heavy weight should be used together with light weights or with exercises simulating the given sport activity. If the special exercise is associated with the prolonged production of moderate force, then it is useful to work with light weights in repeated sets to the point of fatigue or failure\" (PDF p. 298 / printed p. 287).\n\n**Internal conflict, recorded:** the 75-80% ceiling (PDF p. 296 / printed p. 285) conflicts with the sport examples a page later: \"Exercises with heavy weights (80-85% of 1RM) ... are recommended for developing strength-endurance in skaters\", 4-12 reps per set to complete fatigue with 2-4 min rest (Mikhailov & Panov, 1975), and \"Male rowers obtain good results with weights of 50-80% of 1RM\" (Ulrich et al, 1966) (PDF p. 297 / printed p. 286). The summary rule on p. 287 (\"an optimally heavy weight ... together with light weights\" where intense effort is needed) sits closer to the examples than to the ceiling. The book does not reconcile them.\n\n**Why it works** — The ceiling's stated reason: above 75-80% the work is \"constrained to developing strength predominantly.\" The load-to-fatigue advantage is reported from the cited Soviet studies, without a stated mechanism.\n\n**When to apply** — Inference: for football, where strength-endurance means repeated forceful efforts (blocking, pushing a sled), the summary rule fits better than the 25-50% band: a heavy-enough load mixed with lighter or sport-simulating work. The 60-120 reps per minute tempo describes cyclic-sport endurance work more than gridiron work. Eric's baseline: for football the priority stack is alactic and aerobic capacity and power, with glycolytic work \"a \"worst case scenario\"\" absorbed through camp and practice (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 116). Inference: to-fatigue strength-endurance sets would add glycolytic load the department deliberately does not program.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Do not quote the 75-80% ceiling as a firm rule without the 80-85% skater and 50-80% rower examples. The determinants list has no weightings. \"60% of the maximal work capacity time\" is not defined further (60% of the time to failure is the plain reading).\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: 25-50% 1RM at 60-120 reps per minute to fatigue, or to 60% of the maximal work time (PDF p. 296 / printed p. 285).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--strength-endurance-taxonomy-and-absolute-vs-partial]] (Ch3.2.2, the dynamic/static and absolute/partial distinctions this section restates); [[claim--zatsiorsky-and-hansen-show-strength-endurance-training-specificity]]; [[case-study--soviet-sport-specific-strength-endurance-prescriptions]]; [[framework--resistance-training-prescription-by-goal]] (Ch1 Table 1.1, endurance at 40-60% 1RM)\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011Cffac8Cg3EswqWQz45rea","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"af5d3bd3-36e9-47a1-a346-b614d5cdf9a6","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:59:06.835Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"af5d3bd3-36e9-47a1-a346-b614d5cdf9a6","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"624a8cec-33fa-4c0a-a445-c72ce718e6ab","agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XsrER9YjvKxXiryT5TmeGp","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--strength-endurance-load-tempo-and-the-75-80-percent-ceiling.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"de300ff6-cff0-439e-b4b8-ba29bb6ecee0","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:59:06.923Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"create","filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--strength-endurance-load-tempo-and-the-75-80-percent-ceiling.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.5\"\ntopic: strength-endurance loading\ncategory: [strength-endurance, programming, load, tempo]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Strength-Endurance: 25-50% 1RM at 60-120 Reps per Minute, Ideally to Fatigue; Above 75-80% 1RM Trains Strength Instead (but the Book Also Prescribes 80-85%)\n\n**What it is** — Section 5.2.5 (PDF pp. 296-298 / printed pp. 285-287; pp. 296-298 verified against the page images):\n- **Eight determinants.** Training for strength-endurance \"is determined predominantly by: 1. The magnitude of the load 2. The tempo of movement 3. The duration of each movement and each phase of the movements 4. The rest interval between training sessions 5. The length of the training period 6. The initial levels of strength-endurance of the athlete 7. The kinesiological structure of the movement 8. The relative involvement of static and dynamic strength-endurance\" (PDF p. 296 / printed p. 285).\n- **Basic prescription.** \"Repetition work with weights 25-50% of 1RM at a moderate tempo (from 60-120 repetitions/minute) is used for developing strength-endurance. The effectiveness ... will be higher if the work is executed to complete fatigue, although work of shorter duration (60% of the maximal work capacity time) also will yield satisfactory results (Agolinsky, 1953; Korobkov, 1953; Monogarov, 1958; Diachkov, 1961)\" (same page).\n- **Ceiling.** \"When the activity involves repetitively overcoming significant resistance (exceeding 75-80% of 1RM), one cannot enhance endurance, since one becomes constrained to developing strength predominantly (Zatsiorsky, 1966)\" (same page).\n- **Progression.** \"It is necessary to increase the amount of weight and the number of repetitions as strength-endurance increases\" (PDF p. 297 / printed p. 286).\n- **Summary rule.** \"multiple repetition exercises with various weights should be considered the fundamental method for developing strength-endurance, with the load being determined by the dynamics of the given exercise. In cases requiring intense effort, an optimally heavy weight should be used together with light weights or with exercises simulating the given sport activity. If the special exercise is associated with the prolonged production of moderate force, then it is useful to work with light weights in repeated sets to the point of fatigue or failure\" (PDF p. 298 / printed p. 287).\n\n**Internal conflict, recorded:** the 75-80% ceiling (PDF p. 296 / printed p. 285) conflicts with the sport examples a page later: \"Exercises with heavy weights (80-85% of 1RM) ... are recommended for developing strength-endurance in skaters\", 4-12 reps per set to complete fatigue with 2-4 min rest (Mikhailov & Panov, 1975), and \"Male rowers obtain good results with weights of 50-80% of 1RM\" (Ulrich et al, 1966) (PDF p. 297 / printed p. 286). The summary rule on p. 287 (\"an optimally heavy weight ... together with light weights\" where intense effort is needed) sits closer to the examples than to the ceiling. The book does not reconcile them.\n\n**Why it works** — The ceiling's stated reason: above 75-80% the work is \"constrained to developing strength predominantly.\" The load-to-fatigue advantage is reported from the cited Soviet studies, without a stated mechanism.\n\n**When to apply** — Inference: for football, where strength-endurance means repeated forceful efforts (blocking, pushing a sled), the summary rule fits better than the 25-50% band: a heavy-enough load mixed with lighter or sport-simulating work. The 60-120 reps per minute tempo describes cyclic-sport endurance work more than gridiron work. Eric's baseline: for football the priority stack is alactic and aerobic capacity and power, with glycolytic work \"a \"worst case scenario\"\" absorbed through camp and practice (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 116). Inference: to-fatigue strength-endurance sets would add glycolytic load the department deliberately does not program.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Do not quote the 75-80% ceiling as a firm rule without the 80-85% skater and 50-80% rower examples. The determinants list has no weightings. \"60% of the maximal work capacity time\" is not defined further (60% of the time to failure is the plain reading).\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: 25-50% 1RM at 60-120 reps per minute to fatigue, or to 60% of the maximal work time (PDF p. 296 / printed p. 285).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--strength-endurance-taxonomy-and-absolute-vs-partial]] (Ch3.2.2, the dynamic/static and absolute/partial distinctions this section restates); [[claim--zatsiorsky-and-hansen-show-strength-endurance-training-specificity]]; [[case-study--soviet-sport-specific-strength-endurance-prescriptions]]; [[framework--resistance-training-prescription-by-goal]] (Ch1 Table 1.1, endurance at 40-60% 1RM)\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"af5d3bd3-36e9-47a1-a346-b614d5cdf9a6","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"de300ff6-cff0-439e-b4b8-ba29bb6ecee0","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffaccSYpd11vgCgRMLtm","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01E6Yj7jb7kLhZ3hKwG9RPN4","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--zatsiorsky-and-hansen-show-strength-endurance-training-specificity.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: claim\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.5\"\ntopic: specificity of endurance gains\ncategory: [strength-endurance, specificity, isometric, research-findings]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Endurance Training at 60% Raised Isometric Endurance 84%, Dynamic Endurance 93% and Repeated-Isometric Endurance 219%, While Maximal-Resistance Training Left Endurance Unchanged\n\n**What it is** — Two experiments reported in 5.2.5 (PDF pp. 296-297 / printed pp. 285-286, verified against the page images):\n- **Zatsiorsky (1966), elbow flexors, 5 weeks:** \"isometric endurance increased 84% as a result of exercising 10 times a day for 5 weeks with the elbow flexors holding a 60% maximal contraction for as long as possible. Dynamic endurance increased by 93% as a result of performing these arm curls with a weight of 60% of 1RM at a rate of 28 repetitions per minute until it was impossible to increase the tempo further. At the same time, the ability to execute repetitive isometric contractions (60% of maximal strength for 5 seconds of tension and 2 seconds of rest) to failure increased by 219%.\"\n- **Hansen (1963):** \"A similar experiment with 10 dynamic movements executed daily with maximal resistance for 5 weeks resulted in increases in both dynamic and static strength, but dynamic and static endurance remained almost unchanged.\"\n- The authors' conclusion: \"This indicates that it is necessary to optimise the volume of work required to develop strength-endurance.\"\n\nReading note: in the image the \"28\" sits in the shaded page gutter; the first digit is faint but reads as 2.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors give the results without a mechanism beyond the 75-80% ceiling statement that heavy work trains \"strength predominantly\" (see [[heuristic--strength-endurance-load-tempo-and-the-75-80-percent-ceiling]]). Inference: the text's sentence \"At the same time, the ability to execute repetitive isometric contractions ... increased by 219%\" does not say which training group (isometric or dynamic) showed the 219%; the passage is ambiguous.\n\n**When to apply** — As the authors' evidence that endurance gains come from submaximal, long-duration work and not from maximal-resistance training. Inference: a maximal-strength block will not by itself raise local muscular endurance, per Hansen's result.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Single-muscle-group (elbow flexor) studies with no subject numbers or populations given; percentage gains in time-to-failure tests are typically large and should not be compared directly with strength percentages. Which group produced the 219% is unclear. Do not generalise to whole-body or sport endurance.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: Zatsiorsky's 60% elbow flexor protocols versus Hansen's 10 daily maximal movements (PDF pp. 296-297 / printed pp. 285-286).\n\n**Related** — [[heuristic--strength-endurance-load-tempo-and-the-75-80-percent-ceiling]]; [[framework--strength-endurance-taxonomy-and-absolute-vs-partial]]; [[principle--local-muscular-endurance-adaptation-is-specific-to-the-trained-muscles]]; [[claim--isometric-endurance-limits-and-recovery-phases]]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3282,"cache_read_input_tokens":277322,"output_tokens":3467,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":140},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3282},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":3467,"cache_read_input_tokens":277322,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3282,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3282,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01E6Yj7jb7kLhZ3hKwG9RPN4":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--zatsiorsky-and-hansen-show-strength-endurance-training-specificity.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: claim\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.5\"\ntopic: specificity of endurance gains\ncategory: [strength-endurance, specificity, isometric, research-findings]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Endurance Training at 60% Raised Isometric Endurance 84%, Dynamic Endurance 93% and Repeated-Isometric Endurance 219%, While Maximal-Resistance Training Left Endurance Unchanged\n\n**What it is** — Two experiments reported in 5.2.5 (PDF pp. 296-297 / printed pp. 285-286, verified against the page images):\n- **Zatsiorsky (1966), elbow flexors, 5 weeks:** \"isometric endurance increased 84% as a result of exercising 10 times a day for 5 weeks with the elbow flexors holding a 60% maximal contraction for as long as possible. Dynamic endurance increased by 93% as a result of performing these arm curls with a weight of 60% of 1RM at a rate of 28 repetitions per minute until it was impossible to increase the tempo further. At the same time, the ability to execute repetitive isometric contractions (60% of maximal strength for 5 seconds of tension and 2 seconds of rest) to failure increased by 219%.\"\n- **Hansen (1963):** \"A similar experiment with 10 dynamic movements executed daily with maximal resistance for 5 weeks resulted in increases in both dynamic and static strength, but dynamic and static endurance remained almost unchanged.\"\n- The authors' conclusion: \"This indicates that it is necessary to optimise the volume of work required to develop strength-endurance.\"\n\nReading note: in the image the \"28\" sits in the shaded page gutter; the first digit is faint but reads as 2.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors give the results without a mechanism beyond the 75-80% ceiling statement that heavy work trains \"strength predominantly\" (see [[heuristic--strength-endurance-load-tempo-and-the-75-80-percent-ceiling]]). Inference: the text's sentence \"At the same time, the ability to execute repetitive isometric contractions ... increased by 219%\" does not say which training group (isometric or dynamic) showed the 219%; the passage is ambiguous.\n\n**When to apply** — As the authors' evidence that endurance gains come from submaximal, long-duration work and not from maximal-resistance training. Inference: a maximal-strength block will not by itself raise local muscular endurance, per Hansen's result.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Single-muscle-group (elbow flexor) studies with no subject numbers or populations given; percentage gains in time-to-failure tests are typically large and should not be compared directly with strength percentages. Which group produced the 219% is unclear. Do not generalise to whole-body or sport endurance.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: Zatsiorsky's 60% elbow flexor protocols versus Hansen's 10 daily maximal movements (PDF pp. 296-297 / printed pp. 285-286).\n\n**Related** — [[heuristic--strength-endurance-load-tempo-and-the-75-80-percent-ceiling]]; [[framework--strength-endurance-taxonomy-and-absolute-vs-partial]]; [[principle--local-muscular-endurance-adaptation-is-specific-to-the-trained-muscles]]; [[claim--isometric-endurance-limits-and-recovery-phases]]\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011Cffac8Cg3EswqWQz45rea","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"43a9cdf1-2995-407f-8be7-a02b8b6f36b4","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:59:17.854Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"43a9cdf1-2995-407f-8be7-a02b8b6f36b4","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"624a8cec-33fa-4c0a-a445-c72ce718e6ab","agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01E6Yj7jb7kLhZ3hKwG9RPN4","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--zatsiorsky-and-hansen-show-strength-endurance-training-specificity.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"e27155e3-9f55-48bb-9ffd-45a53b95e4fc","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:59:17.931Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"create","filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--zatsiorsky-and-hansen-show-strength-endurance-training-specificity.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: claim\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.5\"\ntopic: specificity of endurance gains\ncategory: [strength-endurance, specificity, isometric, research-findings]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Endurance Training at 60% Raised Isometric Endurance 84%, Dynamic Endurance 93% and Repeated-Isometric Endurance 219%, While Maximal-Resistance Training Left Endurance Unchanged\n\n**What it is** — Two experiments reported in 5.2.5 (PDF pp. 296-297 / printed pp. 285-286, verified against the page images):\n- **Zatsiorsky (1966), elbow flexors, 5 weeks:** \"isometric endurance increased 84% as a result of exercising 10 times a day for 5 weeks with the elbow flexors holding a 60% maximal contraction for as long as possible. Dynamic endurance increased by 93% as a result of performing these arm curls with a weight of 60% of 1RM at a rate of 28 repetitions per minute until it was impossible to increase the tempo further. At the same time, the ability to execute repetitive isometric contractions (60% of maximal strength for 5 seconds of tension and 2 seconds of rest) to failure increased by 219%.\"\n- **Hansen (1963):** \"A similar experiment with 10 dynamic movements executed daily with maximal resistance for 5 weeks resulted in increases in both dynamic and static strength, but dynamic and static endurance remained almost unchanged.\"\n- The authors' conclusion: \"This indicates that it is necessary to optimise the volume of work required to develop strength-endurance.\"\n\nReading note: in the image the \"28\" sits in the shaded page gutter; the first digit is faint but reads as 2.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors give the results without a mechanism beyond the 75-80% ceiling statement that heavy work trains \"strength predominantly\" (see [[heuristic--strength-endurance-load-tempo-and-the-75-80-percent-ceiling]]). Inference: the text's sentence \"At the same time, the ability to execute repetitive isometric contractions ... increased by 219%\" does not say which training group (isometric or dynamic) showed the 219%; the passage is ambiguous.\n\n**When to apply** — As the authors' evidence that endurance gains come from submaximal, long-duration work and not from maximal-resistance training. Inference: a maximal-strength block will not by itself raise local muscular endurance, per Hansen's result.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Single-muscle-group (elbow flexor) studies with no subject numbers or populations given; percentage gains in time-to-failure tests are typically large and should not be compared directly with strength percentages. Which group produced the 219% is unclear. Do not generalise to whole-body or sport endurance.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: Zatsiorsky's 60% elbow flexor protocols versus Hansen's 10 daily maximal movements (PDF pp. 296-297 / printed pp. 285-286).\n\n**Related** — [[heuristic--strength-endurance-load-tempo-and-the-75-80-percent-ceiling]]; [[framework--strength-endurance-taxonomy-and-absolute-vs-partial]]; [[principle--local-muscular-endurance-adaptation-is-specific-to-the-trained-muscles]]; [[claim--isometric-endurance-limits-and-recovery-phases]]\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"43a9cdf1-2995-407f-8be7-a02b8b6f36b4","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
