{"parentUuid":"b3343c6b-e995-4445-a6e4-1d2ba46ce4d7","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffafS91pSCNkPAFonjJm","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01BRz4ErVwFAJnkS4k6qFHAS","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--strength-endurance-load-tempo-and-the-75-80-percent-ceiling.md","old_string":"with glycolytic work \"a \"worst case scenario\"\" absorbed through camp and practice","new_string":"with glycolytic conditioning treated as a \"worst case scenario\" absorbed through camp and practice"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3753,"cache_read_input_tokens":280604,"output_tokens":625,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":127},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3753},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":625,"cache_read_input_tokens":280604,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3753,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3753,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01BRz4ErVwFAJnkS4k6qFHAS":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--strength-endurance-load-tempo-and-the-75-80-percent-ceiling.md","old_string":"with glycolytic work \"a \"worst case scenario\"\" absorbed through camp and practice","new_string":"with glycolytic conditioning treated as a \"worst case scenario\" absorbed through camp and practice"}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011CffaepepF5EdXnDuWj8FN","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"f694c6e1-3c1e-4d80-b7e3-1a3f025b4ee0","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:59:32.192Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"f694c6e1-3c1e-4d80-b7e3-1a3f025b4ee0","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"624a8cec-33fa-4c0a-a445-c72ce718e6ab","agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BRz4ErVwFAJnkS4k6qFHAS","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--strength-endurance-load-tempo-and-the-75-80-percent-ceiling.md has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"59d2647d-d63d-4ee1-9bec-f8a7abf0bde8","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:59:32.304Z","toolUseResult":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--strength-endurance-load-tempo-and-the-75-80-percent-ceiling.md","oldString":"with glycolytic work \"a \"worst case scenario\"\" absorbed through camp and practice","newString":"with glycolytic conditioning treated as a \"worst case scenario\" absorbed through camp and practice","originalFile":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.5\"\ntopic: strength-endurance loading\ncategory: [strength-endurance, programming, load, tempo]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Strength-Endurance: 25-50% 1RM at 60-120 Reps per Minute, Ideally to Fatigue; Above 75-80% 1RM Trains Strength Instead (but the Book Also Prescribes 80-85%)\n\n**What it is** — Section 5.2.5 (PDF pp. 296-298 / printed pp. 285-287; pp. 296-298 verified against the page images):\n- **Eight determinants.** Training for strength-endurance \"is determined predominantly by: 1. The magnitude of the load 2. The tempo of movement 3. The duration of each movement and each phase of the movements 4. The rest interval between training sessions 5. The length of the training period 6. The initial levels of strength-endurance of the athlete 7. The kinesiological structure of the movement 8. The relative involvement of static and dynamic strength-endurance\" (PDF p. 296 / printed p. 285).\n- **Basic prescription.** \"Repetition work with weights 25-50% of 1RM at a moderate tempo (from 60-120 repetitions/minute) is used for developing strength-endurance. The effectiveness ... will be higher if the work is executed to complete fatigue, although work of shorter duration (60% of the maximal work capacity time) also will yield satisfactory results (Agolinsky, 1953; Korobkov, 1953; Monogarov, 1958; Diachkov, 1961)\" (same page).\n- **Ceiling.** \"When the activity involves repetitively overcoming significant resistance (exceeding 75-80% of 1RM), one cannot enhance endurance, since one becomes constrained to developing strength predominantly (Zatsiorsky, 1966)\" (same page).\n- **Progression.** \"It is necessary to increase the amount of weight and the number of repetitions as strength-endurance increases\" (PDF p. 297 / printed p. 286).\n- **Summary rule.** \"multiple repetition exercises with various weights should be considered the fundamental method for developing strength-endurance, with the load being determined by the dynamics of the given exercise. In cases requiring intense effort, an optimally heavy weight should be used together with light weights or with exercises simulating the given sport activity. If the special exercise is associated with the prolonged production of moderate force, then it is useful to work with light weights in repeated sets to the point of fatigue or failure\" (PDF p. 298 / printed p. 287).\n\n**Internal conflict, recorded:** the 75-80% ceiling (PDF p. 296 / printed p. 285) conflicts with the sport examples a page later: \"Exercises with heavy weights (80-85% of 1RM) ... are recommended for developing strength-endurance in skaters\", 4-12 reps per set to complete fatigue with 2-4 min rest (Mikhailov & Panov, 1975), and \"Male rowers obtain good results with weights of 50-80% of 1RM\" (Ulrich et al, 1966) (PDF p. 297 / printed p. 286). The summary rule on p. 287 (\"an optimally heavy weight ... together with light weights\" where intense effort is needed) sits closer to the examples than to the ceiling. The book does not reconcile them.\n\n**Why it works** — The ceiling's stated reason: above 75-80% the work is \"constrained to developing strength predominantly.\" The load-to-fatigue advantage is reported from the cited Soviet studies, without a stated mechanism.\n\n**When to apply** — Inference: for football, where strength-endurance means repeated forceful efforts (blocking, pushing a sled), the summary rule fits better than the 25-50% band: a heavy-enough load mixed with lighter or sport-simulating work. The 60-120 reps per minute tempo describes cyclic-sport endurance work more than gridiron work. Eric's baseline: for football the priority stack is alactic and aerobic capacity and power, with glycolytic work \"a \"worst case scenario\"\" absorbed through camp and practice (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 116). Inference: to-fatigue strength-endurance sets would add glycolytic load the department deliberately does not program.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Do not quote the 75-80% ceiling as a firm rule without the 80-85% skater and 50-80% rower examples. The determinants list has no weightings. \"60% of the maximal work capacity time\" is not defined further (60% of the time to failure is the plain reading).\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: 25-50% 1RM at 60-120 reps per minute to fatigue, or to 60% of the maximal work time (PDF p. 296 / printed p. 285).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--strength-endurance-taxonomy-and-absolute-vs-partial]] (Ch3.2.2, the dynamic/static and absolute/partial distinctions this section restates); [[claim--zatsiorsky-and-hansen-show-strength-endurance-training-specificity]]; [[case-study--soviet-sport-specific-strength-endurance-prescriptions]]; [[framework--resistance-training-prescription-by-goal]] (Ch1 Table 1.1, endurance at 40-60% 1RM)\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":24,"oldLines":7,"newStart":24,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," **Why it works** — The ceiling's stated reason: above 75-80% the work is \"constrained to developing strength predominantly.\" The load-to-fatigue advantage is reported from the cited Soviet studies, without a stated mechanism."," ","-**When to apply** — Inference: for football, where strength-endurance means repeated forceful efforts (blocking, pushing a sled), the summary rule fits better than the 25-50% band: a heavy-enough load mixed with lighter or sport-simulating work. The 60-120 reps per minute tempo describes cyclic-sport endurance work more than gridiron work. Eric's baseline: for football the priority stack is alactic and aerobic capacity and power, with glycolytic work \"a \"worst case scenario\"\" absorbed through camp and practice (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 116). Inference: to-fatigue strength-endurance sets would add glycolytic load the department deliberately does not program.","+**When to apply** — Inference: for football, where strength-endurance means repeated forceful efforts (blocking, pushing a sled), the summary rule fits better than the 25-50% band: a heavy-enough load mixed with lighter or sport-simulating work. The 60-120 reps per minute tempo describes cyclic-sport endurance work more than gridiron work. Eric's baseline: for football the priority stack is alactic and aerobic capacity and power, with glycolytic conditioning treated as a \"worst case scenario\" absorbed through camp and practice (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 116). Inference: to-fatigue strength-endurance sets would add glycolytic load the department deliberately does not program."," "," **When NOT to apply** — Do not quote the 75-80% ceiling as a firm rule without the 80-85% skater and 50-80% rower examples. The determinants list has no weightings. \"60% of the maximal work capacity time\" is not defined further (60% of the time to failure is the plain reading)."," "]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"f694c6e1-3c1e-4d80-b7e3-1a3f025b4ee0","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
