nd":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"2ef7ac64-6342-4af5-a274-b09dee24e551","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"624a8cec-33fa-4c0a-a445-c72ce718e6ab","agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016FzJb5KL4hawSwfibmQyJM","type":"tool_result","content":"**What it is** — Under the subheading \"Strength Training and General Endurance,\" the authors cite \"research showing that strength training leads to a greater rise in haemoglobin content (Petrov & Lapchenkov, 1978) and myoglobin (Pattengale & Holloszy, 1967; Hemmingsen, 1963) than endurance training; and that strength training, even in the beginning stages of training, contributes more to the largest improvements in distance sports than aerobic training (Neminushchii & Filin, 1972; Gavrilenko & Mikhailov, 1981)\" (PDF pp. 261-262 / printed pp. 250-251, verified against the page images). \"Several decades ago, scientists pointed out the importance of local (strength) endurance for cyclic types of sport\" (seven citations, 1962-1974), \"but in our time, it has not been given serious attention,\" because endurance methods aimed \"chiefly at improving the cardiovascular-respiratory functions (mainly by distance training)\" (PDF p. 262 / printed p. 251).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors give no mechanism in this passage. Inference: it follows from their preceding argument that endurance depends on the muscles' strength and oxidative qualities (see Related), but they do not state that link here.","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"7e515c08-abff-4628-ad46-34276b467840","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:59:51.041Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"**What it is** — Under the subheading \"Strength Training and General Endurance,\" the authors cite \"research showing that strength training leads to a greater rise in haemoglobin content (Petrov & Lapchenkov, 1978) and myoglobin (Pattengale & Holloszy, 1967; Hemmingsen, 1963) than endurance training; and that strength training, even in the beginning stages of training, contributes more to the largest improvements in distance sports than aerobic training (Neminushchii & Filin, 1972; Gavrilenko & Mikhailov, 1981)\" (PDF pp. 261-262 / printed pp. 250-251, verified against the page images). \"Several decades ago, scientists pointed out the importance of local (strength) endurance for cyclic types of sport\" (seven citations, 1962-1974), \"but in our time, it has not been given serious attention,\" because endurance methods aimed \"chiefly at improving the cardiovascular-respiratory functions (mainly by distance training)\" (PDF p. 262 / printed p. 251).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors give no mechanism in this passage. Inference: it follows from their preceding argument that endurance depends on the muscles' strength and oxidative qualities (see Related), but they do not state that link here.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"2ef7ac64-6342-4af5-a274-b09dee24e551","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
