{"parentUuid":"4ec33ee3-23b0-452c-9b4e-bd9bf668d215","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffahVtJ3GDMPnpCNtyuC","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Hv6M1q8ka6Qmx7N4UE7zeo","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--strength-endurance-hypertrophy-and-cyclic-endurance.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: claim\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.5\"\ntopic: hypertrophy and endurance\ncategory: [strength-endurance, hypertrophy, endurance-sport, relative-strength]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Strength-Endurance Helps Cyclic Endurance, but Some Popular Strength Exercises and Excess Hypertrophy Hinder It; Never Let Relative Strength Fall\n\n**What it is** — From 5.2.5 (PDF pp. 297-298 / printed pp. 286-287, verified against the page images):\n- \"an increase in strength-endurance contributes to improving endurance in distance runners, skiers and skaters (Popov, 1968; Nabatnikova, 1972; Mikhailov & Panov, 1975). However ... the magnitude of the strength load used is different if, on the one hand, the key quality required is general endurance and, on the other hand, it is strength-endurance.\"\n- \"certain popular strength exercises such as running with a sack of sand weighing 25% of the subject's bodyweight, barbell pressing, squats, 'good mornings', and twisting with a barbell not only do not enhance, but to a certain degree, will even hinder the development of endurance in cyclic type exercises (Maisuradze, 1960).\" In some instances \"the increase in strength is associated with excessive muscle hypertrophy\". \"Endurance and hypertrophy do not increase simultaneously and there is no correlation between them (Maison & Broeker, 1941; McMorris & Elkins, 1954).\"\n- No definite correlation between special strength and muscle mass in cyclic endurance sports (middle distance running, skating, skiing: Chistyakov, 1965; Chernyaev, 1965; Nabatnikova, 1972). But in gymnasts \"a positive linear correlation (R = 0.77) is noticed between strength-endurance and relative strength (Zhalei, 1964). Therefore, in those cases where endurance is associated with the display of significant strength, some muscular hypertrophy does not negatively influence training results.\"\n- Closing rules: \"Strength training should not result in increased muscle mass in those types of sport where the key quality is endurance of moderate work output. If strength-endurance is required, especially in those cases where it is necessary to overcome a large resistance, a small increase in muscle mass is permissible. In no cases, should the training ever produce a decrease in relative strength of any muscle groups in the body\" (PDF p. 298 / printed p. 287).\n\n**Tension with Chapter 4, recorded:** section 4.4 cites research that strength training \"contributes more to the largest improvements in distance sports than aerobic training\" (PDF pp. 261-262 / printed pp. 250-251; see [[claim--strength-training-raises-haemoglobin-myoglobin-and-aids-distance-sport]]), while 5.2.5 says squats, presses and good mornings \"will even hinder the development of endurance in cyclic type exercises\" (PDF p. 297 / printed p. 286). Inference: the two may differ in which strength exercises and loads are meant, but the book does not reconcile them.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' explanation is the hypertrophy by-product and the lack of correlation between endurance and hypertrophy; they give no physiology beyond that.\n\n**When to apply** — Inference: for football the relevant case is the gymnast-like one, endurance \"associated with the display of significant strength\", where some hypertrophy \"does not negatively influence training results.\" The relative-strength rule applies to every position: Inference: track strength per kg of bodymass when linemen are gaining mass, since the authors say training should never lower relative strength.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The Maisuradze (1960) list is a single citation without design or loads beyond the 25% bodyweight sandbag. The correlation and hypertrophy statements cite studies from 1941-1972. Not a reason to avoid squats for endurance athletes in general; the authors' own sport prescriptions (skaters, runners) use squats ([[case-study--soviet-sport-specific-strength-endurance-prescriptions]]), which is itself in tension with the Maisuradze sentence.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: gymnasts, R = 0.77 between strength-endurance and relative strength (Zhalei, 1964), against middle-distance runners, skaters and skiers with no definite strength-mass correlation (PDF p. 297 / printed p. 286).\n\n**Related** — [[claim--strength-training-raises-haemoglobin-myoglobin-and-aids-distance-sport]]; [[claim--endurance-rests-on-muscle-oxidative-and-strength-qualities-not-vo2max]]; [[framework--bodymass-strength-scaling-laws]]; [[heuristic--strength-endurance-load-tempo-and-the-75-80-percent-ceiling]]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":622,"cache_read_input_tokens":286453,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":622,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01Hv6M1q8ka6Qmx7N4UE7zeo":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--strength-endurance-hypertrophy-and-cyclic-endurance.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: claim\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.5\"\ntopic: hypertrophy and endurance\ncategory: [strength-endurance, hypertrophy, endurance-sport, relative-strength]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Strength-Endurance Helps Cyclic Endurance, but Some Popular Strength Exercises and Excess Hypertrophy Hinder It; Never Let Relative Strength Fall\n\n**What it is** — From 5.2.5 (PDF pp. 297-298 / printed pp. 286-287, verified against the page images):\n- \"an increase in strength-endurance contributes to improving endurance in distance runners, skiers and skaters (Popov, 1968; Nabatnikova, 1972; Mikhailov & Panov, 1975). However ... the magnitude of the strength load used is different if, on the one hand, the key quality required is general endurance and, on the other hand, it is strength-endurance.\"\n- \"certain popular strength exercises such as running with a sack of sand weighing 25% of the subject's bodyweight, barbell pressing, squats, 'good mornings', and twisting with a barbell not only do not enhance, but to a certain degree, will even hinder the development of endurance in cyclic type exercises (Maisuradze, 1960).\" In some instances \"the increase in strength is associated with excessive muscle hypertrophy\". \"Endurance and hypertrophy do not increase simultaneously and there is no correlation between them (Maison & Broeker, 1941; McMorris & Elkins, 1954).\"\n- No definite correlation between special strength and muscle mass in cyclic endurance sports (middle distance running, skating, skiing: Chistyakov, 1965; Chernyaev, 1965; Nabatnikova, 1972). But in gymnasts \"a positive linear correlation (R = 0.77) is noticed between strength-endurance and relative strength (Zhalei, 1964). Therefore, in those cases where endurance is associated with the display of significant strength, some muscular hypertrophy does not negatively influence training results.\"\n- Closing rules: \"Strength training should not result in increased muscle mass in those types of sport where the key quality is endurance of moderate work output. If strength-endurance is required, especially in those cases where it is necessary to overcome a large resistance, a small increase in muscle mass is permissible. In no cases, should the training ever produce a decrease in relative strength of any muscle groups in the body\" (PDF p. 298 / printed p. 287).\n\n**Tension with Chapter 4, recorded:** section 4.4 cites research that strength training \"contributes more to the largest improvements in distance sports than aerobic training\" (PDF pp. 261-262 / printed pp. 250-251; see [[claim--strength-training-raises-haemoglobin-myoglobin-and-aids-distance-sport]]), while 5.2.5 says squats, presses and good mornings \"will even hinder the development of endurance in cyclic type exercises\" (PDF p. 297 / printed p. 286). Inference: the two may differ in which strength exercises and loads are meant, but the book does not reconcile them.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' explanation is the hypertrophy by-product and the lack of correlation between endurance and hypertrophy; they give no physiology beyond that.\n\n**When to apply** — Inference: for football the relevant case is the gymnast-like one, endurance \"associated with the display of significant strength\", where some hypertrophy \"does not negatively influence training results.\" The relative-strength rule applies to every position: Inference: track strength per kg of bodymass when linemen are gaining mass, since the authors say training should never lower relative strength.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The Maisuradze (1960) list is a single citation without design or loads beyond the 25% bodyweight sandbag. The correlation and hypertrophy statements cite studies from 1941-1972. Not a reason to avoid squats for endurance athletes in general; the authors' own sport prescriptions (skaters, runners) use squats ([[case-study--soviet-sport-specific-strength-endurance-prescriptions]]), which is itself in tension with the Maisuradze sentence.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: gymnasts, R = 0.77 between strength-endurance and relative strength (Zhalei, 1964), against middle-distance runners, skaters and skiers with no definite strength-mass correlation (PDF p. 297 / printed p. 286).\n\n**Related** — [[claim--strength-training-raises-haemoglobin-myoglobin-and-aids-distance-sport]]; [[claim--endurance-rests-on-muscle-oxidative-and-strength-qualities-not-vo2max]]; [[framework--bodymass-strength-scaling-laws]]; [[heuristic--strength-endurance-load-tempo-and-the-75-80-percent-ceiling]]\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffahGBYJ6sf9uCinrRgZ","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"639a02d7-db9b-4678-83b7-45ae7958e499","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:00:12.687Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"639a02d7-db9b-4678-83b7-45ae7958e499","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"624a8cec-33fa-4c0a-a445-c72ce718e6ab","agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Hv6M1q8ka6Qmx7N4UE7zeo","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--strength-endurance-hypertrophy-and-cyclic-endurance.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"9d9d3e82-0fa6-4258-b578-9ae21e352eaa","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:00:12.791Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"create","filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--strength-endurance-hypertrophy-and-cyclic-endurance.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: claim\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.5\"\ntopic: hypertrophy and endurance\ncategory: [strength-endurance, hypertrophy, endurance-sport, relative-strength]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Strength-Endurance Helps Cyclic Endurance, but Some Popular Strength Exercises and Excess Hypertrophy Hinder It; Never Let Relative Strength Fall\n\n**What it is** — From 5.2.5 (PDF pp. 297-298 / printed pp. 286-287, verified against the page images):\n- \"an increase in strength-endurance contributes to improving endurance in distance runners, skiers and skaters (Popov, 1968; Nabatnikova, 1972; Mikhailov & Panov, 1975). However ... the magnitude of the strength load used is different if, on the one hand, the key quality required is general endurance and, on the other hand, it is strength-endurance.\"\n- \"certain popular strength exercises such as running with a sack of sand weighing 25% of the subject's bodyweight, barbell pressing, squats, 'good mornings', and twisting with a barbell not only do not enhance, but to a certain degree, will even hinder the development of endurance in cyclic type exercises (Maisuradze, 1960).\" In some instances \"the increase in strength is associated with excessive muscle hypertrophy\". \"Endurance and hypertrophy do not increase simultaneously and there is no correlation between them (Maison & Broeker, 1941; McMorris & Elkins, 1954).\"\n- No definite correlation between special strength and muscle mass in cyclic endurance sports (middle distance running, skating, skiing: Chistyakov, 1965; Chernyaev, 1965; Nabatnikova, 1972). But in gymnasts \"a positive linear correlation (R = 0.77) is noticed between strength-endurance and relative strength (Zhalei, 1964). Therefore, in those cases where endurance is associated with the display of significant strength, some muscular hypertrophy does not negatively influence training results.\"\n- Closing rules: \"Strength training should not result in increased muscle mass in those types of sport where the key quality is endurance of moderate work output. If strength-endurance is required, especially in those cases where it is necessary to overcome a large resistance, a small increase in muscle mass is permissible. In no cases, should the training ever produce a decrease in relative strength of any muscle groups in the body\" (PDF p. 298 / printed p. 287).\n\n**Tension with Chapter 4, recorded:** section 4.4 cites research that strength training \"contributes more to the largest improvements in distance sports than aerobic training\" (PDF pp. 261-262 / printed pp. 250-251; see [[claim--strength-training-raises-haemoglobin-myoglobin-and-aids-distance-sport]]), while 5.2.5 says squats, presses and good mornings \"will even hinder the development of endurance in cyclic type exercises\" (PDF p. 297 / printed p. 286). Inference: the two may differ in which strength exercises and loads are meant, but the book does not reconcile them.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' explanation is the hypertrophy by-product and the lack of correlation between endurance and hypertrophy; they give no physiology beyond that.\n\n**When to apply** — Inference: for football the relevant case is the gymnast-like one, endurance \"associated with the display of significant strength\", where some hypertrophy \"does not negatively influence training results.\" The relative-strength rule applies to every position: Inference: track strength per kg of bodymass when linemen are gaining mass, since the authors say training should never lower relative strength.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The Maisuradze (1960) list is a single citation without design or loads beyond the 25% bodyweight sandbag. The correlation and hypertrophy statements cite studies from 1941-1972. Not a reason to avoid squats for endurance athletes in general; the authors' own sport prescriptions (skaters, runners) use squats ([[case-study--soviet-sport-specific-strength-endurance-prescriptions]]), which is itself in tension with the Maisuradze sentence.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: gymnasts, R = 0.77 between strength-endurance and relative strength (Zhalei, 1964), against middle-distance runners, skaters and skiers with no definite strength-mass correlation (PDF p. 297 / printed p. 286).\n\n**Related** — [[claim--strength-training-raises-haemoglobin-myoglobin-and-aids-distance-sport]]; [[claim--endurance-rests-on-muscle-oxidative-and-strength-qualities-not-vo2max]]; [[framework--bodymass-strength-scaling-laws]]; [[heuristic--strength-endurance-load-tempo-and-the-75-80-percent-ceiling]]\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"639a02d7-db9b-4678-83b7-45ae7958e499","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"9d9d3e82-0fa6-4258-b578-9ae21e352eaa","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffahVtJ3GDMPnpCNtyuC","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01BZE3J2dceo3xrL4H3yEpqa","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/case-study--soviet-sport-specific-strength-endurance-prescriptions.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: case-study\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.5\"\ntopic: strength-endurance prescriptions by sport\ncategory: [strength-endurance, programming, endurance-sport, case-study]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Sport-Specific Strength-Endurance Prescriptions: Skiers, Distance Runners, Skaters and Rowers\n\n**What it is** — The authors' examples of applying the general strength-endurance principles \"differently in each specific case\" (PDF p. 297 / printed p. 286, verified against the page image):\n- **Skiers (Chistyakov, 1965):** in the preparatory period, weights \"up to 65% of 1RM\", combined with simulation exercises on a flat surface and roller-ski movements.\n- **Middle and long distance runners (Likachyevskaya & Kovalchuk, 1963):** a barbell of \"55-60% of bodymass\" lifted to head level \"8-10 times rhythmically\"; clean-and-jerks with \"60-85% of bodymass\"; kettlebell jumps from a squat with \"about one-third of bodymass for 18-20 repetitions\"; squats with \"55-60% of bodymass until complete fatigue.\" Also (Popov, 1966): \"60-80% of 1RM for multiple repetitions, jumping and hard running.\"\n- **Skaters (Mikhailov & Panov, 1975):** heavy weights \"(80-85% of 1RM)\" for \"several sets at a moderate tempo for 4-12 repetitions per set until the point of complete fatigue, with 2-4 minutes of rest between sets\"; light and moderate weights \"as rapidly as possible for 15-25 repetitions per set, for 3-5 sets with rest intervals of 5-8 minutes.\" Leg progression: \"increasing the number of barbell squats by 20-30 each week (beginning with 50 squats per set and over a period of 2-3 months increasing this to 200-300 repetitions), then increasing the amount of weight and starting a new cycle of strength training, but beginning with a smaller number of squats.\"\n- **Rowers:** men \"50-80% of 1RM\", women \"30-40% of 1RM\" (Ulrich et al, 1966). Women rowers with light weights \"(18-20kg) for many repetitions\" improved strength-endurance \"up to 40% from initial levels\" (Chumakova, 1964), more than with \"(35-50 kg) executed in sets of 2-3 repetitions\", \"and it is even greater (by 28%) than that produced by a combination of both methods.\"\n\n**Why it works** — The authors present these as applications of the general principles (load set by the dynamics of the sport exercise). No mechanism is given per sport.\n\n**When to apply** — As reference doses when designing strength-endurance work for cyclic athletes. Inference: the skater squat progression (50 to 200-300 reps per set over 2-3 months) is an extreme local-endurance model with no obvious football use; the rower result (light, many reps beating heavy, low-rep sets for strength-endurance) is the transferable point.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The skaters' 80-85% 1RM conflicts with the 75-80% 1RM ceiling stated a page earlier (see [[heuristic--strength-endurance-load-tempo-and-the-75-80-percent-ceiling]]). Loads mix % of 1RM and % of bodymass. The Chumakova \"by 28%\" comparison is ambiguous (28 percentage points or 28% relative is not stated). No sample sizes, durations or outcome tests are given. These are Soviet-era single-source prescriptions.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: the skater squat progression from 50 to 200-300 reps per set before raising the weight and restarting with fewer reps (PDF p. 297 / printed p. 286).\n\n**Related** — [[heuristic--strength-endurance-load-tempo-and-the-75-80-percent-ceiling]]; [[heuristic--strength-endurance-under-demanding-sport-like-conditions]]; [[claim--strength-endurance-hypertrophy-and-cyclic-endurance]]; [[framework--strength-endurance-taxonomy-and-absolute-vs-partial]]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":622,"cache_read_input_tokens":286453,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":622,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01BZE3J2dceo3xrL4H3yEpqa":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/case-study--soviet-sport-specific-strength-endurance-prescriptions.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: case-study\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.5\"\ntopic: strength-endurance prescriptions by sport\ncategory: [strength-endurance, programming, endurance-sport, case-study]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Sport-Specific Strength-Endurance Prescriptions: Skiers, Distance Runners, Skaters and Rowers\n\n**What it is** — The authors' examples of applying the general strength-endurance principles \"differently in each specific case\" (PDF p. 297 / printed p. 286, verified against the page image):\n- **Skiers (Chistyakov, 1965):** in the preparatory period, weights \"up to 65% of 1RM\", combined with simulation exercises on a flat surface and roller-ski movements.\n- **Middle and long distance runners (Likachyevskaya & Kovalchuk, 1963):** a barbell of \"55-60% of bodymass\" lifted to head level \"8-10 times rhythmically\"; clean-and-jerks with \"60-85% of bodymass\"; kettlebell jumps from a squat with \"about one-third of bodymass for 18-20 repetitions\"; squats with \"55-60% of bodymass until complete fatigue.\" Also (Popov, 1966): \"60-80% of 1RM for multiple repetitions, jumping and hard running.\"\n- **Skaters (Mikhailov & Panov, 1975):** heavy weights \"(80-85% of 1RM)\" for \"several sets at a moderate tempo for 4-12 repetitions per set until the point of complete fatigue, with 2-4 minutes of rest between sets\"; light and moderate weights \"as rapidly as possible for 15-25 repetitions per set, for 3-5 sets with rest intervals of 5-8 minutes.\" Leg progression: \"increasing the number of barbell squats by 20-30 each week (beginning with 50 squats per set and over a period of 2-3 months increasing this to 200-300 repetitions), then increasing the amount of weight and starting a new cycle of strength training, but beginning with a smaller number of squats.\"\n- **Rowers:** men \"50-80% of 1RM\", women \"30-40% of 1RM\" (Ulrich et al, 1966). Women rowers with light weights \"(18-20kg) for many repetitions\" improved strength-endurance \"up to 40% from initial levels\" (Chumakova, 1964), more than with \"(35-50 kg) executed in sets of 2-3 repetitions\", \"and it is even greater (by 28%) than that produced by a combination of both methods.\"\n\n**Why it works** — The authors present these as applications of the general principles (load set by the dynamics of the sport exercise). No mechanism is given per sport.\n\n**When to apply** — As reference doses when designing strength-endurance work for cyclic athletes. Inference: the skater squat progression (50 to 200-300 reps per set over 2-3 months) is an extreme local-endurance model with no obvious football use; the rower result (light, many reps beating heavy, low-rep sets for strength-endurance) is the transferable point.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The skaters' 80-85% 1RM conflicts with the 75-80% 1RM ceiling stated a page earlier (see [[heuristic--strength-endurance-load-tempo-and-the-75-80-percent-ceiling]]). Loads mix % of 1RM and % of bodymass. The Chumakova \"by 28%\" comparison is ambiguous (28 percentage points or 28% relative is not stated). No sample sizes, durations or outcome tests are given. These are Soviet-era single-source prescriptions.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: the skater squat progression from 50 to 200-300 reps per set before raising the weight and restarting with fewer reps (PDF p. 297 / printed p. 286).\n\n**Related** — [[heuristic--strength-endurance-load-tempo-and-the-75-80-percent-ceiling]]; [[heuristic--strength-endurance-under-demanding-sport-like-conditions]]; [[claim--strength-endurance-hypertrophy-and-cyclic-endurance]]; [[framework--strength-endurance-taxonomy-and-absolute-vs-partial]]\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011CffahGBYJ6sf9uCinrRgZ","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"ec9d9ead-7fb6-44fa-b423-3d48156f8104","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:00:24.937Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"ec9d9ead-7fb6-44fa-b423-3d48156f8104","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"624a8cec-33fa-4c0a-a445-c72ce718e6ab","agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BZE3J2dceo3xrL4H3yEpqa","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/case-study--soviet-sport-specific-strength-endurance-prescriptions.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"be4794d2-d960-4564-9235-dcbdf8d20737","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:00:25.057Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"create","filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/case-study--soviet-sport-specific-strength-endurance-prescriptions.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: case-study\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.5\"\ntopic: strength-endurance prescriptions by sport\ncategory: [strength-endurance, programming, endurance-sport, case-study]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Sport-Specific Strength-Endurance Prescriptions: Skiers, Distance Runners, Skaters and Rowers\n\n**What it is** — The authors' examples of applying the general strength-endurance principles \"differently in each specific case\" (PDF p. 297 / printed p. 286, verified against the page image):\n- **Skiers (Chistyakov, 1965):** in the preparatory period, weights \"up to 65% of 1RM\", combined with simulation exercises on a flat surface and roller-ski movements.\n- **Middle and long distance runners (Likachyevskaya & Kovalchuk, 1963):** a barbell of \"55-60% of bodymass\" lifted to head level \"8-10 times rhythmically\"; clean-and-jerks with \"60-85% of bodymass\"; kettlebell jumps from a squat with \"about one-third of bodymass for 18-20 repetitions\"; squats with \"55-60% of bodymass until complete fatigue.\" Also (Popov, 1966): \"60-80% of 1RM for multiple repetitions, jumping and hard running.\"\n- **Skaters (Mikhailov & Panov, 1975):** heavy weights \"(80-85% of 1RM)\" for \"several sets at a moderate tempo for 4-12 repetitions per set until the point of complete fatigue, with 2-4 minutes of rest between sets\"; light and moderate weights \"as rapidly as possible for 15-25 repetitions per set, for 3-5 sets with rest intervals of 5-8 minutes.\" Leg progression: \"increasing the number of barbell squats by 20-30 each week (beginning with 50 squats per set and over a period of 2-3 months increasing this to 200-300 repetitions), then increasing the amount of weight and starting a new cycle of strength training, but beginning with a smaller number of squats.\"\n- **Rowers:** men \"50-80% of 1RM\", women \"30-40% of 1RM\" (Ulrich et al, 1966). Women rowers with light weights \"(18-20kg) for many repetitions\" improved strength-endurance \"up to 40% from initial levels\" (Chumakova, 1964), more than with \"(35-50 kg) executed in sets of 2-3 repetitions\", \"and it is even greater (by 28%) than that produced by a combination of both methods.\"\n\n**Why it works** — The authors present these as applications of the general principles (load set by the dynamics of the sport exercise). No mechanism is given per sport.\n\n**When to apply** — As reference doses when designing strength-endurance work for cyclic athletes. Inference: the skater squat progression (50 to 200-300 reps per set over 2-3 months) is an extreme local-endurance model with no obvious football use; the rower result (light, many reps beating heavy, low-rep sets for strength-endurance) is the transferable point.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The skaters' 80-85% 1RM conflicts with the 75-80% 1RM ceiling stated a page earlier (see [[heuristic--strength-endurance-load-tempo-and-the-75-80-percent-ceiling]]). Loads mix % of 1RM and % of bodymass. The Chumakova \"by 28%\" comparison is ambiguous (28 percentage points or 28% relative is not stated). No sample sizes, durations or outcome tests are given. These are Soviet-era single-source prescriptions.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: the skater squat progression from 50 to 200-300 reps per set before raising the weight and restarting with fewer reps (PDF p. 297 / printed p. 286).\n\n**Related** — [[heuristic--strength-endurance-load-tempo-and-the-75-80-percent-ceiling]]; [[heuristic--strength-endurance-under-demanding-sport-like-conditions]]; [[claim--strength-endurance-hypertrophy-and-cyclic-endurance]]; [[framework--strength-endurance-taxonomy-and-absolute-vs-partial]]\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"ec9d9ead-7fb6-44fa-b423-3d48156f8104","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"be4794d2-d960-4564-9235-dcbdf8d20737","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffahVtJ3GDMPnpCNtyuC","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01CvEE63FdXo3osyuTM4fhKv","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--strength-endurance-under-demanding-sport-like-conditions.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.5\"\ntopic: specific resisted strength-endurance work\ncategory: [strength-endurance, dynamic-correspondence, resisted-training]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Train Strength-Endurance Under Demanding Conditions With Movements Close to the Sport: Parachutes, Sleds, Weighted Vests, Boat Drag\n\n**What it is** — \"For developing strength-endurance it is always appropriate to strive to execute the work under demanding conditions, though with movements whose coordination and structure in space and time are close to those of the special exercise. For instance, a runner may run with a small parachute resisting him or pull a loaded sled in the preparatory phases (Popov, 1966). Similarly, gymnasts may use a heavy belt and vest (Zhilei, 1964) and rowers a special water-resistance device attached to the hull of the boat (Chuprun, 1966). In the case of the rowers, strength-endurance as measured by the number of pull-ups, the number of arm flexions in the prone position and the number of barbell presses in 30 seconds increased by 57.1%, 27.8% and 9.1%, respectively\" (PDF p. 297 / printed p. 286, verified against the page image).\n\nThe same idea appears earlier for speed-strength: \"good results are achieved by making the special exercise more demanding\", e.g. \"water resistance in rowing or very small loads on the limbs of track-and-field athletes and gymnasts\" (PDF pp. 277-278 / printed pp. 266-267; that passage belongs to 5.2.3).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reason is that the loaded work keeps the coordination and space-time structure of the sport movement. Inference: this is the dynamic-correspondence criteria applied to endurance work; the passage does not cite 4.3.\n\n**When to apply** — Inference: in football, sled pushes and pulls, resisted sprints and weighted-vest movement drills are the direct analogue, provided the load does not change the movement's structure. The rower test results show the gains can appear in general tests too.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — \"Demanding\" is not quantified; the authors' Ch4.3 parachute material warns that resistance can distort mechanics (see [[claim--parachute-resistance-tradeoffs-for-dynamic-correspondence]]). Note the gymnast citation is spelled \"Zhilei, 1964\" here and \"Zhalei, 1964\" a few lines earlier on the same page; probably the same source, but the book does not say. The rower percentages have no sample size or duration given.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: rowers training with a water-resistance device on the hull improved pull-ups by 57.1%, prone arm flexions by 27.8% and 30-second barbell presses by 9.1% (PDF p. 297 / printed p. 286).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--five-criteria-of-dynamic-correspondence]]; [[claim--parachute-resistance-tradeoffs-for-dynamic-correspondence]]; [[heuristic--parachute-resistance-sizing-progression-and-contrast-release]]; [[framework--strength-endurance-taxonomy-and-absolute-vs-partial]] (Ch3.2.2, \"execute the competition exercises themselves under demanding conditions\"); [[case-study--soviet-sport-specific-strength-endurance-prescriptions]]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":622,"cache_read_input_tokens":286453,"output_tokens":5053,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":330},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":622},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":5053,"cache_read_input_tokens":286453,"cache_creation_input_tokens":622,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":622,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01CvEE63FdXo3osyuTM4fhKv":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--strength-endurance-under-demanding-sport-like-conditions.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.5\"\ntopic: specific resisted strength-endurance work\ncategory: [strength-endurance, dynamic-correspondence, resisted-training]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Train Strength-Endurance Under Demanding Conditions With Movements Close to the Sport: Parachutes, Sleds, Weighted Vests, Boat Drag\n\n**What it is** — \"For developing strength-endurance it is always appropriate to strive to execute the work under demanding conditions, though with movements whose coordination and structure in space and time are close to those of the special exercise. For instance, a runner may run with a small parachute resisting him or pull a loaded sled in the preparatory phases (Popov, 1966). Similarly, gymnasts may use a heavy belt and vest (Zhilei, 1964) and rowers a special water-resistance device attached to the hull of the boat (Chuprun, 1966). In the case of the rowers, strength-endurance as measured by the number of pull-ups, the number of arm flexions in the prone position and the number of barbell presses in 30 seconds increased by 57.1%, 27.8% and 9.1%, respectively\" (PDF p. 297 / printed p. 286, verified against the page image).\n\nThe same idea appears earlier for speed-strength: \"good results are achieved by making the special exercise more demanding\", e.g. \"water resistance in rowing or very small loads on the limbs of track-and-field athletes and gymnasts\" (PDF pp. 277-278 / printed pp. 266-267; that passage belongs to 5.2.3).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reason is that the loaded work keeps the coordination and space-time structure of the sport movement. Inference: this is the dynamic-correspondence criteria applied to endurance work; the passage does not cite 4.3.\n\n**When to apply** — Inference: in football, sled pushes and pulls, resisted sprints and weighted-vest movement drills are the direct analogue, provided the load does not change the movement's structure. The rower test results show the gains can appear in general tests too.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — \"Demanding\" is not quantified; the authors' Ch4.3 parachute material warns that resistance can distort mechanics (see [[claim--parachute-resistance-tradeoffs-for-dynamic-correspondence]]). Note the gymnast citation is spelled \"Zhilei, 1964\" here and \"Zhalei, 1964\" a few lines earlier on the same page; probably the same source, but the book does not say. The rower percentages have no sample size or duration given.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: rowers training with a water-resistance device on the hull improved pull-ups by 57.1%, prone arm flexions by 27.8% and 30-second barbell presses by 9.1% (PDF p. 297 / printed p. 286).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--five-criteria-of-dynamic-correspondence]]; [[claim--parachute-resistance-tradeoffs-for-dynamic-correspondence]]; [[heuristic--parachute-resistance-sizing-progression-and-contrast-release]]; [[framework--strength-endurance-taxonomy-and-absolute-vs-partial]] (Ch3.2.2, \"execute the competition exercises themselves under demanding conditions\"); [[case-study--soviet-sport-specific-strength-endurance-prescriptions]]\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":3,"requestId":"req_011CffahGBYJ6sf9uCinrRgZ","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"0de12abf-3f76-4954-8411-37cdae188c4e","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:00:35.533Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"0de12abf-3f76-4954-8411-37cdae188c4e","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"624a8cec-33fa-4c0a-a445-c72ce718e6ab","agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CvEE63FdXo3osyuTM4fhKv","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--strength-endurance-under-demanding-sport-like-conditions.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"ab66d2ed-ac5e-47c7-9e3b-2802d29b039d","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:00:35.623Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"create","filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--strength-endurance-under-demanding-sport-like-conditions.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.5\"\ntopic: specific resisted strength-endurance work\ncategory: [strength-endurance, dynamic-correspondence, resisted-training]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Train Strength-Endurance Under Demanding Conditions With Movements Close to the Sport: Parachutes, Sleds, Weighted Vests, Boat Drag\n\n**What it is** — \"For developing strength-endurance it is always appropriate to strive to execute the work under demanding conditions, though with movements whose coordination and structure in space and time are close to those of the special exercise. For instance, a runner may run with a small parachute resisting him or pull a loaded sled in the preparatory phases (Popov, 1966). Similarly, gymnasts may use a heavy belt and vest (Zhilei, 1964) and rowers a special water-resistance device attached to the hull of the boat (Chuprun, 1966). In the case of the rowers, strength-endurance as measured by the number of pull-ups, the number of arm flexions in the prone position and the number of barbell presses in 30 seconds increased by 57.1%, 27.8% and 9.1%, respectively\" (PDF p. 297 / printed p. 286, verified against the page image).\n\nThe same idea appears earlier for speed-strength: \"good results are achieved by making the special exercise more demanding\", e.g. \"water resistance in rowing or very small loads on the limbs of track-and-field athletes and gymnasts\" (PDF pp. 277-278 / printed pp. 266-267; that passage belongs to 5.2.3).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reason is that the loaded work keeps the coordination and space-time structure of the sport movement. Inference: this is the dynamic-correspondence criteria applied to endurance work; the passage does not cite 4.3.\n\n**When to apply** — Inference: in football, sled pushes and pulls, resisted sprints and weighted-vest movement drills are the direct analogue, provided the load does not change the movement's structure. The rower test results show the gains can appear in general tests too.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — \"Demanding\" is not quantified; the authors' Ch4.3 parachute material warns that resistance can distort mechanics (see [[claim--parachute-resistance-tradeoffs-for-dynamic-correspondence]]). Note the gymnast citation is spelled \"Zhilei, 1964\" here and \"Zhalei, 1964\" a few lines earlier on the same page; probably the same source, but the book does not say. The rower percentages have no sample size or duration given.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: rowers training with a water-resistance device on the hull improved pull-ups by 57.1%, prone arm flexions by 27.8% and 30-second barbell presses by 9.1% (PDF p. 297 / printed p. 286).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--five-criteria-of-dynamic-correspondence]]; [[claim--parachute-resistance-tradeoffs-for-dynamic-correspondence]]; [[heuristic--parachute-resistance-sizing-progression-and-contrast-release]]; [[framework--strength-endurance-taxonomy-and-absolute-vs-partial]] (Ch3.2.2, \"execute the competition exercises themselves under demanding conditions\"); [[case-study--soviet-sport-specific-strength-endurance-prescriptions]]\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"0de12abf-3f76-4954-8411-37cdae188c4e","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
