{"parentUuid":"d653bb4f-e1d2-4796-931a-6091d86d4eb8","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"624a8cec-33fa-4c0a-a445-c72ce718e6ab","agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PFw2bsnqKMavydGdpPNaBR","type":"tool_result","content":"done\nmy-file lint lines above (none = clean)","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"048565c8-3d17-49cc-bfbc-2ba9a31504d0","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:01:01.388Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"done\nmy-file lint lines above (none = clean)","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"bashEditDiff":{"files":[{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--popular-plyometric-drills-work-through-other-mechanisms.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":19,"oldLines":7,"newStart":19,"newLines":7,"lines":[" - Books omit the tonic versus phasic myotatic reflex in relation to drill types, the positive supportive reaction, the crossed extensor reflex, phased breathing, the maximal-submaximal distinction, and concurrent and sequential prescription."," - \"the so-called plyometric drills in current Western texts are known to enhance performance, but it is not predominantly due to plyometric training, but more to the increased loading of eccentric contraction, the conditioning of connective tissue, the improvement of voluntary starting strength from the quasi-isometric state, the use of prestretch, and enhancement of acceleration strength by increasing the velocity of projectiles such as medicine balls\" (PDF p. 294 / printed p. 283)."," - The physiotherapy link: in PNF a therapist \"sometimes imposes a short, sharp jerk on an eccentrically or isometrically contracting muscle to produce strong muscle contraction in a weakened muscle\", principles that \"preceded sporting plyometrics by several decades\" (same page).","-- Related, from the classification passage: *pseudo-plyometrics* such as aerobics \"power moves\" and many \"water plyos\" lack \"an explosive rebound phase with a short coupling time\" (PDF p. 284 / printed p. 273, verified).","+- Related, from the classification passage: *pseudo-plyometrics* such as aerobics \"power moves\" and many \"water plyos\" lack \"an explosive rebound phase with a short coupling time\" (PDF p. 284 / printed p. 273, verified against the page image)."," "," **Why it works** — The text's argument is definitional: long amortisation phases fall outside the shock method, so the benefit must come from the other listed qualities. It gives no study separating these mechanisms."," "]}]},{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--maximal-plyometric-and-depth-jump-volume-and-rest.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":17,"oldLines":15,"newStart":17,"newLines":15,"lines":[" "," **Model 1: maximal plyometrics dosed like a maximal lift.**"," - \"*Maximal plyometrics* essentially are low-repetition activities ... Just like maximal strength training with weights, these powerful impulses are not meant to be imposed on the musculature every workout, nor are multiple repetitions even possible or advisable. Moreover, a rest interval of 2-4 minutes is recommended between successive repetitions and as much as 10-12 minutes between sets\" (PDF p. 283 / printed p. 272, verified against the page image).","-- Prescription item 6: \"The rest interval between sets of maximal plyometric exercises should be about 10 minutes for power development (strength-speed and speed-strength) and much shorter intervals with submaximal plyometrics to develop explosive-strength endurance\" (PDF p. 285 / printed p. 274, verified).","-- \"several books advocate sessions with over 100 repetitions of plyometric movements ... This volume of *maximal plyometrics* (unlike sub-maximal plyometrics) is neither productive nor safe for any athlete. Maximal plyometrics, like maximal weight lifting, involves single all-out repetitions separated by a few minutes of rest\" (PDF p. 293 / printed p. 282, verified).","+- Prescription item 6: \"The rest interval between sets of maximal plyometric exercises should be about 10 minutes for power development (strength-speed and speed-strength) and much shorter intervals with submaximal plyometrics to develop explosive-strength endurance\" (PDF p. 285 / printed p. 274, verified against the page image).","+- \"several books advocate sessions with over 100 repetitions of plyometric movements ... This volume of *maximal plyometrics* (unlike sub-maximal plyometrics) is neither productive nor safe for any athlete. Maximal plyometrics, like maximal weight lifting, involves single all-out repetitions separated by a few minutes of rest\" (PDF p. 293 / printed p. 282, verified against the page image)."," - \"classical plyometrics are 1 RM movements which require as much as several minutes of rest before recovery is complete\" (PDF p. 294 / printed p. 283, text layer)."," "," **Model 2: depth jumps in sets of 5-10 (credited to \"prolific experience\").**","-- Prescription item 4: \"As an initial guideline, the dosage of plyometric exercises should not exceed 5-8 repetitions in one set\" (PDF p. 285 / printed p. 274, verified).","+- Prescription item 4: \"As an initial guideline, the dosage of plyometric exercises should not exceed 5-8 repetitions in one set\" (PDF p. 285 / printed p. 274, verified against the page image)."," - Depth-jump item 1: with a complex of vertical jumps (Fig 5.11), \"Each exercise is executed in sets of 10 repetitions, with 1-15 minutes of rest between sets\" (PDF p. 286 / printed p. 275; the page image prints \"1-15\", recorded as printed).","-- Depth-jump item 2: \"The optimal dosage of depth-jumps with an energetic vertical take-off in one training session should not exceed 4 sets of 10 jumps for well-conditioned athletes and 2-3 sets of 5-8 jumps for lesser conditioned athletes. Easy running and relaxation exercises for a period of 10-15 minutes should be done between sets\" (same page, verified).","-- Depth-jump item 3: \"Well-conditioned athletes can execute depth-jumps 3 times per week for 2 sets of 10 jumps at the completion of technical training\" (same page, verified).","+- Depth-jump item 2: \"The optimal dosage of depth-jumps with an energetic vertical take-off in one training session should not exceed 4 sets of 10 jumps for well-conditioned athletes and 2-3 sets of 5-8 jumps for lesser conditioned athletes. Easy running and relaxation exercises for a period of 10-15 minutes should be done between sets\" (same page, verified against the page image).","+- Depth-jump item 3: \"Well-conditioned athletes can execute depth-jumps 3 times per week for 2 sets of 10 jumps at the completion of technical training\" (same page, verified against the page image)."," "," **The conflict.** Model 1 says multiple repetitions of maximal plyometrics are not \"possible or advisable\" and separates single reps by 2-4 minutes. Model 2 prescribes sets of 10 energetic depth jumps, up to 40 per session, with rest given only between sets. Model 2 might be read as submaximal work, but depth-jump item 2 specifies \"an energetic vertical take-off\", and the 0.75-1.15 m heights in Ch4 are for advanced jumpers. Rest between sets also varies: about 10 min (p. 274), 10-12 min (p. 272), 10-15 min of easy running (p. 275), and \"1-15 minutes\" (p. 275). The book does not reconcile them."," "]}]},{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--plyometric-surface-footwear-and-simple-apparatus.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":15,"oldLines":9,"newStart":15,"newLines":9,"lines":[" "," **What it is** — Surface and apparatus statements in 5.2.4:"," - \"Footwear or the floor covering should not be very soft or excessively shock absorbent, since this may impair ankle stability, diminish the storage of elastic energy in the SEC (e.g. tendons) and delay the triggering of the positive supportive reaction of the foot (see 6.1.2) when contacting the floor\" (PDF p. 284 / printed p. 273, verified against the page image).","-- The surface \"can also have a significant effect on the outcome of the exercise, as alluded to earlier in comments made about the alterations in motor skill and proprioceptive sensitivity associated with the use of soft-soled shoes and very resilient jumping surfaces. A little known finding is that plyometric and jumping drills performed according to carefully planned progressive patterns in sand can significantly enhance jumping skills in a short period, even as short as 5 days in duration (Montagni & Cardinale, 1996)\" (PDF p. 289 / printed p. 278, verified).","-- For depth landings: Dursenev and Raevsky used \"soft gymnastic mats\", and the authors advise landing \"on suitably firm shock-absorbing surfaces\" (PDF p. 288 / printed p. 277, verified).","-- Simple apparatus: a children's swing near a wall, where the feet push off the wall after swinging from \"a suitable height\", progressed by raising the backswing; longer ropes give a slower repetition rate; uprights in front allow upper-body rebounds. The \"Pogo-Stick\", with intensity \"determined by the height of rebound\", and boxers' skipping are also offered as \"powermetric\" tools (PDF p. 289 / printed p. 278, verified).","+- The surface \"can also have a significant effect on the outcome of the exercise, as alluded to earlier in comments made about the alterations in motor skill and proprioceptive sensitivity associated with the use of soft-soled shoes and very resilient jumping surfaces. A little known finding is that plyometric and jumping drills performed according to carefully planned progressive patterns in sand can significantly enhance jumping skills in a short period, even as short as 5 days in duration (Montagni & Cardinale, 1996)\" (PDF p. 289 / printed p. 278, verified against the page image).","+- For depth landings: Dursenev and Raevsky used \"soft gymnastic mats\", and the authors advise landing \"on suitably firm shock-absorbing surfaces\" (PDF p. 288 / printed p. 277, verified against the page image).","+- Simple apparatus: a children's swing near a wall, where the feet push off the wall after swinging from \"a suitable height\", progressed by raising the backswing; longer ropes give a slower repetition rate; uprights in front allow upper-body rebounds. The \"Pogo-Stick\", with intensity \"determined by the height of rebound\", and boxers' skipping are also offered as \"powermetric\" tools (PDF p. 289 / printed p. 278, verified against the page image)."," "," **Internal tension, recorded:** p. 273 warns against very soft, shock-absorbent surfaces for plyometrics, while p. 278 reports fast jumping-skill gains from progressive drills in sand, and p. 277 pairs a soft-mat landing study with advice to land on \"suitably firm shock-absorbing surfaces.\" The book does not reconcile these. Inference: they may refer to different aims (sand for skill and lower impact; firm surfaces for maximal shock rebounds), but the book does not say so."," "]}]}],"moreFiles":0,"changedFiles":["/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--popular-plyometric-drills-work-through-other-mechanisms.md","/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--maximal-plyometric-and-depth-jump-volume-and-rest.md","/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--plyometric-surface-footwear-and-simple-apparatus.md"]}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"d653bb4f-e1d2-4796-931a-6091d86d4eb8","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"048565c8-3d17-49cc-bfbc-2ba9a31504d0","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","attachment":{"type":"edited_text_file","filename":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--maximal-plyometric-and-depth-jump-volume-and-rest.md","snippet":""},"type":"attachment","uuid":"b3a5e1f4-40af-4221-b34f-f44393890294","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:01:01.406Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nNote: /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--maximal-plyometric-and-depth-jump-volume-and-rest.md changed on disk since you last read it. That's usually deliberate, so take it as the current state rather than reverting it; if the change looks wrong, say so rather than undoing it yourself — otherwise no need to call it out. The changes are not shown here; use Read if you need the current content.\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"b3a5e1f4-40af-4221-b34f-f44393890294","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","attachment":{"type":"edited_text_file","filename":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--plyometric-surface-footwear-and-simple-apparatus.md","snippet":"10\tingest-tier: full-text\n11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n12\t---\n13\t\n14\t# Plyometric Surfaces and Footwear: Not Too Soft for Rebounds, Yet Sand and Soft Mats Have Their Uses; Swings and Pogo Sticks as Cheap Powermetric Tools\n15\t\n16\t**What it is** — Surface and apparatus statements in 5.2.4:\n17\t- \"Footwear or the floor covering should not be very soft or excessively shock absorbent, since this may impair ankle stability, diminish the storage of elastic energy in the SEC (e.g. tendons) and delay the triggering of the positive supportive reaction of the foot (see 6.1.2) when contacting the floor\" (PDF p. 284 / printed p. 273, verified against the page image).\n18\t- The surface \"can also have a significant effect on the outcome of the exercise, as alluded to earlier in comments made about the alterations in motor skill and proprioceptive sensitivity associated with the use of soft-soled shoes and very resilient jumping surfaces. A little known finding is that plyometric and jumping drills performed according to carefully planned progressive patterns in sand can significantly enhance jumping skills in a short period, even as short as 5 days in duration (Montagni & Cardinale, 1996)\" (PDF p. 289 / printed p. 278, verified against the page image).\n19\t- For depth landings: Dursenev and Raevsky used \"soft gymnastic mats\", and the authors advise landing \"on suitably firm shock-absorbing surfaces\" (PDF p. 288 / printed p. 277, verified against the page image).\n20\t- Simple apparatus: a children's swing near a wall, where the feet push off the wall after swinging from \"a suitable height\", progressed by raising the backswing; longer ropes give a slower repetition rate; uprights in front allow upper-body rebounds. The \"Pogo-Stick\", with intensity \"determined by the height of rebound\", and boxers' skipping are also offered as \"powermetric\" tools (PDF p. 289 / printed p. 278, verified against the page image).\n21\t\n22\t**Internal tension, recorded:** p. 273 warns against very soft, shock-absorbent surfaces for plyometrics, while p. 278 reports fast jumping-skill gains from progressive drills in sand, and p. 277 pairs a soft-mat landing study with advice to land on \"suitably firm shock-absorbing surfaces.\" The book does not reconcile these. Inference: they may refer to different aims (sand for skill and lower impact; firm surfaces for maximal shock rebounds), but the book does not say so.\n23\t\n24\t**Why it works** — The p. 273 reasons are stated (ankle stability, SEC elastic storage, the foot's positive supportive reaction). No mechanism is given for the sand result. The swing physics is stated by the authors as rope length setting the oscillation time.\n25\t\n26\t**When to apply** — Choosing where to run maximal rebound work: a firm, not very soft surface and non-cushioned footwear, per p. 273. Inference: sand is an option for early-phase or high-volume submaximal jumping, consistent with the p. 278 finding, but not for maximal depth jumps.\n27\t\n28\t**When NOT to apply** — The Montagni & Cardinale (1996) result is cited without design or measures; \"jumping skills\" is not defined. The swing and pogo stick suggestions are the text's ideas, with no data."},"type":"attachment","uuid":"8aa8035f-3b10-4c75-8996-59ffd595b156","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:01:01.406Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nNote: /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--plyometric-surface-footwear-and-simple-apparatus.md changed on disk since you last read it. That's usually deliberate, so take it as the current state rather than reverting it; if the change looks wrong, say so rather than undoing it yourself — otherwise no need to call it out. Here are the relevant changes (shown with line numbers):\n10\tingest-tier: full-text\n11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n12\t---\n13\t\n14\t# Plyometric Surfaces and Footwear: Not Too Soft for Rebounds, Yet Sand and Soft Mats Have Their Uses; Swings and Pogo Sticks as Cheap Powermetric Tools\n15\t\n16\t**What it is** — Surface and apparatus statements in 5.2.4:\n17\t- \"Footwear or the floor covering should not be very soft or excessively shock absorbent, since this may impair ankle stability, diminish the storage of elastic energy in the SEC (e.g. tendons) and delay the triggering of the positive supportive reaction of the foot (see 6.1.2) when contacting the floor\" (PDF p. 284 / printed p. 273, verified against the page image).\n18\t- The surface \"can also have a significant effect on the outcome of the exercise, as alluded to earlier in comments made about the alterations in motor skill and proprioceptive sensitivity associated with the use of soft-soled shoes and very resilient jumping surfaces. A little known finding is that plyometric and jumping drills performed according to carefully planned progressive patterns in sand can significantly enhance jumping skills in a short period, even as short as 5 days in duration (Montagni & Cardinale, 1996)\" (PDF p. 289 / printed p. 278, verified against the page image).\n19\t- For depth landings: Dursenev and Raevsky used \"soft gymnastic mats\", and the authors advise landing \"on suitably firm shock-absorbing surfaces\" (PDF p. 288 / printed p. 277, verified against the page image).\n20\t- Simple apparatus: a children's swing near a wall, where the feet push off the wall after swinging from \"a suitable height\", progressed by raising the backswing; longer ropes give a slower repetition rate; uprights in front allow upper-body rebounds. The \"Pogo-Stick\", with intensity \"determined by the height of rebound\", and boxers' skipping are also offered as \"powermetric\" tools (PDF p. 289 / printed p. 278, verified against the page image).\n21\t\n22\t**Internal tension, recorded:** p. 273 warns against very soft, shock-absorbent surfaces for plyometrics, while p. 278 reports fast jumping-skill gains from progressive drills in sand, and p. 277 pairs a soft-mat landing study with advice to land on \"suitably firm shock-absorbing surfaces.\" The book does not reconcile these. Inference: they may refer to different aims (sand for skill and lower impact; firm surfaces for maximal shock rebounds), but the book does not say so.\n23\t\n24\t**Why it works** — The p. 273 reasons are stated (ankle stability, SEC elastic storage, the foot's positive supportive reaction). No mechanism is given for the sand result. The swing physics is stated by the authors as rope length setting the oscillation time.\n25\t\n26\t**When to apply** — Choosing where to run maximal rebound work: a firm, not very soft surface and non-cushioned footwear, per p. 273. Inference: sand is an option for early-phase or high-volume submaximal jumping, consistent with the p. 278 finding, but not for maximal depth jumps.\n27\t\n28\t**When NOT to apply** — The Montagni & Cardinale (1996) result is cited without design or measures; \"jumping skills\" is not defined. The swing and pogo stick suggestions are the text's ideas, with no data.\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"8aa8035f-3b10-4c75-8996-59ffd595b156","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","attachment":{"type":"edited_text_file","filename":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--popular-plyometric-drills-work-through-other-mechanisms.md","snippet":"14\t# Most \"Plyometric\" Drills in Popular Western Books Work Through Eccentric Loading, Connective Tissue, Starting Strength and Prestretch, Not the Shock Mechanism\n15\t\n16\t**What it is** — Under \"Popular Information on Plyometrics\" (PDF pp. 293-294 / printed pp. 282-283; p. 293 verified against the page image, p. 294 from the text layer):\n17\t- Popular Western texts (\"e.g. Chu, 1992; Radcliffe & Farantinos, 1985\") present jumping and rebound activities \"many of which do not constitute true plyometric training as originally conceived by Russian scientists\"; they are \"often supplementary or preparatory movements.\"\n18\t- \"Most books classify extended range swinging, passing or catching of medicine balls or other objects as 'plyometric', although the amortisation phase is too long to produce classical plyometrics.\" These drills still help \"eye-hand coordination, eccentric strength and starting strength, but they are not strictly plyometric.\"\n19\t- Books omit the tonic versus phasic myotatic reflex in relation to drill types, the positive supportive reaction, the crossed extensor reflex, phased breathing, the maximal-submaximal distinction, and concurrent and sequential prescription.\n20\t- \"the so-called plyometric drills in current Western texts are known to enhance performance, but it is not predominantly due to plyometric training, but more to the increased loading of eccentric contraction, the conditioning of connective tissue, the improvement of voluntary starting strength from the quasi-isometric state, the use of prestretch, and enhancement of acceleration strength by increasing the velocity of projectiles such as medicine balls\" (PDF p. 294 / printed p. 283).\n21\t- The physiotherapy link: in PNF a therapist \"sometimes imposes a short, sharp jerk on an eccentrically or isometrically contracting muscle to produce strong muscle contraction in a weakened muscle\", principles that \"preceded sporting plyometrics by several decades\" (same page).\n22\t- Related, from the classification passage: *pseudo-plyometrics* such as aerobics \"power moves\" and many \"water plyos\" lack \"an explosive rebound phase with a short coupling time\" (PDF p. 284 / printed p. 273, verified against the page image).\n23\t\n24\t**Why it works** — The text's argument is definitional: long amortisation phases fall outside the shock method, so the benefit must come from the other listed qualities. It gives no study separating these mechanisms.\n25\t\n26\t**When to apply** — Labelling a program's med-ball and jump drills honestly: Inference: a long-amortisation med-ball chest pass or rotational throw is a starting-strength and eccentric drill in the authors' terms, and should not be counted as shock-method volume or dosed with shock-method rest.\n27\t\n28\t**When NOT to apply** — `untested`: the list of \"other mechanisms\" is the text's assertion, with no data. The criticism of specific books is the author's opinion and was not checked against those books. Not a reason to drop these drills: the text says they \"are known to enhance performance.\"\n29\t\n30\t**Example** — The text's own: extended-range medicine ball swinging and catching, called plyometric in popular books, as an example of a drill whose amortisation is too long for classical plyometrics (PDF p. 293 / printed p. 282)."},"type":"attachment","uuid":"18dbd80b-5f6b-4915-890b-e8282ff5e10d","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:01:01.406Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nNote: /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--popular-plyometric-drills-work-through-other-mechanisms.md changed on disk since you last read it. That's usually deliberate, so take it as the current state rather than reverting it; if the change looks wrong, say so rather than undoing it yourself — otherwise no need to call it out. Here are the relevant changes (shown with line numbers):\n14\t# Most \"Plyometric\" Drills in Popular Western Books Work Through Eccentric Loading, Connective Tissue, Starting Strength and Prestretch, Not the Shock Mechanism\n15\t\n16\t**What it is** — Under \"Popular Information on Plyometrics\" (PDF pp. 293-294 / printed pp. 282-283; p. 293 verified against the page image, p. 294 from the text layer):\n17\t- Popular Western texts (\"e.g. Chu, 1992; Radcliffe & Farantinos, 1985\") present jumping and rebound activities \"many of which do not constitute true plyometric training as originally conceived by Russian scientists\"; they are \"often supplementary or preparatory movements.\"\n18\t- \"Most books classify extended range swinging, passing or catching of medicine balls or other objects as 'plyometric', although the amortisation phase is too long to produce classical plyometrics.\" These drills still help \"eye-hand coordination, eccentric strength and starting strength, but they are not strictly plyometric.\"\n19\t- Books omit the tonic versus phasic myotatic reflex in relation to drill types, the positive supportive reaction, the crossed extensor reflex, phased breathing, the maximal-submaximal distinction, and concurrent and sequential prescription.\n20\t- \"the so-called plyometric drills in current Western texts are known to enhance performance, but it is not predominantly due to plyometric training, but more to the increased loading of eccentric contraction, the conditioning of connective tissue, the improvement of voluntary starting strength from the quasi-isometric state, the use of prestretch, and enhancement of acceleration strength by increasing the velocity of projectiles such as medicine balls\" (PDF p. 294 / printed p. 283).\n21\t- The physiotherapy link: in PNF a therapist \"sometimes imposes a short, sharp jerk on an eccentrically or isometrically contracting muscle to produce strong muscle contraction in a weakened muscle\", principles that \"preceded sporting plyometrics by several decades\" (same page).\n22\t- Related, from the classification passage: *pseudo-plyometrics* such as aerobics \"power moves\" and many \"water plyos\" lack \"an explosive rebound phase with a short coupling time\" (PDF p. 284 / printed p. 273, verified against the page image).\n23\t\n24\t**Why it works** — The text's argument is definitional: long amortisation phases fall outside the shock method, so the benefit must come from the other listed qualities. It gives no study separating these mechanisms.\n25\t\n26\t**When to apply** — Labelling a program's med-ball and jump drills honestly: Inference: a long-amortisation med-ball chest pass or rotational throw is a starting-strength and eccentric drill in the authors' terms, and should not be counted as shock-method volume or dosed with shock-method rest.\n27\t\n28\t**When NOT to apply** — `untested`: the list of \"other mechanisms\" is the text's assertion, with no data. The criticism of specific books is the author's opinion and was not checked against those books. Not a reason to drop these drills: the text says they \"are known to enhance performance.\"\n29\t\n30\t**Example** — The text's own: extended-range medicine ball swinging and catching, called plyometric in popular books, as an example of a drill whose amortisation is too long for classical plyometrics (PDF p. 293 / printed p. 282).\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
