{"parentUuid":"f7f1bbc1-b1ba-4d4c-9a76-6558142820de","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d50c13ce-05db-402d-a20c-8f3393d035f7","agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SCAHEtu8KMQGpsfn74BxQG","type":"tool_result","content":"ok\n0\nTOTAL ISSUES: 1\n  30 0\n   1 kind: case-study\n  12 kind: claim\n   4 kind: framework\n  13 kind: heuristic\n   8 confidence: contested\n  18 confidence: supported\n   4 confidence: untested\nclaim--high-volume-plyometrics-do-not-build-rebound-fatigue-resistance.md\nclaim--strength-endurance-hypertrophy-and-cyclic-endurance.md\nclaim--prestretch-benefit-decay-time-differs-by-task.md\nheuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size.md\nheuristic--depth-jump-weekly-frequency-and-seasonal-placement.md\nheuristic--maximal-plyometric-and-depth-jump-volume-and-rest.md\nheuristic--plyometric-surface-footwear-and-simple-apparatus.md\nheuristic--strength-endurance-load-tempo-and-the-75-80-percent-ceiling.md","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"69d1a0ce-4b82-405b-bbaf-f2d96c8d7b2a","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:04:08.042Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"ok\n0\nTOTAL ISSUES: 1\n  30 0\n   1 kind: case-study\n  12 kind: claim\n   4 kind: framework\n  13 kind: heuristic\n   8 confidence: contested\n  18 confidence: supported\n   4 confidence: untested\nclaim--high-volume-plyometrics-do-not-build-rebound-fatigue-resistance.md\nclaim--strength-endurance-hypertrophy-and-cyclic-endurance.md\nclaim--prestretch-benefit-decay-time-differs-by-task.md\nheuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size.md\nheuristic--depth-jump-weekly-frequency-and-seasonal-placement.md\nheuristic--maximal-plyometric-and-depth-jump-volume-and-rest.md\nheuristic--plyometric-surface-footwear-and-simple-apparatus.md\nheuristic--strength-endurance-load-tempo-and-the-75-80-percent-ceiling.md","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"bashEditDiff":{"files":[{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--prestretch-benefit-decay-time-differs-by-task.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":33,"oldLines":7,"newStart":33,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," **When to apply** — When someone quotes one fixed \"the stretch benefit is gone after X ms\" number: the book shows the window depends on the task (unloaded elbow about 0.25 s; loaded bench press up to about 4 s). Inference: for loaded lifts a paused rep still keeps some prestretch benefit for a second or more, per Fig 5.6, while for unloaded fast rebounds the benefit is gone within a few tenths of a second, per Fig 5.7."," ","-**When NOT to apply** — Do not cite Fig 5.6 under a single Wilson year without noting the caption-text conflict. The figure values are read from small plots and are approximate. Fig 5.7 is Siff's own unpublished analysis; the book gives no subject numbers. The fibre-type and reflex explanations are proposals, not demonstrated here.","+**When NOT to apply** — Do not cite Fig 5.6 under a single Wilson year without noting the caption-text conflict. The figure values are read from small plots and are approximate. Fig 5.7 is Siff's own analysis: the book says it \"was derived from some of this work on upper-body non-impact plyometrics\", the research \"conducted in this field by the author in 1994\" (PDF p. 292 / printed p. 281), and gives no subject numbers or publication for it. The fibre-type and reflex explanations are proposals, not demonstrated here."," "," **Example** — The authors' own: unloaded explosive elbow flexion loses its prestretch benefit over 0.1-0.25 s (Fig 5.7), while the bench-press benefit decays over about 4 s (Fig 5.6)."," "]}]},{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--maximal-plyometric-and-depth-jump-volume-and-rest.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":27,"oldLines":7,"newStart":27,"newLines":7,"lines":[" - Depth-jump item 2: \"The optimal dosage of depth-jumps with an energetic vertical take-off in one training session should not exceed 4 sets of 10 jumps for well-conditioned athletes and 2-3 sets of 5-8 jumps for lesser conditioned athletes. Easy running and relaxation exercises for a period of 10-15 minutes should be done between sets\" (same page, verified against the page image)."," - Depth-jump item 3: \"Well-conditioned athletes can execute depth-jumps 3 times per week for 2 sets of 10 jumps at the completion of technical training\" (same page, verified against the page image)."," ","-**The conflict.** Model 1 says multiple repetitions of maximal plyometrics are not \"possible or advisable\" and separates single reps by 2-4 minutes. Model 2 prescribes sets of 10 energetic depth jumps, up to 40 per session, with rest given only between sets. Model 2 might be read as submaximal work, but depth-jump item 2 specifies \"an energetic vertical take-off\", and the 0.75-1.15 m heights in Ch4 are for advanced jumpers. Rest between sets also varies: about 10 min (p. 274), 10-12 min (p. 272), 10-15 min of easy running (p. 275), and \"1-15 minutes\" (p. 275). The book does not reconcile them.","+**The conflict.** Model 1 says multiple repetitions of maximal plyometrics are not \"possible or advisable\" and separates single reps by 2-4 minutes. Model 2 prescribes sets of 10 energetic depth jumps, up to 40 per session, with rest given only between sets. Inference: Model 2 might be read as submaximal work, but depth-jump item 2 specifies \"an energetic vertical take-off\", and the 0.75-1.15 m heights in Ch4 are for advanced jumpers. Rest between sets also varies: about 10 min (p. 274), 10-12 min (p. 272), 10-15 min of easy running (p. 275), and \"1-15 minutes\" (p. 275). The book does not reconcile them."," "," **Why it works** — Model 1's stated reasons: maximal plyometrics are \"extremely demanding on the central nervous system\" and impose \"great mechanical strain on the tendons, in particular\" (PDF p. 293 / printed p. 282). Model 2's stated basis is \"prolific experience with depth-jumps for developing jumping power\" (PDF p. 286 / printed p. 275). Neither gives fatigue or recovery data."," "]}]},{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--plyometric-training-coupling-under-0-15-s-feedforward-and-force-time-pattern.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":18,"oldLines":7,"newStart":18,"newLines":7,"lines":[" 2. **Feedforward control.** \"The activity is not accurately plyometric if the athlete relies upon ongoing feedback processes to control the isometric and concentric actions instead of upon feedforward programmes established before any movement begins\"; true plyometric training \"usually involves ballistic rather than cocontraction processes\" (same page)."," 3. **Force-time pattern.** Later the 0.15 s rule \"requires some qualification\" (PDF p. 281 / printed p. 270). \"One cannot simply distinguish between plyometric and non-plyometric solely on the basis of coupling times, otherwise one would have to classify jogging or even brisk walking as classical plyometrics, because the time taken for the ground reaction force to reach a maximum can be less than 0.15 second. One also has to take the force-time pattern and the rate of force development (RFD) into account\" (PDF p. 283 / printed p. 272, verified against the page image)."," ","-The same pages separate *plyometric actions*, which \"occur as part of many running, jumping, hurdling, striking and other rebounding movements in sport\", from *plyometric training*, \"which applies plyometric actions as a distinct training modality according to a definite methodology\" (PDF p. 279 / printed p. 268). Siff writes this passage (it names Verkhoshansky in the third person: \"Verkhoshansky has always consistently favoured the use of the term 'shock method'\"). He suggests calling the action \"stretch-shortening action\" and the training system \"powermetrics\", and dates the earliest published use of \"plyometrics\" to a Soviet publication (Zanon, 1966). He then concludes that arguing whether an action is \"classically plyometric or not becomes complex and academic, with no real benefit to the prescription of plyometric training\", and that it is better to recognise types of plyometric training (PDF p. 283 / printed p. 272; see [[framework--plyometric-training-classification-impact-intensity-and-function]]).","+The same pages separate *plyometric actions*, which \"occur as part of many running, jumping, hurdling, striking and other rebounding movements in sport\", from *plyometric training*, \"which applies plyometric actions as a distinct training modality according to a definite methodology\" (PDF p. 279 / printed p. 268). Siff writes this passage (it names Verkhoshansky in the third person: \"Verkhoshansky has always consistently favoured the use of the term 'shock method'\"). He suggests calling the action \"stretch-shortening action\" and the training system \"powermetrics\", and writes that \"The earliest published use of the word plyometrics seems to have been in a Soviet publication (Zanon, 1966).\" He then concludes that arguing whether an action is \"classically plyometric or not becomes complex and academic, with no real benefit to the prescription of plyometric training\", and that it is better to recognise types of plyometric training (PDF p. 283 / printed p. 272; see [[framework--plyometric-training-classification-impact-intensity-and-function]])."," "," **Why it works** — The authors' mechanism: the method \"relies on the production of a very brief explosive-isometric and eccentric-isometric phase which precedes the release of the elastic energy stored in the tendons and other components of the series elastic component\" (PDF p. 279 / printed p. 268). For why the time limit varies, see [[claim--prestretch-benefit-decay-time-differs-by-task]] (tonic versus phasic reflexes, fibre type). Inference: jogging passes the 0.15 s test but has a low, unhurried force-time curve, which is why the authors add the force-time and RFD test."," "]}]},{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--strength-endurance-load-tempo-and-the-75-80-percent-ceiling.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":26,"oldLines":7,"newStart":26,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," **When to apply** — Inference: for football, where strength-endurance means repeated forceful efforts (blocking, pushing a sled), the summary rule fits better than the 25-50% band: a heavy-enough load mixed with lighter or sport-simulating work. The 60-120 reps per minute tempo describes cyclic-sport endurance work more than gridiron work. Eric's baseline: for football the priority stack is alactic and aerobic capacity and power, with glycolytic conditioning treated as a \"worst case scenario\" absorbed through camp and practice (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 116). Inference: to-fatigue strength-endurance sets would add glycolytic load the department deliberately does not program."," ","-**When NOT to apply** — Do not quote the 75-80% ceiling as a firm rule without the 80-85% skater and 50-80% rower examples. The determinants list has no weightings. \"60% of the maximal work capacity time\" is not defined further (60% of the time to failure is the plain reading).","+**When NOT to apply** — Do not quote the 75-80% ceiling as a firm rule without the 80-85% skater and 50-80% rower examples. The determinants list has no weightings. \"60% of the maximal work capacity time\" is not defined further (Inference: 60% of the time to failure is the plain reading)."," "," **Example** — The authors' own: 25-50% 1RM at 60-120 reps per minute to fatigue, or to 60% of the maximal work time (PDF p. 296 / printed p. 285)."," "]}]}],"moreFiles":0,"changedFiles":["/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--prestretch-benefit-decay-time-differs-by-task.md","/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--maximal-plyometric-and-depth-jump-volume-and-rest.md","/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--plyometric-training-coupling-under-0-15-s-feedforward-and-force-time-pattern.md","/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--strength-endurance-load-tempo-and-the-75-80-percent-ceiling.md"]}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"f7f1bbc1-b1ba-4d4c-9a76-6558142820de","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
