{"parentUuid":"b8080a10-01be-4c06-89e8-5d38f4e44b9d","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac8240d61f5c36dd4","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cffb2tvfYqei4oDh9Mvmj","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I covered all of batch 5b and stopped where you asked, at the 5.3 heading. That gives 30 new entries: 25 for 5.2.4 and 5 for 5.2.5. I edited no existing entry, did not touch `_book.md` or the vault, and ran no git. The scope file is `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/_scope_supertraining_ch5b.txt`, and all entries are in `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/`.\n\n**By kind:** 13 heuristic, 12 claim, 4 framework, 1 case-study.\n\n**By confidence:** 18 supported, 8 contested, 4 untested, 0 proven.\n- The 4 untested are the material where Siff gives no data: asymmetric plyometrics, non-impact plyometrics, the popular-drills critique, and the brain / state-dependent learning passage.\n- Each of the 8 contested entries records the book contradicting itself, with both pages:\n  1. **Volume and rest.** On p.272 multiple reps of maximal plyometrics are not \"possible or advisable\", with 2-4 min between reps and 10-12 min between sets; p.282 says single all-out reps a few minutes apart. Against that, p.275 gives sets of 10 depth jumps, up to 4×10, with \"1-15\" min or 10-15 min between sets, and p.274 says 5-8 reps per set.\n  2. **Frequency.** On p.275, depth jumps must be at least 3-4 days before a technique session, yet well-conditioned athletes may do them 3 times a week \"at the completion of technical training.\"\n  3. **Main method.** On p.278, \"plyometrics should be the main method\" for highly qualified athletes, but on p.277-278 \"it is futile to rely predominantly on plyometric drills.\" This is recorded in both the method-choice entry and the Nicol entry.\n  4. **Prestretch decay.** The Fig 5.6 caption credits \"Wilson et al, 1991\", but the text gives the 4 s bench-press result to Wilson 1990 and the 0.37 s result to Wilson 1991.\n  5. **Surfaces.** On p.273 floors should not be soft, but p.278 reports gains from sand training, and p.277 pairs a soft-mat study with advice to land on \"firm shock-absorbing\" surfaces.\n  6. **Strength-endurance load.** On p.285, above 75-80% 1RM \"one cannot enhance endurance\", but p.286 prescribes 80-85% for skaters and 50-80% for male rowers.\n  7. **Hypertrophy and endurance.** On p.286, squats and presses \"hinder\" cyclic endurance; 4.4 (p.250-251) says strength training helps distance sport more than aerobic work does.\n\n**Physics:** I kept it in labelled Inference.\n- **Lewis formula:** 21.693 = 9.8 × √4.9, so it is body weight times the mean speed of a free fall through H. It is not take-off power.\n- **Drop height:** momentum at contact is m·√(2gh), so it rises with the square root of drop height. Doubling the height adds about 41%, not 100%.\n\n**Coordinator rows for `_book.md`:**\n\n| # | Section | Printed pp | PDF idx | Entries |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 5.2.4 | Development of Explosive Strength and Reactive Ability | 267-285 | 278-296 | 25 |\n| 5.2.5 | The Development of Strength-Endurance | 285-287 | 296-298 | 5 |\n\n- 5.2.4 starts partway down PDF 278; the top of that page is 5.2.3. 5.2.5 starts partway down PDF 296 and ends on PDF 298, just above the 5.3 heading.\n- Entries per 5.2.4 sub-heading:\n\n| Sub-heading | PDF pages | Entries |\n|---|---|---|\n| Lead-in and The Plyometric Method | 278-279 | 2 |\n| Discrete Training System | 279 | folded into the coupling-time entry |\n| Safety | 280-281 | 1 |\n| Fundamental Theory, incl. the Fig 5.8 classes and intensity scale | 281-284 | 4 |\n| Prescription of Plyometric Exercise, incl. the Nicol and surface material | 284-289 | 12 |\n| Asymmetric | 289-290 | 1 |\n| Non-Impact and Non-Impact in Sports Training | 290-292 | 1 |\n| Resisted and Water | 292-293 | 1 |\n| Popular Information | 293-294 | 2 |\n| Plyometrics and the Brain | 294-295 | 1 |\n| Various Shock Methods | 295-296 | 1 |\n\n- 5.2.5 is not split further: 5 entries.\n\n**Dedup:** I did not re-atomize the F = ma \"same force, different training\" idea, the Bosco fibre-type finding, the strength-deficit rule, or the 3.2.2 strength-endurance definitions, because existing entries already cover them. The new entries link to those instead.\n\n**Reciprocal links I suggest (not made):**\n\n| Existing entry | Link it to |\n|---|---|\n| `framework--shock-method-and-plyometric-terminology` | coupling-time heuristic; classification framework |\n| `claim--optimal-depth-jump-drop-height-for-power-vs-force` and `framework--reactivity-coefficient-and-depth-jump-test` | height heuristic; intensity heuristic |\n| `heuristic--strength-deficit-sets-training-method-choice` | brief-maximal-tension heuristic |\n| `heuristic--match-plyometric-prescription-to-joint-angle-and-fiber-type` | prestretch claim |\n| `framework--strength-endurance-taxonomy-and-absolute-vs-partial` | strength-endurance load heuristic |\n| `claim--strength-training-raises-haemoglobin-myoglobin-and-aids-distance-sport` | hypertrophy claim (the cross-chapter tension) |\n| `principle--equal-force-different-loading-methods` | heavy-squats claim |\n| Zatsiorsky `claim--reversible-muscular-action-is-a-trainable-skill-not-automatic` | coupling-time entry; cognitive entry |\n| Issurin `claim--residual-training-effect-durations-by-motor-ability` | frequency heuristic |\n\n**Other unclear spots in the source:**\n- \"1-15 minutes\" of rest is printed that way. It may be a misprint, so I quoted it as printed rather than correcting it.\n- \"28 repetitions per minute\": the first digit sits in the gutter shading of the page image.\n- \"all of them did 475 jumps\" (and the \"1472 push-offs\") does not say whether the count is per athlete or for the group.\n- The Fig 5.5 caption and the numbered text define electromechanical delay differently.\n- The Fig 5.8 tree ties each category to one intensity level; the text lists the three categories flat.\n- The two breathing rules differ: p.273 allows forced exhalation late in the push-off, while p.276 says the breath is never released during contact.\n- The same gymnast source is spelled \"Zhalei\" and \"Zhilei\" (1964).\n- The strength-deficit sentence on p.282 repeats p.273 and is grammatically broken.\n- The text points to \"see 1.5\" for the shock method, but the Ch1 entry is filed under 1.6.\n\n**Could not read:** nothing was unreadable. I rendered and opened PDF 278-289, 293 and 296-298; only entries citing those pages say \"verified against the page image\". PDF 290-292 and 294-295 are clean prose, and I cited them from the text layer.\n\n**Lint:** zero lines for my 30 files. The full output is:\n```\nsupertraining--verkhoshansky/_book.md: body em dash lines [12, 114, 115]\nTOTAL ISSUES: 1\n```\nThat one issue is in `_book.md`, which you own."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":162,"cache_read_input_tokens":305701,"output_tokens":2740,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":19},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":162},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2740,"cache_read_input_tokens":305701,"cache_creation_input_tokens":162,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":162,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011Cffb2e8fHEfTPuxT9q6Pi","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"5688bcc8-2de6-43e8-8ab3-53eeb577d821","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:04:40.174Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","se